":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"d3da530e-c1a8-466d-990c-e7d0be19d89e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgyM1mQMDKE4d3fs6p3tn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0113aAkgyNHdjNYpid1sk6Ug","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_2_Revu_Pour_Validation && sed -n 986,1048p ERN_Concurrency_Model_WC5.2.md","description":"Read WC5.2 C43-C46"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3889,"cache_read_input_tokens":107241,"output_tokens":433,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3889,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":433,"cache_read_input_tokens":107241,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3889,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3889},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":905,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0113aAkgyNHdjNYpid1sk6Ug":{"command":"cd /Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_2_Revu_Pour_Validation && sed -n 986,1048p ERN_Concurrency_Model_WC5.2.md","description":"Read WC5.2 C43-C46"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"559cf8bd-284d-4a11-b7f6-dfed84bb32b2","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgyM1m7jUDQyK3AJJY7xc","requestRef":"69e52cca-c5d2-41e2-8634-6b319271d973","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"88ad2cd4-7031-4af6-80bb-f6d41aa0a530","timestamp":"2026-11-12T16:25:41.855Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"611d81e9-f0c1-43b4-b72d-88a58900f3c7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"048ce1b3-8891-4aae-bdd2-33b7fc094ee0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hb5T5u991AT6CPs2Hfq4fR","type":"tool_result","content":"## C15. New Concurrent Transition — `deriveSync`\n\n`deriveSync` is a new candidate concurrent transition creating an ordinary derived `Modify` authority from `guarded` over `footprint(guarded)`.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. **Own-mode bound, retained from Spec 1 §43 and O1.1:** `guarded` is active and its **own mode permits `Modify`**. In the `Observe | Modify` mode order this requires own mode `Modify`, **even if `footprint(guarded)` is empty**.\n2. The complete applicable origin-chain and effective-anchor validity requirements of O1.1 hold.\n3. **Exclusive authorization:** `K_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, current_frame)` holds. This predicate is the **only** authorization for `deriveSync`. Neither the current-frame-holder nor the place-holder alternative of ordinary `ExercisableAt` independently enables this new transition. Ordinary O1 retains both alternatives unchanged.\n4. O1.2's leaf, subfootprint and effective-mode conditions hold for the full protected footprint and requested mode `Modify`.\n5. O1.3's structural exclusivity and I1 preservation conditions hold.\n6. O1.4's freshness, legal current-frame holder and resulting-state validity conditions hold.\n\n**Effect**\n\nA fresh, active authority is created with own mode `Modify`, origin `guarded`, full footprint `footprint(guarded)`, and holder equal to the executing context's current frame. No other access right is created.\n\nThis grant is ordinary after creation: `ExercisableAt` remains exactly as in Spec 1 §43 for its exercise, reborrowing, storage and legal release. The synchronized exception concerns **only the authorization for creating this derivative**; it does not weaken the source activity, own-mode bound, anchor, effective-mode or structural exclusivity requirements of O1.\n\n**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject` for the abstract safety transition; a busy controller may make the enclosing library acquisition temporarily non-enabled, as specified in C16.\n\n## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nThe library operation `acquire` combines synchronization-controller acquisition and `deriveSync` into one coherent abstract operation.\n\n**Outcome classification (proposed D-C1)**\n\n1. **`reject` — invalid request:** a failure of entry existence/liveness (C14.1), controller-access authority (C14.3, including the applicable Spec 1 §68.2 contract), or any other applicable authority, register-validity, origin, footprint, invariant, or Spec 1 safety precondition yields `reject`. These failures remain `reject` even when the controller is occupied or `grant ≠ none` in the same state.\n2. **Temporarily non-enabled — contention:** only after all non-contention validity and authority conditions hold, the entry's `grant ≠ none` (C14.2), or occupation of the controller by another acquisition under its valid protocol, makes `acquire` temporarily non-enabled. This is **not** `reject`, `fail`, or an additional `Outcome3` value; no successful transition is produced. Non-enablement may persist indefinitely.\n3. **`ok` — successful acquisition:** all validity and authority obligations hold, the grant is available and the controller is successfully acquired under its protocol, and the successful effect below is established.\n\n`reject` takes precedence over temporary non-enablement **for the same abstract state**; this is a classification of causes and does not assert that a concurrent call has an outcome independent of changing state. Resource `fail` outcomes of `alloc`/`issue` are outside this criterion.\n\nFor `acquire`, the authorization facts C14.1 and C14.3 are safety conditions; C14.2 is a contention condition. The combined `SyncAuthorized` predicate of C14 is therefore insufficient by itself to distinguish `reject` from temporary non-enablement. This does not alter the independent transition `deriveSync` or its C15 conditions.\n\nThe library must establish all non-dynamic safety preconditions before committing a successful acquisition.\n\nIts success:\n\n1. acquires the controller under the applicable synchronization protocol;\n2. creates the valid grant through `deriveSync`;\n3. records the resulting grant identity in `SyncEntry.grant`.\n\nNo partially successful abstract operation may expose an invalid ordinary state.\n\nThe controller's physical linearization, atomic ordering, and correspondence obligations are specified as proposals in C41, C48 and C49 and remain subject to `OB-C05`.\n\n## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\n`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n\nThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available as one coherent library transition.\n\n**Realization obligation, not an assumed abstract precondition:** The controller update must satisfy `K.SharedMutable` and the required event, atomicity and ordering discipline. The physical protocol and its correspondence are specified provisionally in C41, C48 and C49 and remain under `OB-C05`, rather than being treated as proven by the abstract transition.\n\n## C18. Abandoned Grants and Recovery **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nA grant may become inactive through an inherited legal release or frame exit without a corresponding `unlock`; the live registry entry may still record `grant = some g`. This can leave the mutex unavailable indefinitely without itself violating memory safety.\n\n`resetLock` is a candidate library recovery operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. Ordinary `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` holds under Spec 1 §43, requiring the appropriate exclusive exercise through the mutex's holder.\n2. No active descendant of `guarded` covers any protected leaf, as in the relevant no-descendant condition of Spec 1 §78.2.\n3. `ControlOf(entry)` resolves to an existing, usable controller **leaf**, as required by C21.3, and recovery does not invalidate surviving dependent authorities. The controller-initialization invariant is established separately for every live entry by C21.7.\n4. **Controller-access authority [WC5.2]:** The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)`, under the corresponding requirement of Spec 1 §68.2. This condition is independent of, and additional to, `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` in item 1. It authorizes the controller access required by the recovery protocol without granting any new authority.\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** when the applicable recovery and authority conditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** when any applicable authority, registry, dependent-validity, controller-validity, or Spec 1 precondition fails. Controller occupation does not mask such a failure as temporary non-enablement.\n\nThere is no newly introduced `resetLock` waiting outcome and no new prerequisite that the recorded grant first be `none`. This transition is the exceptional recovery protocol described here, not ordinary `unlock`.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe registered grant becomes `none` and the controller's logical state becomes available. Recovery does not revive an inactive grant and does not fabricate authority.\n\n**Realization obligation:** The exclusive recovery discipline and its physical controller update must meet `OB-C05` and the event constraints proposed in C41, C48 and C49; this is not presumed proved by the abstract state transition. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-RC establishes that the earlier C18.1–C18.3 allowed recovery with no controller right; C18.4 excludes that step in the amended model, while W-RL, W-RL-J, the S11 positive recovery and the W-RC positive counterpart remain admissible. The recorded grant may still be `none`.\n\n## C19. Synchronized Administration\n\nA grant protecting an administrative value may permit `MetaRight` only when it provides exercisable `Modify` over every required leaf of that administrative value.\n\nThe grant must therefore cover the entire required administrative footprint.\n\nThis does not bypass Spec 1 §36.\n\nIssuance, recycling, and reset operations retain their existing authority and dependency restrictions.\n\nThe lifetime of a block issued during synchronized administration is determined by its actual origin chain, holders, anchors, and ownership relations, not merely by the lifetime of the synchronization grant.\n\n---\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dbd6c9e8-cca8-4415-a9b4-7e8ab58815ca","timestamp":"2026-11-12T16:25:42.330Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## C15. New Concurrent Transition — `deriveSync`\n\n`deriveSync` is a new candidate concurrent transition creating an ordinary derived `Modify` authority from `guarded` over `footprint(guarded)`.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. **Own-mode bound, retained from Spec 1 §43 and O1.1:** `guarded` is active and its **own mode permits `Modify`**. In the `Observe | Modify` mode order this requires own mode `Modify`, **even if `footprint(guarded)` is empty**.\n2. The complete applicable origin-chain and effective-anchor validity requirements of O1.1 hold.\n3. **Exclusive authorization:** `K_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, current_frame)` holds. This predicate is the **only** authorization for `deriveSync`. Neither the current-frame-holder nor the place-holder alternative of ordinary `ExercisableAt` independently enables this new transition. Ordinary O1 retains both alternatives unchanged.\n4. O1.2's leaf, subfootprint and effective-mode conditions hold for the full protected footprint and requested mode `Modify`.\n5. O1.3's structural exclusivity and I1 preservation conditions hold.\n6. O1.4's freshness, legal current-frame holder and resulting-state validity conditions hold.\n\n**Effect**\n\nA fresh, active authority is created with own mode `Modify`, origin `guarded`, full footprint `footprint(guarded)`, and holder equal to the executing context's current frame. No other access right is created.\n\nThis grant is ordinary after creation: `ExercisableAt` remains exactly as in Spec 1 §43 for its exercise, reborrowing, storage and legal release. The synchronized exception concerns **only the authorization for creating this derivative**; it does not weaken the source activity, own-mode bound, anchor, effective-mode or structural exclusivity requirements of O1.\n\n**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject` for the abstract safety transition; a busy controller may make the enclosing library acquisition temporarily non-enabled, as specified in C16.\n\n## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nThe library operation `acquire` combines synchronization-controller acquisition and `deriveSync` into one coherent abstract operation.\n\n**Outcome classification (proposed D-C1)**\n\n1. **`reject` — invalid request:** a failure of entry existence/liveness (C14.1), controller-access authority (C14.3, including the applicable Spec 1 §68.2 contract), or any other applicable authority, register-validity, origin, footprint, invariant, or Spec 1 safety precondition yields `reject`. These failures remain `reject` even when the controller is occupied or `grant ≠ none` in the same state.\n2. **Temporarily non-enabled — contention:** only after all non-contention validity and authority conditions hold, the entry's `grant ≠ none` (C14.2), or occupation of the controller by another acquisition under its valid protocol, makes `acquire` temporarily non-enabled. This is **not** `reject`, `fail`, or an additional `Outcome3` value; no successful transition is produced. Non-enablement may persist indefinitely.\n3. **`ok` — successful acquisition:** all validity and authority obligations hold, the grant is available and the controller is successfully acquired under its protocol, and the successful effect below is established.\n\n`reject` takes precedence over temporary non-enablement **for the same abstract state**; this is a classification of causes and does not assert that a concurrent call has an outcome independent of changing state. Resource `fail` outcomes of `alloc`/`issue` are outside this criterion.\n\nFor `acquire`, the authorization facts C14.1 and C14.3 are safety conditions; C14.2 is a contention condition. The combined `SyncAuthorized` predicate of C14 is therefore insufficient by itself to distinguish `reject` from temporary non-enablement. This does not alter the independent transition `deriveSync` or its C15 conditions.\n\nThe library must establish all non-dynamic safety preconditions before committing a successful acquisition.\n\nIts success:\n\n1. acquires the controller under the applicable synchronization protocol;\n2. creates the valid grant through `deriveSync`;\n3. records the resulting grant identity in `SyncEntry.grant`.\n\nNo partially successful abstract operation may expose an invalid ordinary state.\n\nThe controller's physical linearization, atomic ordering, and correspondence obligations are specified as proposals in C41, C48 and C49 and remain subject to `OB-C05`.\n\n## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\n`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n\nThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available as one coherent library transition.\n\n**Realization obligation, not an assumed abstract precondition:** The controller update must satisfy `K.SharedMutable` and the required event, atomicity and ordering discipline. The physical protocol and its correspondence are specified provisionally in C41, C48 and C49 and remain under `OB-C05`, rather than being treated as proven by the abstract transition.\n\n## C18. Abandoned Grants and Recovery **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nA grant may become inactive through an inherited legal release or frame exit without a corresponding `unlock`; the live registry entry may still record `grant = some g`. This can leave the mutex unavailable indefinitely without itself violating memory safety.\n\n`resetLock` is a candidate library recovery operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. Ordinary `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` holds under Spec 1 §43, requiring the appropriate exclusive exercise through the mutex's holder.\n2. No active descendant of `guarded` covers any protected leaf, as in the relevant no-descendant condition of Spec 1 §78.2.\n3. `ControlOf(entry)` resolves to an existing, usable controller **leaf**, as required by C21.3, and recovery does not invalidate surviving dependent authorities. The controller-initialization invariant is established separately for every live entry by C21.7.\n4. **Controller-access authority [WC5.2]:** The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)`, under the corresponding requirement of Spec 1 §68.2. This condition is independent of, and additional to, `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` in item 1. It authorizes the controller access required by the recovery protocol without granting any new authority.\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** when the applicable recovery and authority conditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** when any applicable authority, registry, dependent-validity, controller-validity, or Spec 1 precondition fails. Controller occupation does not mask such a failure as temporary non-enablement.\n\nThere is no newly introduced `resetLock` waiting outcome and no new prerequisite that the recorded grant first be `none`. This transition is the exceptional recovery protocol described here, not ordinary `unlock`.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe registered grant becomes `none` and the controller's logical state becomes available. Recovery does not revive an inactive grant and does not fabricate authority.\n\n**Realization obligation:** The exclusive recovery discipline and its physical controller update must meet `OB-C05` and the event constraints proposed in C41, C48 and C49; this is not presumed proved by the abstract state transition. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-RC establishes that the earlier C18.1–C18.3 allowed recovery with no controller right; C18.4 excludes that step in the amended model, while W-RL, W-RL-J, the S11 positive recovery and the W-RC positive counterpart remain admissible. The recorded grant may still be `none`.\n\n## C19. Synchronized Administration\n\nA grant protecting an administrative value may permit `MetaRight` only when it provides exercisable `Modify` over every required leaf of that administrative value.\n\nThe grant must therefore cover the entire required administrative footprint.\n\nThis does not bypass Spec 1 §36.\n\nIssuance, recycling, and reset operations retain their existing authority and dependency restrictions.\n\nThe lifetime of a block issued during synchronized administration is determined by its actual origin chain, holders, anchors, and ownership relations, not merely by the lifetime of the synchronization grant.\n\n---","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"559cf8bd-284d-4a11-b7f6-dfed84bb32b2","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","root":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","branch":"formal/spec2-m4","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"611d81e9-f0c1-43b4-b72d-88a58900f3c7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"88ad2cd4-7031-4af6-80bb-f6d41aa0a530","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"048ce1b3-8891-4aae-bdd2-33b7fc094ee0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0113aAkgyNHdjNYpid1sk6Ug","type":"tool_result","content":"# X. Inter-Context Authority Handoff — Proposed Block D\n\n## C43. Context Capability and Current-Frame Exercise **[WC5.1]**\n\nDefine, provisionally, for state `s`, execution context `c`, and logical leaf `l`:\n\n```\ncap(s, c, l) ∈ { none, Observe, Modify }\n```\n\nFor the safe-handoff proof, it is the greatest **effective** mode on `l` attributable to `c` through active authorities held by its live frames, **including suspended frames**, or held in a place through a valid holder chain, **provided the attributing chain does not pass through the `guarded` authority of any live synchronization entry** **[WC5.1]**. This guarded-chain-excluding capability is `cap5` in the exploratory Lean model. Its `Attr5` attribution relation deliberately treats live `guarded` authorities as unavailable for attribution but permits a valid frame-held grant and its legally held descendants. It does not change the rights of any authority in the frozen Spec 1.\n\n`cap` is **not** `ExercisableAt`. The latter applies the frozen §43 rules to the executing context's **current frame** and is used to decide **kernel** operation preconditions; C22.6 separately restricts which successful kernel operations the **safe interface** may expose. `cap` summarizes guarded-chain-excluding contextual entitlement across the context's frames and relevant held places, including rights not currently exercisable from the top frame. Derivation, reborrowing and frame changes need not terminate a context's `cap` merely because an individual authority becomes inactive or non-exercisable. **[WC5.1]** The unrestricted contextual capability (`cap` in the earlier Lean exploration) is distinguished here as **raw contextual capability**, not the quantity used by L1/L2: W-GR refutes raw capability-loss locality.\n\nFor two distinct contexts, present `cap` values on a leaf are **incompatible** when at least one is `Modify`; for `Confined`, any two present capabilities are incompatible under the additional safe-storage contract. This is a proposed abstraction for L1/L2, not a new sequential mode or authority identity.\n\n## C44. Scope of Exclusive Handoff Ordering\n\nLet `e1` and `e2` be access events in **different contexts**, associated with the same logical leaf and conflicting byte ranges, where at least one is non-atomic. `HandoffOrdered` applies only if the pair is **exclusive**: its relevant context capabilities are incompatible under C43 and the declared storage discipline. It does not impose `hb` between two genuinely concurrent atomic accesses under compatible observation rights.\n\nTypical cases in scope are:\n\n1. accesses to ordinary non-`SharedMutable` storage, when a conflict necessarily includes an exclusive ordinary `write3`;\n2. a mixed atomic/non-atomic pair on `Atomic` storage, because C42 requires exclusive `Modify` for the ordinary member;\n3. conflicting accesses under `Confined`, because the safe-storage contract excludes simultaneous cross-context capacities;\n4. exclusive ordinary access pairs under `Synchronized`.\n\nConflicting **non-atomic** `writeShared` operations on `Synchronized` storage performed under shared compatible `Observe` rights are **not** covered by the exclusive-handoff premise. Their ordering is established by the particular `Synchronized` protocol, not by claiming that the shared rights are exclusive.\n\nThe scope is deliberately narrower than the class of all conflicts, and wider than just final restitution of a spawn parameter. It includes a capacity becoming available to a child **after** its `spawn`, and multi-context relays.\n\n## C45. Loss Marker, L1 and L2 — Scoped Authority Proofs and Open Event Obligations **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair in the scope of C44, write `e1` for the earlier access in the abstract trace, issued by `c′`, and `e2` for the subsequent conflicting access by `c ≠ c′`. Let `r(e1,e2,l)` be the **loss marker** that ends the period containing `e1` during which `cap(·,c′,l)` is incompatible with the capability used by `e2`.\n\nThis marker may denote a release, a parameter yield, a derivation that reduces the relevant effective mode, frame exit, termination, unlock, or a justified holder/ownership change. It is a **pure logical marker** if its effect is only an authority change; a concrete step can additionally emit physical memory or synchronization events. The required loss threshold depends on the later access: a later exclusive write requires no incompatible surviving capability in `c′`; a later shared `Observe` read may be permitted after `c′` has lost its `Modify` exclusivity while retaining compatible `Observe`.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Exploratory proof status (authority machine **and scoped abstract step-event model**, not a complete physical-event proof):**\n\n- **L1 — local capability loss:** `cap5_drop_own` is proved under `DDSound` for all **28 modeled kinds of authority-machine steps**, over reachable encapsulated traces: any strict reduction of a context's guarded-chain-excluding `cap5` originates in that context's own step. For an eligible direct-access exclusive pair, `lossStep_own` further proves a first strictly intermediate loss step `r` of the earlier context. In the one-event-per-step abstraction this gives `po(e1,r)`; placement among physical events inside a realized compound step remains open.\n- **L2 — state compatibility:** `cap5_compat` is proved: distinct contexts cannot simultaneously have incompatible `cap5` on the same leaf under the ordinary `Modify` exclusivity criterion. This is an authority-state theorem, not an ordering theorem.\n- **L2 — trace loss:** `handoff_loss_exists_C` is proved for conflicting **`read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`** step-access pairs, at least one requiring `Modify`, over `EncapsulatedTrace5C` (the direct-exercise `ChainExcl` restriction, together with existing encapsulation and controller restrictions). It identifies a strictly intervening step of the earlier context reducing `cap5` on the leaf. The result transfers to the stronger tested `EncapsulatedTrace5R` by trace inclusion. Without this chain restriction, `handoff_loss_exists` is **refuted** under `EncapsulatedTrace5` by W-GC; `cap5_sound` is likewise refuted there. `cap5_sound_C` is proved under `EncapsulatedTrace5C`.\n\nThese are scoped results from the exploratory Lean formalization, **not** claims that C22.6 has been encoded for every operation or that the complete data-race-freedom theorem has been established. **[WC5.1 — M4]** `handoffOrdered_no_race` and its strict-graph analogue `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` prove that, given `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`, eligible exclusive direct-access pairs are race-free in the corresponding abstract graph. They do **not** prove that arbitrary safe executions always satisfy C46. Full step-to-physical-event correspondence, access coverage beyond these three named primitives, physical `po` mapping, and actual protocol `rf`/`hb` remain obligations.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4]** In the one-event-per-step abstraction, `lossStep_own` identifies the first relevant `r` and establishes its own-context, strictly intermediate position and hence the step-level `po` order. The exact **physical** marker position inside a realized compound step, partial byte footprints, and the concrete `po`/`rf`/`sw`/`hb` mapping remain open. A kernel-admitted trace outside C22.6 may refute the safe handoff premises without changing any frozen rule; such counterexamples must be retained and reported rather than concealed by hypotheses.\n\n## C46. HandoffOrdered — D-C10 **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair within C44's scope, let `r(e1,e2,l)` denote the loss marker identified by the provisional capacity-period construction in C45. The **sole ordering requirement of `HandoffOrdered`** is:\n\n\\[\n  HandoffOrdered(e1,e2,l) \\;:\\!\\iff\\; hb(r(e1,e2,l),e2).\n\\]\n\n**[WC5.1]** The **authority-step parts** of L1 and L2 now have the restricted Lean proofs stated in C45 for `cap5` and the `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` access fragment under guarded-chain exclusion. These proofs are **not additional preconditions** of `HandoffOrdered`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The later `lossStep_own` proof establishes `po(e1,r)` for the scoped abstract one-event-per-step pairs; no **physical** `po` placement or concrete `hb` edge follows merely from authority proof. If the authority machine or concrete realization fails to provide a marker for a relevant pair, that is an unfulfilled proof obligation or counterexample, not permission to assume a hidden marker. The existence of **some** unrelated `sw` event is insufficient. When the event-model mapping has also established `po(e1,r)`, the protocol requirement `hb(r,e2)` implies `hb(e1,e2)` by transitivity. This is a **composition argument**, now proved for the abstract direct-access graph (`handoffOrdered_no_race`, `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`) under its stated run assumptions: C46 still specifies the ordering each admissible handoff protocol must justify, not a claim that R4 or an authority-only trace establishes physical `hb`. The requirement itself is unchanged.\n\nThe requirement applies to both **transmission** and **restitution** of authority. A bare inherited O3 `release` may restore an origin's effective right in the globally coherent abstract state, but it generates **no** `sw` by itself. The resulting trace can be kernel-`ok` and nevertheless be excluded from `SafeConcurrentTrace` if a later cross-context access lacks the required order.\n\n`join` is one reference protocol, not the only legal completion/handoff mechanism (C8). Other mechanisms may be added if they prove the requisite `hb` relation without introducing a new ambient authority source or weakening inherited invariants. Safe-language constructs that ensure these obligations belong to Specs 3/4.\n\n---\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5840dcc5-0182-43fa-89bf-b80baa3bc775","timestamp":"2026-11-12T16:25:42.337Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# X. Inter-Context Authority Handoff — Proposed Block D\n\n## C43. Context Capability and Current-Frame Exercise **[WC5.1]**\n\nDefine, provisionally, for state `s`, execution context `c`, and logical leaf `l`:\n\n```\ncap(s, c, l) ∈ { none, Observe, Modify }\n```\n\nFor the safe-handoff proof, it is the greatest **effective** mode on `l` attributable to `c` through active authorities held by its live frames, **including suspended frames**, or held in a place through a valid holder chain, **provided the attributing chain does not pass through the `guarded` authority of any live synchronization entry** **[WC5.1]**. This guarded-chain-excluding capability is `cap5` in the exploratory Lean model. Its `Attr5` attribution relation deliberately treats live `guarded` authorities as unavailable for attribution but permits a valid frame-held grant and its legally held descendants. It does not change the rights of any authority in the frozen Spec 1.\n\n`cap` is **not** `ExercisableAt`. The latter applies the frozen §43 rules to the executing context's **current frame** and is used to decide **kernel** operation preconditions; C22.6 separately restricts which successful kernel operations the **safe interface** may expose. `cap` summarizes guarded-chain-excluding contextual entitlement across the context's frames and relevant held places, including rights not currently exercisable from the top frame. Derivation, reborrowing and frame changes need not terminate a context's `cap` merely because an individual authority becomes inactive or non-exercisable. **[WC5.1]** The unrestricted contextual capability (`cap` in the earlier Lean exploration) is distinguished here as **raw contextual capability**, not the quantity used by L1/L2: W-GR refutes raw capability-loss locality.\n\nFor two distinct contexts, present `cap` values on a leaf are **incompatible** when at least one is `Modify`; for `Confined`, any two present capabilities are incompatible under the additional safe-storage contract. This is a proposed abstraction for L1/L2, not a new sequential mode or authority identity.\n\n## C44. Scope of Exclusive Handoff Ordering\n\nLet `e1` and `e2` be access events in **different contexts**, associated with the same logical leaf and conflicting byte ranges, where at least one is non-atomic. `HandoffOrdered` applies only if the pair is **exclusive**: its relevant context capabilities are incompatible under C43 and the declared storage discipline. It does not impose `hb` between two genuinely concurrent atomic accesses under compatible observation rights.\n\nTypical cases in scope are:\n\n1. accesses to ordinary non-`SharedMutable` storage, when a conflict necessarily includes an exclusive ordinary `write3`;\n2. a mixed atomic/non-atomic pair on `Atomic` storage, because C42 requires exclusive `Modify` for the ordinary member;\n3. conflicting accesses under `Confined`, because the safe-storage contract excludes simultaneous cross-context capacities;\n4. exclusive ordinary access pairs under `Synchronized`.\n\nConflicting **non-atomic** `writeShared` operations on `Synchronized` storage performed under shared compatible `Observe` rights are **not** covered by the exclusive-handoff premise. Their ordering is established by the particular `Synchronized` protocol, not by claiming that the shared rights are exclusive.\n\nThe scope is deliberately narrower than the class of all conflicts, and wider than just final restitution of a spawn parameter. It includes a capacity becoming available to a child **after** its `spawn`, and multi-context relays.\n\n## C45. Loss Marker, L1 and L2 — Scoped Authority Proofs and Open Event Obligations **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair in the scope of C44, write `e1` for the earlier access in the abstract trace, issued by `c′`, and `e2` for the subsequent conflicting access by `c ≠ c′`. Let `r(e1,e2,l)` be the **loss marker** that ends the period containing `e1` during which `cap(·,c′,l)` is incompatible with the capability used by `e2`.\n\nThis marker may denote a release, a parameter yield, a derivation that reduces the relevant effective mode, frame exit, termination, unlock, or a justified holder/ownership change. It is a **pure logical marker** if its effect is only an authority change; a concrete step can additionally emit physical memory or synchronization events. The required loss threshold depends on the later access: a later exclusive write requires no incompatible surviving capability in `c′`; a later shared `Observe` read may be permitted after `c′` has lost its `Modify` exclusivity while retaining compatible `Observe`.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Exploratory proof status (authority machine **and scoped abstract step-event model**, not a complete physical-event proof):**\n\n- **L1 — local capability loss:** `cap5_drop_own` is proved under `DDSound` for all **28 modeled kinds of authority-machine steps**, over reachable encapsulated traces: any strict reduction of a context's guarded-chain-excluding `cap5` originates in that context's own step. For an eligible direct-access exclusive pair, `lossStep_own` further proves a first strictly intermediate loss step `r` of the earlier context. In the one-event-per-step abstraction this gives `po(e1,r)`; placement among physical events inside a realized compound step remains open.\n- **L2 — state compatibility:** `cap5_compat` is proved: distinct contexts cannot simultaneously have incompatible `cap5` on the same leaf under the ordinary `Modify` exclusivity criterion. This is an authority-state theorem, not an ordering theorem.\n- **L2 — trace loss:** `handoff_loss_exists_C` is proved for conflicting **`read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`** step-access pairs, at least one requiring `Modify`, over `EncapsulatedTrace5C` (the direct-exercise `ChainExcl` restriction, together with existing encapsulation and controller restrictions). It identifies a strictly intervening step of the earlier context reducing `cap5` on the leaf. The result transfers to the stronger tested `EncapsulatedTrace5R` by trace inclusion. Without this chain restriction, `handoff_loss_exists` is **refuted** under `EncapsulatedTrace5` by W-GC; `cap5_sound` is likewise refuted there. `cap5_sound_C` is proved under `EncapsulatedTrace5C`.\n\nThese are scoped results from the exploratory Lean formalization, **not** claims that C22.6 has been encoded for every operation or that the complete data-race-freedom theorem has been established. **[WC5.1 — M4]** `handoffOrdered_no_race` and its strict-graph analogue `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` prove that, given `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`, eligible exclusive direct-access pairs are race-free in the corresponding abstract graph. They do **not** prove that arbitrary safe executions always satisfy C46. Full step-to-physical-event correspondence, access coverage beyond these three named primitives, physical `po` mapping, and actual protocol `rf`/`hb` remain obligations.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4]** In the one-event-per-step abstraction, `lossStep_own` identifies the first relevant `r` and establishes its own-context, strictly intermediate position and hence the step-level `po` order. The exact **physical** marker position inside a realized compound step, partial byte footprints, and the concrete `po`/`rf`/`sw`/`hb` mapping remain open. A kernel-admitted trace outside C22.6 may refute the safe handoff premises without changing any frozen rule; such counterexamples must be retained and reported rather than concealed by hypotheses.\n\n## C46. HandoffOrdered — D-C10 **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair within C44's scope, let `r(e1,e2,l)` denote the loss marker identified by the provisional capacity-period construction in C45. The **sole ordering requirement of `HandoffOrdered`** is:\n\n\\[\n  HandoffOrdered(e1,e2,l) \\;:\\!\\iff\\; hb(r(e1,e2,l),e2).\n\\]\n\n**[WC5.1]** The **authority-step parts** of L1 and L2 now have the restricted Lean proofs stated in C45 for `cap5` and the `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` access fragment under guarded-chain exclusion. These proofs are **not additional preconditions** of `HandoffOrdered`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The later `lossStep_own` proof establishes `po(e1,r)` for the scoped abstract one-event-per-step pairs; no **physical** `po` placement or concrete `hb` edge follows merely from authority proof. If the authority machine or concrete realization fails to provide a marker for a relevant pair, that is an unfulfilled proof obligation or counterexample, not permission to assume a hidden marker. The existence of **some** unrelated `sw` event is insufficient. When the event-model mapping has also established `po(e1,r)`, the protocol requirement `hb(r,e2)` implies `hb(e1,e2)` by transitivity. This is a **composition argument**, now proved for the abstract direct-access graph (`handoffOrdered_no_race`, `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`) under its stated run assumptions: C46 still specifies the ordering each admissible handoff protocol must justify, not a claim that R4 or an authority-only trace establishes physical `hb`. The requirement itself is unchanged.\n\nThe requirement applies to both **transmission** and **restitution** of authority. A bare inherited O3 `release` may restore an origin's effective right in the globally coherent abstract state, but it generates **no** `sw` by itself. The resulting trace can be kernel-`ok` and nevertheless be excluded from `SafeConcurrentTrace` if a later cross-context access lacks the required order.\n\n`join` is one reference protocol, not the only legal completion/handoff mechanism (C8). Other mechanisms may be added if they prove the requisite `hb` relation without introducing a new ambient authority source or weakening inherited invariants. Safe-language constructs that ensure these obligations belong to Specs 3/4.\n\n---","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"559cf8bd-284d-4a11-b7f6-dfed84bb32b2","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","root":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","branch":"formal/spec2-m4","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"88ad2cd4-7031-4af6-80bb-f6d41aa0a530","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"d3da530e-c1a8-466d-990c-e7d0be19d89e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"048ce1b3-8891-4aae-bdd2-33b7fc094ee0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CHRAFrKGaxt3qJpF6gpv44","type":"tool_result","content":"# III. Invariants and Correspondence\n\n## C20. Concurrent Frame and Authority Well-Formedness\n\n`ConcurrentWF` extends frame well-formedness to multiple local stacks and one global parent relationship.\n\nIt must preserve the applicable structural, ownership, holder, and authority conditions of Spec 1.\n\nEvery inherited transition and every new concurrent transition must preserve the applicable global invariants.\n\nReparenting must preserve extended ancestry and validity properties.\n\n## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n\nFor the reference mutex abstraction, the following state properties must be preserved over all **live** entries, except the bound-identity property in item 6, which applies to every entry:\n\n1. **Guarded authority:** `guarded` is active, has own mode `Modify`, and is held by a usable place.\n2. **Grant record:** If `grant = some g`, then the recorded authority exists and `origin(g) = guarded` and `footprint(g) = footprint(guarded)`. `g` need not be active; the invariant must handle legal release and reanchoring without silently weakening these equalities.\n3. **Controller validity:** `ControlOf(entry)` identifies an existing usable **leaf** associated with the holder `slot` and its structural mutex parent `M`.\n4. **Controller uniqueness:** Distinct live entries have distinct `ControlOf` leaves; the guarded-identity uniqueness guard is also respected. The same controller cannot be registered twice under different guarded identities.\n5. **`GrantSubtree`:** If `grant = some g` and `g` is active, every active descendant of `guarded` covering a protected leaf is either `g` itself or a descendant of `g`. This is a distinct invariant, necessary to restore grant availability after legal `unlock`.\n6. **`RegBound`:** Every registered `guarded` identity denotes an existing authority identity, even when its entry is no longer live. This prevents a dormant entry from becoming live merely when a fresh authority is created with its identity.\n7. **Structural initialization:** For each live entry, every structural leaf of the place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n\n`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n\nThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n\n## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6)]**\n\nThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n\n```\nlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n```\n\nIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n\nFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n\n1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`. No independent ordinary derivative may be issued directly from `guarded` while the entry is live.\n2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n4. **Controller-access and state-integrity protocol (WC5 D-C6) [WC5.2]:** For every live synchronization entry, the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when the acting frame holds an applicable `Observe` right under C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3`, out-of-protocol `writeShared`, or any other operation outside the validated controller protocol whose actual access or effect footprint includes the controller leaf.\n\n   This restriction covers physical reads and writes of the controller, changes to its initialization state, and changes that make the controller leaf itself the holder or owner of an authority or allocated resource.\n\n   In particular, while the entry remains live, the restriction excludes:\n   - `store` targeting the controller leaf;\n   - `issue` using the controller leaf as its destination;\n   - `alloc` using the controller leaf itself as the owner of a newly allocated root;\n   - `alloc` whose provider-metadata accesses may write the controller leaf;\n   - any other inherited or compound operation producing an equivalent access or effect on that leaf.\n\n   The restriction is evaluated against the leaves actually affected by an operation, including relevant physical metadata accesses. Merely naming a structural place containing the controller does not establish that the controller leaf itself is accessed or modified.\n\n   In particular, `alloc(owner = M)` is not excluded solely because `ControlOf(entry)` is a structural leaf of `M`. Ownership of storage by the mutex value remains eligible under C13.5. Such an allocation must nevertheless satisfy every applicable inherited precondition and must not independently access or alter the controller leaf through its provider, destination, or other effects.\n\n   A change solely to a dependency relation, without a physical controller access or another prohibited controller-state effect, is not excluded by this item merely because it concerns the controller. All applicable inherited dependency and lifetime requirements remain in force.\n\n   An authority whose footprint includes the controller does not, by that fact alone, constitute an access or effect on the controller.\n\n   Valid internal controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized `resetLock` remain within their respective validated protocols. Controller initialization through `newMutex` remains governed by C13.\n\n   A legal transfer of the whole mutex value `M`, including by-value `spawn`, or of a structurally enclosing value remains eligible under C22.3 and the unchanged O4 preconditions. Operations that make the entry dead remain eligible under C12, C22.5, and the applicable inherited lifetime rules. These exceptions do not waive the preservation of `SyncInv` or the applicable realization obligations.\n\n   This requirement restricts the safe abstraction, not the inherited kernel transitions or their outcomes. A kernel operation may independently return `ok` without being admitted by the safe mutex interface.\n\n   Enforcement of the safe interface belongs to Specs 4 and 10. Complete physical-access and effect-footprint coverage, controller correspondence, and preservation remain obligations under C23, C25, C29, `OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, and `OB-C28`.\n5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n\n6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n\n   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n\n   **Verification boundary [WC5.1]:** `ChainExcl` checks the direct-exercise fragment (`derive`, `split`, `read3`, `write3`, `writeShared`), and `PlaceExcl` checks selected `HasRight` premises; their conjunction is represented exploratorily by `Encap5R`. Both guard-exclusion witnesses and the normal mutex, arena and whole-mutex-transfer witnesses have been checked in that fragment. The complete coverage of all rights and composite phases, including the `resetLock` exception, remains `OB-C28`. A kernel-`ok` step that violates this requirement is excluded by the safe abstraction but does not alter its kernel outcome.\n\nRequirements 1–6 all constrain the allowed traces of the safe abstraction **[WC5.1]**. In particular, requirement 4 excludes ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` from `EncapsulatedTrace` as required by C51, while preserving O9 as a kernel operation. Requirement 6 also excludes accesses and other rights justified through a live `guarded` without the relevant grant. Their enforcement must follow from encapsulation and the public interface, rather than hidden runtime constraints or undisclosed hypotheses on the kernel. Preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under these explicit restrictions is the proof obligation stated in C23.\n\nRequirement 4 is **also** a physical realization/interface obligation; its enforcement is not falsely claimed to follow merely from the authority-only predicate `GrantDiscipline` or the abstract machine `S_C`.\n\n**[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** The final controller-footprint restriction (`CtlRestrF`, `Encap7`/`Run7`) rejects kernel-`ok` out-of-protocol `store`, `issue`, and `alloc(owner = ctl)` while admitting their tested positive counterparts. It admits `depend(ctl, a)` where no physical controller access is represented and `alloc(owner = M)` when the controller itself is unaffected; an `alloc` whose provider includes `ctl` is excluded conservatively for potential metadata writes, which the current kernel-effect model does **not** exhibit. The 28 modeled step kinds have been classified without an admitted out-of-protocol controller-footprint counterexample; `completeness7_run` proves graph-footprint coverage for a live entry under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. This is a scoped formal abstraction result, not a proof of all target physical accesses (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C23. Preservation of Ordinary and Concurrent Operations\n\nFormalization must demonstrate preservation of `ConcurrentWF`, the complete `SyncInv` (including controller uniqueness, structural initialization, `GrantSubtree` and `RegBound`) and `GrantDiscipline` for every applicable inherited and new transition, including `transfer` and by-value `spawn`, under the explicit safe-abstraction restrictions C22.1–3 and C22.5. The reference abstract trace relation must include by-value `spawn` and must not silently omit any applicable kernel operation.\n\nFor inherited operations, the kernel transition remains governed by the unmodified Spec 1. An operation that is a valid kernel step but not exposed by the safe mutex abstraction may violate the **library's** trace discipline without constituting a memory-safety counterexample against the kernel.\n\nA dead entry or recorded inactive grant is permitted where its contract allows it. A live entry with an invalid positional controller, duplicate controller or unauthorized grant is not a valid safe-abstraction state.\n\nResulting-state invariants must be **proved**, not included as circular preconditions when their preservation is the proof obligation. Any lemma conditional on encapsulation must expose that condition in its theorem statement or the reachable-trace assumptions.\n\n## C24. Sequential Conservativity\n\nFor corresponding single-context states and inherited operations O1–O16:\n\n- preconditions and outcomes coincide;\n- projected effects coincide;\n- authority exercise retains the Spec 1 definition;\n- sequential ancestry is recovered;\n- the applicable sequential invariants are preserved.\n\nThe reachable sequential domain under inherited operations must correspond to the sequential domain of Spec 1 under the stated state correspondence.\n\nA transition history involving additional contexts must not return to an invalid single-context authority state.\n\n## C25. Abstract-to-Concrete Realization Obligations\n\nThe abstract machine imposes no universal runtime authority metadata. The synchronization library may maintain justified implementation metadata; the frozen Spec 1 §4 does not forbid it.\n\nA sound implementation of the reference mutex must prove correspondence between the external registry and physical controller, preserve `ControlOf` under legal whole-value transfers, and enforce safe-interface encapsulation including C22.1–6. In particular, the prohibition of `endUse` on `ctl` or a strict structural subplace of `slot` is an interface property; the unchanged kernel O12 remains available to programs outside that abstraction contract.\n\nSuccessful controller acquisition must imply the remaining `deriveSync` obligations through proven state invariants and contractual authorization facts. For the reference mutex, authorization is instantiated using **only** the entry's liveness, grant availability and the requesting frame's controller access. Its proof may use the global kernel invariants and the synchronization invariants but may not place its desired conclusion inside the definition of `SyncAuthorized`.\n\nThe ambition to check only the physical lock word at runtime is a reference optimization target, not a universal requirement of Spec 1. The exact atomicity, event-ordering and data-race obligations are deferred to `OB-C01`–`OB-C06`, with controller realization specifically under `OB-C05`.\n\n---","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8cad8a26-70e8-40ec-a57d-7e204f311bf3","timestamp":"2026-11-12T16:25:42.337Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# III. Invariants and Correspondence\n\n## C20. Concurrent Frame and Authority Well-Formedness\n\n`ConcurrentWF` extends frame well-formedness to multiple local stacks and one global parent relationship.\n\nIt must preserve the applicable structural, ownership, holder, and authority conditions of Spec 1.\n\nEvery inherited transition and every new concurrent transition must preserve the applicable global invariants.\n\nReparenting must preserve extended ancestry and validity properties.\n\n## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n\nFor the reference mutex abstraction, the following state properties must be preserved over all **live** entries, except the bound-identity property in item 6, which applies to every entry:\n\n1. **Guarded authority:** `guarded` is active, has own mode `Modify`, and is held by a usable place.\n2. **Grant record:** If `grant = some g`, then the recorded authority exists and `origin(g) = guarded` and `footprint(g) = footprint(guarded)`. `g` need not be active; the invariant must handle legal release and reanchoring without silently weakening these equalities.\n3. **Controller validity:** `ControlOf(entry)` identifies an existing usable **leaf** associated with the holder `slot` and its structural mutex parent `M`.\n4. **Controller uniqueness:** Distinct live entries have distinct `ControlOf` leaves; the guarded-identity uniqueness guard is also respected. The same controller cannot be registered twice under different guarded identities.\n5. **`GrantSubtree`:** If `grant = some g` and `g` is active, every active descendant of `guarded` covering a protected leaf is either `g` itself or a descendant of `g`. This is a distinct invariant, necessary to restore grant availability after legal `unlock`.\n6. **`RegBound`:** Every registered `guarded` identity denotes an existing authority identity, even when its entry is no longer live. This prevents a dormant entry from becoming live merely when a fresh authority is created with its identity.\n7. **Structural initialization:** For each live entry, every structural leaf of the place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n\n`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n\nThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n\n## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6)]**\n\nThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n\n```\nlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n```\n\nIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n\nFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n\n1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`. No independent ordinary derivative may be issued directly from `guarded` while the entry is live.\n2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n4. **Controller-access and state-integrity protocol (WC5 D-C6) [WC5.2]:** For every live synchronization entry, the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when the acting frame holds an applicable `Observe` right under C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3`, out-of-protocol `writeShared`, or any other operation outside the validated controller protocol whose actual access or effect footprint includes the controller leaf.\n\n   This restriction covers physical reads and writes of the controller, changes to its initialization state, and changes that make the controller leaf itself the holder or owner of an authority or allocated resource.\n\n   In particular, while the entry remains live, the restriction excludes:\n   - `store` targeting the controller leaf;\n   - `issue` using the controller leaf as its destination;\n   - `alloc` using the controller leaf itself as the owner of a newly allocated root;\n   - `alloc` whose provider-metadata accesses may write the controller leaf;\n   - any other inherited or compound operation producing an equivalent access or effect on that leaf.\n\n   The restriction is evaluated against the leaves actually affected by an operation, including relevant physical metadata accesses. Merely naming a structural place containing the controller does not establish that the controller leaf itself is accessed or modified.\n\n   In particular, `alloc(owner = M)` is not excluded solely because `ControlOf(entry)` is a structural leaf of `M`. Ownership of storage by the mutex value remains eligible under C13.5. Such an allocation must nevertheless satisfy every applicable inherited precondition and must not independently access or alter the controller leaf through its provider, destination, or other effects.\n\n   A change solely to a dependency relation, without a physical controller access or another prohibited controller-state effect, is not excluded by this item merely because it concerns the controller. All applicable inherited dependency and lifetime requirements remain in force.\n\n   An authority whose footprint includes the controller does not, by that fact alone, constitute an access or effect on the controller.\n\n   Valid internal controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized `resetLock` remain within their respective validated protocols. Controller initialization through `newMutex` remains governed by C13.\n\n   A legal transfer of the whole mutex value `M`, including by-value `spawn`, or of a structurally enclosing value remains eligible under C22.3 and the unchanged O4 preconditions. Operations that make the entry dead remain eligible under C12, C22.5, and the applicable inherited lifetime rules. These exceptions do not waive the preservation of `SyncInv` or the applicable realization obligations.\n\n   This requirement restricts the safe abstraction, not the inherited kernel transitions or their outcomes. A kernel operation may independently return `ok` without being admitted by the safe mutex interface.\n\n   Enforcement of the safe interface belongs to Specs 4 and 10. Complete physical-access and effect-footprint coverage, controller correspondence, and preservation remain obligations under C23, C25, C29, `OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, and `OB-C28`.\n5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n\n6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n\n   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n\n   **Verification boundary [WC5.1]:** `ChainExcl` checks the direct-exercise fragment (`derive`, `split`, `read3`, `write3`, `writeShared`), and `PlaceExcl` checks selected `HasRight` premises; their conjunction is represented exploratorily by `Encap5R`. Both guard-exclusion witnesses and the normal mutex, arena and whole-mutex-transfer witnesses have been checked in that fragment. The complete coverage of all rights and composite phases, including the `resetLock` exception, remains `OB-C28`. A kernel-`ok` step that violates this requirement is excluded by the safe abstraction but does not alter its kernel outcome.\n\nRequirements 1–6 all constrain the allowed traces of the safe abstraction **[WC5.1]**. In particular, requirement 4 excludes ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` from `EncapsulatedTrace` as required by C51, while preserving O9 as a kernel operation. Requirement 6 also excludes accesses and other rights justified through a live `guarded` without the relevant grant. Their enforcement must follow from encapsulation and the public interface, rather than hidden runtime constraints or undisclosed hypotheses on the kernel. Preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under these explicit restrictions is the proof obligation stated in C23.\n\nRequirement 4 is **also** a physical realization/interface obligation; its enforcement is not falsely claimed to follow merely from the authority-only predicate `GrantDiscipline` or the abstract machine `S_C`.\n\n**[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** The final controller-footprint restriction (`CtlRestrF`, `Encap7`/`Run7`) rejects kernel-`ok` out-of-protocol `store`, `issue`, and `alloc(owner = ctl)` while admitting their tested positive counterparts. It admits `depend(ctl, a)` where no physical controller access is represented and `alloc(owner = M)` when the controller itself is unaffected; an `alloc` whose provider includes `ctl` is excluded conservatively for potential metadata writes, which the current kernel-effect model does **not** exhibit. The 28 modeled step kinds have been classified without an admitted out-of-protocol controller-footprint counterexample; `completeness7_run` proves graph-footprint coverage for a live entry under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. This is a scoped formal abstraction result, not a proof of all target physical accesses (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C23. Preservation of Ordinary and Concurrent Operations\n\nFormalization must demonstrate preservation of `ConcurrentWF`, the complete `SyncInv` (including controller uniqueness, structural initialization, `GrantSubtree` and `RegBound`) and `GrantDiscipline` for every applicable inherited and new transition, including `transfer` and by-value `spawn`, under the explicit safe-abstraction restrictions C22.1–3 and C22.5. The reference abstract trace relation must include by-value `spawn` and must not silently omit any applicable kernel operation.\n\nFor inherited operations, the kernel transition remains governed by the unmodified Spec 1. An operation that is a valid kernel step but not exposed by the safe mutex abstraction may violate the **library's** trace discipline without constituting a memory-safety counterexample against the kernel.\n\nA dead entry or recorded inactive grant is permitted where its contract allows it. A live entry with an invalid positional controller, duplicate controller or unauthorized grant is not a valid safe-abstraction state.\n\nResulting-state invariants must be **proved**, not included as circular preconditions when their preservation is the proof obligation. Any lemma conditional on encapsulation must expose that condition in its theorem statement or the reachable-trace assumptions.\n\n## C24. Sequential Conservativity\n\nFor corresponding single-context states and inherited operations O1–O16:\n\n- preconditions and outcomes coincide;\n- projected effects coincide;\n- authority exercise retains the Spec 1 definition;\n- sequential ancestry is recovered;\n- the applicable sequential invariants are preserved.\n\nThe reachable sequential domain under inherited operations must correspond to the sequential domain of Spec 1 under the stated state correspondence.\n\nA transition history involving additional contexts must not return to an invalid single-context authority state.\n\n## C25. Abstract-to-Concrete Realization Obligations\n\nThe abstract machine imposes no universal runtime authority metadata. The synchronization library may maintain justified implementation metadata; the frozen Spec 1 §4 does not forbid it.\n\nA sound implementation of the reference mutex must prove correspondence between the external registry and physical controller, preserve `ControlOf` under legal whole-value transfers, and enforce safe-interface encapsulation including C22.1–6. In particular, the prohibition of `endUse` on `ctl` or a strict structural subplace of `slot` is an interface property; the unchanged kernel O12 remains available to programs outside that abstraction contract.\n\nSuccessful controller acquisition must imply the remaining `deriveSync` obligations through proven state invariants and contractual authorization facts. For the reference mutex, authorization is instantiated using **only** the entry's liveness, grant availability and the requesting frame's controller access. Its proof may use the global kernel invariants and the synchronization invariants but may not place its desired conclusion inside the definition of `SyncAuthorized`.\n\nThe ambition to check only the physical lock word at runtime is a reference optimization target, not a universal requirement of Spec 1. The exact atomicity, event-ordering and data-race obligations are deferred to `OB-C01`–`OB-C06`, with controller realization specifically under `OB-C05`.\n\n---","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"559cf8bd-284d-4a11-b7f6-dfed84bb32b2","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","root":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","branch":"formal/spec2-m4","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d3da530e-c1a8-466d-990c-e7d0be19d89e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
