ownloads/ERN_WC5_2_Revu_Pour_Validation/ERN_Concurrency_Model_WC5.2.md","description":"Read Spec 1 §73 and WC5.2 C6–C8"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_01C5CvPyvb8EngLzLrapFhAa":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"d79e2c7a-42e4-4c1c-a3d1-1e608a1fbe1e","apiBlockIndex":6,"requestId":"req_011CgyPqM6CKw4Je4oZXvswt","requestRef":"8f9827e2-a498-4b3a-8f52-ef911faf234e","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"fd15bc47-e556-4b2d-996f-a9fd332ffc00","timestamp":"2026-11-12T17:05:35.031Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"fd15bc47-e556-4b2d-996f-a9fd332ffc00","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"048ce1b3-8891-4aae-bdd2-33b7fc094ee0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C5CvPyvb8EngLzLrapFhAa","type":"tool_result","content":"**Preconditions**\n\n1. The current frame is not the initial frame and has a surviving caller.\n2. Results that must survive have already been legally transferred to their destinations; no active authority or dependency may remain invalidly attached to locals that are about to die.\n3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n5. Validity of anchors of surviving values and `AnchorOrderHeld` are checked **in the resulting state**. Other state invariants and resource contracts are respected.\n\n**`ok` effect:**\n\n- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n- reanchoring changes **neither the footprint nor the own mode** of an authority; parameter identities and relations specific to the frame cease their operational role;\n- the calling frame becomes current again.\n\n**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n\n# XIII. Pinning, Statics, and External Commitments\n\nPinning forbids certain movements or ends of lifetime. It is not a general guarantee of content immutability.\n\n-----\n## C6. Frame Exit and Reparenting\n\n`exitFrameC` applies O7 to the current **non-initial frame of the executing context's local stack**. That frame must have a surviving caller in the same local stack. This explicitly transposes O7.1 without making ordinary frame exit applicable to a context-initial frame.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The applicable conditions O7.2–O7.5 hold for the global authority and dependency state, under the extended ancestry relation.\n2. In particular, no surviving active authority, owned place or required anchor is invalidated by the exit or its reanchoring.\n\n**Effect**\n\nLet `f` be the exiting frame and `p = parent(f)` its surviving immediate caller. Apply the applicable O7 effects. Every surviving context-initial frame whose immediate parent was `f` receives the new parent `p`. All other ancestry links are unchanged. Reparenting creates no right, does not revive storage, and does not independently authorize an otherwise invalid exit.\n\n**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The resulting frame relation must be well-founded, and the complete transition must preserve the applicable authority and anchor invariants, including `AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld`. Proofs must account for the effect of the extended ancestry relation without treating final-state invariants as assumed preconditions.\n\nThe transition introduces no implicit wait for the child contexts.\n\n## C7. Context Termination — `terminate`\n\n`terminate` applies the corresponding O7 validity requirements to the initial frame of a terminating non-initial context, after its nested call frames have legally ended.\n\nIts effects include:\n\n- ending the terminating context's initial frame;\n- performing applicable releases and legal reanchoring;\n- reparenting surviving context-initial frames as specified in C6;\n- removing the terminating context from the live-context set;\n- establishing its completed lifecycle state.\n\nCompletion does not automatically end resources independently and legally owned by surviving contexts.\n\nThe initial base context remains subject to its distinct base-frame restrictions.\n\n## C8. Reference Completion Operation — `join`\n\n`join` is a candidate reference operation for observing completion of a previously created context. Its argument is a context identifier; this candidate does not introduce an additional completion-capability mechanism.\n\n- If the identifier is unknown to the execution, the operation returns `reject`.\n- If the identified context is live, successful completion observation is not yet enabled. Awaiting completion is not a new `Outcome3` result.\n- If the identified context has a recorded completed lifecycle state, `join` returns `ok`.\n\n**Successful effect:** The abstract state is unchanged, including `C`, `F_C`, `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D`. No consumed or “joined” marker is introduced by this candidate.\n\nThe complete event-ordering consequences of successful completion observation are deferred to the concurrent event model. `join` is not the only possible completion protocol, and its existence does not impose structured concurrency universally.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6a701437-42eb-4806-ae10-ae0899f4a640","timestamp":"2026-11-12T17:05:35.202Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**Preconditions**\n\n1. The current frame is not the initial frame and has a surviving caller.\n2. Results that must survive have already been legally transferred to their destinations; no active authority or dependency may remain invalidly attached to locals that are about to die.\n3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n5. Validity of anchors of surviving values and `AnchorOrderHeld` are checked **in the resulting state**. Other state invariants and resource contracts are respected.\n\n**`ok` effect:**\n\n- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n- reanchoring changes **neither the footprint nor the own mode** of an authority; parameter identities and relations specific to the frame cease their operational role;\n- the calling frame becomes current again.\n\n**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n\n# XIII. Pinning, Statics, and External Commitments\n\nPinning forbids certain movements or ends of lifetime. It is not a general guarantee of content immutability.\n\n-----\n## C6. Frame Exit and Reparenting\n\n`exitFrameC` applies O7 to the current **non-initial frame of the executing context's local stack**. That frame must have a surviving caller in the same local stack. This explicitly transposes O7.1 without making ordinary frame exit applicable to a context-initial frame.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The applicable conditions O7.2–O7.5 hold for the global authority and dependency state, under the extended ancestry relation.\n2. In particular, no surviving active authority, owned place or required anchor is invalidated by the exit or its reanchoring.\n\n**Effect**\n\nLet `f` be the exiting frame and `p = parent(f)` its surviving immediate caller. Apply the applicable O7 effects. Every surviving context-initial frame whose immediate parent was `f` receives the new parent `p`. All other ancestry links are unchanged. Reparenting creates no right, does not revive storage, and does not independently authorize an otherwise invalid exit.\n\n**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The resulting frame relation must be well-founded, and the complete transition must preserve the applicable authority and anchor invariants, including `AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld`. Proofs must account for the effect of the extended ancestry relation without treating final-state invariants as assumed preconditions.\n\nThe transition introduces no implicit wait for the child contexts.\n\n## C7. Context Termination — `terminate`\n\n`terminate` applies the corresponding O7 validity requirements to the initial frame of a terminating non-initial context, after its nested call frames have legally ended.\n\nIts effects include:\n\n- ending the terminating context's initial frame;\n- performing applicable releases and legal reanchoring;\n- reparenting surviving context-initial frames as specified in C6;\n- removing the terminating context from the live-context set;\n- establishing its completed lifecycle state.\n\nCompletion does not automatically end resources independently and legally owned by surviving contexts.\n\nThe initial base context remains subject to its distinct base-frame restrictions.\n\n## C8. Reference Completion Operation — `join`\n\n`join` is a candidate reference operation for observing completion of a previously created context. Its argument is a context identifier; this candidate does not introduce an additional completion-capability mechanism.\n\n- If the identifier is unknown to the execution, the operation returns `reject`.\n- If the identified context is live, successful completion observation is not yet enabled. Awaiting completion is not a new `Outcome3` result.\n- If the identified context has a recorded completed lifecycle state, `join` returns `ok`.\n\n**Successful effect:** The abstract state is unchanged, including `C`, `F_C`, `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D`. No consumed or “joined” marker is introduced by this candidate.\n\nThe complete event-ordering consequences of successful completion observation are deferred to the concurrent event model. `join` is not the only possible completion protocol, and its existence does not impose structured concurrency universally.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d79e2c7a-42e4-4c1c-a3d1-1e608a1fbe1e","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","root":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","branch":"formal/spec2-m4","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fd15bc47-e556-4b2d-996f-a9fd332ffc00","permission