{"parentUuid":"07f93d6e-edf3-4fba-8e28-d1df659dffac","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ad10e2fd-52be-47f7-a4f7-932245537372","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PDB1pLGBXKuKeXAFrWN95f","type":"tool_result","content":"## C32. Synchronizes-With — `sw`\n\nThe following are **candidate reference** sources of synchronization:\n\n1. **Atomic protocol:** a release-capable write/RMW (including a `seq_cst` store or RMW) and an acquire-capable read/RMW (including a `seq_cst` load or RMW) on the same atomic unit may establish `sw` when the acquire reads the release through the applicable `rf` relationship and the relevant memory orders provide release/acquire semantics. **Address identity alone is insufficient.** The initial Lean fragment uses **direct `rf`** only; release-sequence-mediated synchronization awaits explicit modeling.\n2. **Context creation:** the parent's `spawn(c)` event `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` event, with all prior parent events and subsequent child events related through `po`.\n3. **Completion observation:** `complete(c)` of the terminated context `sw`-precedes a successful `join(c)`, with the completing context's preceding events ordered before `complete(c)` and the joining context's subsequent events ordered after `join(c)`.\n\nA pure `release` (O3), `derive`, `endUse`, or other authority marker **does not itself create `sw`**. A bare `spawn` in the abstract interleaving does not order parent operations executed **after** that `spawn` before the child's later operations. A `join(c)` orders only the events of the context whose completion it actually observes, subject to already established transitive edges; it does not implicitly join live descendants or unrelated contexts.\n\nAdditional synchronization mechanisms are admissible only under an explicit contract with a proof that their event edges have the claimed semantics.\n\n## C33. Happens-Before and Data Races\n\n## C49. LockOrder, Recovery, and Delegation\n\n**`LockOrder`** is the obligation that the protected accesses in an earlier critical section of one mutex are `hb`-before conflicting protected accesses in a later critical section whose lock-acquisition event receives the preceding release. The proof must use the concrete `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, and grant-lifecycle relations, not merely the total order of abstract `SyncEntry` updates or the fact that controller identities match.\n\nFor a successful `resetLock`, the library must prove exclusive recovery authority under C18, a coherent physical controller update, and the event-order consequences of any protected memory that becomes newly accessible. Recovery is **not automatically** a substitute for an acquire/release edge missing from an abandoned grant. A proposed recovery protocol that cannot establish necessary ordering is not justified for safe concurrent access. **[WC5.1 — M4]** C18 imposes no precondition on the recorded grant state when `resetLock(e)` executes: the recorded grant may be `none`. The exploratory kernel admits `resetLock` on an already free lock, as witnessed by W-RL. This does **not** introduce any new C18 precondition or imply a new `sw` edge.\n\nWhen a grant is reborrowed, passed to another frame, or transmitted between contexts, the reference implementation must show how ordinary authority rules, grant constraints, and the relevant handoff protocol preserve `hb` across the whole path. Exiting a frame that released a grant without `unlock` does not imply controller release, and a logically returned capability does not imply a physical publication.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Strict abstract mutex-edge evidence:** The first experimental graph connected `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` by entry identity alone. W-RL (`resetLock(e)` between them) and W-TR (whole-mutex `transfer` changing `ControlOf(e)`) prove that this approximation can invent `hb`. The corrected **abstract** edge is absent if an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs, or if `ControlOf(e)` becomes undefined or changes leaf from the state after `unlock` through the state before `acquire`. The strict graph excludes W-RL/W-TR, preserves W-RL₀/W-RL-J/W-TR-J and the earlier reference witnesses, and proves conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps`. **Any abstraction of the C48 mutex `sw` edge must respect interventions on the controller; the definitive physical rule remains the unchanged direct-`rf` condition on the same atomic unit in C32/C48.** **[WC5.2 — M4]** For the **constructed reference controller graph**, `bridge` now proves the direct-read implication; `completeness7_run` classifies the modeled controller footprints for `Run7` under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. The two strict abstract guards still do **not** prove that a target physical acquisition reads the earlier release (`OB-C05`), or that all compiled metadata and composite-step accesses are covered (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C50. Realization, Visibility, and Physical Correspondence\n\n607:## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights\n608-\n609-`AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld` are defined in Section 44. `NoDangling` prohibits an **active** authority from covering a leaf belonging to an unusable place; the invariant does not say that a leaf is initialized. `NoOrphan` requires every active authority held by a place to be connected to a frame in the stack through a **finite, non-repeating chain** of authorities: the first link is held by a live frame, and each active link **structurally covers the entirety** of the place holding the next link. The link need not have an effective mode over that place. `NoOrphan` guarantees neither the exercisability of all stored authorities nor freedom from blocking.\n610-\n611-## 55. ArenaInv — Invariant of the Arena Abstraction\n612-\n613-`ArenaInv` is an invariant of the **library contract**, not an additional predicate of kernel state. For every live arena:\n614-\n615-- `storage` designates an existing, usable place; the footprint of `internal` is exactly the footprint of `storage`;\n595:## 51. I2 — Conservation of Rights\n596-\n597-A derived authority covers only a subfootprint of what its origin chain permits and does not elevate its mode. Derivation and splitting restrictions do not allow duplication of an exclusive right over a leaf. Every active authority is linked to a valid origin according to state and reanchoring rules: an origin is **valid** if and only if it is active, so the origin of an active derived authority is active. Partial suspension follows from `effective`.\n598-\n599:## 52. I3 — Validity of Anchors and Dependencies\n600-\n601-Every explicit anchor of a still-usable place is valid. An operation cannot invalidate an anchor retained by a place that remains usable. Transitive propagation of dependencies from contained values does not change this obligation. Dependencies are not permissions and are insufficient to justify an access.\n602-\n603:## 53. I4 — Consumption and Movement\n604-\n605-A `Moved` leaf cannot be read as if it still contained its previous value. Moving a value does not preserve two copies of an exclusive authority. Initialization state and authority-exercise state are separate checks.\n606-\n607-## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d045028b-4bd2-4a4e-b0db-152dde646880","timestamp":"2026-11-12T22:54:26.525Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## C32. Synchronizes-With — `sw`\n\nThe following are **candidate reference** sources of synchronization:\n\n1. **Atomic protocol:** a release-capable write/RMW (including a `seq_cst` store or RMW) and an acquire-capable read/RMW (including a `seq_cst` load or RMW) on the same atomic unit may establish `sw` when the acquire reads the release through the applicable `rf` relationship and the relevant memory orders provide release/acquire semantics. **Address identity alone is insufficient.** The initial Lean fragment uses **direct `rf`** only; release-sequence-mediated synchronization awaits explicit modeling.\n2. **Context creation:** the parent's `spawn(c)` event `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` event, with all prior parent events and subsequent child events related through `po`.\n3. **Completion observation:** `complete(c)` of the terminated context `sw`-precedes a successful `join(c)`, with the completing context's preceding events ordered before `complete(c)` and the joining context's subsequent events ordered after `join(c)`.\n\nA pure `release` (O3), `derive`, `endUse`, or other authority marker **does not itself create `sw`**. A bare `spawn` in the abstract interleaving does not order parent operations executed **after** that `spawn` before the child's later operations. A `join(c)` orders only the events of the context whose completion it actually observes, subject to already established transitive edges; it does not implicitly join live descendants or unrelated contexts.\n\nAdditional synchronization mechanisms are admissible only under an explicit contract with a proof that their event edges have the claimed semantics.\n\n## C33. Happens-Before and Data Races\n\n## C49. LockOrder, Recovery, and Delegation\n\n**`LockOrder`** is the obligation that the protected accesses in an earlier critical section of one mutex are `hb`-before conflicting protected accesses in a later critical section whose lock-acquisition event receives the preceding release. The proof must use the concrete `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, and grant-lifecycle relations, not merely the total order of abstract `SyncEntry` updates or the fact that controller identities match.\n\nFor a successful `resetLock`, the library must prove exclusive recovery authority under C18, a coherent physical controller update, and the event-order consequences of any protected memory that becomes newly accessible. Recovery is **not automatically** a substitute for an acquire/release edge missing from an abandoned grant. A proposed recovery protocol that cannot establish necessary ordering is not justified for safe concurrent access. **[WC5.1 — M4]** C18 imposes no precondition on the recorded grant state when `resetLock(e)` executes: the recorded grant may be `none`. The exploratory kernel admits `resetLock` on an already free lock, as witnessed by W-RL. This does **not** introduce any new C18 precondition or imply a new `sw` edge.\n\nWhen a grant is reborrowed, passed to another frame, or transmitted between contexts, the reference implementation must show how ordinary authority rules, grant constraints, and the relevant handoff protocol preserve `hb` across the whole path. Exiting a frame that released a grant without `unlock` does not imply controller release, and a logically returned capability does not imply a physical publication.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Strict abstract mutex-edge evidence:** The first experimental graph connected `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` by entry identity alone. W-RL (`resetLock(e)` between them) and W-TR (whole-mutex `transfer` changing `ControlOf(e)`) prove that this approximation can invent `hb`. The corrected **abstract** edge is absent if an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs, or if `ControlOf(e)` becomes undefined or changes leaf from the state after `unlock` through the state before `acquire`. The strict graph excludes W-RL/W-TR, preserves W-RL₀/W-RL-J/W-TR-J and the earlier reference witnesses, and proves conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps`. **Any abstraction of the C48 mutex `sw` edge must respect interventions on the controller; the definitive physical rule remains the unchanged direct-`rf` condition on the same atomic unit in C32/C48.** **[WC5.2 — M4]** For the **constructed reference controller graph**, `bridge` now proves the direct-read implication; `completeness7_run` classifies the modeled controller footprints for `Run7` under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. The two strict abstract guards still do **not** prove that a target physical acquisition reads the earlier release (`OB-C05`), or that all compiled metadata and composite-step accesses are covered (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C50. Realization, Visibility, and Physical Correspondence\n\n607:## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights\n608-\n609-`AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld` are defined in Section 44. `NoDangling` prohibits an **active** authority from covering a leaf belonging to an unusable place; the invariant does not say that a leaf is initialized. `NoOrphan` requires every active authority held by a place to be connected to a frame in the stack through a **finite, non-repeating chain** of authorities: the first link is held by a live frame, and each active link **structurally covers the entirety** of the place holding the next link. The link need not have an effective mode over that place. `NoOrphan` guarantees neither the exercisability of all stored authorities nor freedom from blocking.\n610-\n611-## 55. ArenaInv — Invariant of the Arena Abstraction\n612-\n613-`ArenaInv` is an invariant of the **library contract**, not an additional predicate of kernel state. For every live arena:\n614-\n615-- `storage` designates an existing, usable place; the footprint of `internal` is exactly the footprint of `storage`;\n595:## 51. I2 — Conservation of Rights\n596-\n597-A derived authority covers only a subfootprint of what its origin chain permits and does not elevate its mode. Derivation and splitting restrictions do not allow duplication of an exclusive right over a leaf. Every active authority is linked to a valid origin according to state and reanchoring rules: an origin is **valid** if and only if it is active, so the origin of an active derived authority is active. Partial suspension follows from `effective`.\n598-\n599:## 52. I3 — Validity of Anchors and Dependencies\n600-\n601-Every explicit anchor of a still-usable place is valid. An operation cannot invalidate an anchor retained by a place that remains usable. Transitive propagation of dependencies from contained values does not change this obligation. Dependencies are not permissions and are insufficient to justify an access.\n602-\n603:## 53. I4 — Consumption and Movement\n604-\n605-A `Moved` leaf cannot be read as if it still contained its previous value. Moving a value does not preserve two copies of an exclusive authority. Initialization state and authority-exercise state are separate checks.\n606-\n607-## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8762c3be-c067-4670-a1dc-2a917ec2c8d5","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","root":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","branch":"formal/spec2-m4","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"ernlang/ern","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","platform":"macos"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"07f93d6e-edf3-4fba-8e28-d1df659dffac","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
