e-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/formal","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"f548872a-19a3-48c5-b67c-0e677f818c8c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ad10e2fd-52be-47f7-a4f7-932245537372","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KkMEiGYKffn5nZjxmJEYX9","type":"tool_result","content":"7:**Design baseline:** WC4 (C1–C25) is carried forward, with the previously identified C16–C18 and C22 candidate changes and the explicitly identified C13.11, C18.4, and C22.4 amendments evaluated in the WC5.2 exploratory Lean program. The prior candidate-status qualifications for D2, D3, P1, P2, and C22.5 are retained. D-C1 through D-C10 and the event-model refinements remain proposals, not validated or frozen decisions.\n230:## C13. Reference Construction — `newMutex`\n252:**Controller-initialization clarification [WC5.2]:** This is an abstract registration effect: the operation changes no structural leaf's abstract initialization state. C13.10 already requires every structural leaf of `M`, including `ctl`, to be `Init` before construction; `newMutex` does not change an `Uninit` leaf to `Init`. Separately, the physical realization establishes the controller's initial available synchronization value by a **non-atomic write** to `ctl`, justified by C13.11 and subject to the applicable O10.1/O10.2 requirements. The term *controller initialization* in C31 and C48 denotes that physical synchronization-state write, not a transition of abstract structural initialization state. The non-atomic write is represented as an initial modification in `mo` under C31; it does not itself establish `sw`. Its concrete event, the ordering of this write with accesses in other execution contexts, and the correspondence with the initially available registry entry remain obligations under C27–C29, C42, C48 and `OB-C05`. The authority-only kernel state `S_C` does not establish the physical write or its ordering.\n256:**Interface and realization obligations:** The safe public interface must prevent falsification of the positional controller association (C13.3); the controller synchronization protocol (C13.6) must be enforced; and the initial physical controller state must correspond to the newly available abstract registry entry. C13.11 supplies a required authority premise for the initial ordinary write, but does not by itself prove its ordering with other contexts or its target realization. These are not claimed to follow from the authority-only state `S_C`. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-NC demonstrates that C13.1–C13.10 alone admit construction without controller rights; the added C13.11 rejects it in the amended model, while all 16 previously recorded construction steps satisfy the new premise. The abstract `newMutex` effect is unchanged.\n301:**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject` for the abstract safety transition; a busy controller may make the enclosing library acquisition temporarily non-enabled, as specified in C16.\n303:## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n329:## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n345:This candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n451:   In particular, `alloc(owner = M)` is not excluded solely because `ControlOf(entry)` is a structural leaf of `M`. Ownership of storage by the mutex value remains eligible under C13.5. Such an allocation must nevertheless satisfy every applicable inherited precondition and must not independently access or alter the controller leaf through its provider, destination, or other effects.\n457:   Valid internal controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized `resetLock` remain within their respective validated protocols. Controller initialization through `newMutex` remains governed by C13.\n530:| `OB-C13` | Preservation of H1 ancestry and validity under context reparenting |\n533:| `OB-C16` | Unified live administrative identity guard across arenas and mutexes, consistent with Spec 1 §56; integration and verification deferred to later milestones |\n534:| `OB-C17` | **[WC5.1 — M4] L1, scoped exploratory proof:** `cap5_drop_own` covers all 28 modeled step kinds under `DDSound` and reachable encapsulated authority traces. `lossStep_own` further establishes for eligible direct-access conflicting pairs a first own-context loss step `r` strictly between the two accesses; with one event per abstract step, `po(e1,r)` follows. Internal composite-step ordering and physical access coverage remain open (`OB-C19`, `OB-C20`). Raw `cap` still fails L1 (W-GR). |\n545:| `OB-C28` | **[WC5.1/5.2] Full C22.6 coverage:** prove the guarded-chain exclusion for all rights not yet modeled in the exploratory rule: `issue` destination rights (§77.2, §77.6) and its internal phases; `newMutex` C13.2; `unlock` C17.2–C17.4; `newArena` §§76.1–76.2; the special `resetLock` C18.1 recovery rule (confirmed not enforced by any current `Encap5R` conjunct, although `ResetLockOk` admits a free lock with `grant = none`); and authorities grounding rights in `transfer` §63.4, `endUse` §69.2 and `free` §70.3 (including applicable `spawnVal` phases). Check other remaining compound rights and `reset` phases, enforce the restriction in the safe interface, prove relevant preservation, and test legitimate operations with a live mutex as well as the no-live-mutex case. |\n579:| S18 | A whole-value transfer would merge the controller association of two live mutex entries | Candidate `reject` under O4 §63.3 when the destination live mutex has initialized structural leaves. Positive: legal `transfer(M, M′)` into a destination not holding a live mutex entry |\n650:| W-NC | Old C13.1–C13.10 admit controller construction from a frame without controller rights; new C13.11 excludes it, with all 16 previously recorded `newMutex` steps satisfying the additional `Modify` test. |\n687:Evaluate S1a–S18 and their positive or negative counterparts, using verdicts grounded in the frozen specifications and explicitly identified candidate rules.\n715:5. **Mutex and `join` [WC5.2 — M4]:** abstract `join_ordersS` and conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps` are retained; W-RL and W-TR refute the overbroad mutex edge. The **constructed** controller history now proves the conditional strict-edge `SWS ⇒ rf ⇒ sw` bridge, and the converse under `CtlFixed`, with the final 28-kind footprint classification and authority checks of C13.11/C18.4. **Still to prove:** preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under the modified C16–C18 verdict rules and **complete** C22 D-C6/C22.6 encapsulation, and a dedicated witness where ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` is kernel-`ok` but excluded by `Encap` from the safe interface. These two tasks are not discharged by the finite event/bridge proofs. Physical `LockOrder` from **actual** target `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, failed-CAS events, abandoned-grant realizations, full C22.6 coverage including recovery, and physical correspondence remain independent open requirements.\n974:The reference `unlock` and `resetLock` must prove their own controller-update correspondence. Their logical atomicity in C17/C18 does **not** establish physical release semantics. Failure to discharge a realization obligation means the corresponding protocol is not yet justified as safe, not that an additional unchecked primitive is implicitly available.\n1063:The reference `newMutex` initializes a physical controller in a state corresponding to `grant = none` and must establish the positional, identity and initialization requirements of C12–C13, C21 and Spec 1 §56. As clarified in C13, this is a physical non-atomic write of the controller synchronization value to an **already abstractly `Init`** leaf, not the abstract initialization of an `Uninit` structural leaf. C13.11 requires ordinary `Modify` authority, with applicable O10.1/O10.2 conditions. Its concrete metadata operations have events as required by C27/C29.\n---\n1:# ERN — Concurrency Model Specification v0\n3:## Working Candidate 5.2 — Execution Contexts, Authority Transmission, and Concurrent Event Semantics **[WC5.2]**\n21:# I. Concurrent Execution Machine\n23:## C1. Execution Contexts\n33:## C2. Concurrent State and External Contracts\n53:## C3. Frame Ancestry — H1\n71:## C4. Context Creation — `spawn`\n96:## C5. Provisional By-Value Transmission at `spawn`\n124:## C6. Frame Exit and Reparenting\n141:## C7. Context Termination — `terminate`\n157:## C8. Reference Completion Operation — `join`\n169:## C9. Cross-Context Authorities and Dependencies\n185:## C10. External Contexts\n195:# II. Synchronized Authority Derivation\n197:## C11. Separation of Mechanisms\n209:## C12. Synchronization Registry\n230:## C13. Reference Construction — `newMutex`\n260:## C14. Concurrent External Contract — `SyncAuthorized`\n282:## C15. New Concurrent Transition — `deriveSync`\n303:## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n329:## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n353:## C18. Abandoned Grants and Recovery **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n379:## C19. Synchronized Administration\n393:# III. Invariants and Correspondence\n395:## C20. Concurrent Frame and Authority Well-Formedness\n405:## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n421:## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6)]**\n478:## C23. Preservation of Ordinary and Concurrent Operations\n488:## C24. Sequential Conservativity\n502:## C25. Abstract-to-Concrete Realization Obligations\n514:# IV. Open Obligations\n551:# V. Adversarial Corpus — Corrected Seed Cases\n581:### Guarded-Chain and Transfer Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 ter–2 sexies and S10b **[WC5.1]**\n592:### Handoff Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 bis and 2 septies (authority and abstract-event verdicts proved) **[WC5.1 — M4]**\n617:### Finite Event-Model Witnesses — G1–G19 **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n637:### Controller Realization and Encapsulation Witnesses **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n660:# VI. Exploratory Verification Requirements\n664:## Milestone 1 — Abstract Model\n679:## Milestone 2 — Refinement\n685:## Milestone 3 — Adversarial Validation\n693:## Milestone 4 — Event Model, Handoff, and Data-Race-Freedom Validation\n731:# VII. Concurrent Event Model — Proposed Block C\n735:## C26. Executions, Abstract Steps, and Event Graphs\n751:## C27. Event Attributes and Realization Origin\n765:## C28. Program Order, Lifecycle Markers, and Within-Step Order\n786:## C29. Pure-Authority Erasure and Concrete-Access Coverage\n798:# VIII. Conflicts, Ordering, and Consistency — Proposed Block C\n800:## C30. Byte-Level Conflicts and Access Pairs\n808:## C31. Reads-From and Modification Order\n819:## C32. Synchronizes-With — `sw`\n831:## C33. Happens-Before and Data Races\n852:## C34. Admissible Event Graphs and Reference Memory Models\n881:# IX. Shared-Mutation Disciplines and Atomics — Proposed Block D\n883:## C35. Companion Shared-Mutation Contract — `SharedDiscipline`\n899:## C36. Classification of Inherited Accesses — D-C2\n911:## C37. Concurrent Atomic Read — `readShared`\n928:## C38. Atomic Units, Concrete Layouts, and Multi-Leaf Writes — D-C5\n938:## C39. Atomic Shared Writes and RMWs\n946:## C40. Memory Orders and Operation Legality — D-C7\n968:## C41. Atomic Protocol Obligations and Failure Paths","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"11a60e78-78f2-4711-9d2b-4c7c40632da1","timestamp":"2026-11-12T23:38:31.282Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"7:**Design baseline:** WC4 (C1–C25) is carried forward, with the previously identified C16–C18 and C22 candidate changes and the explicitly identified C13.11, C18.4, and C22.4 amendments evaluated in the WC5.2 exploratory Lean program. The prior candidate-status qualifications for D2, D3, P1, P2, and C22.5 are retained. D-C1 through D-C10 and the event-model refinements remain proposals, not validated or frozen decisions.\n230:## C13. Reference Cons