{"parentUuid":"11e36b0e-4cf4-48da-aad1-47d9deebd3f7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"af17f01e-fb36-4c87-8f58-dbe0f151d425","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01336ptTrJiaXk2Rzx1m7TAe","type":"tool_result","content":"603:## 53. I4 — Consumption and Movement\n607:## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights\n611:## 55. ArenaInv — Invariant of the Arena Abstraction\n624:## 56. Uniqueness of Live Administration\n630:## 57. Main Invariant\n652:## 58. Transition Outcomes\n664:## 59. Implicit Preservation of Modes and Anchors\n668:## 60. O1 — Derivation of an Authority\n679:## 61. O2 — Splitting Authorities\n692:## 62. O3 — End of a Derived Borrow\n708:## 63. O4 — Consuming Value Transfer\n734:## 64. O5 — Explicit Attachment of an Anchor\n747:## 65. O8 — Controlled Storage of an Authority\n765:## 66. O9 — Safe Reading of Regions\n777:## 67. O10 — Ordinary Write\n787:## 68. O11 — Write Under Shared Mutation\n803:## 69. O12 — Logical End of a Value's Use\n814:## 70. O13 — Release of an Owned Storage Unit\n825:## 71. O14 — Allocation of a New Storage Root\n845:## 72. O6 — Entering a Frame\n858:## 73. O7 — Exiting a Frame\n882:## 74. O15 — Dynamic Pinning\n888:## 75. O16 — Removal of Dynamic Pinning\n900:## 76. O17 — Creation of an Administered Arena\n915:## 77. O18 — Issuing an Arena Block\n934:## 78. O19 — Collective Arena Reset\n950:## 79. Non-Escaping Values and Returns\n981:## 80. Parametric Contracts of the Access Discipline\n997:## 81. Boundaries Remaining to Be Specified\n----\n5:**I5:** every pinned leaf, statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (an active pinning obligation, Section 74), belongs to a usable place.\n59:3. No active descendant of the authority prevents its release; no other still-usable value may lose a required anchor.\n60:4. If the authority is stored in its holding place, that place is exempted from the check “this authority still appears among the anchors” **only because the effect removes that anchor at the same time**. An anchor with the same identity attached by `depend` to another usable place continues to block the step.\n73:1. `source` and `destination` are usable, and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over their relevant footprints.\n77:5. **No in-place source leaf is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). Storage merely **owned** by the source, which is not itself moved by the transfer of that value, does not prevent this transfer.\n78:6. Dependencies of all still-usable places following the value remain valid; non-escape constraints and `AnchorOrder` are checked in the **resulting state**, including transferred anchors of owned places that remain usable.\n79:7. All moved held authorities, including those of owned storage, satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` in the resulting state; no authority may escape a local anchor on the grounds that its containing value was transferred.\n81:9. **Destination usability:** `destination` is usable in the resulting state.\n87:- places owned by `r` become owned by `corr(r)`; their roots follow this owner according to `RootHeld`;\n89:- destination places receive the transferred values and rights without automatically moving storage not owned by a source part.\n99:1. `result` is a usable place over which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n113:2. `target` is a usable **leaf** under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It holds no other **active** authority. Its data already being `Init` does not by itself prohibit the operation.\n114:3. The stored authority covers neither a leaf of `target` nor the leaves of storage transitively owned by it.\n120:Overwriting an active authority already held by the leaf is prohibited; its explicit release may make the target reusable. The resulting-state clause does not by itself prohibit all cycles possible after other operations: general freedom from blocking (`NoLock`) is not adopted here.\n130:1. Every leaf being read belongs to a usable place and is `Init`.\n142:1. Targeted leaves belong to usable places, and `access` is exercisable in `Modify` mode with **effective** `Modify` right over each leaf.\n152:1. Every leaf in the region is usable and declared `K.SharedMutable`.\n162:The logical end of a value does not necessarily entail deallocation of its storage. The following operations treat initialization, ownership, and physical release separately.\n168:1. `place` is usable and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over it.\n169:2. No active descendant over the leaves of `place` **or storage that dies with it** is held outside the places dying with `place`, in a way that would prevent rights from returning or being invalidated. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from this check. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (held by `place` or by a place dying with it, transitively owned storage included) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n170:3. No place **remaining usable after the effect** depends on an authority held by `place`, its root, or an authority that would become inactive when an owned holder dies.\n171:4. **No leaf whose use ends, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). A dynamic pinning obligation must be legally removed by `unpin` before this step.\n173:**`ok` effect:** The leaves of value `place` become `Uninit`; `place` **remains usable as a place** and may legally be reinitialized. Authorities held by `place` and dead substorages are released; dependencies of the ended value are removed; transitively owned places that cease to live are marked dead. The operation does not automatically trigger physical deallocation in the elementary memory machine: an unfreed allocation may constitute a leak, which this guarantee does not prohibit. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n175:## 70. O13 — Release of an Owned Storage Unit\n179:1. `place` is a **root of storage owned by a value** (`OwnedRoot`), not an ordinary local directly owned by a frame and not part of an aggregate.\n181:3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify`; no active derivative over the footprint being freed is held outside holders that die; no surviving place is anchored to an authority of the released region or of its dead holders. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from the check on active derivatives. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (an authority of the released region, its root included, or an authority whose holder dies) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n184:**`ok` effect:** `place` and the storage that ceases to live are marked dead; authorities of those regions, including their corresponding roots, become inactive, as do authorities whose holders die. Surviving dependencies must remain valid. Every realization of `free` must satisfy the corresponding deallocation relation of the elementary memory machine (Part III). **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n191:2. The provider is a usable place, and **all leaves of the provider** are `Init`; updates to its metadata are permitted through exercisable `Modify` or a valid `writeShared` over `SharedMutable` metadata.\n192:3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify` over `owner`; new identities and ownership relations must respect `WF3`, `RootHeld`, and liveness restrictions.\n198:- `ok(s')`: creation of a **new storage root** with fresh identity, owned by `owner`; its leaves start `Uninit`, its new root `Modify` authority with no origin is held by `owner`; provider metadata is updated only in accordance with its modification rights.\n225:3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n226:4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n231:- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n232:- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n233:- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n237:**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n245:**Preconditions:** `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over the relevant leaves, and the operation respects applicable pinning obligations.\n251:**Preconditions:** `unpin` designates one active dynamic pinning obligation, installed by `pin` on `place`; `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify`, and contract `K.ExternalDone`, applied to that obligation, attests that all relevant external use has ended.\n255:**Associated static rule (`StaticPinnedBottom`):** Statics designated by `K.Pinned` must be places **directly** owned by the **base frame**, not merely transitively owned by a static value. This restriction ensures that they do not die when an intermediate frame exits.\n265:1. `storage` is an existing, usable place under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It may be local, static, allocated, or a block issued by a previous arena.\n281:2. `region` belongs to the administered storage; `destination` is a usable **leaf** holding no active authority; the destination can legally receive the block value in mode `Modify`. The fact that `destination` is already `Init` is not in itself grounds for rejection, in accordance with O8.2.\n301:3. No still-usable place depends, directly or transitively, on an anchor in the **strict origin subtree of `internal`** (`internal` itself is excluded) that covers a leaf of the administered storage. Under precondition 2 and invariant I3, this condition follows from the absence of active descendants covering the storage, and remains explicit in the abstraction contract. Validity obligations of other nested arenas remain satisfied.\n313:The second-class nature of access authorities does not prohibit constructing a value carrying a derived authority or returning a result dependent on a caller-owned resource. What is prohibited is artificially extending the validity of a local origin.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"283af15d-eea7-46a9-ab80-c10a34702042","timestamp":"2026-11-13T01:39:42.035Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"603:## 53. I4 — Consumption and Movement\n607:## 54. Anchor Ordering and Absence of Orphan Rights\n611:## 55. ArenaInv — Invariant of the Arena Abstraction\n624:## 56. Uniqueness of Live Administration\n630:## 57. Main Invariant\n652:## 58. Transition Outcomes\n664:## 59. Implicit Preservation of Modes and Anchors\n668:## 60. O1 — Derivation of an Authority\n679:## 61. O2 — Splitting Authorities\n692:## 62. O3 — End of a Derived Borrow\n708:## 63. O4 — Consuming Value Transfer\n734:## 64. O5 — Explicit Attachment of an Anchor\n747:## 65. O8 — Controlled Storage of an Authority\n765:## 66. O9 — Safe Reading of Regions\n777:## 67. O10 — Ordinary Write\n