{"parentUuid":"304c2fec-5c87-4343-9805-4d9ede3d0df9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch2TVmTK9EKFvh7QBA2AL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HznmPBVq2YHjmUXTy6t5gT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":1,"limit":170},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11948,"cache_read_input_tokens":135620,"output_tokens":121,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11948,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":121,"cache_read_input_tokens":135620,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11948,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11948},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":745,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HznmPBVq2YHjmUXTy6t5gT":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":1,"limit":170}},"serverClassifierRequest":"7cde01cc-c28e-4b10-8ad9-34aa0258d442","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch2TVmFRbgKNRMSUyB3wz","requestRef":"844db481-e442-4484-8f52-f312299389e6","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"9b6ba694-c7f6-444a-9c3e-f6ebf2edc01e","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:25.657Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"9b6ba694-c7f6-444a-9c3e-f6ebf2edc01e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HznmPBVq2YHjmUXTy6t5gT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ERN — Concurrency Model Specification v0\n2\t\n3\t## Working Candidate 5.3 — Execution Contexts, Authority Transmission, and Concurrent Event Semantics **[WC5.3]**\n4\t\n5\t**Status:** Non-normative working candidate for exploratory design, proof, and adversarial testing; no normative stabilization is claimed.\n6\t\n7\t**Design baseline:** WC5.2 (the reviewed, three-editorial-retouch version) is carried forward without changing its existing candidate rules. The language owner has accepted the C13.11, C18.4, and C22.4 amendments and the explanatory clarifications of C21.5 and C22.1 as design decisions for this next non-normative working candidate; these decisions do **not** freeze Spec 2. The earlier C16–C18 and C22 candidate material, the prior candidate-status qualifications for D2, D3, P1, P2, and C22.5, and the remaining D-C1 through D-C10/event-model proposals retain their previously stated status. Neither the frozen ERN Charter v0 nor Spec 1 v0.1 is changed.\n8\t\n9\t**Higher authority:** ERN — Language Charter v0.\n10\t\n11\t**Frozen dependency:** ERN — Memory Model Specification v0.1.\n12\t\n13\t**Scope:** WC4 authority/concurrency baseline, proposed event and memory-order model, atomic/shared-mutation disciplines, cross-context handoff ordering, reference synchronization protocols, and layered data-race-freedom obligations.\n14\t\n15\t**Deferred or unresolved:** Complete admissibility semantics for all memory orders, global `seq_cst` constraints, release sequences, the no-thin-air decision, cancellation, broader synchronization protocols, and all unproved refinement and data-race-freedom obligations. This candidate states proposed rules and the precise remaining proof boundaries. **[WC5.3 — Milestone 4 evidence]** Alongside the earlier abstract-event/handoff results and bounded RC11 tests, a finite one-atomic-unit `EventConsistent` fragment and a conditional reference-mutex `rf`/`sw` bridge have been proved in exploratory Lean. They do not establish full physical `rf`/`mo` correspondence, safe-program data-race freedom, initialization ordering in every context, or a complete ERN memory model. The latest reported audit, after the complete-encapsulation and `KillKinds` passes, has **2,822** theorems (**1,878** non-internal) with no axioms beyond `{propext, Quot.sound}`. The guarded-chain premise inventory, capability provenance, dying-place classification, `spawnVal` pre-step capacity, and the three-case protected-data result have been proved in the reported exploratory model under their specified reachable-`RunFull` hypotheses; these are not independently re-executed proofs and do not establish physical realization or data-race freedom.\n16\t\n17\tThe rules in this candidate are proposed additions for Spec 2. No frozen rule of Spec 1 is amended.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t# I. Concurrent Execution Machine\n22\t\n23\t## C1. Execution Contexts\n24\t\n25\tAn **execution context** is a semantic execution unit with an identified current frame and an ordered sequence of operations within that context.\n26\t\n27\tAn execution context is not necessarily an operating-system thread, task, or scheduler unit.\n28\t\n29\tContext identity does not confer access authority.\n30\t\n31\tThe presence of multiple contexts does not duplicate any allocation, place, authority, or validity dependency.\n32\t\n33\t## C2. Concurrent State and External Contracts\n34\t\n35\tThe candidate concurrent authority machine is:\n36\t\n37\t\\[ S_C=(C,F_C,P,I,A,D) \\]\n38\t\n39\twhere:\n40\t\n41\t- `C` identifies execution contexts and their lifecycle states.\n42\t- `F_C` contains their local frame stacks and extended frame-parent relation.\n43\t- `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D` retain their meanings from Spec 1.\n44\t\n45\tThe authority and dependency state is globally coherent. Library administration states, including synchronization registries, remain separate from `S_C`.\n46\t\n47\tThe concurrency specification introduces an external contract extension `K_C` with a projection to the unchanged Spec 1 contract `K`. A synchronization registry may be supplied as an **external argument** to `K_C.SyncAuthorized` by the reference abstraction. It does not become a component of `S_C`. This is the candidate P2 convention, subject to formal verification of its state-dependent soundness.\n48\t\n49\tFor an inherited operation executed by context `c`, all occurrences of “the current frame” refer to the top of the live local stack of `c`. References to live frames, usable places, ownership chains, authority holders and orphan conditions range over the globally live frame structure. An inherited `K.Satisfiable` obligation for a context-executed allocation is evaluated on the `S₃` view of the executing context (`SC.view(s,c)` in the exploratory model); this is candidate P1 and must preserve the soundness requirements of Spec 1's unchanged `K`.\n50\t\n51\tFor a single context with no active concurrent mechanism, these interpretations and the projection of `K_C` must coincide with Spec 1.\n52\t\n53\t## C3. Frame Ancestry — H1\n54\t\n55\tEach live context has a nonempty local stack.\n56\t\n57\tFrame identities are globally unique.\n58\t\n59\tThe initial context contains the base frame of Spec 1, which never exits.\n60\t\n61\tA newly created ERN context receives an initial frame whose parent is the frame that created the context.\n62\t\n63\tThe extended `ancestor` relation follows the frame-parent links, including links across contexts.\n64\t\n65\tIt is reflexive, acyclic apart from reflexivity, and well-founded through its strict-parent relation.\n66\t\n67\tOn corresponding single-context states, it coincides exactly with Spec 1 §39.\n68\t\n69\tAncestry alone creates neither ownership nor access authority.\n70\t\n71\t## C4. Context Creation — `spawn`\n72\t\n73\t`spawn` is a proposed concurrent transition modeled on O6.\n74\t\n75\t**Preconditions**\n76\t\n77\t1. The creating context and its current frame are live.\n78\t2. The new context and frame identities are fresh.\n79\t3. Every transmitted access parameter is an active derived authority held by the creating frame.\n80\t4. Parameter transmission yields each such authority to the new frame, without duplication.\n81\t5. The applicable mode, origin, footprint, local-shape, anchoring and origin-protection conditions of O6 hold under the re-indexed frame interpretation. The origin-protection condition O6.3 is evaluated in the final state from the new context's current frame; O6.4 (`AnchorOrderHeld`) is also checked in the final state.\n82\t6. Any transmitted value satisfies the provisional composite by-value contract in C5.\n83\t\n84\t**Effects**\n85\t\n86\tThe new context is given an initial frame parented to the creating frame. Its local places and corresponding root authorities are created as in the applicable local-creation effect of O6. Access parameters are yielded to the new frame, which becomes their sole holder. No authority is copied.\n87\t\n88\tFor `spawn` without by-value arguments, these actions constitute the reference transition. For `spawn` with by-value arguments, the internal order is specified in C5 and the parameter-yield effect occurs after value transfer.\n89\t\n90\tAn active `Modify` derivative transferred to the child continues to suppress conflicting effective exercise through its origin in the creator, by Spec 1 §43.\n91\t\n92\t**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The complete transition must preserve `ConcurrentWF`, the applicable global authority invariants, `AnchorOrder`, `AnchorOrderHeld`, `NoDangling` and `NoOrphan`. Their preservation must be proved from the concrete preconditions and effects, not assumed as a conjunct of the precondition.\n93\t\n94\t**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject`.\n95\t\n96\t## C5. Provisional By-Value Transmission at `spawn`\n97\t\n98\tA `spawn` may provisionally transmit an owned value into a freshly created local destination in the new context. This is a **single composite candidate transition** with the following ordered phases, not an ordinary sequential execution of O6 followed by O4:\n99\t\n100\t1. **Create:** create the new context and initial frame, fresh local places, and their root authorities, **without yet yielding the access parameters**. This intermediate construction establishes destination places and their `Uninit` leaves, while the creator remains the executing frame for source checks.\n101\t2. **Transfer:** transfer each by-value source into its designated fresh destination. All non-resulting-state O4 requirements, including source-side `Modify` exercise and the external-authority restriction of O4.4, are evaluated **at the start of the corresponding transfer phase**, after local construction but before any access parameter is yielded. For multiple by-value arguments, the candidate uses their explicit argument order and evaluates each against the state produced by earlier transfers. Source and destination footprints are disjoint and their ordered structural shapes match.\n102\t3. **Yield:** at the beginning of this phase, in the state produced by the preceding value transfers, each transmitted authority must again satisfy the applicable parameter requirements of Spec 1 §72.1: it is active, derived, and held by the creator frame. Yield the declared access parameters to the child initial frame without duplication. Requirements that apply before the composite remain independently required.\n103\t4. **Final checks:** evaluate O4's expressly resulting-state conditions (including §§63.6–63.9 as applicable), O6.3 and O6.4, and the applicable anchor and parameter-passing conditions on the final state. Preserve the correspondence of all effects to O4 and O6.\n104\t\n105\t**Additional candidate preconditions**\n106\t\n107\t1. The creator has exercisable `Modify` over every source's relevant footprint at its transfer phase; the source is usable and its required leaves are `Init`.\n108\t2. Every destination is a fresh local root of the child, with corresponding `Uninit` leaves, no derived authorities and a `Modify` root authority held by the child frame under `RootHeld`.\n109\t3. Only O4.1's **ordinary destination-side exercisability requirement** is replaced by the special fresh-destination construction condition. The source-side exercise condition remains required.\n110\t4. Every other applicable precondition of O4 remains required, including pinning, validity, external-derived-authority restrictions, self-blocking prevention, and final destination usability.\n111\t5. **No access parameter to be yielded may cover any leaf of any by-value source transferred by the same composite.** The test uses the declared source footprints before consumption, preventing a parameter from remaining associated with leaves becoming `Moved`.\n112\t6. No exclusive authority, dependent right or owned resource is duplicated by the composite.\n113\t\n114\t**Effects**\n115\t\n116\tThe transfer phase marks consumed source leaves `Moved`, corresponding destination leaves `Init`, and relocates the corresponding held authorities, ownership links and dependencies by O4's effects. The yield phase changes holders of the access parameters and creates no rights.\n117\t\n118\t**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The final transition must preserve `ConcurrentWF`, I1–I4, the applicable anchor ordering properties, `NoDangling` and `NoOrphan`. These must be established by proofs of the composite, not inserted as assumed final-state conjuncts.\n119\t\n120\t**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject`.\n121\t\n122\tThis is an experimental calling convention for Spec 2, not a finalized rule for Spec 3. The exact proof obligations remain open under `OB-C09` and `OB-C14`.\n123\t\n124\t## C6. Frame Exit and Reparenting\n125\t\n126\t`exitFrameC` applies O7 to the current **non-initial frame of the executing context's local stack**. That frame must have a surviving caller in the same local stack. This explicitly transposes O7.1 without making ordinary frame exit applicable to a context-initial frame.\n127\t\n128\t**Preconditions**\n129\t\n130\t1. The applicable conditions O7.2–O7.5 hold for the global authority and dependency state, under the extended ancestry relation.\n131\t2. In particular, no surviving active authority, owned place or required anchor is invalidated by the exit or its reanchoring.\n132\t\n133\t**Effect**\n134\t\n135\tLet `f` be the exiting frame and `p = parent(f)` its surviving immediate caller. Apply the applicable O7 effects. Every surviving context-initial frame whose immediate parent was `f` receives the new parent `p`. All other ancestry links are unchanged. Reparenting creates no right, does not revive storage, and does not independently authorize an otherwise invalid exit.\n136\t\n137\t**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The resulting frame relation must be well-founded, and the complete transition must preserve the applicable authority and anchor invariants, including `AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld`. Proofs must account for the effect of the extended ancestry relation without treating final-state invariants as assumed preconditions.\n138\t\n139\tThe transition introduces no implicit wait for the child contexts.\n140\t\n141\t## C7. Context Termination — `terminate`\n142\t\n143\t`terminate` applies the corresponding O7 validity requirements to the initial frame of a terminating non-initial context, after its nested call frames have legally ended.\n144\t\n145\tIts effects include:\n146\t\n147\t- ending the terminating context's initial frame;\n148\t- performing applicable releases and legal reanchoring;\n149\t- reparenting surviving context-initial frames as specified in C6;\n150\t- removing the terminating context from the live-context set;\n151\t- establishing its completed lifecycle state.\n152\t\n153\tCompletion does not automatically end resources independently and legally owned by surviving contexts.\n154\t\n155\tThe initial base context remains subject to its distinct base-frame restrictions.\n156\t\n157\t## C8. Reference Completion Operation — `join`\n158\t\n159\t`join` is a candidate reference operation for observing completion of a previously created context. Its argument is a context identifier; this candidate does not introduce an additional completion-capability mechanism.\n160\t\n161\t- If the identifier is unknown to the execution, the operation returns `reject`.\n162\t- If the identified context is live, successful completion observation is not yet enabled. Awaiting completion is not a new `Outcome3` result.\n163\t- If the identified context has a recorded completed lifecycle state, `join` returns `ok`.\n164\t\n165\t**Successful effect:** The abstract state is unchanged, including `C`, `F_C`, `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D`. No consumed or “joined” marker is introduced by this candidate.\n166\t\n167\tThe complete event-ordering consequences of successful completion observation are deferred to the concurrent event model. `join` is not the only possible completion protocol, and its existence does not impose structured concurrency universally.\n168\t\n169\t## C9. Cross-Context Authorities and Dependencies\n170\t"}]},"uuid":"96aea384-4956-4a44-b5eb-8fa373ab0019","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:25.682Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","content":"# ERN — Concurrency Model Specification v0\n\n## Working Candidate 5.3 — Execution Contexts, Authority Transmission, and Concurrent Event Semantics **[WC5.3]**\n\n**Status:** Non-normative working candidate for exploratory design, proof, and adversarial testing; no normative stabilization is claimed.\n\n**Design baseline:** WC5.2 (the reviewed, three-editorial-retouch version) is carried forward without changing its existing candidate rules. The language owner has accepted the C13.11, C18.4, and C22.4 amendments and the explanatory clarifications of C21.5 and C22.1 as design decisions for this next non-normative working candidate; these decisions do **not** freeze Spec 2. The earlier C16–C18 and C22 candidate material, the prior candidate-status qualifications for D2, D3, P1, P2, and C22.5, and the remaining D-C1 through D-C10/event-model proposals retain their previously stated status. Neither the frozen ERN Charter v0 nor Spec 1 v0.1 is changed.\n\n**Higher authority:** ERN — Language Charter v0.\n\n**Frozen dependency:** ERN — Memory Model Specification v0.1.\n\n**Scope:** WC4 authority/concurrency baseline, proposed event and memory-order model, atomic/shared-mutation disciplines, cross-context handoff ordering, reference synchronization protocols, and layered data-race-freedom obligations.\n\n**Deferred or unresolved:** Complete admissibility semantics for all memory orders, global `seq_cst` constraints, release sequences, the no-thin-air decision, cancellation, broader synchronization protocols, and all unproved refinement and data-race-freedom obligations. This candidate states proposed rules and the precise remaining proof boundaries. **[WC5.3 — Milestone 4 evidence]** Alongside the earlier abstract-event/handoff results and bounded RC11 tests, a finite one-atomic-unit `EventConsistent` fragment and a conditional reference-mutex `rf`/`sw` bridge have been proved in exploratory Lean. They do not establish full physical `rf`/`mo` correspondence, safe-program data-race freedom, initialization ordering in every context, or a complete ERN memory model. The latest reported audit, after the complete-encapsulation and `KillKinds` passes, has **2,822** theorems (**1,878** non-internal) with no axioms beyond `{propext, Quot.sound}`. The guarded-chain premise inventory, capability provenance, dying-place classification, `spawnVal` pre-step capacity, and the three-case protected-data result have been proved in the reported exploratory model under their specified reachable-`RunFull` hypotheses; these are not independently re-executed proofs and do not establish physical realization or data-race freedom.\n\nThe rules in this candidate are proposed additions for Spec 2. No frozen rule of Spec 1 is amended.\n\n---\n\n# I. Concurrent Execution Machine\n\n## C1. Execution Contexts\n\nAn **execution context** is a semantic execution unit with an identified current frame and an ordered sequence of operations within that context.\n\nAn execution context is not necessarily an operating-system thread, task, or scheduler unit.\n\nContext identity does not confer access authority.\n\nThe presence of multiple contexts does not duplicate any allocation, place, authority, or validity dependency.\n\n## C2. Concurrent State and External Contracts\n\nThe candidate concurrent authority machine is:\n\n\\[ S_C=(C,F_C,P,I,A,D) \\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- `C` identifies execution contexts and their lifecycle states.\n- `F_C` contains their local frame stacks and extended frame-parent relation.\n- `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D` retain their meanings from Spec 1.\n\nThe authority and dependency state is globally coherent. Library administration states, including synchronization registries, remain separate from `S_C`.\n\nThe concurrency specification introduces an external contract extension `K_C` with a projection to the unchanged Spec 1 contract `K`. A synchronization registry may be supplied as an **external argument** to `K_C.SyncAuthorized` by the reference abstraction. It does not become a component of `S_C`. This is the candidate P2 convention, subject to formal verification of its state-dependent soundness.\n\nFor an inherited operation executed by context `c`, all occurrences of “the current frame” refer to the top of the live local stack of `c`. References to live frames, usable places, ownership chains, authority holders and orphan conditions range over the globally live frame structure. An inherited `K.Satisfiable` obligation for a context-executed allocation is evaluated on the `S₃` view of the executing context (`SC.view(s,c)` in the exploratory model); this is candidate P1 and must preserve the soundness requirements of Spec 1's unchanged `K`.\n\nFor a single context with no active concurrent mechanism, these interpretations and the projection of `K_C` must coincide with Spec 1.\n\n## C3. Frame Ancestry — H1\n\nEach live context has a nonempty local stack.\n\nFrame identities are globally unique.\n\nThe initial context contains the base frame of Spec 1, which never exits.\n\nA newly created ERN context receives an initial frame whose parent is the frame that created the context.\n\nThe extended `ancestor` relation follows the frame-parent links, including links across contexts.\n\nIt is reflexive, acyclic apart from reflexivity, and well-founded through its strict-parent relation.\n\nOn corresponding single-context states, it coincides exactly with Spec 1 §39.\n\nAncestry alone creates neither ownership nor access authority.\n\n## C4. Context Creation — `spawn`\n\n`spawn` is a proposed concurrent transition modeled on O6.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The creating context and its current frame are live.\n2. The new context and frame identities are fresh.\n3. Every transmitted access parameter is an active derived authority held by the creating frame.\n4. Parameter transmission yields each such authority to the new frame, without duplication.\n5. The applicable mode, origin, footprint, local-shape, anchoring and origin-protection conditions of O6 hold under the re-indexed frame interpretation. The origin-protection condition O6.3 is evaluated in the final state from the new context's current frame; O6.4 (`AnchorOrderHeld`) is also checked in the final state.\n6. Any transmitted value satisfies the provisional composite by-value contract in C5.\n\n**Effects**\n\nThe new context is given an initial frame parented to the creating frame. Its local places and corresponding root authorities are created as in the applicable local-creation effect of O6. Access parameters are yielded to the new frame, which becomes their sole holder. No authority is copied.\n\nFor `spawn` without by-value arguments, these actions constitute the reference transition. For `spawn` with by-value arguments, the internal order is specified in C5 and the parameter-yield effect occurs after value transfer.\n\nAn active `Modify` derivative transferred to the child continues to suppress conflicting effective exercise through its origin in the creator, by Spec 1 §43.\n\n**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The complete transition must preserve `ConcurrentWF`, the applicable global authority invariants, `AnchorOrder`, `AnchorOrderHeld`, `NoDangling` and `NoOrphan`. Their preservation must be proved from the concrete preconditions and effects, not assumed as a conjunct of the precondition.\n\n**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject`.\n\n## C5. Provisional By-Value Transmission at `spawn`\n\nA `spawn` may provisionally transmit an owned value into a freshly created local destination in the new context. This is a **single composite candidate transition** with the following ordered phases, not an ordinary sequential execution of O6 followed by O4:\n\n1. **Create:** create the new context and initial frame, fresh local places, and their root authorities, **without yet yielding the access parameters**. This intermediate construction establishes destination places and their `Uninit` leaves, while the creator remains the executing frame for source checks.\n2. **Transfer:** transfer each by-value source into its designated fresh destination. All non-resulting-state O4 requirements, including source-side `Modify` exercise and the external-authority restriction of O4.4, are evaluated **at the start of the corresponding transfer phase**, after local construction but before any access parameter is yielded. For multiple by-value arguments, the candidate uses their explicit argument order and evaluates each against the state produced by earlier transfers. Source and destination footprints are disjoint and their ordered structural shapes match.\n3. **Yield:** at the beginning of this phase, in the state produced by the preceding value transfers, each transmitted authority must again satisfy the applicable parameter requirements of Spec 1 §72.1: it is active, derived, and held by the creator frame. Yield the declared access parameters to the child initial frame without duplication. Requirements that apply before the composite remain independently required.\n4. **Final checks:** evaluate O4's expressly resulting-state conditions (including §§63.6–63.9 as applicable), O6.3 and O6.4, and the applicable anchor and parameter-passing conditions on the final state. Preserve the correspondence of all effects to O4 and O6.\n\n**Additional candidate preconditions**\n\n1. The creator has exercisable `Modify` over every source's relevant footprint at its transfer phase; the source is usable and its required leaves are `Init`.\n2. Every destination is a fresh local root of the child, with corresponding `Uninit` leaves, no derived authorities and a `Modify` root authority held by the child frame under `RootHeld`.\n3. Only O4.1's **ordinary destination-side exercisability requirement** is replaced by the special fresh-destination construction condition. The source-side exercise condition remains required.\n4. Every other applicable precondition of O4 remains required, including pinning, validity, external-derived-authority restrictions, self-blocking prevention, and final destination usability.\n5. **No access parameter to be yielded may cover any leaf of any by-value source transferred by the same composite.** The test uses the declared source footprints before consumption, preventing a parameter from remaining associated with leaves becoming `Moved`.\n6. No exclusive authority, dependent right or owned resource is duplicated by the composite.\n\n**Effects**\n\nThe transfer phase marks consumed source leaves `Moved`, corresponding destination leaves `Init`, and relocates the corresponding held authorities, ownership links and dependencies by O4's effects. The yield phase changes holders of the access parameters and creates no rights.\n\n**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The final transition must preserve `ConcurrentWF`, I1–I4, the applicable anchor ordering properties, `NoDangling` and `NoOrphan`. These must be established by proofs of the composite, not inserted as assumed final-state conjuncts.\n\n**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject`.\n\nThis is an experimental calling convention for Spec 2, not a finalized rule for Spec 3. The exact proof obligations remain open under `OB-C09` and `OB-C14`.\n\n## C6. Frame Exit and Reparenting\n\n`exitFrameC` applies O7 to the current **non-initial frame of the executing context's local stack**. That frame must have a surviving caller in the same local stack. This explicitly transposes O7.1 without making ordinary frame exit applicable to a context-initial frame.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The applicable conditions O7.2–O7.5 hold for the global authority and dependency state, under the extended ancestry relation.\n2. In particular, no surviving active authority, owned place or required anchor is invalidated by the exit or its reanchoring.\n\n**Effect**\n\nLet `f` be the exiting frame and `p = parent(f)` its surviving immediate caller. Apply the applicable O7 effects. Every surviving context-initial frame whose immediate parent was `f` receives the new parent `p`. All other ancestry links are unchanged. Reparenting creates no right, does not revive storage, and does not independently authorize an otherwise invalid exit.\n\n**Preservation obligations, not additional preconditions:** The resulting frame relation must be well-founded, and the complete transition must preserve the applicable authority and anchor invariants, including `AnchorOrder` and `AnchorOrderHeld`. Proofs must account for the effect of the extended ancestry relation without treating final-state invariants as assumed preconditions.\n\nThe transition introduces no implicit wait for the child contexts.\n\n## C7. Context Termination — `terminate`\n\n`terminate` applies the corresponding O7 validity requirements to the initial frame of a terminating non-initial context, after its nested call frames have legally ended.\n\nIts effects include:\n\n- ending the terminating context's initial frame;\n- performing applicable releases and legal reanchoring;\n- reparenting surviving context-initial frames as specified in C6;\n- removing the terminating context from the live-context set;\n- establishing its completed lifecycle state.\n\nCompletion does not automatically end resources independently and legally owned by surviving contexts.\n\nThe initial base context remains subject to its distinct base-frame restrictions.\n\n## C8. Reference Completion Operation — `join`\n\n`join` is a candidate reference operation for observing completion of a previously created context. Its argument is a context identifier; this candidate does not introduce an additional completion-capability mechanism.\n\n- If the identifier is unknown to the execution, the operation returns `reject`.\n- If the identified context is live, successful completion observation is not yet enabled. Awaiting completion is not a new `Outcome3` result.\n- If the identified context has a recorded completed lifecycle state, `join` returns `ok`.\n\n**Successful effect:** The abstract state is unchanged, including `C`, `F_C`, `P`, `I`, `A`, and `D`. No consumed or “joined” marker is introduced by this candidate.\n\nThe complete event-ordering consequences of successful completion observation are deferred to the concurrent event model. `join` is not the only possible completion protocol, and its existence does not impose structured concurrency universally.\n\n## C9. Cross-Context Authorities and Dependencies\n","numLines":170,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1202}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9b6ba694-c7f6-444a-9c3e-f6ebf2edc01e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
