{"parentUuid":"f35bea5a-15f1-4552-a4f6-4305af210671","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch2TVusayQLQhdWhuCYRo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VxWMnfsyGfnbD3A7Za8KKZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":230,"limit":280},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5279,"cache_read_input_tokens":147568,"output_tokens":411,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":289},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5279,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":411,"cache_read_input_tokens":147568,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5279,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5279},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":799,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VxWMnfsyGfnbD3A7Za8KKZ":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":230,"limit":280}},"serverClassifierRequest":"aaa62b2c-8f40-4a38-a376-3747497daec3","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch2TVuewU3G9MgiVyuGq4","requestRef":"1b5f0647-f13f-4c6b-a39f-820c3967691c","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"df806f57-4b7c-46c6-a191-adaf63cab0d9","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:29.843Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"df806f57-4b7c-46c6-a191-adaf63cab0d9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VxWMnfsyGfnbD3A7Za8KKZ","type":"tool_result","content":"230\t## C13. Reference Construction — `newMutex`\n231\t\n232\t`newMutex` is a library operation establishing a synchronized authority-administration entry. Let `slot` be the place holding `guarded`, `M` its immediate structural parent and `ctl = ControlOf(M,slot)` its controller leaf.\n233\t\n234\t**Preconditions**\n235\t\n236\t1. `guarded` is active, of own mode `Modify`, and legally held by the usable `slot`.\n237\t2. The constructing frame has ordinary exercisable `Modify` over `guarded` under Spec 1 §43.\n238\t3. The declared structural positions identify the usable `slot` and a usable controller **leaf** `ctl` in the same `M`.\n239\t4. `footprint(guarded) ∩ footprint(M) = ∅`. This is the reference mutex's structural disjointness requirement, allowing the control value `M` to be shared under compatible `Observe` authorities while `guarded` exclusively protects a distinct storage footprint.\n240\t5. If `guarded` is stored by O8, the construction respects O8.3. In the reference arrangement, the protected root `T` is owned by `M`, not by `slot`; its leaves are not thereby included in `footprint(M)`.\n241\t6. `K.SharedMutable(ctl)` holds for the controller leaf.\n242\t7. The `guarded` identity is not registered as a live administrative identity by any other registered administration. This is an unconditional candidate unified consistency guard, following the non-duplication principle of Spec 1 §56.\n243\t8. **Controller uniqueness:** no other live synchronization entry is associated with `ctl`. The association is injective among live entries.\n244\t9. No active descendant of `guarded` covers a leaf of `footprint(guarded)`.\n245\t10. **Initialization:** all structural leaves in `footprint(M)` are `Init` before construction. The operation does not implicitly initialize them. Storage merely owned by `M`, but structurally outside `footprint(M)`, is not included in this requirement solely by ownership.\n246\t11. **Controller-initialization authority [WC5.2]:** The constructing frame has ordinary exercisable `Modify` authority with effective mode `Modify` over the controller leaf `ctl`, under Spec 1 §43 and the applicable ordinary-write requirements of O10.1 (§67.1). The ordinary-write exclusivity requirement of O10.2 (Spec 1 §67.2) also applies to this non-atomic write; its derivation from the reachable-state invariants is not separately established by the cited Lean checks. This authority is required to justify the non-atomic physical write establishing the controller's initial available state. A controller-access right sufficient only for an `Observe`-mode shared mutation under Spec 1 §68.2 does not satisfy this requirement.\n247\t\n248\t**Effect**\n249\t\n250\tThe library registers an initially available `SyncEntry { guarded, grant = none }`, without creating another access authority over the protected storage or initializing any leaf of `M`. Library administration state remains separate from `S_C`.\n251\t\n252\t**Controller-initialization clarification [WC5.2]:** This is an abstract registration effect: the operation changes no structural leaf's abstract initialization state. C13.10 already requires every structural leaf of `M`, including `ctl`, to be `Init` before construction; `newMutex` does not change an `Uninit` leaf to `Init`. Separately, the physical realization establishes the controller's initial available synchronization value by a **non-atomic write** to `ctl`, justified by C13.11 and subject to the applicable O10.1/O10.2 requirements. The term *controller initialization* in C31 and C48 denotes that physical synchronization-state write, not a transition of abstract structural initialization state. The non-atomic write is represented as an initial modification in `mo` under C31; it does not itself establish `sw`. Its concrete event, the ordering of this write with accesses in other execution contexts, and the correspondence with the initially available registry entry remain obligations under C27–C29, C42, C48 and `OB-C05`. The authority-only kernel state `S_C` does not establish the physical write or its ordering.\n253\t\n254\t**Preservation obligation:** The construction must establish the entire `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` suite for the new and previously live entries. This is not an additional resulting-state precondition.\n255\t\n256\t**Interface and realization obligations:** The safe public interface must prevent falsification of the positional controller association (C13.3); the controller synchronization protocol (C13.6) must be enforced; and the initial physical controller state must correspond to the newly available abstract registry entry. C13.11 supplies a required authority premise for the initial ordinary write, but does not by itself prove its ordering with other contexts or its target realization. These are not claimed to follow from the authority-only state `S_C`. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-NC demonstrates that C13.1–C13.10 alone admit construction without controller rights; the added C13.11 rejects it in the amended model, while all 16 previously recorded construction steps satisfy the new premise. The abstract `newMutex` effect is unchanged.\n257\t\n258\tA reference representation may place the protected value in a distinct logical owned-storage root. No separate physical allocation is implied.\n259\t\n260\t## C14. Concurrent External Contract — `SyncAuthorized`\n261\t\n262\t`K_C` extends the unchanged Spec 1 contract `K`. The proposed concurrent authorization predicate accepts the **current logical synchronization registry as an explicit external argument**:\n263\t\n264\t```\n265\tK_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, requesting_frame)\n266\t```\n267\t\n268\tThe registry is supplied by the library contract and is not part of the kernel state `S_C`. It must refer to the same logical registry state used for the candidate library transition.\n269\t\n270\tFor the reference mutex, the library's **authorization facts**, and only these facts, are:\n271\t\n272\t1. There exists a live registered entry with the requested `guarded` identity.\n273\t2. That entry has `grant = none`.\n274\t3. The requesting frame has the required effective and exercisable access to `ControlOf(entry)` under the controller-access obligation corresponding to Spec 1 §68.2.\n275\t\n276\tThe library must prove, under `ConcurrentWF`, the applicable Spec 1 invariants, `SyncInv`, `GrantDiscipline`, and the abstraction's encapsulation contract, that these facts imply the remaining authority prerequisites for `deriveSync`, including origin-chain validity, sufficient effective mode, absence of blocking descendants and I1 conservation.\n277\t\n278\tThe opaque authorization predicate must not be defined by simply conjoining the conclusions the library is required to prove. Its instantiation is state-dependent, and correctness must be reestablished for every enabled acquisition.\n279\t\n280\t`SyncAuthorized` does not create an authority, does not introduce ambient context rights, and cannot be used outside the corresponding valid synchronization contract. The precise synchronized physical realization remains deferred under `OB-C05`.\n281\t\n282\t## C15. New Concurrent Transition — `deriveSync`\n283\t\n284\t`deriveSync` is a new candidate concurrent transition creating an ordinary derived `Modify` authority from `guarded` over `footprint(guarded)`.\n285\t\n286\t**Preconditions**\n287\t\n288\t1. **Own-mode bound, retained from Spec 1 §43 and O1.1:** `guarded` is active and its **own mode permits `Modify`**. In the `Observe | Modify` mode order this requires own mode `Modify`, **even if `footprint(guarded)` is empty**.\n289\t2. The complete applicable origin-chain and effective-anchor validity requirements of O1.1 hold.\n290\t3. **Exclusive authorization:** `K_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, current_frame)` holds. This predicate is the **only** authorization for `deriveSync`. Neither the current-frame-holder nor the place-holder alternative of ordinary `ExercisableAt` independently enables this new transition. Ordinary O1 retains both alternatives unchanged.\n291\t4. O1.2's leaf, subfootprint and effective-mode conditions hold for the full protected footprint and requested mode `Modify`.\n292\t5. O1.3's structural exclusivity and I1 preservation conditions hold.\n293\t6. O1.4's freshness, legal current-frame holder and resulting-state validity conditions hold.\n294\t\n295\t**Effect**\n296\t\n297\tA fresh, active authority is created with own mode `Modify`, origin `guarded`, full footprint `footprint(guarded)`, and holder equal to the executing context's current frame. No other access right is created.\n298\t\n299\tThis grant is ordinary after creation: `ExercisableAt` remains exactly as in Spec 1 §43 for its exercise, reborrowing, storage and legal release. The synchronized exception concerns **only the authorization for creating this derivative**; it does not weaken the source activity, own-mode bound, anchor, effective-mode or structural exclusivity requirements of O1.\n300\t\n301\t**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject` for the abstract safety transition; a busy controller may make the enclosing library acquisition temporarily non-enabled, as specified in C16.\n302\t\n303\t## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n304\t\n305\tThe library operation `acquire` combines synchronization-controller acquisition and `deriveSync` into one coherent abstract operation.\n306\t\n307\t**Outcome classification (proposed D-C1)**\n308\t\n309\t1. **`reject` — invalid request:** a failure of entry existence/liveness (C14.1), controller-access authority (C14.3, including the applicable Spec 1 §68.2 contract), or any other applicable authority, register-validity, origin, footprint, invariant, or Spec 1 safety precondition yields `reject`. These failures remain `reject` even when the controller is occupied or `grant ≠ none` in the same state.\n310\t2. **Temporarily non-enabled — contention:** only after all non-contention validity and authority conditions hold, the entry's `grant ≠ none` (C14.2), or occupation of the controller by another acquisition under its valid protocol, makes `acquire` temporarily non-enabled. This is **not** `reject`, `fail`, or an additional `Outcome3` value; no successful transition is produced. Non-enablement may persist indefinitely.\n311\t3. **`ok` — successful acquisition:** all validity and authority obligations hold, the grant is available and the controller is successfully acquired under its protocol, and the successful effect below is established.\n312\t\n313\t`reject` takes precedence over temporary non-enablement **for the same abstract state**; this is a classification of causes and does not assert that a concurrent call has an outcome independent of changing state. Resource `fail` outcomes of `alloc`/`issue` are outside this criterion.\n314\t\n315\tFor `acquire`, the authorization facts C14.1 and C14.3 are safety conditions; C14.2 is a contention condition. The combined `SyncAuthorized` predicate of C14 is therefore insufficient by itself to distinguish `reject` from temporary non-enablement. This does not alter the independent transition `deriveSync` or its C15 conditions.\n316\t\n317\tThe library must establish all non-dynamic safety preconditions before committing a successful acquisition.\n318\t\n319\tIts success:\n320\t\n321\t1. acquires the controller under the applicable synchronization protocol;\n322\t2. creates the valid grant through `deriveSync`;\n323\t3. records the resulting grant identity in `SyncEntry.grant`.\n324\t\n325\tNo partially successful abstract operation may expose an invalid ordinary state.\n326\t\n327\tThe controller's physical linearization, atomic ordering, and correspondence obligations are specified as proposals in C41, C48 and C49 and remain subject to `OB-C05`.\n328\t\n329\t## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n330\t\n331\t`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n332\t\n333\t**Abstract preconditions**\n334\t\n335\t1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n336\t2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n337\t3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n338\t4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n339\t\n340\t**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n341\t\n342\t- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n343\t- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n344\t\n345\tThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n346\t\n347\t**Abstract effect**\n348\t\n349\tThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available as one coherent library transition.\n350\t\n351\t**Realization obligation, not an assumed abstract precondition:** The controller update must satisfy `K.SharedMutable` and the required event, atomicity and ordering discipline. The physical protocol and its correspondence are specified provisionally in C41, C48 and C49 and remain under `OB-C05`, rather than being treated as proven by the abstract transition.\n352\t\n353\t## C18. Abandoned Grants and Recovery **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n354\t\n355\tA grant may become inactive through an inherited legal release or frame exit without a corresponding `unlock`; the live registry entry may still record `grant = some g`. This can leave the mutex unavailable indefinitely without itself violating memory safety.\n356\t\n357\t`resetLock` is a candidate library recovery operation.\n358\t\n359\t**Abstract preconditions**\n360\t\n361\t1. Ordinary `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` holds under Spec 1 §43, requiring the appropriate exclusive exercise through the mutex's holder.\n362\t2. No active descendant of `guarded` covers any protected leaf, as in the relevant no-descendant condition of Spec 1 §78.2.\n363\t3. `ControlOf(entry)` resolves to an existing, usable controller **leaf**, as required by C21.3, and recovery does not invalidate surviving dependent authorities. The controller-initialization invariant is established separately for every live entry by C21.7.\n364\t4. **Controller-access authority [WC5.2]:** The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)`, under the corresponding requirement of Spec 1 §68.2. This condition is independent of, and additional to, `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` in item 1. It authorizes the controller access required by the recovery protocol without granting any new authority.\n365\t\n366\t**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n367\t\n368\t- **`ok`** when the applicable recovery and authority conditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n369\t- **`reject`** when any applicable authority, registry, dependent-validity, controller-validity, or Spec 1 precondition fails. Controller occupation does not mask such a failure as temporary non-enablement.\n370\t\n371\tThere is no newly introduced `resetLock` waiting outcome and no new prerequisite that the recorded grant first be `none`. This transition is the exceptional recovery protocol described here, not ordinary `unlock`.\n372\t\n373\t**Abstract effect**\n374\t\n375\tThe registered grant becomes `none` and the controller's logical state becomes available. Recovery does not revive an inactive grant and does not fabricate authority.\n376\t\n377\t**Realization obligation:** The exclusive recovery discipline and its physical controller update must meet `OB-C05` and the event constraints proposed in C41, C48 and C49; this is not presumed proved by the abstract state transition. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-RC establishes that the earlier C18.1–C18.3 allowed recovery with no controller right; C18.4 excludes that step in the amended model, while W-RL, W-RL-J, the S11 positive recovery and the W-RC positive counterpart remain admissible. The recorded grant may still be `none`.\n378\t\n379\t## C19. Synchronized Administration\n380\t\n381\tA grant protecting an administrative value may permit `MetaRight` only when it provides exercisable `Modify` over every required leaf of that administrative value.\n382\t\n383\tThe grant must therefore cover the entire required administrative footprint.\n384\t\n385\tThis does not bypass Spec 1 §36.\n386\t\n387\tIssuance, recycling, and reset operations retain their existing authority and dependency restrictions.\n388\t\n389\tThe lifetime of a block issued during synchronized administration is determined by its actual origin chain, holders, anchors, and ownership relations, not merely by the lifetime of the synchronization grant.\n390\t\n391\t---\n392\t\n393\t# III. Invariants and Correspondence\n394\t\n395\t## C20. Concurrent Frame and Authority Well-Formedness\n396\t\n397\t`ConcurrentWF` extends frame well-formedness to multiple local stacks and one global parent relationship.\n398\t\n399\tIt must preserve the applicable structural, ownership, holder, and authority conditions of Spec 1.\n400\t\n401\tEvery inherited transition and every new concurrent transition must preserve the applicable global invariants.\n402\t\n403\tReparenting must preserve extended ancestry and validity properties.\n404\t\n405\t## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n406\t\n407\tFor the reference mutex abstraction, the following state properties must be preserved over all **live** entries, except the bound-identity property in item 6, which applies to every entry:\n408\t\n409\t1. **Guarded authority:** `guarded` is active, has own mode `Modify`, and is held by a usable place.\n410\t2. **Grant record:** If `grant = some g`, then the recorded authority exists and `origin(g) = guarded` and `footprint(g) = footprint(guarded)`. `g` need not be active; the invariant must handle legal release and reanchoring without silently weakening these equalities.\n411\t3. **Controller validity:** `ControlOf(entry)` identifies an existing usable **leaf** associated with the holder `slot` and its structural mutex parent `M`.\n412\t4. **Controller uniqueness:** Distinct live entries have distinct `ControlOf` leaves; the guarded-identity uniqueness guard is also respected. The same controller cannot be registered twice under different guarded identities.\n413\t5. **`GrantSubtree`:** If `grant = some g` and `g` is active, every active descendant of `guarded` covering a protected leaf is either `g` itself or a descendant of `g`. This is a distinct invariant, necessary to restore grant availability after legal `unlock`.\n414\t\n415\t   **Clarification — inactive recorded grants and surviving derivatives [WC5.3]:** The condition above applies when the recorded grant is active. It does not require a recorded inactive grant to remain an ancestor, under the current `origin` relation, of every legally surviving derivative.\n416\t\n417\t   **[Exploratory evidence — Milestone 4, tranche 3]** In the exploratory model, for every execution admitted under the complete encapsulation contract of C22 (`RunFull`) from the initial state, the active strict transitive descendants of `guarded` that cover a protected leaf all stem from the most recent successful `acquire` of that entry (`last_acquire`); when `grant = none` there is none (`empty_without_grant`). An authority that enters this set at a step is either the grant recorded by that step's `acquire`, or is created by that step with, as its origin, an authority already in the set. No existing authority enters the set by any admitted step, legal reanchoring included (`closure_run`). A reference proof remains an obligation under C23 and `OB-C28`.\n418\t\n419\t   Legal reanchoring under Spec 1 §73.4 may change the direct `origin` of an existing surviving authority, including making it a direct descendant of `guarded`, without creating an authority or introducing an existing authority into the transitive descendant set. Consequently, when the recorded grant is inactive, a covering derivative may remain active under `guarded` even though its current origin chain no longer contains the recorded grant.\n420\t\n421\t   While any such surviving derivative remains active and covers a protected leaf, `resetLock` cannot succeed (C18.2).\n422\t\n423\t   When `grant = none`, `GrantDiscipline` in C22 requires that no active descendant of `guarded` cover a protected leaf.\n424\t\n425\t   These statements clarify consequences for reachable admitted executions. They add no independent state conjunct to `SyncInv`, require no historical-origin field, and do not prohibit otherwise legal surviving derivatives. They establish authority provenance, not the `happens-before` ordering required by C46.\n426\t6. **`RegBound`:** Every registered `guarded` identity denotes an existing authority identity, even when its entry is no longer live. This prevents a dormant entry from becoming live merely when a fresh authority is created with its identity.\n427\t7. **Structural initialization:** For each live entry, every structural leaf of the place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n428\t\n429\t`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n430\t\n431\tThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n432\t\n433\t## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6); WC5.3 (item 1 clarification)]**\n434\t\n435\tThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n436\t\n437\t```\n438\tlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n439\t⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n440\t   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n441\t```\n442\t\n443\tIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n444\t\n445\tFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n446\t\n447\t1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded` while the entry is live.\n448\t\n449\t   No operation exposed by the safe abstraction, including a compound operation that performs an internal `derive` or `split`, creates an ordinary authority whose origin at creation is `guarded` while the entry is live.\n450\t\n451\t   **Clarification [WC5.3]:** This restriction concerns the creation of authorities by operations exposed through the safe abstraction. It is not a state invariant forbidding an existing authority from having `origin = guarded` after a legal reanchoring.\n452\t\n453\t   In particular, reanchoring performed under Spec 1 §73.4 during a legal `exitFrame` or `terminate` may change the direct `origin` of an already existing surviving derivative to `guarded`. Such reanchoring does not issue a new derivative, does not constitute an independent `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`, and does not introduce an existing authority from outside the transitive descendant set of `guarded` into that set while the entry remains live.\n454\t\n455\t   Ordinary derivation from an active acquired grant, from its descendants, or from a legally surviving descendant remains eligible under the applicable inherited preconditions and the complete holder-chain restrictions of C22.6. Such derivation is not directly from `guarded` merely because its source is a descendant of `guarded`.\n456\t\n457\t   The creation of a synchronized grant directly from `guarded` remains restricted to the validated `acquire` protocol through C22.2.\n458\t2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n459\t3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n460\t4. **Controller-access and state-integrity protocol (WC5 D-C6) [WC5.2]:** For every live synchronization entry, the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when the acting frame holds an applicable `Observe` right under C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3`, out-of-protocol `writeShared`, or any other operation outside the validated controller protocol whose actual access or effect footprint includes the controller leaf.\n461\t\n462\t   This restriction covers physical reads and writes of the controller, changes to its initialization state, and changes that make the controller leaf itself the holder or owner of an authority or allocated resource.\n463\t\n464\t   In particular, while the entry remains live, the restriction excludes:\n465\t   - `store` targeting the controller leaf;\n466\t   - `issue` using the controller leaf as its destination;\n467\t   - `alloc` using the controller leaf itself as the owner of a newly allocated root;\n468\t   - `alloc` whose provider-metadata accesses may write the controller leaf;\n469\t   - any other inherited or compound operation producing an equivalent access or effect on that leaf.\n470\t\n471\t   The restriction is evaluated against the leaves actually affected by an operation, including relevant physical metadata accesses. Merely naming a structural place containing the controller does not establish that the controller leaf itself is accessed or modified.\n472\t\n473\t   In particular, `alloc(owner = M)` is not excluded solely because `ControlOf(entry)` is a structural leaf of `M`. Ownership of storage by the mutex value remains eligible under C13.5. Such an allocation must nevertheless satisfy every applicable inherited precondition and must not independently access or alter the controller leaf through its provider, destination, or other effects.\n474\t\n475\t   A change solely to a dependency relation, without a physical controller access or another prohibited controller-state effect, is not excluded by this item merely because it concerns the controller. All applicable inherited dependency and lifetime requirements remain in force.\n476\t\n477\t   An authority whose footprint includes the controller does not, by that fact alone, constitute an access or effect on the controller.\n478\t\n479\t   Valid internal controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized `resetLock` remain within their respective validated protocols. Controller initialization through `newMutex` remains governed by C13.\n480\t\n481\t   A legal transfer of the whole mutex value `M`, including by-value `spawn`, or of a structurally enclosing value remains eligible under C22.3 and the unchanged O4 preconditions. Operations that make the entry dead remain eligible under C12, C22.5, and the applicable inherited lifetime rules. These exceptions do not waive the preservation of `SyncInv` or the applicable realization obligations.\n482\t\n483\t   This requirement restricts the safe abstraction, not the inherited kernel transitions or their outcomes. A kernel operation may independently return `ok` without being admitted by the safe mutex interface.\n484\t\n485\t   Enforcement of the safe interface belongs to Specs 4 and 10. Complete physical-access and effect-footprint coverage, controller correspondence, and preservation remain obligations under C23, C25, C29, `OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, and `OB-C28`.\n486\t5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n487\t\n488\t6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n489\t\n490\t   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n491\t\n492\t   **Verification boundary [WC5.3 — exploratory evidence]:** The earlier `ChainExcl`/`PlaceExcl` (`Encap5R`) fragment has been extended by `Prem` and `PremG`, covering the rights read in the 28 modeled step kinds, including compound phases and the C13.11/C18.4 additions. `EncapFull = Encap7 ∧ PremG`, with `RunFull` restricted to actual kernel-`ok` steps. The reported `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, and `runFull_runAmF` theorems establish the relevant inclusion and step-premise correspondence, under their declared model scope; the syntactic inventory's exhaustiveness still partly relies on definition inspection. W-GI (`issue` destination) and W-RX A (nested `resetLock`) are kernel-`ok` and accepted by the older amended model but excluded by `RunFull`; their admitted twins remain. `resetLock_rights` proves the narrowly scoped recovery exception under the reachable-`RunFull` hypotheses. The `RunFull` results extend the model's holder-chain coverage, **not** its unmodeled physical byte-event or target safe-interface coverage. `OB-C28` remains open for actual enforcement in Specs 4/10, the §78.1 `reset`-premise model gap, parts of premise-inventory exhaustiveness, and the dependent physical/event obligations. A kernel-`ok` step that violates this requirement is excluded by the safe abstraction without changing its kernel outcome.\n493\t\n494\tRequirements 1–6 all constrain the allowed traces of the safe abstraction **[WC5.1]**. In particular, requirement 4 excludes ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` from `EncapsulatedTrace` as required by C51, while preserving O9 as a kernel operation. Requirement 6 also excludes accesses and other rights justified through a live `guarded` without the relevant grant. Their enforcement must follow from encapsulation and the public interface, rather than hidden runtime constraints or undisclosed hypotheses on the kernel. Preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under these explicit restrictions is the proof obligation stated in C23.\n495\t\n496\tRequirement 4 is **also** a physical realization/interface obligation; its enforcement is not falsely claimed to follow merely from the authority-only predicate `GrantDiscipline` or the abstract machine `S_C`.\n497\t\n498\t**[WC5.3 — complete-encapsulation exploratory evidence]** Under `DDSound`, `StaticsOK`, the model's initial state, and reachable `RunFull` traces, `capacity_run` establishes, for each modeled touched leaf, the capability that the step's clause requires; no capability is required of the acting context on a leaf that dies in the step (`endUse`, `free`, `exitFrame`, `terminate`), and that exception is bounded by the next two results. `spawnVal_cap_pre` extends the result to each source in the state before a composite by-value spawn. `protected_not_dying_run` proves that a leaf protected by an entry live both before and after a step does not die; `other_cap_dying_run` excludes capabilities in other contexts on a leaf that dies. `usable_run` proves for **every place** that a step other than `endUse`, `free`, `exitFrame`, or `terminate` cannot change it from usable to unusable. `closure_run` establishes no entry of a pre-existing authority into the active covering-descendant set of `guarded`; `empty_without_grant` and `last_acquire` establish its origin in the latest acquisition. `three_cases` and `protected_cap_run` characterize the provenance of capacities on a protected leaf when the entry remains live across the step, including abandoned grants with lawful surviving derivatives. These are abstract trace results, not universal claims over arbitrary initial states, actual physical accesses, or the `hb` order required by C46.\n499\t\n500\t**[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** The final controller-footprint restriction (`CtlRestrF`, `Encap7`/`Run7`) rejects kernel-`ok` out-of-protocol `store`, `issue`, and `alloc(owner = ctl)` while admitting their tested positive counterparts. It admits `depend(ctl, a)` where no physical controller access is represented and `alloc(owner = M)` when the controller itself is unaffected; an `alloc` whose provider includes `ctl` is excluded conservatively for potential metadata writes, which the current kernel-effect model does **not** exhibit. The 28 modeled step kinds have been classified without an admitted out-of-protocol controller-footprint counterexample; `completeness7_run` proves graph-footprint coverage for a live entry under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. This is a scoped formal abstraction result, not a proof of all target physical accesses (`OB-C20`).\n501\t\n502\t## C23. Preservation of Ordinary and Concurrent Operations\n503\t\n504\tFormalization must demonstrate preservation of `ConcurrentWF`, the complete `SyncInv` (including controller uniqueness, structural initialization, `GrantSubtree` and `RegBound`) and `GrantDiscipline` for every applicable inherited and new transition, including `transfer` and by-value `spawn`, under the explicit safe-abstraction restrictions C22.1–3 and C22.5. The reference abstract trace relation must include by-value `spawn` and must not silently omit any applicable kernel operation.\n505\t\n506\tFor inherited operations, the kernel transition remains governed by the unmodified Spec 1. An operation that is a valid kernel step but not exposed by the safe mutex abstraction may violate the **library's** trace discipline without constituting a memory-safety counterexample against the kernel.\n507\t\n508\tA dead entry or recorded inactive grant is permitted where its contract allows it. A live entry with an invalid positional controller, duplicate controller or unauthorized grant is not a valid safe-abstraction state.\n509\t"}]},"uuid":"9cae6250-2356-4117-8900-146cf9743cec","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:30.042Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","content":"## C13. Reference Construction — `newMutex`\n\n`newMutex` is a library operation establishing a synchronized authority-administration entry. Let `slot` be the place holding `guarded`, `M` its immediate structural parent and `ctl = ControlOf(M,slot)` its controller leaf.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `guarded` is active, of own mode `Modify`, and legally held by the usable `slot`.\n2. The constructing frame has ordinary exercisable `Modify` over `guarded` under Spec 1 §43.\n3. The declared structural positions identify the usable `slot` and a usable controller **leaf** `ctl` in the same `M`.\n4. `footprint(guarded) ∩ footprint(M) = ∅`. This is the reference mutex's structural disjointness requirement, allowing the control value `M` to be shared under compatible `Observe` authorities while `guarded` exclusively protects a distinct storage footprint.\n5. If `guarded` is stored by O8, the construction respects O8.3. In the reference arrangement, the protected root `T` is owned by `M`, not by `slot`; its leaves are not thereby included in `footprint(M)`.\n6. `K.SharedMutable(ctl)` holds for the controller leaf.\n7. The `guarded` identity is not registered as a live administrative identity by any other registered administration. This is an unconditional candidate unified consistency guard, following the non-duplication principle of Spec 1 §56.\n8. **Controller uniqueness:** no other live synchronization entry is associated with `ctl`. The association is injective among live entries.\n9. No active descendant of `guarded` covers a leaf of `footprint(guarded)`.\n10. **Initialization:** all structural leaves in `footprint(M)` are `Init` before construction. The operation does not implicitly initialize them. Storage merely owned by `M`, but structurally outside `footprint(M)`, is not included in this requirement solely by ownership.\n11. **Controller-initialization authority [WC5.2]:** The constructing frame has ordinary exercisable `Modify` authority with effective mode `Modify` over the controller leaf `ctl`, under Spec 1 §43 and the applicable ordinary-write requirements of O10.1 (§67.1). The ordinary-write exclusivity requirement of O10.2 (Spec 1 §67.2) also applies to this non-atomic write; its derivation from the reachable-state invariants is not separately established by the cited Lean checks. This authority is required to justify the non-atomic physical write establishing the controller's initial available state. A controller-access right sufficient only for an `Observe`-mode shared mutation under Spec 1 §68.2 does not satisfy this requirement.\n\n**Effect**\n\nThe library registers an initially available `SyncEntry { guarded, grant = none }`, without creating another access authority over the protected storage or initializing any leaf of `M`. Library administration state remains separate from `S_C`.\n\n**Controller-initialization clarification [WC5.2]:** This is an abstract registration effect: the operation changes no structural leaf's abstract initialization state. C13.10 already requires every structural leaf of `M`, including `ctl`, to be `Init` before construction; `newMutex` does not change an `Uninit` leaf to `Init`. Separately, the physical realization establishes the controller's initial available synchronization value by a **non-atomic write** to `ctl`, justified by C13.11 and subject to the applicable O10.1/O10.2 requirements. The term *controller initialization* in C31 and C48 denotes that physical synchronization-state write, not a transition of abstract structural initialization state. The non-atomic write is represented as an initial modification in `mo` under C31; it does not itself establish `sw`. Its concrete event, the ordering of this write with accesses in other execution contexts, and the correspondence with the initially available registry entry remain obligations under C27–C29, C42, C48 and `OB-C05`. The authority-only kernel state `S_C` does not establish the physical write or its ordering.\n\n**Preservation obligation:** The construction must establish the entire `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` suite for the new and previously live entries. This is not an additional resulting-state precondition.\n\n**Interface and realization obligations:** The safe public interface must prevent falsification of the positional controller association (C13.3); the controller synchronization protocol (C13.6) must be enforced; and the initial physical controller state must correspond to the newly available abstract registry entry. C13.11 supplies a required authority premise for the initial ordinary write, but does not by itself prove its ordering with other contexts or its target realization. These are not claimed to follow from the authority-only state `S_C`. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-NC demonstrates that C13.1–C13.10 alone admit construction without controller rights; the added C13.11 rejects it in the amended model, while all 16 previously recorded construction steps satisfy the new premise. The abstract `newMutex` effect is unchanged.\n\nA reference representation may place the protected value in a distinct logical owned-storage root. No separate physical allocation is implied.\n\n## C14. Concurrent External Contract — `SyncAuthorized`\n\n`K_C` extends the unchanged Spec 1 contract `K`. The proposed concurrent authorization predicate accepts the **current logical synchronization registry as an explicit external argument**:\n\n```\nK_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, requesting_frame)\n```\n\nThe registry is supplied by the library contract and is not part of the kernel state `S_C`. It must refer to the same logical registry state used for the candidate library transition.\n\nFor the reference mutex, the library's **authorization facts**, and only these facts, are:\n\n1. There exists a live registered entry with the requested `guarded` identity.\n2. That entry has `grant = none`.\n3. The requesting frame has the required effective and exercisable access to `ControlOf(entry)` under the controller-access obligation corresponding to Spec 1 §68.2.\n\nThe library must prove, under `ConcurrentWF`, the applicable Spec 1 invariants, `SyncInv`, `GrantDiscipline`, and the abstraction's encapsulation contract, that these facts imply the remaining authority prerequisites for `deriveSync`, including origin-chain validity, sufficient effective mode, absence of blocking descendants and I1 conservation.\n\nThe opaque authorization predicate must not be defined by simply conjoining the conclusions the library is required to prove. Its instantiation is state-dependent, and correctness must be reestablished for every enabled acquisition.\n\n`SyncAuthorized` does not create an authority, does not introduce ambient context rights, and cannot be used outside the corresponding valid synchronization contract. The precise synchronized physical realization remains deferred under `OB-C05`.\n\n## C15. New Concurrent Transition — `deriveSync`\n\n`deriveSync` is a new candidate concurrent transition creating an ordinary derived `Modify` authority from `guarded` over `footprint(guarded)`.\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. **Own-mode bound, retained from Spec 1 §43 and O1.1:** `guarded` is active and its **own mode permits `Modify`**. In the `Observe | Modify` mode order this requires own mode `Modify`, **even if `footprint(guarded)` is empty**.\n2. The complete applicable origin-chain and effective-anchor validity requirements of O1.1 hold.\n3. **Exclusive authorization:** `K_C.SyncAuthorized(state, sync_registry, guarded, current_frame)` holds. This predicate is the **only** authorization for `deriveSync`. Neither the current-frame-holder nor the place-holder alternative of ordinary `ExercisableAt` independently enables this new transition. Ordinary O1 retains both alternatives unchanged.\n4. O1.2's leaf, subfootprint and effective-mode conditions hold for the full protected footprint and requested mode `Modify`.\n5. O1.3's structural exclusivity and I1 preservation conditions hold.\n6. O1.4's freshness, legal current-frame holder and resulting-state validity conditions hold.\n\n**Effect**\n\nA fresh, active authority is created with own mode `Modify`, origin `guarded`, full footprint `footprint(guarded)`, and holder equal to the executing context's current frame. No other access right is created.\n\nThis grant is ordinary after creation: `ExercisableAt` remains exactly as in Spec 1 §43 for its exercise, reborrowing, storage and legal release. The synchronized exception concerns **only the authorization for creating this derivative**; it does not weaken the source activity, own-mode bound, anchor, effective-mode or structural exclusivity requirements of O1.\n\n**Outcome:** `ok` or `reject` for the abstract safety transition; a busy controller may make the enclosing library acquisition temporarily non-enabled, as specified in C16.\n\n## C16. Reference Acquisition — `acquire` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nThe library operation `acquire` combines synchronization-controller acquisition and `deriveSync` into one coherent abstract operation.\n\n**Outcome classification (proposed D-C1)**\n\n1. **`reject` — invalid request:** a failure of entry existence/liveness (C14.1), controller-access authority (C14.3, including the applicable Spec 1 §68.2 contract), or any other applicable authority, register-validity, origin, footprint, invariant, or Spec 1 safety precondition yields `reject`. These failures remain `reject` even when the controller is occupied or `grant ≠ none` in the same state.\n2. **Temporarily non-enabled — contention:** only after all non-contention validity and authority conditions hold, the entry's `grant ≠ none` (C14.2), or occupation of the controller by another acquisition under its valid protocol, makes `acquire` temporarily non-enabled. This is **not** `reject`, `fail`, or an additional `Outcome3` value; no successful transition is produced. Non-enablement may persist indefinitely.\n3. **`ok` — successful acquisition:** all validity and authority obligations hold, the grant is available and the controller is successfully acquired under its protocol, and the successful effect below is established.\n\n`reject` takes precedence over temporary non-enablement **for the same abstract state**; this is a classification of causes and does not assert that a concurrent call has an outcome independent of changing state. Resource `fail` outcomes of `alloc`/`issue` are outside this criterion.\n\nFor `acquire`, the authorization facts C14.1 and C14.3 are safety conditions; C14.2 is a contention condition. The combined `SyncAuthorized` predicate of C14 is therefore insufficient by itself to distinguish `reject` from temporary non-enablement. This does not alter the independent transition `deriveSync` or its C15 conditions.\n\nThe library must establish all non-dynamic safety preconditions before committing a successful acquisition.\n\nIts success:\n\n1. acquires the controller under the applicable synchronization protocol;\n2. creates the valid grant through `deriveSync`;\n3. records the resulting grant identity in `SyncEntry.grant`.\n\nNo partially successful abstract operation may expose an invalid ordinary state.\n\nThe controller's physical linearization, atomic ordering, and correspondence obligations are specified as proposals in C41, C48 and C49 and remain subject to `OB-C05`.\n\n## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\n`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n\nThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available as one coherent library transition.\n\n**Realization obligation, not an assumed abstract precondition:** The controller update must satisfy `K.SharedMutable` and the required event, atomicity and ordering discipline. The physical protocol and its correspondence are specified provisionally in C41, C48 and C49 and remain under `OB-C05`, rather than being treated as proven by the abstract transition.\n\n## C18. Abandoned Grants and Recovery **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\nA grant may become inactive through an inherited legal release or frame exit without a corresponding `unlock`; the live registry entry may still record `grant = some g`. This can leave the mutex unavailable indefinitely without itself violating memory safety.\n\n`resetLock` is a candidate library recovery operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. Ordinary `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` holds under Spec 1 §43, requiring the appropriate exclusive exercise through the mutex's holder.\n2. No active descendant of `guarded` covers any protected leaf, as in the relevant no-descendant condition of Spec 1 §78.2.\n3. `ControlOf(entry)` resolves to an existing, usable controller **leaf**, as required by C21.3, and recovery does not invalidate surviving dependent authorities. The controller-initialization invariant is established separately for every live entry by C21.7.\n4. **Controller-access authority [WC5.2]:** The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)`, under the corresponding requirement of Spec 1 §68.2. This condition is independent of, and additional to, `ExercisableAt(guarded, Modify)` in item 1. It authorizes the controller access required by the recovery protocol without granting any new authority.\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** when the applicable recovery and authority conditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** when any applicable authority, registry, dependent-validity, controller-validity, or Spec 1 precondition fails. Controller occupation does not mask such a failure as temporary non-enablement.\n\nThere is no newly introduced `resetLock` waiting outcome and no new prerequisite that the recorded grant first be `none`. This transition is the exceptional recovery protocol described here, not ordinary `unlock`.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe registered grant becomes `none` and the controller's logical state becomes available. Recovery does not revive an inactive grant and does not fabricate authority.\n\n**Realization obligation:** The exclusive recovery discipline and its physical controller update must meet `OB-C05` and the event constraints proposed in C41, C48 and C49; this is not presumed proved by the abstract state transition. **[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** W-RC establishes that the earlier C18.1–C18.3 allowed recovery with no controller right; C18.4 excludes that step in the amended model, while W-RL, W-RL-J, the S11 positive recovery and the W-RC positive counterpart remain admissible. The recorded grant may still be `none`.\n\n## C19. Synchronized Administration\n\nA grant protecting an administrative value may permit `MetaRight` only when it provides exercisable `Modify` over every required leaf of that administrative value.\n\nThe grant must therefore cover the entire required administrative footprint.\n\nThis does not bypass Spec 1 §36.\n\nIssuance, recycling, and reset operations retain their existing authority and dependency restrictions.\n\nThe lifetime of a block issued during synchronized administration is determined by its actual origin chain, holders, anchors, and ownership relations, not merely by the lifetime of the synchronization grant.\n\n---\n\n# III. Invariants and Correspondence\n\n## C20. Concurrent Frame and Authority Well-Formedness\n\n`ConcurrentWF` extends frame well-formedness to multiple local stacks and one global parent relationship.\n\nIt must preserve the applicable structural, ownership, holder, and authority conditions of Spec 1.\n\nEvery inherited transition and every new concurrent transition must preserve the applicable global invariants.\n\nReparenting must preserve extended ancestry and validity properties.\n\n## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n\nFor the reference mutex abstraction, the following state properties must be preserved over all **live** entries, except the bound-identity property in item 6, which applies to every entry:\n\n1. **Guarded authority:** `guarded` is active, has own mode `Modify`, and is held by a usable place.\n2. **Grant record:** If `grant = some g`, then the recorded authority exists and `origin(g) = guarded` and `footprint(g) = footprint(guarded)`. `g` need not be active; the invariant must handle legal release and reanchoring without silently weakening these equalities.\n3. **Controller validity:** `ControlOf(entry)` identifies an existing usable **leaf** associated with the holder `slot` and its structural mutex parent `M`.\n4. **Controller uniqueness:** Distinct live entries have distinct `ControlOf` leaves; the guarded-identity uniqueness guard is also respected. The same controller cannot be registered twice under different guarded identities.\n5. **`GrantSubtree`:** If `grant = some g` and `g` is active, every active descendant of `guarded` covering a protected leaf is either `g` itself or a descendant of `g`. This is a distinct invariant, necessary to restore grant availability after legal `unlock`.\n\n   **Clarification — inactive recorded grants and surviving derivatives [WC5.3]:** The condition above applies when the recorded grant is active. It does not require a recorded inactive grant to remain an ancestor, under the current `origin` relation, of every legally surviving derivative.\n\n   **[Exploratory evidence — Milestone 4, tranche 3]** In the exploratory model, for every execution admitted under the complete encapsulation contract of C22 (`RunFull`) from the initial state, the active strict transitive descendants of `guarded` that cover a protected leaf all stem from the most recent successful `acquire` of that entry (`last_acquire`); when `grant = none` there is none (`empty_without_grant`). An authority that enters this set at a step is either the grant recorded by that step's `acquire`, or is created by that step with, as its origin, an authority already in the set. No existing authority enters the set by any admitted step, legal reanchoring included (`closure_run`). A reference proof remains an obligation under C23 and `OB-C28`.\n\n   Legal reanchoring under Spec 1 §73.4 may change the direct `origin` of an existing surviving authority, including making it a direct descendant of `guarded`, without creating an authority or introducing an existing authority into the transitive descendant set. Consequently, when the recorded grant is inactive, a covering derivative may remain active under `guarded` even though its current origin chain no longer contains the recorded grant.\n\n   While any such surviving derivative remains active and covers a protected leaf, `resetLock` cannot succeed (C18.2).\n\n   When `grant = none`, `GrantDiscipline` in C22 requires that no active descendant of `guarded` cover a protected leaf.\n\n   These statements clarify consequences for reachable admitted executions. They add no independent state conjunct to `SyncInv`, require no historical-origin field, and do not prohibit otherwise legal surviving derivatives. They establish authority provenance, not the `happens-before` ordering required by C46.\n6. **`RegBound`:** Every registered `guarded` identity denotes an existing authority identity, even when its entry is no longer live. This prevents a dormant entry from becoming live merely when a fresh authority is created with its identity.\n7. **Structural initialization:** For each live entry, every structural leaf of the place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n\n`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n\nThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n\n## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6); WC5.3 (item 1 clarification)]**\n\nThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n\n```\nlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n```\n\nIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n\nFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n\n1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded` while the entry is live.\n\n   No operation exposed by the safe abstraction, including a compound operation that performs an internal `derive` or `split`, creates an ordinary authority whose origin at creation is `guarded` while the entry is live.\n\n   **Clarification [WC5.3]:** This restriction concerns the creation of authorities by operations exposed through the safe abstraction. It is not a state invariant forbidding an existing authority from having `origin = guarded` after a legal reanchoring.\n\n   In particular, reanchoring performed under Spec 1 §73.4 during a legal `exitFrame` or `terminate` may change the direct `origin` of an already existing surviving derivative to `guarded`. Such reanchoring does not issue a new derivative, does not constitute an independent `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`, and does not introduce an existing authority from outside the transitive descendant set of `guarded` into that set while the entry remains live.\n\n   Ordinary derivation from an active acquired grant, from its descendants, or from a legally surviving descendant remains eligible under the applicable inherited preconditions and the complete holder-chain restrictions of C22.6. Such derivation is not directly from `guarded` merely because its source is a descendant of `guarded`.\n\n   The creation of a synchronized grant directly from `guarded` remains restricted to the validated `acquire` protocol through C22.2.\n2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n4. **Controller-access and state-integrity protocol (WC5 D-C6) [WC5.2]:** For every live synchronization entry, the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when the acting frame holds an applicable `Observe` right under C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3`, out-of-protocol `writeShared`, or any other operation outside the validated controller protocol whose actual access or effect footprint includes the controller leaf.\n\n   This restriction covers physical reads and writes of the controller, changes to its initialization state, and changes that make the controller leaf itself the holder or owner of an authority or allocated resource.\n\n   In particular, while the entry remains live, the restriction excludes:\n   - `store` targeting the controller leaf;\n   - `issue` using the controller leaf as its destination;\n   - `alloc` using the controller leaf itself as the owner of a newly allocated root;\n   - `alloc` whose provider-metadata accesses may write the controller leaf;\n   - any other inherited or compound operation producing an equivalent access or effect on that leaf.\n\n   The restriction is evaluated against the leaves actually affected by an operation, including relevant physical metadata accesses. Merely naming a structural place containing the controller does not establish that the controller leaf itself is accessed or modified.\n\n   In particular, `alloc(owner = M)` is not excluded solely because `ControlOf(entry)` is a structural leaf of `M`. Ownership of storage by the mutex value remains eligible under C13.5. Such an allocation must nevertheless satisfy every applicable inherited precondition and must not independently access or alter the controller leaf through its provider, destination, or other effects.\n\n   A change solely to a dependency relation, without a physical controller access or another prohibited controller-state effect, is not excluded by this item merely because it concerns the controller. All applicable inherited dependency and lifetime requirements remain in force.\n\n   An authority whose footprint includes the controller does not, by that fact alone, constitute an access or effect on the controller.\n\n   Valid internal controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized `resetLock` remain within their respective validated protocols. Controller initialization through `newMutex` remains governed by C13.\n\n   A legal transfer of the whole mutex value `M`, including by-value `spawn`, or of a structurally enclosing value remains eligible under C22.3 and the unchanged O4 preconditions. Operations that make the entry dead remain eligible under C12, C22.5, and the applicable inherited lifetime rules. These exceptions do not waive the preservation of `SyncInv` or the applicable realization obligations.\n\n   This requirement restricts the safe abstraction, not the inherited kernel transitions or their outcomes. A kernel operation may independently return `ok` without being admitted by the safe mutex interface.\n\n   Enforcement of the safe interface belongs to Specs 4 and 10. Complete physical-access and effect-footprint coverage, controller correspondence, and preservation remain obligations under C23, C25, C29, `OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, and `OB-C28`.\n5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n\n6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n\n   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n\n   **Verification boundary [WC5.3 — exploratory evidence]:** The earlier `ChainExcl`/`PlaceExcl` (`Encap5R`) fragment has been extended by `Prem` and `PremG`, covering the rights read in the 28 modeled step kinds, including compound phases and the C13.11/C18.4 additions. `EncapFull = Encap7 ∧ PremG`, with `RunFull` restricted to actual kernel-`ok` steps. The reported `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, and `runFull_runAmF` theorems establish the relevant inclusion and step-premise correspondence, under their declared model scope; the syntactic inventory's exhaustiveness still partly relies on definition inspection. W-GI (`issue` destination) and W-RX A (nested `resetLock`) are kernel-`ok` and accepted by the older amended model but excluded by `RunFull`; their admitted twins remain. `resetLock_rights` proves the narrowly scoped recovery exception under the reachable-`RunFull` hypotheses. The `RunFull` results extend the model's holder-chain coverage, **not** its unmodeled physical byte-event or target safe-interface coverage. `OB-C28` remains open for actual enforcement in Specs 4/10, the §78.1 `reset`-premise model gap, parts of premise-inventory exhaustiveness, and the dependent physical/event obligations. A kernel-`ok` step that violates this requirement is excluded by the safe abstraction without changing its kernel outcome.\n\nRequirements 1–6 all constrain the allowed traces of the safe abstraction **[WC5.1]**. In particular, requirement 4 excludes ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` from `EncapsulatedTrace` as required by C51, while preserving O9 as a kernel operation. Requirement 6 also excludes accesses and other rights justified through a live `guarded` without the relevant grant. Their enforcement must follow from encapsulation and the public interface, rather than hidden runtime constraints or undisclosed hypotheses on the kernel. Preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under these explicit restrictions is the proof obligation stated in C23.\n\nRequirement 4 is **also** a physical realization/interface obligation; its enforcement is not falsely claimed to follow merely from the authority-only predicate `GrantDiscipline` or the abstract machine `S_C`.\n\n**[WC5.3 — complete-encapsulation exploratory evidence]** Under `DDSound`, `StaticsOK`, the model's initial state, and reachable `RunFull` traces, `capacity_run` establishes, for each modeled touched leaf, the capability that the step's clause requires; no capability is required of the acting context on a leaf that dies in the step (`endUse`, `free`, `exitFrame`, `terminate`), and that exception is bounded by the next two results. `spawnVal_cap_pre` extends the result to each source in the state before a composite by-value spawn. `protected_not_dying_run` proves that a leaf protected by an entry live both before and after a step does not die; `other_cap_dying_run` excludes capabilities in other contexts on a leaf that dies. `usable_run` proves for **every place** that a step other than `endUse`, `free`, `exitFrame`, or `terminate` cannot change it from usable to unusable. `closure_run` establishes no entry of a pre-existing authority into the active covering-descendant set of `guarded`; `empty_without_grant` and `last_acquire` establish its origin in the latest acquisition. `three_cases` and `protected_cap_run` characterize the provenance of capacities on a protected leaf when the entry remains live across the step, including abandoned grants with lawful surviving derivatives. These are abstract trace results, not universal claims over arbitrary initial states, actual physical accesses, or the `hb` order required by C46.\n\n**[WC5.2 — exploratory evidence]** The final controller-footprint restriction (`CtlRestrF`, `Encap7`/`Run7`) rejects kernel-`ok` out-of-protocol `store`, `issue`, and `alloc(owner = ctl)` while admitting their tested positive counterparts. It admits `depend(ctl, a)` where no physical controller access is represented and `alloc(owner = M)` when the controller itself is unaffected; an `alloc` whose provider includes `ctl` is excluded conservatively for potential metadata writes, which the current kernel-effect model does **not** exhibit. The 28 modeled step kinds have been classified without an admitted out-of-protocol controller-footprint counterexample; `completeness7_run` proves graph-footprint coverage for a live entry under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. This is a scoped formal abstraction result, not a proof of all target physical accesses (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C23. Preservation of Ordinary and Concurrent Operations\n\nFormalization must demonstrate preservation of `ConcurrentWF`, the complete `SyncInv` (including controller uniqueness, structural initialization, `GrantSubtree` and `RegBound`) and `GrantDiscipline` for every applicable inherited and new transition, including `transfer` and by-value `spawn`, under the explicit safe-abstraction restrictions C22.1–3 and C22.5. The reference abstract trace relation must include by-value `spawn` and must not silently omit any applicable kernel operation.\n\nFor inherited operations, the kernel transition remains governed by the unmodified Spec 1. An operation that is a valid kernel step but not exposed by the safe mutex abstraction may violate the **library's** trace discipline without constituting a memory-safety counterexample against the kernel.\n\nA dead entry or recorded inactive grant is permitted where its contract allows it. A live entry with an invalid positional controller, duplicate controller or unauthorized grant is not a valid safe-abstraction state.\n","numLines":280,"startLine":230,"totalLines":1202}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"df806f57-4b7c-46c6-a191-adaf63cab0d9","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
