{"parentUuid":"9cae6250-2356-4117-8900-146cf9743cec","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch2TWEW1tBBMPK24K7iLR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017cibo5qfgCGC26yfWx53fU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":538,"limit":240},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13103,"cache_read_input_tokens":152847,"output_tokens":121,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13103,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":121,"cache_read_input_tokens":152847,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13103},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":646,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017cibo5qfgCGC26yfWx53fU":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":538,"limit":240}},"serverClassifierRequest":"933c4688-2092-4bc4-8cf4-32609aa89f07","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch2TWEFczM1NeukXBci1g","requestRef":"e60e12eb-dbd4-46c8-9e25-c251b0d0462c","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"85321898-696a-499c-a49f-da57a466b0b3","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:32.219Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"85321898-696a-499c-a49f-da57a466b0b3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017cibo5qfgCGC26yfWx53fU","type":"tool_result","content":"538\t# IV. Open Obligations\n539\t\n540\t| ID | Obligation |\n541\t|---|---|\n542\t| `OB-C01` | Concurrent event model and definition of permitted executions |\n543\t| `OB-C02` | Conflict relations, synchronizes-with relations, and `happens-before` |\n544\t| `OB-C03` | Atomic operations, memory orders, and permitted observations |\n545\t| `OB-C04` | **[WC5.1 — M4]** `LockOrder` for successive protected critical sections: `lock_ordersS` and its finer `lock_ordersS_gaps` variant are proved in the abstract strict event graph **only when** (i) intervening successful acquisitions are each followed by an `unlock` ordered after that acquisition and before the final acquisition, (ii) no disqualifying `resetLock` intervenes across the required unlock–acquire gaps, and (iii) the controller remains defined and stable across those gaps. The finer `lock_ordersS_gaps` theorem requires the two gap conditions only for the relevant successive unlock–acquire pairs. **[WC5.2 — M4]** The reference `bridge` and `bridge_sw_ref` establish the direct-`rf`/`sw` counterpart of a strict unlock–acquire edge in the constructed controller graph under the declared realization, but do not establish target physical refinement or universal `LockOrder`. The general obligation remains open. |\n546\t| `OB-C05` | **[WC5.2 — M4]** A conditional correspondence between the reference mutex authority trace and its **constructed** atomic event graph is proved (`ctl_invariant`, `ctlGraph_coherent`, `bridge`, `bridge_sw_ref`, with `bridge_converse` under `CtlFixed`). The required physical atomic realization, correctness of `mo`/`rf` construction for target executions, initial-write ordering across contexts, failed CAS events, recovery ordering and complete `LockOrder` remain open. |\n547\t| `OB-C06` | Data-race freedom for all permitted safe executions |\n548\t| `OB-C07` | Type-system rules for cross-context transferability and shareability — Spec 4 |\n549\t| `OB-C08` | Encapsulation and library contract enforcement — Specs 4 and 10 |\n550\t| `OB-C09` | Final by-value passing conventions, cancellation, abnormal termination, and cleanup — Spec 3 |\n551\t| `OB-C10` | External execution contexts and interoperability — Spec 11 |\n552\t| `OB-C11` | **[WC5.1 — M4]** Event-order consequences of `join`, completion, and frame-exit protocols: `spawn_ordersS` and `join_ordersS` are proved for the scoped abstract step graph (with stated lifecycle hypotheses). Placement of a distinct within-step `complete(c)` marker and physical event correspondence remain open. |\n553\t| `OB-C12` | Concrete correspondence and refinement obligations of Spec 1 §82 |\n554\t| `OB-C13` | Preservation of H1 ancestry and validity under context reparenting |\n555\t| `OB-C14` | Correctness of the provisional composite by-value `spawn` |\n556\t| `OB-C15` | Correctness of the external `SyncAuthorized` contract and its relationship to library state |\n557\t| `OB-C16` | Unified live administrative identity guard across arenas and mutexes, consistent with Spec 1 §56; integration and verification deferred to later milestones |\n558\t| `OB-C17` | **[WC5.1 — M4] L1, scoped exploratory proof:** `cap5_drop_own` covers all 28 modeled step kinds under `DDSound` and reachable encapsulated authority traces. `lossStep_own` further establishes for eligible direct-access conflicting pairs a first own-context loss step `r` strictly between the two accesses; with one event per abstract step, `po(e1,r)` follows. Internal composite-step ordering and physical access coverage remain open (`OB-C19`, `OB-C20`). Raw `cap` still fails L1 (W-GR). |\n559\t| `OB-C18` | **[WC5.1 — M4] L2 and scoped composition proved exploratorily:** `cap5_compat` gives state incompatibility; `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` give the trace bridge under `Encap5C` (inherited by `Encap5R`); `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` proves `HandoffOrderedS → ¬ RaceS` for the abstract exclusive access-pair model under its explicit run hypotheses. `spawn_ordersS`, `join_ordersS`, and conditional `lock_ordersS` establish reference edges only in that abstract graph. The weaker `Encap5` trace claim is refuted (W-GC). Direct physical `rf`, all generated accesses, and safe-program enforcement remain open (`OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, `OB-C24`). |\n560\t| `OB-C19` | **Erasure:** prove removal of pure authority markers preserves `hb` between surviving physical/synchronization events under `po` projection (C29) |\n561\t| `OB-C20` | **Coverage:** account for each physical byte access of every realization and justify its authority, byte range, atomicity, and validity, including compound steps (C27, C29, C50) |\n562\t| `OB-C21` | **Restated T1:** prove the appropriate directional projection and conditional equality for inherited `writeShared` under `K_C.SharedDiscipline`; report the missing Spec 1 Lean representation of O11.3 |\n563\t| `OB-C22` | **[WC5.1 — M4] No-thin-air:** herdtools7 7.58 A4 observes the load-buffering outcome with `no-thin-air` skipped and excludes it under reference `rc11.cat`. Whether ERN adopts `acyclic(po ∪ rf)` remains open; target compilation costs and a justified compilation scheme have not been established. `NoThinAir_RC11` remains an optional named hypothesis. |\n564\t| `OB-C23` | **[WC5.1 — M4] Complete atomic model:** release sequences, full global `seq_cst` constraints, coherent `rf`/`mo`/RMW admissibility, and a proved comparison to RC11 remain open. Bounded reference tests A1–E1 were executed; E1 exhibits a same-thread atomic non-RMW store extending the release sequence in `rc11.cat`, unlike the provisional C++20-form extension proposed in C48 (not executed as an ERN model). **[WC5.2 — M4]** A finite `EventConsistent` fragment for one atomic unit now formalizes five named conjuncts, and G18 exhibits a race under direct-`rf` `sw` only; neither full release-sequence alternative nor global SC nor RC11 equivalence has been proved. No inclusion/equivalence is claimed. |\n565\t| `OB-C24` | **Safe-program soundness:** prove in Specs 3/4 and safe libraries that well-typed safe executions meet `SafeConcurrentTrace`, including C22/C42/C46 and `Confined`/`Synchronized` contracts |\n566\t| `OB-C25` | **[WC5.1 — M4] Handoff witnesses, abstract graph results established:** the WC4-derived authority verdicts of W-HO, W-HO′, W-HO″ are proved; in the abstract step-event model each has `Race`/`RaceS`, lacks the relevant `hb`/`HBS`, and fails `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`. W-HO-J, W-HO″-J, and `lc_trace` provide positive ordered and non-racing counterparts. Physical event-mapping and general safe-program exclusions remain open. |\n567\t| `OB-C26` | **[WC5.2 — M4] Event consistency (partially discharged):** a finite, nonvacuous, single-atomic-unit `EventConsistent` model with `RfWF`, `MoTotal`, `RmwAtomic`, `Coherent` and `HbIrrefl` is implemented with initial values, valid reads, successful RMWs, and a failed-CAS **read-only event** (G13); 19 witness graphs pass the stated verdicts. General byte-event mapping, multiple locations, all failed-CAS paths, global SC, release sequences and full permitted-execution semantics remain open. |\n568\t| `OB-C27` | **Target realization:** show required atomic sizes/alignments, controller linearization, `LockOrder`, handoff synchronization and physical access-after-invalidation safety on each supported target |\n569\t| `OB-C28` | **[WC5.3 — model coverage established; realization open]** The complete C22.6 authority-premise restriction is represented exploratorily by `PremG`/`EncapFull`/`RunFull`; `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, `runFull_runAmF`, and `resetLock_rights` establish the reported model-scope checks. W-GI/W-RX counterexamples are excluded, the 28 modeled step kinds (including compound phases) are classified, the legitimate reference traces and 46 reported `RunFull` witness traces are checked. `capacity_run`, `protected_cap_run`, `three_cases`, `closure_run`, `last_acquire`, `spawnVal_cap_pre`, `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, and `usable_run` establish their named properties from the **model initial state** under `DDSound`, `StaticsOK`, and `RunFull`, not from arbitrary valid states. **Still open:** enforcing the restriction in the actual safe interface (Specs 4/10); the missing §78.1 `MetaRight` read by the `reset` model step; portions of exhaustive premise coverage supported by manual definition inspection rather than a general theorem; C46 ordering for recovery after an abandoned grant; and physical-access/effect coverage, including `alloc` provider metadata (`OB-C20`, `OB-C05`). The abstract result neither supplies universal `hb` ordering nor proves DRF. |\n570\t\n571\tNo complete concurrency safety claim follows from this partial candidate alone.\n572\t\n573\t---\n574\t\n575\t# V. Adversarial Corpus — Corrected Seed Cases\n576\t\n577\tThe following cases are seeds for the established adversarial validation process. Each executable negative case requires a positive counterpart.\n578\t\n579\tExisting Spec 1 verdicts and candidate-specific verdicts remain distinguishable.\n580\t\n581\t| Case | Scenario | Expected verdict and basis |\n582\t|---|---|---|\n583\t| S1a | A parent exits while its own local storage is still required by an active borrowed authority in a child | `reject`, under O7 and `NoDangling`; positive: end the dependent use before exit |\n584\t| S1b | A creating frame exits while the child's authority is anchored in storage belonging to a surviving ancestor, with legal reanchoring and reparenting | Candidate `ok`, subject to O7.4–O7.5 and extended ancestry; negative: invalid surviving anchor |\n585\t| S2 | Valid synchronized grant is acquired for ordinary protected `T` | Candidate `ok`, under `deriveSync` and its contract; negative: competing full-footprint grant |\n586\t| S3 | A second context directly attempts `issue` on a shared arena without an exercisable administrative `Modify` grant | `reject` under O18 (§77.1), because `MetaRight` is not established; positive: synchronized acquisition of the required grant followed by legal `issue` |\n587\t| S4 | A detached child receives an independently owned value through the provisional `spawn` composite and survives its creator | Candidate `ok` if all transfer and frame invariants hold; negative: unresolved local-anchor dependency |\n588\t| S5a | `writeShared(T)` where protected `T` is ordinary, not `SharedMutable` | `reject`, O11.1; positive: authorized ordinary write under a grant |\n589\t| S5b | Shared mutation of a controller outside its declared synchronization discipline | O11.3 unsatisfied; classification depends on the safe/unsafe interface; positive: authorized controller update |\n590\t| S6 | Two conflicting protected ordinary accesses have nonoverlapping grants but insufficient required memory ordering | Must not be permitted as a safe data-racing execution; deferred to event semantics; positive: `LockOrder`-compliant execution |\n591\t| S7 | Legal mutex transfer preserves positional controller association | Candidate `ok`; negative: an attempted transfer violating an applicable O4 precondition |\n592\t| S8 | A callee exercises a grant it has not received | `reject` under §43; positive: legal parameter transmission |\n593\t| S9 | Acquiring mutex `M1` must not grant access to `M2` of the same type | Instance-isolation property; positive: independent valid acquisitions of both mutexes |\n594\t| S10 | From `MX₃`, `f0` holds grant `g` while `f1` receives `m := derive(root_M, Modify)` and has an uninitialized local `M′`; `f1` transfers `M → M′`. | **Expected `ok`** under §63.7: `g` is frame-held in `f0`, not a moved place-held authority subject to `AnchorOrderHeld` (§44). In the resulting state `exitFrameC(f1)` → expected `reject` (§73.4); `transfer(M′, M)` → expected `ok` (§63.3, source leaves now `Moved`); subsequent `exitFrameC(f1)` → expected `ok`. All are corpus-v3 expectations, pending a sequential Lean witness; no frozen rule is changed. |\n595\t| S11 | In `MX₄`, the child enters frame `f1′` **yielding `o1′ := derive(o1, Observe)`** so that C14.3 holds; it then acquires `g`, exits `f1′` without `unlock`, and the parent attempts acquisition and recovery. | Expected: `exitFrameC(f1′)` → `ok` (grant inactive but registry records it); parent's `acquire(e)` → **temporarily non-enabled** (C14.2); parent's `resetLock(e)` → `reject` (C18.1: `Modify` not exercisable while `o1` remains active). Positive: child `terminate`, parent `join(c1)`, `resetLock(e)` → `ok`, then `acquire(e)` → `ok`. The setup requires yielding `o1′`; the verdicts are corpus-v3 expectations subject to D-C1's proposed outcome semantics. |\n596\t| S12 | An exclusively authorized ordinary `write3(lock)` bypasses the mutex controller protocol | Kernel O10 may return `ok`; **safe-abstraction encapsulation / controller protocol** is violated (not necessarily the authority-only `GrantDiscipline` predicate); positive: protocol-controlled update |\n597\t| S13 | Creator transmits a `Modify` derivative to a child and then attempts conflicting ordinary mutation through the origin | `reject` while the derivative blocks effective rights, §43/O10; positive: mutation after the derivative and all blocking descendants have legally ended and completion has been established, or simultaneous modification of genuinely disjoint leaves after a legal `split` |\n598\t| S14 | Two contexts perform initialized ordinary reads using compatible `Observe` authorities on the same resource | Authority-machine `ok` under O9 and I1, subject to all applicable read conditions; positive paired with a rejected incompatible-authority construction |\n599\t| S15 | An entire grant is yielded to a callee and released on frame exit without being returned | Grant no longer available for the caller's `unlock`; possible leaked lock; positive: pass a fresh derivative so that the caller retains its grant and can unlock after the derivative ends |\n600\t| S16 | `transfer(slot, Y)` extracts the place directly holding `guarded` from live `M`, leaving no positional controller | O4 kernel transition `ok` under the tested setup; excluded by C22 abstraction encapsulation; without that exclusion `SyncInv` controller validity fails. Positive: legal `transfer(M, M′)` preserves `ControlOf`, with or without an active grant |\n601\t| S17a | `endUse(ctl)` while a mutex entry is live and its `guarded` authority survives | O12 kernel transition may be `ok`; excluded by C22.5. Without the restriction `ctlInit` fails. Positive: after the mutex entry has ceased to be live, `endUse(ctl)` is permitted by the encapsulation contract when O12's own preconditions hold |\n602\t| S17b | `endUse(q)` on a strict structural subplace `q` of a composite `slot` while the entry is live | O12 kernel transition may be `ok`; excluded by C22.5. Without the restriction `slotInit` fails. Positive: legal termination of `slot` or enclosing `M` when all applicable O12 safety requirements hold |\n603\t| S18 | A whole-value transfer would merge the controller association of two live mutex entries | Candidate `reject` under O4 §63.3 when the destination live mutex has initialized structural leaves. Positive: legal `transfer(M, M′)` into a destination not holding a live mutex entry |\n604\t\n605\t### Guarded-Chain and Transfer Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 ter–2 sexies and S10b **[WC5.1]**\n606\t\n607\tThe following are **authority-machine and exploratory encapsulation** verdicts. None is by itself a concrete data-race or event-ordering proof. The current report confirms the guarded witnesses through the corpus version available to that report; any later corpus revisions must be reconciled without silently changing expected outcomes.\n608\t\n609\t| Witness | Authority-machine sequence and obtained result | Safe-abstraction verdict and outstanding boundary |\n610\t|---|---|---|\n611\t| **W-GR** — direct access through `guarded` | In shared mutex state `MX₄`, `c1 : read3(T)` using `guarded` is kernel-`ok`; `c0 : acquire(e)` and then `c0 : write3(T)` using grant `g` are each `ok`. `c1`'s read while `g` is active is kernel-`reject` (§43). | The initial unguarded read was admitted by old `EncapsulatedTrace`; it is excluded by `ChainExcl`/C22.6. The unrestricted-context `cap` loses `c1`'s rights at `c0`'s `acquire`, refuting raw L1. Physical event-order proof remains separate. |\n612\t| **W-GC** — indirect access through a holder chain | `z := derive(root_Y, Modify)` is stored in protected `T`, so `c1 : read3(Y)` using `z` is kernel-`ok` via the chain `z → T → guarded → slot → o1`, even without a grant; later `acquire` and protected access by `c0` are also kernel-`ok`. | `GuardedExcl` (testing only the argument) and `Encap5` admit the unguarded read; C22.6's whole-chain `ChainExcl` excludes it. `cap5_sound` and trace L2 are refuted under weak `Encap5`, but proved for the direct-access fragment under `Encap5C`. A grant-authorized access through `z` remains admitted. |\n613\t| **W-GS** — right on a place used by `store` | The mutex owner `c0` shares only the controller right with `c1`, then performs `store(z2,T)` **without** acquiring the grant; the kernel admits this operation. `c1 : acquire(e)` and `c1 : read3(T)` through its grant are also kernel-`ok`. | `Encap5C` still admits the unguarded `store`, because `ChainExcl` covers direct exercised authority arguments but not `HasRight(T)`. The tested `PlaceExcl` excludes this step, while `c0 : acquire(e); store(z2,T); unlock(e)` is admitted by `Encap5R`. That was the older `PlaceExcl` fragment; the later `PremG`/`RunFull` inventory covers modeled rights, while `OB-C28` retains its stated enforcement and modeling gaps. |\n614\t| **S10b** — local-anchor escape attempt | In `f0`, `D` is an `Uninit` local; `f1` creates `L`, `L2`, and `X` as `Uninit`, then initializes `X` with `write3(X)`, derives `x` and stores `x` in `L`. `transfer(L,D)` is kernel-`reject` under §63.7 (`anchorOrderHeld`) and §63.6 (`anchorOrder`), while `transfer(L,L2)` is `ok`. | Both verdicts are proved by the **sequential Spec 1 Lean model**, not by a new concurrent or event rule. No change to the frozen Spec 1 is proposed. |\n615\t\n616\t### Handoff Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 bis and 2 septies (authority and abstract-event verdicts proved) **[WC5.1 — M4]**\n617\t\n618\tThe following three traces are additions to the WC4 seed corpus. **[WC5.1] Their abstract authority-machine verdicts, originally proposed from the frozen Spec 1 and WC4 candidate rules, have now been proved in the Milestone 3 close-out Lean corpus.** The occurrence of “expected” in the retained row descriptions below records those original reference verdicts, **not** an unproved current status. The separate Milestone 4 **abstract step-event graph** has now established missing `hb` paths, `Race`, and failure of `HandoffOrdered` for the three witnesses, under the tested `Run5R` traces; the strict graph `HBS` preserves those counterexamples. This does not prove any particular concrete byte-level realization. Any later mismatch in a more complete event model must be reported, not silently repaired.\n619\t\n620\t| Witness | Authority-machine sequence and expected verdicts | Positive/negative companion and event-model question |\n621\t|---|---|---|\n622\t| **W-HO** — unjoined return | In `c0`, local `X` is `Init`; `a := derive(root_X, Modify)` → `ok`; `spawn c1` yielding `a` → `ok`; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` → expected `ok`; `c1 : release(a)` → expected `ok` (O3); while `c1` is still live, `c0 : write3(X)` via `root_X` without `join` → expected `ok` (§43/O10). | Negative companion S13: the parent's conflicting write while `a` remains active → `reject`. Positive synchronized companion S13(a): legal `release`, `terminate`, `join`, then parent write → `ok`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** No `hb`/`HBS` path for the conflicting pair; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved in the abstract graph (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n623\t| **W-HO′** — delayed child use after parent's descendant | `c0 : a := derive(root_X, Modify)` → `ok`; `b := derive(a, Modify)` → `ok`; `spawn c1` yielding `a` but retaining `b` in `c0` → **expected `ok`, now validated by Lean**; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` while `b` is active → expected `reject` (§43.3); `c0 : write3(X)` via `b` → expected `ok`; `c0 : release(b)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` → expected `ok`. | First child write is the negative counterpart of its later allowed write. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The earlier `spawn` does **not** order the parent's later write before the child's newly enabled write. The abstract O3 `release(b)` supplies no `sw`; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n624\t| **W-HO″** — grandchild not joined | `c0` yields `a` to `c1`; `c1` derives and yields `a₂` to `c2`; `c2 : write3(X)` → expected `ok`; `c2 : release(a₂)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : release(a)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : terminate` → expected `ok`, with `c2` live and its initial frame reparented as in C6; `c0 : join(c1)` → expected `ok`; `c0 : write3(X)` via `root_X`, without joining `c2` → expected `ok`. | Negative companion: `c1 : release(a)` **before** `c2 : release(a₂)` → expected `reject` (O3.3). Positive event-model companion requires a genuinely **transitive** completion/handoff protocol, not just `join(c1)`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** No `hb`/`HBS` link orders the grandchild's earlier write before the parent's later write, despite `join(c1)`; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n625\t\n626\t**[WC5.1 — M4] Positive abstract-event counterparts and strict-mutex-edge witnesses (corpus v9 §§ 2 septies–2 octies):**\n627\t\n628\t| Witness | Event-model result (exploratory Lean, not physical realization) |\n629\t|---|---|\n630\t| **W-HO-J** | `c1 : terminate`, then `c0 : join(c1)` before `c0` writes: the conflicting accesses are `HBS`-ordered; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` hold (`W_HOJ_strict`). |\n631\t| **W-HO″-J** | `c2 : terminate`, `c1 : join(c2)`, then `c1 : terminate`, `c0 : join(c1)` before the last write: the complete transitive join chain gives `HBS`, `HandoffOrderedS`, and `¬ RaceS` (`W_HO3J_strict`). |\n632\t| **`lc_trace`** | Protected read by `c1`, `unlock(e)`, later `acquire(e)` by `c0` and protected write: the uninterrupted strict reference-mutex edge gives `HBS`, `HandoffOrderedS`, and `¬ RaceS` (`lc_strict`). |\n633\t| **W-RL₀** | `unlock(e)` followed by a successful next `acquire(e)` without controller intervention: strict abstract edge is present; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_RL0_strict`). |\n634\t| **W-RL** | A free-lock `resetLock(e)` intervenes between `unlock(e)` and the next `acquire(e)`: the **old abstract** edge incorrectly orders the two writes (`W_RL_defect`), but the **strict** graph removes it; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` (`W_RL_strict`). `resetLock` is kernel-`ok` even when `grant = none`, per C18. |\n635\t| **W-RL-J** | W-RL with `c1 : terminate` and `c0 : join(c1)` before recovery and later write: ordered by the join; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_RLJ_strict`). |\n636\t| **W-TR** | A whole-mutex `transfer(M,M′)` changes `ControlOf(e)` from `lock` to `lock′` between `unlock` and next `acquire`: the **old abstract** edge wrongly orders the writes (`W_TR_defect`), while the strict graph has `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` (`W_TR_strict`). The abstract authority trace is admitted. |\n637\t| **W-TR-J** | W-TR with termination and successful join before the later conflicting write: ordered by the join; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_TRJ_strict`). |\n638\t\n639\tThe old graph linked `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` solely by registry index. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The tested strict abstraction suppresses that edge when an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs or `ControlOf(e)` is undefined/changes leaf between unlock completion and the later acquisition. It excludes the two counterexamples; it **does not** prove the concrete controller's required direct `rf` synchronization in C48, nor classify all controller-mutating operations. The internal ordering of events inside compound steps is not part of these proofs.\n640\t\n641\t### Finite Event-Model Witnesses — G1–G19 **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n642\t\n643\tThese are executable **finite-fragment** results, independent of the authority machine, not claims about a complete ERN memory model. Each graph has one atomic location with values and one ordinary leaf used for conflict detection without values. `EventConsistent = RfWF ∧ MoTotal ∧ RmwAtomic ∧ Coherent ∧ HbIrrefl`; `Coherent` checks irreflexivity of `hb ; eco?`. `WellTyped` is checked separately. `sw` uses direct `rf` only; full `seq_cst`, release sequences, and `NoThinAir_RC11` are not modeled.\n644\t\n645\t| Graphs | Verified finite-fragment outcome |\n646\t|---|---|\n647\t| G1, G2 | Release/acquire message passing: G1 has `sw` and ordered ordinary accesses; G2 reads initial `0`, so does not gain `sw`. |\n648\t| G3–G5 | Weakening release and/or acquire removes `sw` and exposes the conflicting ordinary pair as a race. |\n649\t| G6 | `seq_cst` release/acquire roles give the tested `sw`, **without** a global SC proof. |\n650\t| G7–G8 | Mixed `writeShared`/ordinary access can race; an ordinary exclusive write is included in `mo`. |\n651\t| G9–G10 | Two atomic writes do not constitute a data race; two conflicting ordinary writes without `hb` do. |\n652\t| G11–G12 | A valid RMW chain is coherent; two distinct successful RMWs reading the same source are rejected by `RmwAtomic` in both tested `mo` orders. |\n653\t| G13 | A failed CAS is a read-only event, not a modification. |\n654\t| G14–G16 | Lock-shaped acquire/release direct-`rf` synchronization is present for the reference orders, absent when release or acquire is weakened. |\n655\t| G17 | An `acq_rel` RMW directly read by an `acquire` load establishes the tested `sw`. |\n656\t| G18 | The direct-`rf` fragment exhibits a race after an intervening same-thread non-RMW atomic store; `rc11.cat` E1 does not. The fragment has **no release-sequence semantics**, so it does not choose between C++20-form and RC11 extensions. |\n657\t| G19 | `readShared(x, release)` is ill-typed. |\n658\t\n659\tThe generic `hb_trans`, `hb_closure`, and `eco_irrefl` theorems and the backward-edge G1 variant supplement the six general event lemmas. The fragment's finite transitive-closure decision does not presume edges increase with event enumeration. `eco_irrefl` requires `RfWF`, `MoTotal`, and `RmwAtomic` only; no no-thin-air conclusion is drawn.\n660\t\n661\t### Controller Realization and Encapsulation Witnesses **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n662\t\n663\tThe following are **declared-realization** and authority-trace results. They do not independently verify the physical instructions, metadata effects, or the byte-event traces of a compiled implementation.\n664\t\n665\t| Witness | Formal result and boundary |\n666\t|---|---|\n667\t| `lc_trace`, W-RL₀ | The strict `unlock(e)` to later `acquire(e)` edge has direct `rf` and reference release/acquire `sw`; no controller intervention occurs. |\n668\t| W-RL, W-RL-J | An intervening `resetLock(e)` writes `0` under reference `relaxed` order, so the later RMW reads the recovery write rather than the previous `unlock`; the reference lock contributes no `sw`. W-RL-J orders the protected accesses through `join`, separately. |\n669\t| W-TR | Whole-mutex transfer changes `ControlOf`; it refutes the converse `rf ⇒ SWS` **without** `CtlFixed`. The one-controller graph does not constitute a physical transfer realization. |\n670\t| `store_cex`, `issue_cex`, `alloc_cex` | Kernel-`ok` steps on a live controller are excluded by final C22.4; positive destinations outside the controller are admitted. |\n671\t| `alloc(owner = M)`, `depend(ctl, a)` | Eligible under the final C22.4 where no prohibited controller effect occurs; the restriction is narrower than the earlier provisional footprint test. |\n672\t| `alloc(provider = M, owner = ctl′)` | Kernel-`ok` but excluded by C22.4 because provider-metadata writes **may** affect the controller; the metadata write is represented conservatively by the footprint, not executed in the current kernel effect model. |\n673\t| W-RC | Old C18.1–C18.3 admit controller recovery from a frame without controller rights; new C18.4 excludes this step, while the legitimate recovery counterparts remain admitted. |\n674\t| W-NC | Old C13.1–C13.10 admit controller construction from a frame without controller rights; new C13.11 excludes it, with all 16 previously recorded `newMutex` steps satisfying the additional `Modify` test. |\n675\t\n676\t### Full Encapsulation and Abandoned-Grant Witnesses — Corpus v14 **[WC5.3 — M4]**\n677\t\n678\tThe expected verdicts below derive from the inherited model and WC5.2 candidate clauses; the cited Lean results are exploratory checks, **not** independent verification of the implementation or of all physical access events.\n679\t\n680\t| Witness | Kernel and safe-abstraction verdict | Proof result and boundary |\n681\t|---|---|---|\n682\t| **W-GI** — `issue` into protected `T` without a grant | Kernel-`ok` and admitted by `RunAmF`; excluded by `RunFull` (`¬ PremG` for destination and internal-store rights). | `W_GI_current`, `W_GI_1_excluded`; the grant-authorized `issue`/`unlock` twin is `RunFull` (`W_GI_twin`). |\n683\t| **W-RX A/B** — recovery of an inner mutex under a live outer mutex | The nested setup and unauthorized inner `resetLock` are kernel-`ok`; `RunFull` excludes A because both the inner `slot` exercise and controller access pass through the outer `guarded`. | `W_RX_setup`, `W_RX_A_excluded`; B acquires the outer grant first and is `RunFull` (`W_RX_B`); `resetLock_rights` establishes the scoped exception. |\n684\t| **W-AG** — abandoned recorded grant, surviving derivative `x` | The eleven authority steps are `RunFull`. The frame exit releases `g` and reanchors `x` to `guarded` without creating an authority. Reacquisition, unlock or reset cannot succeed while `x` is active. After `x` is released, `resetLock` and reacquisition are admissible. | `W_AG_run`, `W_AG_6_facts`, `W_AG_a`–`W_AG_d`; in the **strict abstract event graph**, the two protected writes have no `hb` path (`W_AG_strict`, `RaceS`). This is not a proof of a concrete target race. |\n685\t| **W-AG-J** — recovery after `join` | The sequence is `RunFull`, with the writer terminating and the recovering context joining before `resetLock`. | `W_AGJ_run`, `W_AGJ_strict`: the strict graph orders the two writes by `terminate → join` and has `¬ RaceS`. |\n686\t| **W-AG′** — further derivation and reanchoring after grant abandonment | The survivor `x` legally derives `y`; after `terminate`, `y` survives, becomes a **direct** child of `guarded`, but was already in its transitive descendant set. `resetLock` still has no `ok` outcome while `y` covers a protected leaf. | `W_AGp_run`, `W_AGp_neg`; illustrates the distinction between creating an authority, changing `origin`, and entering the guarded descendant set. |\n687\t\n688\tThe reported complete-encapsulation tests also replay the earlier W-GR/W-GC/W-GS rejected steps and their admitted alternatives, along with S4/S9 and other legitimate traces. `protected_cap_run`, `three_cases`, `closure_run`, `last_acquire`, `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, `spawnVal_cap_pre`, and `usable_run` give general **abstract** results under their explicit `RunFull` reachability assumptions. None discharges C46 for all safe runs or the concrete physical correspondence obligations.\n689\t\n690\tOn reachable initial-state traces, the final footprint proof (`completeness7_run`) classifies all 28 modeled step kinds under the fixed-controller hypothesis. The proof applies to modeled footprints and keeps all physical-access obligations under `OB-C20`. `Run6 ⊆ Run7` is proved on reachable states satisfying `SyncInv`/C21.3, not on arbitrary malformed states. The construction and recovery authority rules are separate conditions of `RunAmF`.\n691\t\n692\tThe concurrent verdicts remain conditional wherever the complete event-order and safe-execution semantics have not yet been defined. The strict abstract graph establishes the reported witness outcomes within its stated scope, but it does not discharge the complete physical event semantics or safe-program obligations.\n693\t\n694\tA proof or successful trace in the authority machine does not by itself establish absence of data races.\n695\t\n696\t---\n697\t\n698\t# VI. Exploratory Verification Requirements\n699\t\n700\tThe candidate is input to the established exploratory formalization process. It is not committed as normative text.\n701\t\n702\t## Milestone 1 — Abstract Model\n703\t\n704\tFormalize the concurrent authority state and the proposed frame, synchronization, and transfer transitions.\n705\t\n706\tEstablish or refute:\n707\t\n708\t- conservation of inherited sequential behavior;\n709\t- preservation of authority and frame invariants;\n710\t- validity of reparenting;\n711\t- preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline`;\n712\t- correctness of `deriveSync` under explicit `K_C` obligations;\n713\t- correctness of the provisional by-value `spawn` composite.\n714\t\n715\tUnproved properties must remain reported as open.\n716\t\n717\t## Milestone 2 — Refinement\n718\t\n719\tEstablish the applicable correspondence to the frozen memory machine and its allocation, byte, ownership, holder, and validity conditions.\n720\t\n721\tReport any property whose realization depends on the deferred atomic event model.\n722\t\n723\t## Milestone 3 — Adversarial Validation\n724\t\n725\tEvaluate S1a–S18 and their positive or negative counterparts, using verdicts grounded in the frozen specifications and explicitly identified candidate rules.\n726\t\n727\tDo not infer expected verdicts from model results.\n728\t\n729\tNo failed obligation may be bypassed by an undisclosed hypothesis or a weakened theorem.\n730\t\n731\t## Milestone 4 — Event Model, Handoff, and Data-Race-Freedom Validation\n732\t\n733\tMilestone 4 remains a **verification plan** for full event semantics and data-race freedom. **[WC5.2 — M4]** Its authority-handoff, abstract strict-step graph, finite `EventConsistent` fragment, conditional reference-mutex controller graph and bounded RC11 tests have produced scoped results, not a full physical event/refinement or safe-program proof. Its inputs are the WC4-derived authority machine, the completed Milestone 3 corpus, the subsequent guarded-chain adversarial witnesses, and the proposed C26–C53 semantics. Any assumption used in a theorem must appear in its statement or declared model scope. No theorem may be represented as universally proved merely because a named `SafeConcurrentTrace` or `EventConsistent` predicate assumes the required result.\n734\t\n735\t**Authority-handoff progress [WC5.1] — exploratory Lean 4, not normative:** W-HO, W-HO′ and W-HO″ authority verdicts were established at the Milestone 3 close-out; W-GR, W-GC and W-GS demonstrate that the prior safe-mutex encapsulation was incomplete. `cap5_drop_own` (28 step kinds, under `DDSound`) and `cap5_compat` are proved. Under the tested direct-access holder-chain restriction, `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` are proved for `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared`, and the stronger `PlaceExcl` restriction inherits them. W-GS is excluded by the tested place-right restriction; legitimate reference mutex cycles, guarded reads under a grant, arena `issue` under its grant, and whole-mutex transfers are admitted in the reported replay corpus. Those earlier authority-handoff results are retained; the **latest** exploratory concurrency audit after the complete-encapsulation complement and `KillKinds` pass reports **2,822 `Ern.Concurrency*` theorems (1,878 non-internal)**, `lake build` successful without warnings, and no axioms beyond `{propext, Quot.sound}`. These are reported tool results, not an independent re-execution in this document.\n736\t\n737\t**Abstract event progress [WC5.1 — M4]:** A step-event graph (one event per authority-machine step, with `start(c)` markers and `terminate(c)` standing in for `complete(c)`) now has formal `po`, abstract `sw`, `hb`, and `Race`; `hb` is irreflexive and decidable. `lossStep_own` and `handoffOrdered_no_race` establish the scoped handoff composition. `spawn_orders` and `join_orders` are proved. The first mutex edge was too permissive for W-RL and W-TR; the added strict graph gives `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`, `spawn_ordersS`, `join_ordersS`, `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps` (conditional), and `reg_stable`. All v9 abstract-event witnesses have expected verdicts. That **strict abstract step graph** contains **no `rf`, `mo`, values, atomic memory orders, or complete physical access mapping**; a separate finite event fragment and controller correspondence model now supply some of those relations without replacing the strict graph.\n738\t\n739\t**RC11 reference-test progress [WC5.1 — M4]:** herdtools7 **7.58**, with unmodified `rc11.cat`, has executed **17 litmus tests** (groups A–E, including the direct-exclusivity D1–D3 series and release-sequence test E1), with expected outcomes. `release`/`acquire` orders the tested ordinary publication; changing either side of the reference lock to `relaxed` permits a race. RMW acquisition exclusivity is independent of memory order in the tested cases; skipping `no-thin-air` enables the forbidden load-buffering outcome. E1 exhibits a reference-model release-sequence behavior not included by the candidate C++20-form extension. These are bounded reference observations, not ERN-model proof.\n740\t\n741\t**Finite event-fragment progress [WC5.2 — M4]:** `EventModel` now defines finite `rf`, `mo`, `po`, direct `sw`, `hb`, `eco` and the five-conjunct `EventConsistent` condition on one atomic unit; both consistency and finite reachability are decidable. Six general lemmas and four complements (including `hb_trans`, `hb_closure`, `eco_irrefl`, and an enumeratively backward synchronization witness) are reported proved. G1–G19 have their stated verdicts. G18 executes a **direct-read-only fragment** race, without implementing either alternative release-sequence definition.\n742\t\n743\t**Reference mutex progress [WC5.2 — M4]:** `ctl_invariant` links the logical recorded grant with the last modeled controller modification; `ctlGraph_coherent` establishes the finite consistency property under its explicit trace assumptions. `bridge` establishes `SWS ⇒ rf`, `bridge_sw_ref` obtains `sw` under the reference release/acquire orders, and `bridge_converse` obtains `rf ⇒ SWS` under `CtlFixed`. The final safe-interface footprint restriction classifies 28 modeled step kinds without a counterexample and proves `completeness7_run` under reachable initial-state and fixed-controller hypotheses; W-RC and W-NC motivate the added authority rules. None of these constructions proves that a real implementation produces the declared `rf`/`mo` history.\n744\t\n745\t**Complete-encapsulation progress [WC5.3 — M4]:** The 28-kind right-premise inventory (`Prem`, `PremG`), `EncapFull`, and `RunFull` extend coverage to `issue`, `resetLock`, `newMutex`, `unlock`, `newArena`, composite phases, and recorded `resetLock` exceptions. Reported `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, and `runFull_runAmF` prove the relevant model correspondence, and `resetLock_rights` verifies the exception. `capacity_run` and `spawnVal_cap_pre` cover abstract touched-leaf capacities; `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, and `usable_run` establish the lifetime properties, including the exhaustive four-kind unusability classification. `closure_run`, `empty_without_grant`, `last_acquire`, `three_cases`, and `protected_cap_run` establish provenance after normal acquisition and abandoned grants without historical-origin state. W-GI/W-RX are excluded, W-AG/W-AG-J/W-AG′ have their expected authority and strict-event results, and existing positive traces remain admitted. The report checks 46 `RunFull` traces (33 earlier and 13 replayed). All these are from the model's initial state with `DDSound` and `StaticsOK`, not universally from arbitrary well-formed states or real target events.\n746\t\n747\t**Still open [WC5.3 — M4]:** actual safe-interface enforcement and the residual `OB-C28` model gaps (§78.1 `MetaRight` of `reset` and inventory points verified only by inspection); physical byte and within-step marker correspondence (`OB-C20`); all-context ordering of the non-atomic `newMutex` initialization; actual target `rf`/`mo` and full `LockOrder` refinement (`OB-C05`); failed-CAS retries and controller relocation; additional atomic units, global `seq_cst`, release sequences and the no-thin-air choice; complete DRF and safe-program soundness. `HandoffOrderedS` has been demonstrated on **seven positive traces**, but has not been established for every safe program (`OB-C24`).\n748\t\n749\t**Lean 4 proof program**\n750\t\n751\t1. **Adversarial baseline [WC5.1]:** W-HO, W-HO′ and W-HO″ authority-machine verdicts, including the W-HO′ `spawn` and relevant reparenting/negative checks, were established in the Milestone 3 close-out. Keep those proofs and the guarded-chain corpus as the abstract baseline. Event-graph counterexamples for W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″ and the positive W-HO-J/W-HO″-J witnesses are now proved at the **abstract strict-graph** level (`Corpus/HandoffEvents`, `Corpus/MutexEdgeStrict`); concrete-event checks remain open.\n752\t2. **Event graph construction [WC5.2 — M4, scoped]:** the abstract single-event-per-step lifecycle graph remains proved within its declared scope. Separately, the **finite single-atomic-unit fragment** now defines `rf`, `mo`, direct `sw`, `hb`, `eco` and a nonvacuous, five-conjunct `EventConsistent` with initial values, typed atomic events, successful RMWs and read-only failed comparisons. **Remaining:** integrate the two graphs and define the actual event mapping of every included `SimRunC` step, including cumulative `acc`/`sync`, `start(c)` and a distinct `complete(c)`, within-step order, byte footprints, additional atomic units, and full permitted executions.\n753\t3. **Synchronization [WC5.2 — M4]:** `spawn_ordersS` and `join_ordersS` prove scoped lifecycle `hb` consequences in the abstract graph. W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″ lack the relevant `HBS` and fail C46; their synchronized companions have strict `HBS` paths. The finite `EventModel` proves direct-`rf` release/acquire `sw` for its graph, and the constructed controller bridge proves the reference lock `sw` under its declared realization. **Remaining:** complete lifecycle-event integration, actual physical atomic observations and implementation correspondence (`OB-C05`).\n754\t4. **Authority handoff [WC5.1]:** retain the already proved `cap5_drop_own` (28 step kinds), `cap5_compat`, `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` with their exact hypotheses and **`read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` scope**. Retain the completed `RunFull`/`PremG` modeled C22.6 premise checks while proving actual safe-interface enforcement and closing the §78.1/inventory gaps (`OB-C28`); extend abstract access proofs to physically realized operations (`OB-C20`); `lossStep_own` now establishes the strictly intervening, own-context loss step and `po(e1,r)` in the one-event-per-step graph; `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` proves the scoped composition if C46 ordering is supplied. Physical marker placement and protocol-wide C46 establishment remain open. Context-exclusive handoff for ordinary leaves and D-C9 mixed atomic/ordinary pairs remains an event/interface obligation. Do not confuse R4 simultaneous non-conflict with event ordering.\n755\t5. **Mutex and `join` [WC5.3 — M4]:** abstract `join_ordersS` and conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps` are retained; W-RL and W-TR refute the overbroad mutex edge. The **constructed** controller history proves the conditional strict-edge `SWS ⇒ rf ⇒ sw` bridge, and the converse under `CtlFixed`, with the final 28-kind footprint classification and authority checks of C13.11/C18.4. The newer `PremG`/`RunFull` results prove **modeled C22.6 right-premise coverage** and the scoped `resetLock` exception, with `runFull_inv` preserving the inherited invariants under its stated reachable-trace hypotheses. **Still to prove:** reference soundness of the modified C16–C18 *outcome classification*, actual safe-library enforcement of the C22.1–6 restrictions, the residual §78.1/inventory points in `OB-C28`, and a dedicated witness where ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` is kernel-`ok` but excluded by `Encap` from the safe interface. These are not discharged by the finite event/bridge proofs. Physical `LockOrder` from **actual** target `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, failed-CAS events, abandoned-grant realization and physical correspondence remain independent open obligations.\n756\t6. **Shared disciplines and data races:** formalize `Atomic`, `Synchronized`, and transferrable `Confined`, D-C2–D-C5, D-C7, D-C9, and C51. Prove the case-partitioned DRF composition theorem only to the strength justified by its hypotheses, with generic `Synchronized` obligations flagged as assumptions until discharged by specific protocols.\n757\t7. **Conservativity and refinement:** restate and prove T1 in view of O11.3's missing Lean representation; independently prove pure-marker erasure, concrete-byte coverage and the relation to R1–R3, R4′, `LayoutWF`, and Spec 1 §82. If the model cannot justify an `issue`, move, physical controller access, or a byte operation after invalidation, report the missing premise.\n758\t8. **Parametric consistency [WC5.2 — M4]:** herdtools7 A4 experimentally isolates the RC11 `no-thin-air` condition. The **abstract strict-step graph** has no `rf`; the separate finite Lean fragment has `rf` but does **not** adopt `NoThinAir_RC11`, nor does the five-conjunct consistency predicate prove no-thin-air. Retain the rule as a **named optional hypothesis** for future complete models and report any theorem dependencies. Release sequences and full global `seq_cst` remain open. Record the theorem-assumption/axiom audit and any counterexamples without weakening failed statements.\n759\t\n760\t**herdtools7 comparison program**\n761\t\n762\t- **[WC5.1 — M4] Partially exercised under RC11 reference:** 17 litmus programs cover load buffering (A4), message passing (A1–A3), reference lock RMW and CAS (B1–B6), RMW exclusivity (D1–D3), and mixed atomic/non-atomic conflicts (C1–C3), with the release-sequence case E1. Still open: store buffering, failed CAS variants, RMW-extended release sequences, fences, `acq_rel`, and target-specific execution.\n763\t- **[WC5.1 — M4] Partial comparison only:** reference `rc11.cat` rejects the load-buffering outcome through `no-thin-air` (A4), and its same-thread non-RMW atomic-store release-sequence extension (E1) differs from the proposed C++20-form extension in C48. **[WC5.2 — M4]** G18 has now been executed in an ERN **direct-`rf`-only finite fragment** and is racy there; no ERN model implementing the C++20-form release sequence has been executed. The distinct C++20-form prediction remains analytical, and neither inclusion nor equivalence with RC11 is proved. Complete RC11-to-ERN inclusion/equivalence and full global SC comparisons remain open.\n764\t- For W-HO, W-HO′, and W-HO″, use a concrete mapping of authority effects to physical synchronization before interpreting any litmus outcome. Pure authority markers do not turn into hardware synchronization by themselves.\n765\t- Examine target lowering on x86-TSO and weakly ordered targets including ARM/POWER where relevant. The effect and possible cost of a no-thin-air rule must be justified by an appropriate compilation scheme and measurement, not guessed from static type information.\n766\t\n767\t**Milestone-4 evidence required:** named definitions and theorem statements, proof-module references, explicit admissibility assumptions, witness outcomes and positive counterparts, nonvacuity checks, any unresolved physical-model obligations, and an independent audit of axioms and prohibited proof shortcuts. Completing this plan is not itself sufficient to freeze Spec 2: sound safe-language exposure (Specs 3/4), physical refinement, and deferred memory-model decisions remain separate obligations.\n768\t\n769\t---\n770\t\n771\t# VII. Concurrent Event Model — Proposed Block C\n772\t\n773\tThe clauses C26–C53 below are **working proposals**, not amendments to the frozen Memory Model. They provide the candidate C/D extension to C1–C25 and are not claims that the corresponding Lean obligations have already been discharged. Their labels refer to the working decisions D-C1–D-C10 in the WC5 framing document.\n774\t\n775\t## C26. Executions, Abstract Steps, and Event Graphs\n776\t\n777\tA **candidate concurrent execution** consists of:"}]},"uuid":"d7c0f7cf-c4ff-4b87-a947-d559241d1cd0","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:32.428Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","content":"# IV. Open Obligations\n\n| ID | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| `OB-C01` | Concurrent event model and definition of permitted executions |\n| `OB-C02` | Conflict relations, synchronizes-with relations, and `happens-before` |\n| `OB-C03` | Atomic operations, memory orders, and permitted observations |\n| `OB-C04` | **[WC5.1 — M4]** `LockOrder` for successive protected critical sections: `lock_ordersS` and its finer `lock_ordersS_gaps` variant are proved in the abstract strict event graph **only when** (i) intervening successful acquisitions are each followed by an `unlock` ordered after that acquisition and before the final acquisition, (ii) no disqualifying `resetLock` intervenes across the required unlock–acquire gaps, and (iii) the controller remains defined and stable across those gaps. The finer `lock_ordersS_gaps` theorem requires the two gap conditions only for the relevant successive unlock–acquire pairs. **[WC5.2 — M4]** The reference `bridge` and `bridge_sw_ref` establish the direct-`rf`/`sw` counterpart of a strict unlock–acquire edge in the constructed controller graph under the declared realization, but do not establish target physical refinement or universal `LockOrder`. The general obligation remains open. |\n| `OB-C05` | **[WC5.2 — M4]** A conditional correspondence between the reference mutex authority trace and its **constructed** atomic event graph is proved (`ctl_invariant`, `ctlGraph_coherent`, `bridge`, `bridge_sw_ref`, with `bridge_converse` under `CtlFixed`). The required physical atomic realization, correctness of `mo`/`rf` construction for target executions, initial-write ordering across contexts, failed CAS events, recovery ordering and complete `LockOrder` remain open. |\n| `OB-C06` | Data-race freedom for all permitted safe executions |\n| `OB-C07` | Type-system rules for cross-context transferability and shareability — Spec 4 |\n| `OB-C08` | Encapsulation and library contract enforcement — Specs 4 and 10 |\n| `OB-C09` | Final by-value passing conventions, cancellation, abnormal termination, and cleanup — Spec 3 |\n| `OB-C10` | External execution contexts and interoperability — Spec 11 |\n| `OB-C11` | **[WC5.1 — M4]** Event-order consequences of `join`, completion, and frame-exit protocols: `spawn_ordersS` and `join_ordersS` are proved for the scoped abstract step graph (with stated lifecycle hypotheses). Placement of a distinct within-step `complete(c)` marker and physical event correspondence remain open. |\n| `OB-C12` | Concrete correspondence and refinement obligations of Spec 1 §82 |\n| `OB-C13` | Preservation of H1 ancestry and validity under context reparenting |\n| `OB-C14` | Correctness of the provisional composite by-value `spawn` |\n| `OB-C15` | Correctness of the external `SyncAuthorized` contract and its relationship to library state |\n| `OB-C16` | Unified live administrative identity guard across arenas and mutexes, consistent with Spec 1 §56; integration and verification deferred to later milestones |\n| `OB-C17` | **[WC5.1 — M4] L1, scoped exploratory proof:** `cap5_drop_own` covers all 28 modeled step kinds under `DDSound` and reachable encapsulated authority traces. `lossStep_own` further establishes for eligible direct-access conflicting pairs a first own-context loss step `r` strictly between the two accesses; with one event per abstract step, `po(e1,r)` follows. Internal composite-step ordering and physical access coverage remain open (`OB-C19`, `OB-C20`). Raw `cap` still fails L1 (W-GR). |\n| `OB-C18` | **[WC5.1 — M4] L2 and scoped composition proved exploratorily:** `cap5_compat` gives state incompatibility; `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` give the trace bridge under `Encap5C` (inherited by `Encap5R`); `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` proves `HandoffOrderedS → ¬ RaceS` for the abstract exclusive access-pair model under its explicit run hypotheses. `spawn_ordersS`, `join_ordersS`, and conditional `lock_ordersS` establish reference edges only in that abstract graph. The weaker `Encap5` trace claim is refuted (W-GC). Direct physical `rf`, all generated accesses, and safe-program enforcement remain open (`OB-C05`, `OB-C20`, `OB-C24`). |\n| `OB-C19` | **Erasure:** prove removal of pure authority markers preserves `hb` between surviving physical/synchronization events under `po` projection (C29) |\n| `OB-C20` | **Coverage:** account for each physical byte access of every realization and justify its authority, byte range, atomicity, and validity, including compound steps (C27, C29, C50) |\n| `OB-C21` | **Restated T1:** prove the appropriate directional projection and conditional equality for inherited `writeShared` under `K_C.SharedDiscipline`; report the missing Spec 1 Lean representation of O11.3 |\n| `OB-C22` | **[WC5.1 — M4] No-thin-air:** herdtools7 7.58 A4 observes the load-buffering outcome with `no-thin-air` skipped and excludes it under reference `rc11.cat`. Whether ERN adopts `acyclic(po ∪ rf)` remains open; target compilation costs and a justified compilation scheme have not been established. `NoThinAir_RC11` remains an optional named hypothesis. |\n| `OB-C23` | **[WC5.1 — M4] Complete atomic model:** release sequences, full global `seq_cst` constraints, coherent `rf`/`mo`/RMW admissibility, and a proved comparison to RC11 remain open. Bounded reference tests A1–E1 were executed; E1 exhibits a same-thread atomic non-RMW store extending the release sequence in `rc11.cat`, unlike the provisional C++20-form extension proposed in C48 (not executed as an ERN model). **[WC5.2 — M4]** A finite `EventConsistent` fragment for one atomic unit now formalizes five named conjuncts, and G18 exhibits a race under direct-`rf` `sw` only; neither full release-sequence alternative nor global SC nor RC11 equivalence has been proved. No inclusion/equivalence is claimed. |\n| `OB-C24` | **Safe-program soundness:** prove in Specs 3/4 and safe libraries that well-typed safe executions meet `SafeConcurrentTrace`, including C22/C42/C46 and `Confined`/`Synchronized` contracts |\n| `OB-C25` | **[WC5.1 — M4] Handoff witnesses, abstract graph results established:** the WC4-derived authority verdicts of W-HO, W-HO′, W-HO″ are proved; in the abstract step-event model each has `Race`/`RaceS`, lacks the relevant `hb`/`HBS`, and fails `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`. W-HO-J, W-HO″-J, and `lc_trace` provide positive ordered and non-racing counterparts. Physical event-mapping and general safe-program exclusions remain open. |\n| `OB-C26` | **[WC5.2 — M4] Event consistency (partially discharged):** a finite, nonvacuous, single-atomic-unit `EventConsistent` model with `RfWF`, `MoTotal`, `RmwAtomic`, `Coherent` and `HbIrrefl` is implemented with initial values, valid reads, successful RMWs, and a failed-CAS **read-only event** (G13); 19 witness graphs pass the stated verdicts. General byte-event mapping, multiple locations, all failed-CAS paths, global SC, release sequences and full permitted-execution semantics remain open. |\n| `OB-C27` | **Target realization:** show required atomic sizes/alignments, controller linearization, `LockOrder`, handoff synchronization and physical access-after-invalidation safety on each supported target |\n| `OB-C28` | **[WC5.3 — model coverage established; realization open]** The complete C22.6 authority-premise restriction is represented exploratorily by `PremG`/`EncapFull`/`RunFull`; `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, `runFull_runAmF`, and `resetLock_rights` establish the reported model-scope checks. W-GI/W-RX counterexamples are excluded, the 28 modeled step kinds (including compound phases) are classified, the legitimate reference traces and 46 reported `RunFull` witness traces are checked. `capacity_run`, `protected_cap_run`, `three_cases`, `closure_run`, `last_acquire`, `spawnVal_cap_pre`, `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, and `usable_run` establish their named properties from the **model initial state** under `DDSound`, `StaticsOK`, and `RunFull`, not from arbitrary valid states. **Still open:** enforcing the restriction in the actual safe interface (Specs 4/10); the missing §78.1 `MetaRight` read by the `reset` model step; portions of exhaustive premise coverage supported by manual definition inspection rather than a general theorem; C46 ordering for recovery after an abandoned grant; and physical-access/effect coverage, including `alloc` provider metadata (`OB-C20`, `OB-C05`). The abstract result neither supplies universal `hb` ordering nor proves DRF. |\n\nNo complete concurrency safety claim follows from this partial candidate alone.\n\n---\n\n# V. Adversarial Corpus — Corrected Seed Cases\n\nThe following cases are seeds for the established adversarial validation process. Each executable negative case requires a positive counterpart.\n\nExisting Spec 1 verdicts and candidate-specific verdicts remain distinguishable.\n\n| Case | Scenario | Expected verdict and basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1a | A parent exits while its own local storage is still required by an active borrowed authority in a child | `reject`, under O7 and `NoDangling`; positive: end the dependent use before exit |\n| S1b | A creating frame exits while the child's authority is anchored in storage belonging to a surviving ancestor, with legal reanchoring and reparenting | Candidate `ok`, subject to O7.4–O7.5 and extended ancestry; negative: invalid surviving anchor |\n| S2 | Valid synchronized grant is acquired for ordinary protected `T` | Candidate `ok`, under `deriveSync` and its contract; negative: competing full-footprint grant |\n| S3 | A second context directly attempts `issue` on a shared arena without an exercisable administrative `Modify` grant | `reject` under O18 (§77.1), because `MetaRight` is not established; positive: synchronized acquisition of the required grant followed by legal `issue` |\n| S4 | A detached child receives an independently owned value through the provisional `spawn` composite and survives its creator | Candidate `ok` if all transfer and frame invariants hold; negative: unresolved local-anchor dependency |\n| S5a | `writeShared(T)` where protected `T` is ordinary, not `SharedMutable` | `reject`, O11.1; positive: authorized ordinary write under a grant |\n| S5b | Shared mutation of a controller outside its declared synchronization discipline | O11.3 unsatisfied; classification depends on the safe/unsafe interface; positive: authorized controller update |\n| S6 | Two conflicting protected ordinary accesses have nonoverlapping grants but insufficient required memory ordering | Must not be permitted as a safe data-racing execution; deferred to event semantics; positive: `LockOrder`-compliant execution |\n| S7 | Legal mutex transfer preserves positional controller association | Candidate `ok`; negative: an attempted transfer violating an applicable O4 precondition |\n| S8 | A callee exercises a grant it has not received | `reject` under §43; positive: legal parameter transmission |\n| S9 | Acquiring mutex `M1` must not grant access to `M2` of the same type | Instance-isolation property; positive: independent valid acquisitions of both mutexes |\n| S10 | From `MX₃`, `f0` holds grant `g` while `f1` receives `m := derive(root_M, Modify)` and has an uninitialized local `M′`; `f1` transfers `M → M′`. | **Expected `ok`** under §63.7: `g` is frame-held in `f0`, not a moved place-held authority subject to `AnchorOrderHeld` (§44). In the resulting state `exitFrameC(f1)` → expected `reject` (§73.4); `transfer(M′, M)` → expected `ok` (§63.3, source leaves now `Moved`); subsequent `exitFrameC(f1)` → expected `ok`. All are corpus-v3 expectations, pending a sequential Lean witness; no frozen rule is changed. |\n| S11 | In `MX₄`, the child enters frame `f1′` **yielding `o1′ := derive(o1, Observe)`** so that C14.3 holds; it then acquires `g`, exits `f1′` without `unlock`, and the parent attempts acquisition and recovery. | Expected: `exitFrameC(f1′)` → `ok` (grant inactive but registry records it); parent's `acquire(e)` → **temporarily non-enabled** (C14.2); parent's `resetLock(e)` → `reject` (C18.1: `Modify` not exercisable while `o1` remains active). Positive: child `terminate`, parent `join(c1)`, `resetLock(e)` → `ok`, then `acquire(e)` → `ok`. The setup requires yielding `o1′`; the verdicts are corpus-v3 expectations subject to D-C1's proposed outcome semantics. |\n| S12 | An exclusively authorized ordinary `write3(lock)` bypasses the mutex controller protocol | Kernel O10 may return `ok`; **safe-abstraction encapsulation / controller protocol** is violated (not necessarily the authority-only `GrantDiscipline` predicate); positive: protocol-controlled update |\n| S13 | Creator transmits a `Modify` derivative to a child and then attempts conflicting ordinary mutation through the origin | `reject` while the derivative blocks effective rights, §43/O10; positive: mutation after the derivative and all blocking descendants have legally ended and completion has been established, or simultaneous modification of genuinely disjoint leaves after a legal `split` |\n| S14 | Two contexts perform initialized ordinary reads using compatible `Observe` authorities on the same resource | Authority-machine `ok` under O9 and I1, subject to all applicable read conditions; positive paired with a rejected incompatible-authority construction |\n| S15 | An entire grant is yielded to a callee and released on frame exit without being returned | Grant no longer available for the caller's `unlock`; possible leaked lock; positive: pass a fresh derivative so that the caller retains its grant and can unlock after the derivative ends |\n| S16 | `transfer(slot, Y)` extracts the place directly holding `guarded` from live `M`, leaving no positional controller | O4 kernel transition `ok` under the tested setup; excluded by C22 abstraction encapsulation; without that exclusion `SyncInv` controller validity fails. Positive: legal `transfer(M, M′)` preserves `ControlOf`, with or without an active grant |\n| S17a | `endUse(ctl)` while a mutex entry is live and its `guarded` authority survives | O12 kernel transition may be `ok`; excluded by C22.5. Without the restriction `ctlInit` fails. Positive: after the mutex entry has ceased to be live, `endUse(ctl)` is permitted by the encapsulation contract when O12's own preconditions hold |\n| S17b | `endUse(q)` on a strict structural subplace `q` of a composite `slot` while the entry is live | O12 kernel transition may be `ok`; excluded by C22.5. Without the restriction `slotInit` fails. Positive: legal termination of `slot` or enclosing `M` when all applicable O12 safety requirements hold |\n| S18 | A whole-value transfer would merge the controller association of two live mutex entries | Candidate `reject` under O4 §63.3 when the destination live mutex has initialized structural leaves. Positive: legal `transfer(M, M′)` into a destination not holding a live mutex entry |\n\n### Guarded-Chain and Transfer Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 ter–2 sexies and S10b **[WC5.1]**\n\nThe following are **authority-machine and exploratory encapsulation** verdicts. None is by itself a concrete data-race or event-ordering proof. The current report confirms the guarded witnesses through the corpus version available to that report; any later corpus revisions must be reconciled without silently changing expected outcomes.\n\n| Witness | Authority-machine sequence and obtained result | Safe-abstraction verdict and outstanding boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| **W-GR** — direct access through `guarded` | In shared mutex state `MX₄`, `c1 : read3(T)` using `guarded` is kernel-`ok`; `c0 : acquire(e)` and then `c0 : write3(T)` using grant `g` are each `ok`. `c1`'s read while `g` is active is kernel-`reject` (§43). | The initial unguarded read was admitted by old `EncapsulatedTrace`; it is excluded by `ChainExcl`/C22.6. The unrestricted-context `cap` loses `c1`'s rights at `c0`'s `acquire`, refuting raw L1. Physical event-order proof remains separate. |\n| **W-GC** — indirect access through a holder chain | `z := derive(root_Y, Modify)` is stored in protected `T`, so `c1 : read3(Y)` using `z` is kernel-`ok` via the chain `z → T → guarded → slot → o1`, even without a grant; later `acquire` and protected access by `c0` are also kernel-`ok`. | `GuardedExcl` (testing only the argument) and `Encap5` admit the unguarded read; C22.6's whole-chain `ChainExcl` excludes it. `cap5_sound` and trace L2 are refuted under weak `Encap5`, but proved for the direct-access fragment under `Encap5C`. A grant-authorized access through `z` remains admitted. |\n| **W-GS** — right on a place used by `store` | The mutex owner `c0` shares only the controller right with `c1`, then performs `store(z2,T)` **without** acquiring the grant; the kernel admits this operation. `c1 : acquire(e)` and `c1 : read3(T)` through its grant are also kernel-`ok`. | `Encap5C` still admits the unguarded `store`, because `ChainExcl` covers direct exercised authority arguments but not `HasRight(T)`. The tested `PlaceExcl` excludes this step, while `c0 : acquire(e); store(z2,T); unlock(e)` is admitted by `Encap5R`. That was the older `PlaceExcl` fragment; the later `PremG`/`RunFull` inventory covers modeled rights, while `OB-C28` retains its stated enforcement and modeling gaps. |\n| **S10b** — local-anchor escape attempt | In `f0`, `D` is an `Uninit` local; `f1` creates `L`, `L2`, and `X` as `Uninit`, then initializes `X` with `write3(X)`, derives `x` and stores `x` in `L`. `transfer(L,D)` is kernel-`reject` under §63.7 (`anchorOrderHeld`) and §63.6 (`anchorOrder`), while `transfer(L,L2)` is `ok`. | Both verdicts are proved by the **sequential Spec 1 Lean model**, not by a new concurrent or event rule. No change to the frozen Spec 1 is proposed. |\n\n### Handoff Witnesses — Corpus §§ 2 bis and 2 septies (authority and abstract-event verdicts proved) **[WC5.1 — M4]**\n\nThe following three traces are additions to the WC4 seed corpus. **[WC5.1] Their abstract authority-machine verdicts, originally proposed from the frozen Spec 1 and WC4 candidate rules, have now been proved in the Milestone 3 close-out Lean corpus.** The occurrence of “expected” in the retained row descriptions below records those original reference verdicts, **not** an unproved current status. The separate Milestone 4 **abstract step-event graph** has now established missing `hb` paths, `Race`, and failure of `HandoffOrdered` for the three witnesses, under the tested `Run5R` traces; the strict graph `HBS` preserves those counterexamples. This does not prove any particular concrete byte-level realization. Any later mismatch in a more complete event model must be reported, not silently repaired.\n\n| Witness | Authority-machine sequence and expected verdicts | Positive/negative companion and event-model question |\n|---|---|---|\n| **W-HO** — unjoined return | In `c0`, local `X` is `Init`; `a := derive(root_X, Modify)` → `ok`; `spawn c1` yielding `a` → `ok`; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` → expected `ok`; `c1 : release(a)` → expected `ok` (O3); while `c1` is still live, `c0 : write3(X)` via `root_X` without `join` → expected `ok` (§43/O10). | Negative companion S13: the parent's conflicting write while `a` remains active → `reject`. Positive synchronized companion S13(a): legal `release`, `terminate`, `join`, then parent write → `ok`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** No `hb`/`HBS` path for the conflicting pair; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved in the abstract graph (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n| **W-HO′** — delayed child use after parent's descendant | `c0 : a := derive(root_X, Modify)` → `ok`; `b := derive(a, Modify)` → `ok`; `spawn c1` yielding `a` but retaining `b` in `c0` → **expected `ok`, now validated by Lean**; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` while `b` is active → expected `reject` (§43.3); `c0 : write3(X)` via `b` → expected `ok`; `c0 : release(b)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : write3(X)` via `a` → expected `ok`. | First child write is the negative counterpart of its later allowed write. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The earlier `spawn` does **not** order the parent's later write before the child's newly enabled write. The abstract O3 `release(b)` supplies no `sw`; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n| **W-HO″** — grandchild not joined | `c0` yields `a` to `c1`; `c1` derives and yields `a₂` to `c2`; `c2 : write3(X)` → expected `ok`; `c2 : release(a₂)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : release(a)` → expected `ok`; `c1 : terminate` → expected `ok`, with `c2` live and its initial frame reparented as in C6; `c0 : join(c1)` → expected `ok`; `c0 : write3(X)` via `root_X`, without joining `c2` → expected `ok`. | Negative companion: `c1 : release(a)` **before** `c2 : release(a₂)` → expected `reject` (O3.3). Positive event-model companion requires a genuinely **transitive** completion/handoff protocol, not just `join(c1)`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** No `hb`/`HBS` link orders the grandchild's earlier write before the parent's later write, despite `join(c1)`; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` are proved (`W_HO_all_strict`, `W_HO_all_raceS`). |\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Positive abstract-event counterparts and strict-mutex-edge witnesses (corpus v9 §§ 2 septies–2 octies):**\n\n| Witness | Event-model result (exploratory Lean, not physical realization) |\n|---|---|\n| **W-HO-J** | `c1 : terminate`, then `c0 : join(c1)` before `c0` writes: the conflicting accesses are `HBS`-ordered; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` hold (`W_HOJ_strict`). |\n| **W-HO″-J** | `c2 : terminate`, `c1 : join(c2)`, then `c1 : terminate`, `c0 : join(c1)` before the last write: the complete transitive join chain gives `HBS`, `HandoffOrderedS`, and `¬ RaceS` (`W_HO3J_strict`). |\n| **`lc_trace`** | Protected read by `c1`, `unlock(e)`, later `acquire(e)` by `c0` and protected write: the uninterrupted strict reference-mutex edge gives `HBS`, `HandoffOrderedS`, and `¬ RaceS` (`lc_strict`). |\n| **W-RL₀** | `unlock(e)` followed by a successful next `acquire(e)` without controller intervention: strict abstract edge is present; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_RL0_strict`). |\n| **W-RL** | A free-lock `resetLock(e)` intervenes between `unlock(e)` and the next `acquire(e)`: the **old abstract** edge incorrectly orders the two writes (`W_RL_defect`), but the **strict** graph removes it; `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` (`W_RL_strict`). `resetLock` is kernel-`ok` even when `grant = none`, per C18. |\n| **W-RL-J** | W-RL with `c1 : terminate` and `c0 : join(c1)` before recovery and later write: ordered by the join; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_RLJ_strict`). |\n| **W-TR** | A whole-mutex `transfer(M,M′)` changes `ControlOf(e)` from `lock` to `lock′` between `unlock` and next `acquire`: the **old abstract** edge wrongly orders the writes (`W_TR_defect`), while the strict graph has `RaceS` and `¬ HandoffOrderedS` (`W_TR_strict`). The abstract authority trace is admitted. |\n| **W-TR-J** | W-TR with termination and successful join before the later conflicting write: ordered by the join; `HandoffOrderedS` and `¬ RaceS` (`W_TRJ_strict`). |\n\nThe old graph linked `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` solely by registry index. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The tested strict abstraction suppresses that edge when an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs or `ControlOf(e)` is undefined/changes leaf between unlock completion and the later acquisition. It excludes the two counterexamples; it **does not** prove the concrete controller's required direct `rf` synchronization in C48, nor classify all controller-mutating operations. The internal ordering of events inside compound steps is not part of these proofs.\n\n### Finite Event-Model Witnesses — G1–G19 **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n\nThese are executable **finite-fragment** results, independent of the authority machine, not claims about a complete ERN memory model. Each graph has one atomic location with values and one ordinary leaf used for conflict detection without values. `EventConsistent = RfWF ∧ MoTotal ∧ RmwAtomic ∧ Coherent ∧ HbIrrefl`; `Coherent` checks irreflexivity of `hb ; eco?`. `WellTyped` is checked separately. `sw` uses direct `rf` only; full `seq_cst`, release sequences, and `NoThinAir_RC11` are not modeled.\n\n| Graphs | Verified finite-fragment outcome |\n|---|---|\n| G1, G2 | Release/acquire message passing: G1 has `sw` and ordered ordinary accesses; G2 reads initial `0`, so does not gain `sw`. |\n| G3–G5 | Weakening release and/or acquire removes `sw` and exposes the conflicting ordinary pair as a race. |\n| G6 | `seq_cst` release/acquire roles give the tested `sw`, **without** a global SC proof. |\n| G7–G8 | Mixed `writeShared`/ordinary access can race; an ordinary exclusive write is included in `mo`. |\n| G9–G10 | Two atomic writes do not constitute a data race; two conflicting ordinary writes without `hb` do. |\n| G11–G12 | A valid RMW chain is coherent; two distinct successful RMWs reading the same source are rejected by `RmwAtomic` in both tested `mo` orders. |\n| G13 | A failed CAS is a read-only event, not a modification. |\n| G14–G16 | Lock-shaped acquire/release direct-`rf` synchronization is present for the reference orders, absent when release or acquire is weakened. |\n| G17 | An `acq_rel` RMW directly read by an `acquire` load establishes the tested `sw`. |\n| G18 | The direct-`rf` fragment exhibits a race after an intervening same-thread non-RMW atomic store; `rc11.cat` E1 does not. The fragment has **no release-sequence semantics**, so it does not choose between C++20-form and RC11 extensions. |\n| G19 | `readShared(x, release)` is ill-typed. |\n\nThe generic `hb_trans`, `hb_closure`, and `eco_irrefl` theorems and the backward-edge G1 variant supplement the six general event lemmas. The fragment's finite transitive-closure decision does not presume edges increase with event enumeration. `eco_irrefl` requires `RfWF`, `MoTotal`, and `RmwAtomic` only; no no-thin-air conclusion is drawn.\n\n### Controller Realization and Encapsulation Witnesses **[WC5.2 — M4]**\n\nThe following are **declared-realization** and authority-trace results. They do not independently verify the physical instructions, metadata effects, or the byte-event traces of a compiled implementation.\n\n| Witness | Formal result and boundary |\n|---|---|\n| `lc_trace`, W-RL₀ | The strict `unlock(e)` to later `acquire(e)` edge has direct `rf` and reference release/acquire `sw`; no controller intervention occurs. |\n| W-RL, W-RL-J | An intervening `resetLock(e)` writes `0` under reference `relaxed` order, so the later RMW reads the recovery write rather than the previous `unlock`; the reference lock contributes no `sw`. W-RL-J orders the protected accesses through `join`, separately. |\n| W-TR | Whole-mutex transfer changes `ControlOf`; it refutes the converse `rf ⇒ SWS` **without** `CtlFixed`. The one-controller graph does not constitute a physical transfer realization. |\n| `store_cex`, `issue_cex`, `alloc_cex` | Kernel-`ok` steps on a live controller are excluded by final C22.4; positive destinations outside the controller are admitted. |\n| `alloc(owner = M)`, `depend(ctl, a)` | Eligible under the final C22.4 where no prohibited controller effect occurs; the restriction is narrower than the earlier provisional footprint test. |\n| `alloc(provider = M, owner = ctl′)` | Kernel-`ok` but excluded by C22.4 because provider-metadata writes **may** affect the controller; the metadata write is represented conservatively by the footprint, not executed in the current kernel effect model. |\n| W-RC | Old C18.1–C18.3 admit controller recovery from a frame without controller rights; new C18.4 excludes this step, while the legitimate recovery counterparts remain admitted. |\n| W-NC | Old C13.1–C13.10 admit controller construction from a frame without controller rights; new C13.11 excludes it, with all 16 previously recorded `newMutex` steps satisfying the additional `Modify` test. |\n\n### Full Encapsulation and Abandoned-Grant Witnesses — Corpus v14 **[WC5.3 — M4]**\n\nThe expected verdicts below derive from the inherited model and WC5.2 candidate clauses; the cited Lean results are exploratory checks, **not** independent verification of the implementation or of all physical access events.\n\n| Witness | Kernel and safe-abstraction verdict | Proof result and boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| **W-GI** — `issue` into protected `T` without a grant | Kernel-`ok` and admitted by `RunAmF`; excluded by `RunFull` (`¬ PremG` for destination and internal-store rights). | `W_GI_current`, `W_GI_1_excluded`; the grant-authorized `issue`/`unlock` twin is `RunFull` (`W_GI_twin`). |\n| **W-RX A/B** — recovery of an inner mutex under a live outer mutex | The nested setup and unauthorized inner `resetLock` are kernel-`ok`; `RunFull` excludes A because both the inner `slot` exercise and controller access pass through the outer `guarded`. | `W_RX_setup`, `W_RX_A_excluded`; B acquires the outer grant first and is `RunFull` (`W_RX_B`); `resetLock_rights` establishes the scoped exception. |\n| **W-AG** — abandoned recorded grant, surviving derivative `x` | The eleven authority steps are `RunFull`. The frame exit releases `g` and reanchors `x` to `guarded` without creating an authority. Reacquisition, unlock or reset cannot succeed while `x` is active. After `x` is released, `resetLock` and reacquisition are admissible. | `W_AG_run`, `W_AG_6_facts`, `W_AG_a`–`W_AG_d`; in the **strict abstract event graph**, the two protected writes have no `hb` path (`W_AG_strict`, `RaceS`). This is not a proof of a concrete target race. |\n| **W-AG-J** — recovery after `join` | The sequence is `RunFull`, with the writer terminating and the recovering context joining before `resetLock`. | `W_AGJ_run`, `W_AGJ_strict`: the strict graph orders the two writes by `terminate → join` and has `¬ RaceS`. |\n| **W-AG′** — further derivation and reanchoring after grant abandonment | The survivor `x` legally derives `y`; after `terminate`, `y` survives, becomes a **direct** child of `guarded`, but was already in its transitive descendant set. `resetLock` still has no `ok` outcome while `y` covers a protected leaf. | `W_AGp_run`, `W_AGp_neg`; illustrates the distinction between creating an authority, changing `origin`, and entering the guarded descendant set. |\n\nThe reported complete-encapsulation tests also replay the earlier W-GR/W-GC/W-GS rejected steps and their admitted alternatives, along with S4/S9 and other legitimate traces. `protected_cap_run`, `three_cases`, `closure_run`, `last_acquire`, `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, `spawnVal_cap_pre`, and `usable_run` give general **abstract** results under their explicit `RunFull` reachability assumptions. None discharges C46 for all safe runs or the concrete physical correspondence obligations.\n\nOn reachable initial-state traces, the final footprint proof (`completeness7_run`) classifies all 28 modeled step kinds under the fixed-controller hypothesis. The proof applies to modeled footprints and keeps all physical-access obligations under `OB-C20`. `Run6 ⊆ Run7` is proved on reachable states satisfying `SyncInv`/C21.3, not on arbitrary malformed states. The construction and recovery authority rules are separate conditions of `RunAmF`.\n\nThe concurrent verdicts remain conditional wherever the complete event-order and safe-execution semantics have not yet been defined. The strict abstract graph establishes the reported witness outcomes within its stated scope, but it does not discharge the complete physical event semantics or safe-program obligations.\n\nA proof or successful trace in the authority machine does not by itself establish absence of data races.\n\n---\n\n# VI. Exploratory Verification Requirements\n\nThe candidate is input to the established exploratory formalization process. It is not committed as normative text.\n\n## Milestone 1 — Abstract Model\n\nFormalize the concurrent authority state and the proposed frame, synchronization, and transfer transitions.\n\nEstablish or refute:\n\n- conservation of inherited sequential behavior;\n- preservation of authority and frame invariants;\n- validity of reparenting;\n- preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline`;\n- correctness of `deriveSync` under explicit `K_C` obligations;\n- correctness of the provisional by-value `spawn` composite.\n\nUnproved properties must remain reported as open.\n\n## Milestone 2 — Refinement\n\nEstablish the applicable correspondence to the frozen memory machine and its allocation, byte, ownership, holder, and validity conditions.\n\nReport any property whose realization depends on the deferred atomic event model.\n\n## Milestone 3 — Adversarial Validation\n\nEvaluate S1a–S18 and their positive or negative counterparts, using verdicts grounded in the frozen specifications and explicitly identified candidate rules.\n\nDo not infer expected verdicts from model results.\n\nNo failed obligation may be bypassed by an undisclosed hypothesis or a weakened theorem.\n\n## Milestone 4 — Event Model, Handoff, and Data-Race-Freedom Validation\n\nMilestone 4 remains a **verification plan** for full event semantics and data-race freedom. **[WC5.2 — M4]** Its authority-handoff, abstract strict-step graph, finite `EventConsistent` fragment, conditional reference-mutex controller graph and bounded RC11 tests have produced scoped results, not a full physical event/refinement or safe-program proof. Its inputs are the WC4-derived authority machine, the completed Milestone 3 corpus, the subsequent guarded-chain adversarial witnesses, and the proposed C26–C53 semantics. Any assumption used in a theorem must appear in its statement or declared model scope. No theorem may be represented as universally proved merely because a named `SafeConcurrentTrace` or `EventConsistent` predicate assumes the required result.\n\n**Authority-handoff progress [WC5.1] — exploratory Lean 4, not normative:** W-HO, W-HO′ and W-HO″ authority verdicts were established at the Milestone 3 close-out; W-GR, W-GC and W-GS demonstrate that the prior safe-mutex encapsulation was incomplete. `cap5_drop_own` (28 step kinds, under `DDSound`) and `cap5_compat` are proved. Under the tested direct-access holder-chain restriction, `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` are proved for `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared`, and the stronger `PlaceExcl` restriction inherits them. W-GS is excluded by the tested place-right restriction; legitimate reference mutex cycles, guarded reads under a grant, arena `issue` under its grant, and whole-mutex transfers are admitted in the reported replay corpus. Those earlier authority-handoff results are retained; the **latest** exploratory concurrency audit after the complete-encapsulation complement and `KillKinds` pass reports **2,822 `Ern.Concurrency*` theorems (1,878 non-internal)**, `lake build` successful without warnings, and no axioms beyond `{propext, Quot.sound}`. These are reported tool results, not an independent re-execution in this document.\n\n**Abstract event progress [WC5.1 — M4]:** A step-event graph (one event per authority-machine step, with `start(c)` markers and `terminate(c)` standing in for `complete(c)`) now has formal `po`, abstract `sw`, `hb`, and `Race`; `hb` is irreflexive and decidable. `lossStep_own` and `handoffOrdered_no_race` establish the scoped handoff composition. `spawn_orders` and `join_orders` are proved. The first mutex edge was too permissive for W-RL and W-TR; the added strict graph gives `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`, `spawn_ordersS`, `join_ordersS`, `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps` (conditional), and `reg_stable`. All v9 abstract-event witnesses have expected verdicts. That **strict abstract step graph** contains **no `rf`, `mo`, values, atomic memory orders, or complete physical access mapping**; a separate finite event fragment and controller correspondence model now supply some of those relations without replacing the strict graph.\n\n**RC11 reference-test progress [WC5.1 — M4]:** herdtools7 **7.58**, with unmodified `rc11.cat`, has executed **17 litmus tests** (groups A–E, including the direct-exclusivity D1–D3 series and release-sequence test E1), with expected outcomes. `release`/`acquire` orders the tested ordinary publication; changing either side of the reference lock to `relaxed` permits a race. RMW acquisition exclusivity is independent of memory order in the tested cases; skipping `no-thin-air` enables the forbidden load-buffering outcome. E1 exhibits a reference-model release-sequence behavior not included by the candidate C++20-form extension. These are bounded reference observations, not ERN-model proof.\n\n**Finite event-fragment progress [WC5.2 — M4]:** `EventModel` now defines finite `rf`, `mo`, `po`, direct `sw`, `hb`, `eco` and the five-conjunct `EventConsistent` condition on one atomic unit; both consistency and finite reachability are decidable. Six general lemmas and four complements (including `hb_trans`, `hb_closure`, `eco_irrefl`, and an enumeratively backward synchronization witness) are reported proved. G1–G19 have their stated verdicts. G18 executes a **direct-read-only fragment** race, without implementing either alternative release-sequence definition.\n\n**Reference mutex progress [WC5.2 — M4]:** `ctl_invariant` links the logical recorded grant with the last modeled controller modification; `ctlGraph_coherent` establishes the finite consistency property under its explicit trace assumptions. `bridge` establishes `SWS ⇒ rf`, `bridge_sw_ref` obtains `sw` under the reference release/acquire orders, and `bridge_converse` obtains `rf ⇒ SWS` under `CtlFixed`. The final safe-interface footprint restriction classifies 28 modeled step kinds without a counterexample and proves `completeness7_run` under reachable initial-state and fixed-controller hypotheses; W-RC and W-NC motivate the added authority rules. None of these constructions proves that a real implementation produces the declared `rf`/`mo` history.\n\n**Complete-encapsulation progress [WC5.3 — M4]:** The 28-kind right-premise inventory (`Prem`, `PremG`), `EncapFull`, and `RunFull` extend coverage to `issue`, `resetLock`, `newMutex`, `unlock`, `newArena`, composite phases, and recorded `resetLock` exceptions. Reported `premG_prem`, `prem_of_step`, and `runFull_runAmF` prove the relevant model correspondence, and `resetLock_rights` verifies the exception. `capacity_run` and `spawnVal_cap_pre` cover abstract touched-leaf capacities; `protected_not_dying_run`, `other_cap_dying_run`, and `usable_run` establish the lifetime properties, including the exhaustive four-kind unusability classification. `closure_run`, `empty_without_grant`, `last_acquire`, `three_cases`, and `protected_cap_run` establish provenance after normal acquisition and abandoned grants without historical-origin state. W-GI/W-RX are excluded, W-AG/W-AG-J/W-AG′ have their expected authority and strict-event results, and existing positive traces remain admitted. The report checks 46 `RunFull` traces (33 earlier and 13 replayed). All these are from the model's initial state with `DDSound` and `StaticsOK`, not universally from arbitrary well-formed states or real target events.\n\n**Still open [WC5.3 — M4]:** actual safe-interface enforcement and the residual `OB-C28` model gaps (§78.1 `MetaRight` of `reset` and inventory points verified only by inspection); physical byte and within-step marker correspondence (`OB-C20`); all-context ordering of the non-atomic `newMutex` initialization; actual target `rf`/`mo` and full `LockOrder` refinement (`OB-C05`); failed-CAS retries and controller relocation; additional atomic units, global `seq_cst`, release sequences and the no-thin-air choice; complete DRF and safe-program soundness. `HandoffOrderedS` has been demonstrated on **seven positive traces**, but has not been established for every safe program (`OB-C24`).\n\n**Lean 4 proof program**\n\n1. **Adversarial baseline [WC5.1]:** W-HO, W-HO′ and W-HO″ authority-machine verdicts, including the W-HO′ `spawn` and relevant reparenting/negative checks, were established in the Milestone 3 close-out. Keep those proofs and the guarded-chain corpus as the abstract baseline. Event-graph counterexamples for W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″ and the positive W-HO-J/W-HO″-J witnesses are now proved at the **abstract strict-graph** level (`Corpus/HandoffEvents`, `Corpus/MutexEdgeStrict`); concrete-event checks remain open.\n2. **Event graph construction [WC5.2 — M4, scoped]:** the abstract single-event-per-step lifecycle graph remains proved within its declared scope. Separately, the **finite single-atomic-unit fragment** now defines `rf`, `mo`, direct `sw`, `hb`, `eco` and a nonvacuous, five-conjunct `EventConsistent` with initial values, typed atomic events, successful RMWs and read-only failed comparisons. **Remaining:** integrate the two graphs and define the actual event mapping of every included `SimRunC` step, including cumulative `acc`/`sync`, `start(c)` and a distinct `complete(c)`, within-step order, byte footprints, additional atomic units, and full permitted executions.\n3. **Synchronization [WC5.2 — M4]:** `spawn_ordersS` and `join_ordersS` prove scoped lifecycle `hb` consequences in the abstract graph. W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″ lack the relevant `HBS` and fail C46; their synchronized companions have strict `HBS` paths. The finite `EventModel` proves direct-`rf` release/acquire `sw` for its graph, and the constructed controller bridge proves the reference lock `sw` under its declared realization. **Remaining:** complete lifecycle-event integration, actual physical atomic observations and implementation correspondence (`OB-C05`).\n4. **Authority handoff [WC5.1]:** retain the already proved `cap5_drop_own` (28 step kinds), `cap5_compat`, `cap5_sound_C` and `handoff_loss_exists_C` with their exact hypotheses and **`read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` scope**. Retain the completed `RunFull`/`PremG` modeled C22.6 premise checks while proving actual safe-interface enforcement and closing the §78.1/inventory gaps (`OB-C28`); extend abstract access proofs to physically realized operations (`OB-C20`); `lossStep_own` now establishes the strictly intervening, own-context loss step and `po(e1,r)` in the one-event-per-step graph; `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` proves the scoped composition if C46 ordering is supplied. Physical marker placement and protocol-wide C46 establishment remain open. Context-exclusive handoff for ordinary leaves and D-C9 mixed atomic/ordinary pairs remains an event/interface obligation. Do not confuse R4 simultaneous non-conflict with event ordering.\n5. **Mutex and `join` [WC5.3 — M4]:** abstract `join_ordersS` and conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps` are retained; W-RL and W-TR refute the overbroad mutex edge. The **constructed** controller history proves the conditional strict-edge `SWS ⇒ rf ⇒ sw` bridge, and the converse under `CtlFixed`, with the final 28-kind footprint classification and authority checks of C13.11/C18.4. The newer `PremG`/`RunFull` results prove **modeled C22.6 right-premise coverage** and the scoped `resetLock` exception, with `runFull_inv` preserving the inherited invariants under its stated reachable-trace hypotheses. **Still to prove:** reference soundness of the modified C16–C18 *outcome classification*, actual safe-library enforcement of the C22.1–6 restrictions, the residual §78.1/inventory points in `OB-C28`, and a dedicated witness where ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` is kernel-`ok` but excluded by `Encap` from the safe interface. These are not discharged by the finite event/bridge proofs. Physical `LockOrder` from **actual** target `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, failed-CAS events, abandoned-grant realization and physical correspondence remain independent open obligations.\n6. **Shared disciplines and data races:** formalize `Atomic`, `Synchronized`, and transferrable `Confined`, D-C2–D-C5, D-C7, D-C9, and C51. Prove the case-partitioned DRF composition theorem only to the strength justified by its hypotheses, with generic `Synchronized` obligations flagged as assumptions until discharged by specific protocols.\n7. **Conservativity and refinement:** restate and prove T1 in view of O11.3's missing Lean representation; independently prove pure-marker erasure, concrete-byte coverage and the relation to R1–R3, R4′, `LayoutWF`, and Spec 1 §82. If the model cannot justify an `issue`, move, physical controller access, or a byte operation after invalidation, report the missing premise.\n8. **Parametric consistency [WC5.2 — M4]:** herdtools7 A4 experimentally isolates the RC11 `no-thin-air` condition. The **abstract strict-step graph** has no `rf`; the separate finite Lean fragment has `rf` but does **not** adopt `NoThinAir_RC11`, nor does the five-conjunct consistency predicate prove no-thin-air. Retain the rule as a **named optional hypothesis** for future complete models and report any theorem dependencies. Release sequences and full global `seq_cst` remain open. Record the theorem-assumption/axiom audit and any counterexamples without weakening failed statements.\n\n**herdtools7 comparison program**\n\n- **[WC5.1 — M4] Partially exercised under RC11 reference:** 17 litmus programs cover load buffering (A4), message passing (A1–A3), reference lock RMW and CAS (B1–B6), RMW exclusivity (D1–D3), and mixed atomic/non-atomic conflicts (C1–C3), with the release-sequence case E1. Still open: store buffering, failed CAS variants, RMW-extended release sequences, fences, `acq_rel`, and target-specific execution.\n- **[WC5.1 — M4] Partial comparison only:** reference `rc11.cat` rejects the load-buffering outcome through `no-thin-air` (A4), and its same-thread non-RMW atomic-store release-sequence extension (E1) differs from the proposed C++20-form extension in C48. **[WC5.2 — M4]** G18 has now been executed in an ERN **direct-`rf`-only finite fragment** and is racy there; no ERN model implementing the C++20-form release sequence has been executed. The distinct C++20-form prediction remains analytical, and neither inclusion nor equivalence with RC11 is proved. Complete RC11-to-ERN inclusion/equivalence and full global SC comparisons remain open.\n- For W-HO, W-HO′, and W-HO″, use a concrete mapping of authority effects to physical synchronization before interpreting any litmus outcome. Pure authority markers do not turn into hardware synchronization by themselves.\n- Examine target lowering on x86-TSO and weakly ordered targets including ARM/POWER where relevant. The effect and possible cost of a no-thin-air rule must be justified by an appropriate compilation scheme and measurement, not guessed from static type information.\n\n**Milestone-4 evidence required:** named definitions and theorem statements, proof-module references, explicit admissibility assumptions, witness outcomes and positive counterparts, nonvacuity checks, any unresolved physical-model obligations, and an independent audit of axioms and prohibited proof shortcuts. Completing this plan is not itself sufficient to freeze Spec 2: sound safe-language exposure (Specs 3/4), physical refinement, and deferred memory-model decisions remain separate obligations.\n\n---\n\n# VII. Concurrent Event Model — Proposed Block C\n\nThe clauses C26–C53 below are **working proposals**, not amendments to the frozen Memory Model. They provide the candidate C/D extension to C1–C25 and are not claims that the corresponding Lean obligations have already been discharged. Their labels refer to the working decisions D-C1–D-C10 in the WC5 framing document.\n\n## C26. Executions, Abstract Steps, and Event Graphs\n\nA **candidate concurrent execution** consists of:","numLines":240,"startLine":538,"totalLines":1202}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"85321898-696a-499c-a49f-da57a466b0b3","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
