{"parentUuid":"a5c3efc6-b3ea-4da3-a5bb-877ae5fc9b00","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XPrZE4J4f8VspVPNvaURY2","type":"tool_result","content":"1026\t# X. Inter-Context Authority Handoff — Proposed Block D\n1027\t\n1028\t## C43. Context Capability and Current-Frame Exercise **[WC5.1]**\n1029\t\n1030\tDefine, provisionally, for state `s`, execution context `c`, and logical leaf `l`:\n1031\t\n1032\t```\n1033\tcap(s, c, l) ∈ { none, Observe, Modify }\n1034\t```\n1035\t\n1036\tFor the safe-handoff proof, it is the greatest **effective** mode on `l` attributable to `c` through active authorities held by its live frames, **including suspended frames**, or held in a place through a valid holder chain, **provided the attributing chain does not pass through the `guarded` authority of any live synchronization entry** **[WC5.1]**. This guarded-chain-excluding capability is `cap5` in the exploratory Lean model. Its `Attr5` attribution relation deliberately treats live `guarded` authorities as unavailable for attribution but permits a valid frame-held grant and its legally held descendants. It does not change the rights of any authority in the frozen Spec 1.\n1037\t\n1038\t`cap` is **not** `ExercisableAt`. The latter applies the frozen §43 rules to the executing context's **current frame** and is used to decide **kernel** operation preconditions; C22.6 separately restricts which successful kernel operations the **safe interface** may expose. `cap` summarizes guarded-chain-excluding contextual entitlement across the context's frames and relevant held places, including rights not currently exercisable from the top frame. Derivation, reborrowing and frame changes need not terminate a context's `cap` merely because an individual authority becomes inactive or non-exercisable. **[WC5.1]** The unrestricted contextual capability (`cap` in the earlier Lean exploration) is distinguished here as **raw contextual capability**, not the quantity used by L1/L2: W-GR refutes raw capability-loss locality.\n1039\t\n1040\tFor two distinct contexts, present `cap` values on a leaf are **incompatible** when at least one is `Modify`; for `Confined`, any two present capabilities are incompatible under the additional safe-storage contract. This is a proposed abstraction for L1/L2, not a new sequential mode or authority identity.\n1041\t\n1042\t## C44. Scope of Exclusive Handoff Ordering\n1043\t\n1044\tLet `e1` and `e2` be access events in **different contexts**, associated with the same logical leaf and conflicting byte ranges, where at least one is non-atomic. `HandoffOrdered` applies only if the pair is **exclusive**: its relevant context capabilities are incompatible under C43 and the declared storage discipline. It does not impose `hb` between two genuinely concurrent atomic accesses under compatible observation rights.\n1045\t\n1046\tTypical cases in scope are:\n1047\t\n1048\t1. accesses to ordinary non-`SharedMutable` storage, when a conflict necessarily includes an exclusive ordinary `write3`;\n1049\t2. a mixed atomic/non-atomic pair on `Atomic` storage, because C42 requires exclusive `Modify` for the ordinary member;\n1050\t3. conflicting accesses under `Confined`, because the safe-storage contract excludes simultaneous cross-context capacities;\n1051\t4. exclusive ordinary access pairs under `Synchronized`.\n1052\t\n1053\tConflicting **non-atomic** `writeShared` operations on `Synchronized` storage performed under shared compatible `Observe` rights are **not** covered by the exclusive-handoff premise. Their ordering is established by the particular `Synchronized` protocol, not by claiming that the shared rights are exclusive.\n1054\t\n1055\tThe scope is deliberately narrower than the class of all conflicts, and wider than just final restitution of a spawn parameter. It includes a capacity becoming available to a child **after** its `spawn`, and multi-context relays.\n1056\t\n1057\t## C45. Loss Marker, L1 and L2 — Scoped Authority Proofs and Open Event Obligations **[WC5.1]**\n1058\t\n1059\tFor a pair in the scope of C44, write `e1` for the earlier access in the abstract trace, issued by `c′`, and `e2` for the subsequent conflicting access by `c ≠ c′`. Let `r(e1,e2,l)` be the **loss marker** that ends the period containing `e1` during which `cap(·,c′,l)` is incompatible with the capability used by `e2`.\n1060\t\n1061\tThis marker may denote a release, a parameter yield, a derivation that reduces the relevant effective mode, frame exit, termination, unlock, or a justified holder/ownership change. It is a **pure logical marker** if its effect is only an authority change; a concrete step can additionally emit physical memory or synchronization events. The required loss threshold depends on the later access: a later exclusive write requires no incompatible surviving capability in `c′`; a later shared `Observe` read may be permitted after `c′` has lost its `Modify` exclusivity while retaining compatible `Observe`.\n1062\t\n1063\t**[WC5.1 — M4] Exploratory proof status (authority machine **and scoped abstract step-event model**, not a complete physical-event proof):**\n1064\t\n1065\t- **L1 — local capability loss:** `cap5_drop_own` is proved under `DDSound` for all **28 modeled kinds of authority-machine steps**, over reachable encapsulated traces: any strict reduction of a context's guarded-chain-excluding `cap5` originates in that context's own step. For an eligible direct-access exclusive pair, `lossStep_own` further proves a first strictly intermediate loss step `r` of the earlier context. In the one-event-per-step abstraction this gives `po(e1,r)`; placement among physical events inside a realized compound step remains open.\n1066\t- **L2 — state compatibility:** `cap5_compat` is proved: distinct contexts cannot simultaneously have incompatible `cap5` on the same leaf under the ordinary `Modify` exclusivity criterion. This is an authority-state theorem, not an ordering theorem.\n1067\t- **L2 — trace loss:** `handoff_loss_exists_C` is proved for conflicting **`read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`** step-access pairs, at least one requiring `Modify`, over `EncapsulatedTrace5C` (the direct-exercise `ChainExcl` restriction, together with existing encapsulation and controller restrictions). It identifies a strictly intervening step of the earlier context reducing `cap5` on the leaf. The result transfers to the stronger tested `EncapsulatedTrace5R` by trace inclusion. Without this chain restriction, `handoff_loss_exists` is **refuted** under `EncapsulatedTrace5` by W-GC; `cap5_sound` is likewise refuted there. `cap5_sound_C` is proved under `EncapsulatedTrace5C`.\n1068\t\n1069\tThese are scoped results from the exploratory Lean formalization, **not** claims that C22.6 has been encoded for every operation or that the complete data-race-freedom theorem has been established. **[WC5.1 — M4]** `handoffOrdered_no_race` and its strict-graph analogue `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` prove that, given `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`, eligible exclusive direct-access pairs are race-free in the corresponding abstract graph. They do **not** prove that arbitrary safe executions always satisfy C46. Full step-to-physical-event correspondence, access coverage beyond these three named primitives, physical `po` mapping, and actual protocol `rf`/`hb` remain obligations.\n1070\t\n1071\t**[WC5.1 — M4]** In the one-event-per-step abstraction, `lossStep_own` identifies the first relevant `r` and establishes its own-context, strictly intermediate position and hence the step-level `po` order. The exact **physical** marker position inside a realized compound step, partial byte footprints, and the concrete `po`/`rf`/`sw`/`hb` mapping remain open. A kernel-admitted trace outside C22.6 may refute the safe handoff premises without changing any frozen rule; such counterexamples must be retained and reported rather than concealed by hypotheses.\n1072\t\n1073\t## C46. HandoffOrdered — D-C10 **[WC5.1]**\n1074\t\n1075\tFor a pair within C44's scope, let `r(e1,e2,l)` denote the loss marker identified by the provisional capacity-period construction in C45. The **sole ordering requirement of `HandoffOrdered`** is:\n1076\t\n1077\t\\[\n1078\t  HandoffOrdered(e1,e2,l) \\;:\\!\\iff\\; hb(r(e1,e2,l),e2).\n1079\t\\]\n1080\t\n1081\t**[WC5.1]** The **authority-step parts** of L1 and L2 now have the restricted Lean proofs stated in C45 for `cap5` and the `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` access fragment under guarded-chain exclusion. These proofs are **not additional preconditions** of `HandoffOrdered`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The later `lossStep_own` proof establishes `po(e1,r)` for the scoped abstract one-event-per-step pairs; no **physical** `po` placement or concrete `hb` edge follows merely from authority proof. If the authority machine or concrete realization fails to provide a marker for a relevant pair, that is an unfulfilled proof obligation or counterexample, not permission to assume a hidden marker. The existence of **some** unrelated `sw` event is insufficient. When the event-model mapping has also established `po(e1,r)`, the protocol requirement `hb(r,e2)` implies `hb(e1,e2)` by transitivity. This is a **composition argument**, now proved for the abstract direct-access graph (`handoffOrdered_no_race`, `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`) under its stated run assumptions: C46 still specifies the ordering each admissible handoff protocol must justify, not a claim that R4 or an authority-only trace establishes physical `hb`. The requirement itself is unchanged.\n1082\t\n1083\tThe requirement applies to both **transmission** and **restitution** of authority. A bare inherited O3 `release` may restore an origin's effective right in the globally coherent abstract state, but it generates **no** `sw` by itself. The resulting trace can be kernel-`ok` and nevertheless be excluded from `SafeConcurrentTrace` if a later cross-context access lacks the required order.\n1084\t\n1085\t`join` is one reference protocol, not the only legal completion/handoff mechanism (C8). Other mechanisms may be added if they prove the requisite `hb` relation without introducing a new ambient authority source or weakening inherited invariants. Safe-language constructs that ensure these obligations belong to Specs 3/4.\n1086\t\n1087\t---\n1088\t\n1089\t# XI. Event Realization of Reference Protocols — Proposed Block D\n1090\t\n1091\t## C47. Spawn, Completion, and Join Events\n1092\t\n1093\tFor the reference by-reference `spawn` protocol, the creator's authority-yield **loss marker** precedes its `spawn` synchronization event within the parent context; the latter `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` marker. The initial child frame and parameter holder changes still obey C4/C5, and neither the event protocol nor `start(c)` duplicates an authority. A parent's operation executed after `spawn` is not ordered before a child's operation merely because the parent was the creator.\n1094\t\n1095\tFor `terminate`, every earlier event of the terminating context is `po`-before `complete(c)`; a successful `join(c)` in another context is the target of `sw` from that `complete(c)` event. The `join` event is placed in the caller's `po` before subsequent operations. By transitivity, a context's accesses preceding its completion are `hb`-before the caller's accesses following its successful join. An unsuccessful or non-enabled `join` contributes no such completed-observation edge.\n1096\t\n1097\tThe reference safe passing discipline may delay a creator's re-use of transferred exclusive capacity until appropriate completion observation, but C8 does **not** impose this restriction on all possible kernel traces or require universal structured concurrency. For a chain of delegated authorities, a successful join of an intermediate context does not imply completion of a surviving descendant. Correct reference reuse may require **transitive** joining or an equivalent ordering protocol.\n1098\t\n1099\tFor by-value `spawn`, access events emitted by the transfers belong to the actual producing context/phase, and their order must preserve C5's create–transfer–yield structure. Whether those byte accesses are correctly ordered with all other physical uses is part of the separate realization and safe-execution proofs, not a consequence of the logical transfer alone.\n1100\t\n1101\t## C48. Reference Mutex: Acquire, Unlock, and Grant Events\n1102\t\n1103\tThe reference `newMutex` initializes a physical controller in a state corresponding to `grant = none` and must establish the positional, identity and initialization requirements of C12–C13, C21 and Spec 1 §56. As clarified in C13, this is a physical non-atomic write of the controller synchronization value to an **already abstractly `Init`** leaf, not the abstract initialization of an `Uninit` structural leaf. C13.11 requires ordinary `Modify` authority, with applicable O10.1/O10.2 conditions. Its concrete metadata operations have events as required by C27/C29.\n1104\t\n1105\tA successful `acquire` has an atomic controller-acquisition event, for example a successful CAS/RMW with an acquire-capable memory order. This event must observe (`rf`) the appropriate released/available controller state, satisfy atomic coherence, and linearize the reservation of the controller before the new grant is made externally exercisable. A **gain marker** is placed `po`-after the successful acquire event. A failed CAS does not grant authority; a busy controller and an invalid request remain classified under C16, with `reject` taking priority per abstract state.\n1106\t\n1107\tAn `unlock` has a logical **loss marker** for the released grant, followed in `po` by a corresponding atomic release publication of the controller's available state. The abstract library transition remains coherent and does not expose an intermediate invalid grant/controller pairing. When a later acquiring RMW **directly reads** this release modification (`rf`), the protocol yields the requisite `sw` and thus `hb` from the preceding protected critical section to the succeeding one.\n1108\t\n1109\tThe event-level atomicity of this logical transition, the absence of an observable partial state, `SyncInv`/`GrantDiscipline` preservation and the correspondence to the concrete controller are still obligations of `OB-C05` and C25. C48 proposes the required event behavior; it does **not** claim these obligations have been discharged.\n1110\t\n1111\tThe initial Lean fragment limits the mutex `sw` and `LockOrder` proof to the **direct `rf`** pattern. Protocols with intervening RMWs or release sequences require an extension. When such sequences are added, the candidate uses the C++20 form in which intervening RMWs, but not ordinary same-thread stores, extend a release sequence. **[WC5.1 — M4] Reference comparison performed:** herdtools7 7.58 E1, with the unmodified `rc11.cat`, admits a release sequence extended by a **same-thread atomic non-RMW** `store(f, 2, relaxed)` following `store(f, 1, release)`. A later `acquire` reading the `2` is synchronized with the earlier `release` in that reference; the tested ordinary data read has no race. The candidate C++20-form rule stated here **does not** extend that release sequence through a non-RMW atomic store, so the analogous race has now been **observed by executing G18 in the finite ERN direct-`rf` fragment**, but **not** by executing an ERN implementation of the C++20-form release-sequence rule. Because G18 has no release-sequence semantics at all, it does not decide whether that candidate extension or the `rc11.cat` extension is correct for ERN. This is a documented comparative divergence, not a new normative selection or evidence of an ERN compiler realization.\n1112\t\n1113\t**[WC5.2 — M4 reference controller evidence]** Under the declared model (`newMutex`: non-atomic initial `0` write; successful `acquire`: CAS/RMW reading `0` and writing `1`; `unlock`: atomic `0` release; `resetLock`: atomic `0` relaxed), with `mo` constructed from protocol steps and every successful RMW reading its `mo` predecessor, `ctl_invariant` and `ctlGraph_coherent` hold for the modeled traces. The `bridge` theorem proves that the strict abstract unlock-to-acquire edge induces a direct `rf` from that unlock to the acquisition; `bridge_sw_ref` gives `sw` under reference release/acquire orders. Conversely `bridge_converse` recovers the strict edge from direct `rf` under `CtlFixed`, and W-TR refutes that converse without controller stability. This proof is **conditional on the constructed event graph and declared realization**; it does not prove that compiled controller operations have that history. Successful CAS attempts are modeled; failed attempts, actual byte metadata and internal gain/loss marker events remain open.\n1114\t\n1115\t## C49. LockOrder, Recovery, and Delegation\n1116\t\n1117\t**`LockOrder`** is the obligation that the protected accesses in an earlier critical section of one mutex are `hb`-before conflicting protected accesses in a later critical section whose lock-acquisition event receives the preceding release. The proof must use the concrete `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, and grant-lifecycle relations, not merely the total order of abstract `SyncEntry` updates or the fact that controller identities match.\n1118\t\n1119\tFor a successful `resetLock`, the library must prove exclusive recovery authority under C18, a coherent physical controller update, and the event-order consequences of any protected memory that becomes newly accessible. Recovery is **not automatically** a substitute for an acquire/release edge missing from an abandoned grant. A proposed recovery protocol that cannot establish necessary ordering is not justified for safe concurrent access. **[WC5.1 — M4]** C18 imposes no precondition on the recorded grant state when `resetLock(e)` executes: the recorded grant may be `none`. The exploratory kernel admits `resetLock` on an already free lock, as witnessed by W-RL. This does **not** introduce any new C18 precondition or imply a new `sw` edge.\n1120\t\n1121\tWhen a grant is reborrowed, passed to another frame, or transmitted between contexts, the reference implementation must show how ordinary authority rules, grant constraints, and the relevant handoff protocol preserve `hb` across the whole path. Exiting a frame that released a grant without `unlock` does not imply controller release, and a logically returned capability does not imply a physical publication.\n1122\t\n1123\t**[WC5.1 — M4] Strict abstract mutex-edge evidence:** The first experimental graph connected `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` by entry identity alone. W-RL (`resetLock(e)` between them) and W-TR (whole-mutex `transfer` changing `ControlOf(e)`) prove that this approximation can invent `hb`. The corrected **abstract** edge is absent if an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs, or if `ControlOf(e)` becomes undefined or changes leaf from the state after `unlock` through the state before `acquire`. The strict graph excludes W-RL/W-TR, preserves W-RL₀/W-RL-J/W-TR-J and the earlier reference witnesses, and proves conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps`. **Any abstraction of the C48 mutex `sw` edge must respect interventions on the controller; the definitive physical rule remains the unchanged direct-`rf` condition on the same atomic unit in C32/C48.** **[WC5.2 — M4]** For the **constructed reference controller graph**, `bridge` now proves the direct-read implication; `completeness7_run` classifies the modeled controller footprints for `Run7` under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. The two strict abstract guards still do **not** prove that a target physical acquisition reads the earlier release (`OB-C05`), or that all compiled metadata and composite-step accesses are covered (`OB-C20`).\n1124\t\n1125\t## C50. Realization, Visibility, and Physical Correspondence\n1126\t\n1127\tThe implementation must relate every relevant successful `S_C` operation and its associated event graph to a concrete memory execution under the frozen Spec 1 §82 representation obligations. For `Atomic` storage this includes unit indivisibility, alignment, coherent `rf`/`mo`, and the compilation of its memory orders. For ordinary, `Synchronized`, and `Confined` storage it includes byte-level access coverage, validity, exclusivity and the required event ordering.\n1128\t\n1129\tThis obligation is **stronger** than establishing a sequence of abstract `ok` outcomes. It includes preservation of actual initialized bytes, pointer provenance, invalidation conditions, owners and holders, and physically executable synchronization operations. In particular, no successful abstraction proof may conceal an unmodeled physical access after invalidation (§82.7 / `OB-C12`).\n1130\t\n1131\tClaims about compilation to x86-TSO, ARM, or POWER, including the potential cost of a proposed no-thin-air axiom, must be supported by a target-specific compilation or refinement argument. The initial event fragment is not itself such an argument.\n1132\t\n1133\t---\n1134\t\n1135\t# XII. Safe Executions and Data-Race Freedom — Proposed Block D\n1136\t\n1137\t## C51. EncapsulatedTrace and SafeConcurrentTrace **[WC5.1]**\n1138\t\n1139\tTwo **distinct** restrictions apply to completed traces:\n1140\t\n1141\t1. `EncapsulatedTrace`: the safe **reference-mutex** trace contract of C22, including the WC5 D-C6 restriction against ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` and **[WC5.1]** the C22.6 restriction on all right-justification holder chains traversing a live `guarded` (subject to the specific C18.1 `resetLock` exception). This restricts the **public safe interface**, not O9/O10 or any inherited kernel operation. A kernel-`ok` `read3(lock)` or a kernel-`ok` access through live `guarded` without the required grant can be outside this contract without being a counterexample to Spec 1. The earlier `Encap5R` checks only a subset of C22.6; the later `EncapFull`/`RunFull` exploratory model covers the 28 modeled step kinds and their inventoried right premises, while `OB-C28` retains safe-interface enforcement and the stated modeling/exhaustiveness gaps.\n1142\t2. `SafeConcurrentTrace`: a proposed subset of `EncapsulatedTrace` in which (a) all relevant event-graph consistency conditions hold; (b) `Atomic` accesses obey C37–C42; (c) `Confined` has at most one context with a present **guarded-chain-excluding** capability in each state and any later cross-context conflict satisfies C46; (d) every non-atomic conflict under a `Synchronized` contract is either protected by a proven exclusive handoff or ordered by an established synchronization mechanism; and (e) all applicable `HandoffOrdered`, lifecycle, and `LockOrder` obligations are fulfilled. **[WC5.1]** Its safe-interface premise includes all of C22.6, not just the currently encoded `ChainExcl`/`PlaceExcl` subset; proof of target interface soundness and physical access coverage remains open, notwithstanding the modeled `RunFull` premise-coverage results.\n1143\t\n1144\t`SafeConcurrentTrace` is a **semantic safe-execution contract**. It is not the set of all kernel-`ok` traces. The earlier witness `read3(lock)` concurrent with `writeShared(lock)` demonstrates why `EncapsulatedTrace` in WC4 was not enough; under C42/C22 it is excluded from the candidate safe interface while the ordinary kernel operation remains available.\n1145\t\n1146\tThe statement “a safe, well-typed ERN program generates only `SafeConcurrentTrace` executions” is a **separate soundness obligation** of Specs 3/4 (and the relevant safe libraries), not a theorem derived merely from a name or by construction of this predicate.\n1147\t\n1148\t## C52. Candidate Data-Race-Freedom Theorem and Proof Partition **[WC5.1]**\n1149\t\n1150\t**Target theorem (open, not yet proved).** Every execution graph satisfying the stated `EventConsistent` obligations and arising from a `SafeConcurrentTrace` is free from data races as defined in C33 and frozen Spec 1 §18.\n1151\t\n1152\tThe proof is deliberately partitioned by **conflicting access pair**, not just by storage label:\n1153\t\n1154\t| Pair or discipline | Intended argument | Independent proof boundary |\n1155\t|---|---|---|\n1156\t| Ordinary non-`SharedMutable` leaves | Conflicts require exclusive ordinary mutation. Show the loss marker exists (L2) and follows the prior access in `po` (L1); a certified handoff provides `hb(r,e2)` and therefore `hb(e1,e2)`. | **[WC5.1 — M4]** `lossStep_own` and `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` now prove the abstract direct-access composition conditional on C46. Proving C46 for all safe executions and concrete physical events remains open; R4 alone is insufficient. |\n1157\t| `Atomic`, atomic/atomic | Both accesses are atomic, so the non-atomic conjunct of the Spec 1 §18 race definition is false. | Atomicity of entire unit and `rf`/`mo` coherence must still be established. |\n1158\t| `Atomic`, mixed or non-atomic/non-atomic | C42 restricts every ordinary member to effective/exercisable `Modify`, giving abstract exclusivity, then uses certified handoff ordering for accesses of distinct contexts. | D-C9 enforcement, L1/L2, C46, concrete access coverage. |\n1159\t| `Confined` | At most one context has an attributable capability at a time; cross-context conflicting accesses require an ordered handoff. | Confinement contract and L1/L2/C46; exclusivity alone does not imply `hb`. |\n1160\t| `Synchronized` with exclusive ordinary writer | Use the ordinary exclusive-handoff argument. | L1/L2/C46 and proof of the particular protocol. |\n1161\t| `Synchronized` under shared compatible observation | The generic storage contract requires conflicting non-atomic accesses to be sufficiently `hb`-ordered. | **Composition case**: the requirement is an assumption until each declared mechanism proves its actual `sw` and `hb` edges. |\n1162\t\n1163\tThe generic `Synchronized` case is **not presented as a non-circular proof**. Likewise, assuming `HandoffOrdered` in a generic trace establishes only a composition theorem. **[WC5.1]** The former L1/L2 proof boundaries in the table now have **scoped authority-machine proofs** (`cap5_drop_own`, `cap5_compat`, `cap5_sound_C`, `handoff_loss_exists_C`) for directly modeled `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` accesses under the tested chain restriction. They are **not** proofs for physical accesses generated by `store`, `transfer`, `issue`, allocation, or other compound operations; those require `OB-C20` and safe-interface enforcement, notwithstanding the expanded `RunFull` abstract premise proofs under `OB-C28`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The abstract graph now proves the first own-context loss marker with step-level `po` (`lossStep_own`), the **conditional** composition `HandoffOrderedS → ¬ RaceS` (`handoffOrdered_no_raceS`), negative missing-`HBS` results for W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″, and positive ordered counterparts W-HO-J/W-HO″-J/`lc_trace`. W-RL/W-TR show why the old mutex edge was overbroad; their strict-graph failures and join-based positive companions are proved. These are scoped **one-event-per-step** results, not concrete data-race-freedom for all realizations. The substantial remaining work is physical marker placement/byte-event coverage, concrete `rf`/`mo`/`sw` and controller `LockOrder`, safe-program/interface soundness, and proving C46 for every safe execution.\n1164\t\n1165\tA reference-mutex theorem may conclude DRF for protected ordinary accesses only after the concrete controller protocol supplies `LockOrder` and the requisite `rf`/`sw` links. A `.cat` model that simply discards racy graphs does not prove that safe ERN programs could not generate such graphs before that filter.\n1166\t\n1167\t## C53. Proof Boundary, Conservativity, and No Freeze\n1168\t\n1169\tThe following obligations remain separate and **unproved unless supported by named proofs**:\n1170\t\n1171\t1. **Inherited-operation conservativity:** the unchanged Spec 1 O1–O16 must retain their projected kernel meaning. Because the exploratory Spec 1 Lean `WriteSharedPre` does not explicitly represent O11.3's shared-discipline predicate, T1 must be **restated and proved** for `K_C.SharedDiscipline`: every concurrency-`ok` inherited write projects to a Spec 1 `ok` under its valid `K`, with equality of relevant admissible sets only under explicitly established matching/accept-all discipline assumptions. No equality is asserted without proof.\n1172\t2. **Authority lemmas:** L1, L2, and any preservation properties required by the chosen definition of `cap` and holder attribution.\n1173\t3. **Graph and realization lemmas:** pure-authority erasure, coverage of physical accesses, full `EventConsistent` coherence beyond the finite one-unit fragment, **target** direct-`rf` protocol correctness beyond the conditional constructed-controller bridge, and reconstruction/preservation under Spec 1 §82.\n1174\t4. **Program/interface soundness:** safe interfaces exclude kernel-admissible operations outside C22/C42; the typing and library rules of Specs 3/4 prove that safe programs yield `SafeConcurrentTrace`.\n1175\t5. **Full memory-model selection:** no-thin-air, release sequences, global `seq_cst` semantics, cross-target compilation, and the proven relationship (inclusion or equivalence, if appropriate) of the final ERN model to any reference model.\n1176\t\n1177\tThe baseline comparison is **three-level**: complete external RC11 reference; the explicitly scoped exploratory ERN-C/D Lean fragment; and the eventual normative ERN model. No equivalence is implied by similar names or successful litmus tests. The working decisions D-C1–D-C10 remain proposals pending consolidation and proof, and no clause of the frozen Charter or Spec 1 v0.1 is changed here.\n1178\t\n1179\t---\n1180\t\n1181\t# Drafting choices\n1182\t\n1183\tThese choices resolve ambiguities for this **non-normative working draft**; they are not amendments to the frozen Charter or Spec 1, not approved D-C decisions, and not substitutes for the proof obligations above.\n1184\t\n1185\t1. **Preservation of WC4 [WC5.3]:** The following clauses are textually identical to WC4: C1–C12, C14–C15, C19–C20, C23 and C24. C25 differs from WC4 only in the corrected reference to C22.1–6. The original seven numbered `SyncInv` invariants of C21 are unchanged; explanatory material follows C21.5 without adding an invariant. C13 retains the accepted item 11 and controller-initialization clarification; C18 retains the accepted item 4 and leaves C18.1–C18.3, outcomes, and effects unchanged; C22 retains the accepted item 4 and the earlier item 6, and clarifies item 1 without narrowing its ban on **any** ordinary authority issued directly from `guarded`, including by compound steps. Items C22.2–3 and C22.5–6 retain their rule text. C16–C18 remain marked **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]** as earlier candidate material. The WC4 Part V seed table, including S10 and S11 corrections, is preserved. Other revisions record Milestone 4 proof evidence, reference fixes, and unresolved obligations; no other candidate rule is silently changed.\n1186\t2. **`SyncAuthorized` and contention:** C14's original combined authorization predicate is preserved exactly. C16 classifies the *enclosing library call*, separating C14.1/C14.3 invalidity (`reject`) from C14.2 or physical controller occupation (temporarily non-enabled). This choice does not rewrite C14 or C15 and requires a refined Lean operation classification.\n1187\t3. **Additional event clauses:** new C26–C53 clauses are appended as Parts VII–XII after the preserved WC4 Parts I–VI, so the original parts remain complete and in their original order. Attribute flags `acc` and `sync` may coexist; pure authority markers have neither. `start(c)`/`complete(c)` are separate lifecycle markers, avoiding a contradiction when the child's first substantive operation is an authority step.\n1188\t4. **Access mapping and memory representation:** one high-level step may generate several physical access events; `transfer`, by-value `spawn`, `store`, `issue`, and library metadata cannot be declared physically inert. Only **pure** logical markers are candidates for erasure, with `po` preserved by projection.\n1189\t5. **`SharedMutable`/O11.3:** the existing predicate `K.SharedMutable` is not replaced. `K_C.SharedDiscipline` is additional. In this draft, “applies” in O11.3 means that the discipline is **satisfied by the access**; the open Lean representation gap and conditional T1 are reported rather than hidden. A region mixing unsupported discipline kinds is rejected by the reference contract, pending any separately justified composite design.\n1190\t6. **Atomicity and operation syntax:** the candidate concurrency-order annotations on inherited `writeShared` do not revise the frozen sequential O11 signature; a successful conditional atomic RMW is a `writeShared` mutation, while a failed comparison is a `readShared` observation. The new `readShared` is restricted to an entire single `Atomic` leaf. A multi-leaf atomic write is `reject`, not an implicit sequence of atomic stores. `read3`/`write3` remain non-atomic at the kernel, and D-C9 is a safe-interface rule.\n1191\t7. **Memory-model scope:** the order names include `seq_cst` but the exploratory Lean fragment excludes full global SC constraints and release sequences until they are formalized. RC11 is reference-only; `acyclic(po ∪ rf)` is an optional named assumption, not the chosen ERN normative no-thin-air rule. The proposed later release-sequence extension uses the C++20 form, with any mismatch from reference RC11 stated during comparison.\n1192\t8. **Handoff and completion [WC5.1/5.2]:** `HandoffOrdered` remains limited to exclusive conflicting pairs with a non-atomic member, with guarded-chain-excluding `cap` per `(context, leaf)` and a candidate event loss marker `r`. `join` is not exclusive, a bare O3 `release` creates no `sw`, and a child or grandchild that has not been appropriately ordered remains a possible source of an unsafe trace. The **scoped authority-step parts** of L1/L2 and the **abstract step-event** `po`/conditional `hb` composition are now proved as described in C45–C46. Concrete physical `po`/`rf`/`hb`, complete access coverage, and universal safe-program soundness are not.\n1193\t9. **Scope of the claimed DRF result:** `SafeConcurrentTrace` excludes kernel-permitted unsafe-interface behaviors; its DRF statement is a **target theorem**, not a completed guarantee. The generic `Synchronized` case is a composition assumption, while the obligation that well-typed safe code produces such traces belongs to Specs 3/4 and specific libraries. Physical refinement and absence of accesses after invalidation remain separate.\n1194\t\n1195\t10. **[WC5.1] Guarded-chain interpretation:** C22.6 is a proposed safe-interface trace restriction. It requires at least one valid holder-chain justification avoiding any live `guarded`, not all hypothetical derivations to avoid it; the full set of right premises and relevant pre/post/intermediate states must be covered. It does not alter Spec 1 §43. `resetLock` requires the narrowly scoped C18.1 own-`guarded` exception, with the `slot`-holder justification constrained against **other** live guards. Model-level premise coverage is now supported by `PremG`/`RunFull` and named theorems; `OB-C28` remains open for target safe-interface enforcement, §78.1 `reset` metadata premises and the manually inspected inventory points. This is not a physical-access proof.\n1196\t11. **[WC5.1] `cap5` and proof status:** the proof-oriented C43 `cap` is the guarded-chain-excluding `cap5`, distinct from raw contextual capacity (refuted L1 under W-GR). The report proves step-local loss for 28 kinds and state compatibility, and proves trace loss for the explicit access fragment under `Encap5C`/`Encap5R`. Step-level `po` and conditional abstract `hb` composition are proved (not unconditional C46 for all safe traces); concrete physical `po`/`hb`, `rf`/`mo`, complete byte-access coverage, and safe-language exposure remain unproved.\n1197\t12. **[WC5.3 — M4] Evidence boundary:** corpus v14, the earlier strict-step-event and herdtools reports, `spec2-m4-eventmodel.md`, `spec2-m4-mutexreal.md`, and `spec2-m4-encap.md` (final complement including `KillKinds`) document the finite event fragment, controller-model and complete-encapsulation proofs, and adversarial witnesses. The latest reported Lean audit has **2,822 theorems (1,878 non-internal)**, axioms confined to `{propext, Quot.sound}`, and successful `lake build`; this drafting pass has **not** independently re-executed them. `rc11.cat` E1 is a reference-model result; G18 is an executed **direct-`rf` fragment** result, not an executed C++20 release-sequence model. The modeled `RunFull`/`PremG` results have precise initial-state, `DDSound`, and `StaticsOK` scopes. Actual target physical direct-`rf`, all composite **physical events**, initialization ordering and safe-language soundness remain open. The earlier `Run6 ⊆ Run7` inclusion is proved for reachable initial-state runs only.\n1198\t\n1199\t**End of Working Candidate 5.3 — complete non-normative exploratory candidate, Milestone 4 complete-encapsulation evidence incorporated. [WC5.3]**\n1200\t"}]},"uuid":"2e4563c7-e25c-4b1c-ab31-acf85fa4533d","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:35.683Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","content":"# X. Inter-Context Authority Handoff — Proposed Block D\n\n## C43. Context Capability and Current-Frame Exercise **[WC5.1]**\n\nDefine, provisionally, for state `s`, execution context `c`, and logical leaf `l`:\n\n```\ncap(s, c, l) ∈ { none, Observe, Modify }\n```\n\nFor the safe-handoff proof, it is the greatest **effective** mode on `l` attributable to `c` through active authorities held by its live frames, **including suspended frames**, or held in a place through a valid holder chain, **provided the attributing chain does not pass through the `guarded` authority of any live synchronization entry** **[WC5.1]**. This guarded-chain-excluding capability is `cap5` in the exploratory Lean model. Its `Attr5` attribution relation deliberately treats live `guarded` authorities as unavailable for attribution but permits a valid frame-held grant and its legally held descendants. It does not change the rights of any authority in the frozen Spec 1.\n\n`cap` is **not** `ExercisableAt`. The latter applies the frozen §43 rules to the executing context's **current frame** and is used to decide **kernel** operation preconditions; C22.6 separately restricts which successful kernel operations the **safe interface** may expose. `cap` summarizes guarded-chain-excluding contextual entitlement across the context's frames and relevant held places, including rights not currently exercisable from the top frame. Derivation, reborrowing and frame changes need not terminate a context's `cap` merely because an individual authority becomes inactive or non-exercisable. **[WC5.1]** The unrestricted contextual capability (`cap` in the earlier Lean exploration) is distinguished here as **raw contextual capability**, not the quantity used by L1/L2: W-GR refutes raw capability-loss locality.\n\nFor two distinct contexts, present `cap` values on a leaf are **incompatible** when at least one is `Modify`; for `Confined`, any two present capabilities are incompatible under the additional safe-storage contract. This is a proposed abstraction for L1/L2, not a new sequential mode or authority identity.\n\n## C44. Scope of Exclusive Handoff Ordering\n\nLet `e1` and `e2` be access events in **different contexts**, associated with the same logical leaf and conflicting byte ranges, where at least one is non-atomic. `HandoffOrdered` applies only if the pair is **exclusive**: its relevant context capabilities are incompatible under C43 and the declared storage discipline. It does not impose `hb` between two genuinely concurrent atomic accesses under compatible observation rights.\n\nTypical cases in scope are:\n\n1. accesses to ordinary non-`SharedMutable` storage, when a conflict necessarily includes an exclusive ordinary `write3`;\n2. a mixed atomic/non-atomic pair on `Atomic` storage, because C42 requires exclusive `Modify` for the ordinary member;\n3. conflicting accesses under `Confined`, because the safe-storage contract excludes simultaneous cross-context capacities;\n4. exclusive ordinary access pairs under `Synchronized`.\n\nConflicting **non-atomic** `writeShared` operations on `Synchronized` storage performed under shared compatible `Observe` rights are **not** covered by the exclusive-handoff premise. Their ordering is established by the particular `Synchronized` protocol, not by claiming that the shared rights are exclusive.\n\nThe scope is deliberately narrower than the class of all conflicts, and wider than just final restitution of a spawn parameter. It includes a capacity becoming available to a child **after** its `spawn`, and multi-context relays.\n\n## C45. Loss Marker, L1 and L2 — Scoped Authority Proofs and Open Event Obligations **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair in the scope of C44, write `e1` for the earlier access in the abstract trace, issued by `c′`, and `e2` for the subsequent conflicting access by `c ≠ c′`. Let `r(e1,e2,l)` be the **loss marker** that ends the period containing `e1` during which `cap(·,c′,l)` is incompatible with the capability used by `e2`.\n\nThis marker may denote a release, a parameter yield, a derivation that reduces the relevant effective mode, frame exit, termination, unlock, or a justified holder/ownership change. It is a **pure logical marker** if its effect is only an authority change; a concrete step can additionally emit physical memory or synchronization events. The required loss threshold depends on the later access: a later exclusive write requires no incompatible surviving capability in `c′`; a later shared `Observe` read may be permitted after `c′` has lost its `Modify` exclusivity while retaining compatible `Observe`.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Exploratory proof status (authority machine **and scoped abstract step-event model**, not a complete physical-event proof):**\n\n- **L1 — local capability loss:** `cap5_drop_own` is proved under `DDSound` for all **28 modeled kinds of authority-machine steps**, over reachable encapsulated traces: any strict reduction of a context's guarded-chain-excluding `cap5` originates in that context's own step. For an eligible direct-access exclusive pair, `lossStep_own` further proves a first strictly intermediate loss step `r` of the earlier context. In the one-event-per-step abstraction this gives `po(e1,r)`; placement among physical events inside a realized compound step remains open.\n- **L2 — state compatibility:** `cap5_compat` is proved: distinct contexts cannot simultaneously have incompatible `cap5` on the same leaf under the ordinary `Modify` exclusivity criterion. This is an authority-state theorem, not an ordering theorem.\n- **L2 — trace loss:** `handoff_loss_exists_C` is proved for conflicting **`read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`** step-access pairs, at least one requiring `Modify`, over `EncapsulatedTrace5C` (the direct-exercise `ChainExcl` restriction, together with existing encapsulation and controller restrictions). It identifies a strictly intervening step of the earlier context reducing `cap5` on the leaf. The result transfers to the stronger tested `EncapsulatedTrace5R` by trace inclusion. Without this chain restriction, `handoff_loss_exists` is **refuted** under `EncapsulatedTrace5` by W-GC; `cap5_sound` is likewise refuted there. `cap5_sound_C` is proved under `EncapsulatedTrace5C`.\n\nThese are scoped results from the exploratory Lean formalization, **not** claims that C22.6 has been encoded for every operation or that the complete data-race-freedom theorem has been established. **[WC5.1 — M4]** `handoffOrdered_no_race` and its strict-graph analogue `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` prove that, given `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`, eligible exclusive direct-access pairs are race-free in the corresponding abstract graph. They do **not** prove that arbitrary safe executions always satisfy C46. Full step-to-physical-event correspondence, access coverage beyond these three named primitives, physical `po` mapping, and actual protocol `rf`/`hb` remain obligations.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4]** In the one-event-per-step abstraction, `lossStep_own` identifies the first relevant `r` and establishes its own-context, strictly intermediate position and hence the step-level `po` order. The exact **physical** marker position inside a realized compound step, partial byte footprints, and the concrete `po`/`rf`/`sw`/`hb` mapping remain open. A kernel-admitted trace outside C22.6 may refute the safe handoff premises without changing any frozen rule; such counterexamples must be retained and reported rather than concealed by hypotheses.\n\n## C46. HandoffOrdered — D-C10 **[WC5.1]**\n\nFor a pair within C44's scope, let `r(e1,e2,l)` denote the loss marker identified by the provisional capacity-period construction in C45. The **sole ordering requirement of `HandoffOrdered`** is:\n\n\\[\n  HandoffOrdered(e1,e2,l) \\;:\\!\\iff\\; hb(r(e1,e2,l),e2).\n\\]\n\n**[WC5.1]** The **authority-step parts** of L1 and L2 now have the restricted Lean proofs stated in C45 for `cap5` and the `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` access fragment under guarded-chain exclusion. These proofs are **not additional preconditions** of `HandoffOrdered`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The later `lossStep_own` proof establishes `po(e1,r)` for the scoped abstract one-event-per-step pairs; no **physical** `po` placement or concrete `hb` edge follows merely from authority proof. If the authority machine or concrete realization fails to provide a marker for a relevant pair, that is an unfulfilled proof obligation or counterexample, not permission to assume a hidden marker. The existence of **some** unrelated `sw` event is insufficient. When the event-model mapping has also established `po(e1,r)`, the protocol requirement `hb(r,e2)` implies `hb(e1,e2)` by transitivity. This is a **composition argument**, now proved for the abstract direct-access graph (`handoffOrdered_no_race`, `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`) under its stated run assumptions: C46 still specifies the ordering each admissible handoff protocol must justify, not a claim that R4 or an authority-only trace establishes physical `hb`. The requirement itself is unchanged.\n\nThe requirement applies to both **transmission** and **restitution** of authority. A bare inherited O3 `release` may restore an origin's effective right in the globally coherent abstract state, but it generates **no** `sw` by itself. The resulting trace can be kernel-`ok` and nevertheless be excluded from `SafeConcurrentTrace` if a later cross-context access lacks the required order.\n\n`join` is one reference protocol, not the only legal completion/handoff mechanism (C8). Other mechanisms may be added if they prove the requisite `hb` relation without introducing a new ambient authority source or weakening inherited invariants. Safe-language constructs that ensure these obligations belong to Specs 3/4.\n\n---\n\n# XI. Event Realization of Reference Protocols — Proposed Block D\n\n## C47. Spawn, Completion, and Join Events\n\nFor the reference by-reference `spawn` protocol, the creator's authority-yield **loss marker** precedes its `spawn` synchronization event within the parent context; the latter `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` marker. The initial child frame and parameter holder changes still obey C4/C5, and neither the event protocol nor `start(c)` duplicates an authority. A parent's operation executed after `spawn` is not ordered before a child's operation merely because the parent was the creator.\n\nFor `terminate`, every earlier event of the terminating context is `po`-before `complete(c)`; a successful `join(c)` in another context is the target of `sw` from that `complete(c)` event. The `join` event is placed in the caller's `po` before subsequent operations. By transitivity, a context's accesses preceding its completion are `hb`-before the caller's accesses following its successful join. An unsuccessful or non-enabled `join` contributes no such completed-observation edge.\n\nThe reference safe passing discipline may delay a creator's re-use of transferred exclusive capacity until appropriate completion observation, but C8 does **not** impose this restriction on all possible kernel traces or require universal structured concurrency. For a chain of delegated authorities, a successful join of an intermediate context does not imply completion of a surviving descendant. Correct reference reuse may require **transitive** joining or an equivalent ordering protocol.\n\nFor by-value `spawn`, access events emitted by the transfers belong to the actual producing context/phase, and their order must preserve C5's create–transfer–yield structure. Whether those byte accesses are correctly ordered with all other physical uses is part of the separate realization and safe-execution proofs, not a consequence of the logical transfer alone.\n\n## C48. Reference Mutex: Acquire, Unlock, and Grant Events\n\nThe reference `newMutex` initializes a physical controller in a state corresponding to `grant = none` and must establish the positional, identity and initialization requirements of C12–C13, C21 and Spec 1 §56. As clarified in C13, this is a physical non-atomic write of the controller synchronization value to an **already abstractly `Init`** leaf, not the abstract initialization of an `Uninit` structural leaf. C13.11 requires ordinary `Modify` authority, with applicable O10.1/O10.2 conditions. Its concrete metadata operations have events as required by C27/C29.\n\nA successful `acquire` has an atomic controller-acquisition event, for example a successful CAS/RMW with an acquire-capable memory order. This event must observe (`rf`) the appropriate released/available controller state, satisfy atomic coherence, and linearize the reservation of the controller before the new grant is made externally exercisable. A **gain marker** is placed `po`-after the successful acquire event. A failed CAS does not grant authority; a busy controller and an invalid request remain classified under C16, with `reject` taking priority per abstract state.\n\nAn `unlock` has a logical **loss marker** for the released grant, followed in `po` by a corresponding atomic release publication of the controller's available state. The abstract library transition remains coherent and does not expose an intermediate invalid grant/controller pairing. When a later acquiring RMW **directly reads** this release modification (`rf`), the protocol yields the requisite `sw` and thus `hb` from the preceding protected critical section to the succeeding one.\n\nThe event-level atomicity of this logical transition, the absence of an observable partial state, `SyncInv`/`GrantDiscipline` preservation and the correspondence to the concrete controller are still obligations of `OB-C05` and C25. C48 proposes the required event behavior; it does **not** claim these obligations have been discharged.\n\nThe initial Lean fragment limits the mutex `sw` and `LockOrder` proof to the **direct `rf`** pattern. Protocols with intervening RMWs or release sequences require an extension. When such sequences are added, the candidate uses the C++20 form in which intervening RMWs, but not ordinary same-thread stores, extend a release sequence. **[WC5.1 — M4] Reference comparison performed:** herdtools7 7.58 E1, with the unmodified `rc11.cat`, admits a release sequence extended by a **same-thread atomic non-RMW** `store(f, 2, relaxed)` following `store(f, 1, release)`. A later `acquire` reading the `2` is synchronized with the earlier `release` in that reference; the tested ordinary data read has no race. The candidate C++20-form rule stated here **does not** extend that release sequence through a non-RMW atomic store, so the analogous race has now been **observed by executing G18 in the finite ERN direct-`rf` fragment**, but **not** by executing an ERN implementation of the C++20-form release-sequence rule. Because G18 has no release-sequence semantics at all, it does not decide whether that candidate extension or the `rc11.cat` extension is correct for ERN. This is a documented comparative divergence, not a new normative selection or evidence of an ERN compiler realization.\n\n**[WC5.2 — M4 reference controller evidence]** Under the declared model (`newMutex`: non-atomic initial `0` write; successful `acquire`: CAS/RMW reading `0` and writing `1`; `unlock`: atomic `0` release; `resetLock`: atomic `0` relaxed), with `mo` constructed from protocol steps and every successful RMW reading its `mo` predecessor, `ctl_invariant` and `ctlGraph_coherent` hold for the modeled traces. The `bridge` theorem proves that the strict abstract unlock-to-acquire edge induces a direct `rf` from that unlock to the acquisition; `bridge_sw_ref` gives `sw` under reference release/acquire orders. Conversely `bridge_converse` recovers the strict edge from direct `rf` under `CtlFixed`, and W-TR refutes that converse without controller stability. This proof is **conditional on the constructed event graph and declared realization**; it does not prove that compiled controller operations have that history. Successful CAS attempts are modeled; failed attempts, actual byte metadata and internal gain/loss marker events remain open.\n\n## C49. LockOrder, Recovery, and Delegation\n\n**`LockOrder`** is the obligation that the protected accesses in an earlier critical section of one mutex are `hb`-before conflicting protected accesses in a later critical section whose lock-acquisition event receives the preceding release. The proof must use the concrete `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, and grant-lifecycle relations, not merely the total order of abstract `SyncEntry` updates or the fact that controller identities match.\n\nFor a successful `resetLock`, the library must prove exclusive recovery authority under C18, a coherent physical controller update, and the event-order consequences of any protected memory that becomes newly accessible. Recovery is **not automatically** a substitute for an acquire/release edge missing from an abandoned grant. A proposed recovery protocol that cannot establish necessary ordering is not justified for safe concurrent access. **[WC5.1 — M4]** C18 imposes no precondition on the recorded grant state when `resetLock(e)` executes: the recorded grant may be `none`. The exploratory kernel admits `resetLock` on an already free lock, as witnessed by W-RL. This does **not** introduce any new C18 precondition or imply a new `sw` edge.\n\nWhen a grant is reborrowed, passed to another frame, or transmitted between contexts, the reference implementation must show how ordinary authority rules, grant constraints, and the relevant handoff protocol preserve `hb` across the whole path. Exiting a frame that released a grant without `unlock` does not imply controller release, and a logically returned capability does not imply a physical publication.\n\n**[WC5.1 — M4] Strict abstract mutex-edge evidence:** The first experimental graph connected `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` by entry identity alone. W-RL (`resetLock(e)` between them) and W-TR (whole-mutex `transfer` changing `ControlOf(e)`) prove that this approximation can invent `hb`. The corrected **abstract** edge is absent if an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs, or if `ControlOf(e)` becomes undefined or changes leaf from the state after `unlock` through the state before `acquire`. The strict graph excludes W-RL/W-TR, preserves W-RL₀/W-RL-J/W-TR-J and the earlier reference witnesses, and proves conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps`. **Any abstraction of the C48 mutex `sw` edge must respect interventions on the controller; the definitive physical rule remains the unchanged direct-`rf` condition on the same atomic unit in C32/C48.** **[WC5.2 — M4]** For the **constructed reference controller graph**, `bridge` now proves the direct-read implication; `completeness7_run` classifies the modeled controller footprints for `Run7` under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. The two strict abstract guards still do **not** prove that a target physical acquisition reads the earlier release (`OB-C05`), or that all compiled metadata and composite-step accesses are covered (`OB-C20`).\n\n## C50. Realization, Visibility, and Physical Correspondence\n\nThe implementation must relate every relevant successful `S_C` operation and its associated event graph to a concrete memory execution under the frozen Spec 1 §82 representation obligations. For `Atomic` storage this includes unit indivisibility, alignment, coherent `rf`/`mo`, and the compilation of its memory orders. For ordinary, `Synchronized`, and `Confined` storage it includes byte-level access coverage, validity, exclusivity and the required event ordering.\n\nThis obligation is **stronger** than establishing a sequence of abstract `ok` outcomes. It includes preservation of actual initialized bytes, pointer provenance, invalidation conditions, owners and holders, and physically executable synchronization operations. In particular, no successful abstraction proof may conceal an unmodeled physical access after invalidation (§82.7 / `OB-C12`).\n\nClaims about compilation to x86-TSO, ARM, or POWER, including the potential cost of a proposed no-thin-air axiom, must be supported by a target-specific compilation or refinement argument. The initial event fragment is not itself such an argument.\n\n---\n\n# XII. Safe Executions and Data-Race Freedom — Proposed Block D\n\n## C51. EncapsulatedTrace and SafeConcurrentTrace **[WC5.1]**\n\nTwo **distinct** restrictions apply to completed traces:\n\n1. `EncapsulatedTrace`: the safe **reference-mutex** trace contract of C22, including the WC5 D-C6 restriction against ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` and **[WC5.1]** the C22.6 restriction on all right-justification holder chains traversing a live `guarded` (subject to the specific C18.1 `resetLock` exception). This restricts the **public safe interface**, not O9/O10 or any inherited kernel operation. A kernel-`ok` `read3(lock)` or a kernel-`ok` access through live `guarded` without the required grant can be outside this contract without being a counterexample to Spec 1. The earlier `Encap5R` checks only a subset of C22.6; the later `EncapFull`/`RunFull` exploratory model covers the 28 modeled step kinds and their inventoried right premises, while `OB-C28` retains safe-interface enforcement and the stated modeling/exhaustiveness gaps.\n2. `SafeConcurrentTrace`: a proposed subset of `EncapsulatedTrace` in which (a) all relevant event-graph consistency conditions hold; (b) `Atomic` accesses obey C37–C42; (c) `Confined` has at most one context with a present **guarded-chain-excluding** capability in each state and any later cross-context conflict satisfies C46; (d) every non-atomic conflict under a `Synchronized` contract is either protected by a proven exclusive handoff or ordered by an established synchronization mechanism; and (e) all applicable `HandoffOrdered`, lifecycle, and `LockOrder` obligations are fulfilled. **[WC5.1]** Its safe-interface premise includes all of C22.6, not just the currently encoded `ChainExcl`/`PlaceExcl` subset; proof of target interface soundness and physical access coverage remains open, notwithstanding the modeled `RunFull` premise-coverage results.\n\n`SafeConcurrentTrace` is a **semantic safe-execution contract**. It is not the set of all kernel-`ok` traces. The earlier witness `read3(lock)` concurrent with `writeShared(lock)` demonstrates why `EncapsulatedTrace` in WC4 was not enough; under C42/C22 it is excluded from the candidate safe interface while the ordinary kernel operation remains available.\n\nThe statement “a safe, well-typed ERN program generates only `SafeConcurrentTrace` executions” is a **separate soundness obligation** of Specs 3/4 (and the relevant safe libraries), not a theorem derived merely from a name or by construction of this predicate.\n\n## C52. Candidate Data-Race-Freedom Theorem and Proof Partition **[WC5.1]**\n\n**Target theorem (open, not yet proved).** Every execution graph satisfying the stated `EventConsistent` obligations and arising from a `SafeConcurrentTrace` is free from data races as defined in C33 and frozen Spec 1 §18.\n\nThe proof is deliberately partitioned by **conflicting access pair**, not just by storage label:\n\n| Pair or discipline | Intended argument | Independent proof boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ordinary non-`SharedMutable` leaves | Conflicts require exclusive ordinary mutation. Show the loss marker exists (L2) and follows the prior access in `po` (L1); a certified handoff provides `hb(r,e2)` and therefore `hb(e1,e2)`. | **[WC5.1 — M4]** `lossStep_own` and `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` now prove the abstract direct-access composition conditional on C46. Proving C46 for all safe executions and concrete physical events remains open; R4 alone is insufficient. |\n| `Atomic`, atomic/atomic | Both accesses are atomic, so the non-atomic conjunct of the Spec 1 §18 race definition is false. | Atomicity of entire unit and `rf`/`mo` coherence must still be established. |\n| `Atomic`, mixed or non-atomic/non-atomic | C42 restricts every ordinary member to effective/exercisable `Modify`, giving abstract exclusivity, then uses certified handoff ordering for accesses of distinct contexts. | D-C9 enforcement, L1/L2, C46, concrete access coverage. |\n| `Confined` | At most one context has an attributable capability at a time; cross-context conflicting accesses require an ordered handoff. | Confinement contract and L1/L2/C46; exclusivity alone does not imply `hb`. |\n| `Synchronized` with exclusive ordinary writer | Use the ordinary exclusive-handoff argument. | L1/L2/C46 and proof of the particular protocol. |\n| `Synchronized` under shared compatible observation | The generic storage contract requires conflicting non-atomic accesses to be sufficiently `hb`-ordered. | **Composition case**: the requirement is an assumption until each declared mechanism proves its actual `sw` and `hb` edges. |\n\nThe generic `Synchronized` case is **not presented as a non-circular proof**. Likewise, assuming `HandoffOrdered` in a generic trace establishes only a composition theorem. **[WC5.1]** The former L1/L2 proof boundaries in the table now have **scoped authority-machine proofs** (`cap5_drop_own`, `cap5_compat`, `cap5_sound_C`, `handoff_loss_exists_C`) for directly modeled `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` accesses under the tested chain restriction. They are **not** proofs for physical accesses generated by `store`, `transfer`, `issue`, allocation, or other compound operations; those require `OB-C20` and safe-interface enforcement, notwithstanding the expanded `RunFull` abstract premise proofs under `OB-C28`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The abstract graph now proves the first own-context loss marker with step-level `po` (`lossStep_own`), the **conditional** composition `HandoffOrderedS → ¬ RaceS` (`handoffOrdered_no_raceS`), negative missing-`HBS` results for W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″, and positive ordered counterparts W-HO-J/W-HO″-J/`lc_trace`. W-RL/W-TR show why the old mutex edge was overbroad; their strict-graph failures and join-based positive companions are proved. These are scoped **one-event-per-step** results, not concrete data-race-freedom for all realizations. The substantial remaining work is physical marker placement/byte-event coverage, concrete `rf`/`mo`/`sw` and controller `LockOrder`, safe-program/interface soundness, and proving C46 for every safe execution.\n\nA reference-mutex theorem may conclude DRF for protected ordinary accesses only after the concrete controller protocol supplies `LockOrder` and the requisite `rf`/`sw` links. A `.cat` model that simply discards racy graphs does not prove that safe ERN programs could not generate such graphs before that filter.\n\n## C53. Proof Boundary, Conservativity, and No Freeze\n\nThe following obligations remain separate and **unproved unless supported by named proofs**:\n\n1. **Inherited-operation conservativity:** the unchanged Spec 1 O1–O16 must retain their projected kernel meaning. Because the exploratory Spec 1 Lean `WriteSharedPre` does not explicitly represent O11.3's shared-discipline predicate, T1 must be **restated and proved** for `K_C.SharedDiscipline`: every concurrency-`ok` inherited write projects to a Spec 1 `ok` under its valid `K`, with equality of relevant admissible sets only under explicitly established matching/accept-all discipline assumptions. No equality is asserted without proof.\n2. **Authority lemmas:** L1, L2, and any preservation properties required by the chosen definition of `cap` and holder attribution.\n3. **Graph and realization lemmas:** pure-authority erasure, coverage of physical accesses, full `EventConsistent` coherence beyond the finite one-unit fragment, **target** direct-`rf` protocol correctness beyond the conditional constructed-controller bridge, and reconstruction/preservation under Spec 1 §82.\n4. **Program/interface soundness:** safe interfaces exclude kernel-admissible operations outside C22/C42; the typing and library rules of Specs 3/4 prove that safe programs yield `SafeConcurrentTrace`.\n5. **Full memory-model selection:** no-thin-air, release sequences, global `seq_cst` semantics, cross-target compilation, and the proven relationship (inclusion or equivalence, if appropriate) of the final ERN model to any reference model.\n\nThe baseline comparison is **three-level**: complete external RC11 reference; the explicitly scoped exploratory ERN-C/D Lean fragment; and the eventual normative ERN model. No equivalence is implied by similar names or successful litmus tests. The working decisions D-C1–D-C10 remain proposals pending consolidation and proof, and no clause of the frozen Charter or Spec 1 v0.1 is changed here.\n\n---\n\n# Drafting choices\n\nThese choices resolve ambiguities for this **non-normative working draft**; they are not amendments to the frozen Charter or Spec 1, not approved D-C decisions, and not substitutes for the proof obligations above.\n\n1. **Preservation of WC4 [WC5.3]:** The following clauses are textually identical to WC4: C1–C12, C14–C15, C19–C20, C23 and C24. C25 differs from WC4 only in the corrected reference to C22.1–6. The original seven numbered `SyncInv` invariants of C21 are unchanged; explanatory material follows C21.5 without adding an invariant. C13 retains the accepted item 11 and controller-initialization clarification; C18 retains the accepted item 4 and leaves C18.1–C18.3, outcomes, and effects unchanged; C22 retains the accepted item 4 and the earlier item 6, and clarifies item 1 without narrowing its ban on **any** ordinary authority issued directly from `guarded`, including by compound steps. Items C22.2–3 and C22.5–6 retain their rule text. C16–C18 remain marked **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]** as earlier candidate material. The WC4 Part V seed table, including S10 and S11 corrections, is preserved. Other revisions record Milestone 4 proof evidence, reference fixes, and unresolved obligations; no other candidate rule is silently changed.\n2. **`SyncAuthorized` and contention:** C14's original combined authorization predicate is preserved exactly. C16 classifies the *enclosing library call*, separating C14.1/C14.3 invalidity (`reject`) from C14.2 or physical controller occupation (temporarily non-enabled). This choice does not rewrite C14 or C15 and requires a refined Lean operation classification.\n3. **Additional event clauses:** new C26–C53 clauses are appended as Parts VII–XII after the preserved WC4 Parts I–VI, so the original parts remain complete and in their original order. Attribute flags `acc` and `sync` may coexist; pure authority markers have neither. `start(c)`/`complete(c)` are separate lifecycle markers, avoiding a contradiction when the child's first substantive operation is an authority step.\n4. **Access mapping and memory representation:** one high-level step may generate several physical access events; `transfer`, by-value `spawn`, `store`, `issue`, and library metadata cannot be declared physically inert. Only **pure** logical markers are candidates for erasure, with `po` preserved by projection.\n5. **`SharedMutable`/O11.3:** the existing predicate `K.SharedMutable` is not replaced. `K_C.SharedDiscipline` is additional. In this draft, “applies” in O11.3 means that the discipline is **satisfied by the access**; the open Lean representation gap and conditional T1 are reported rather than hidden. A region mixing unsupported discipline kinds is rejected by the reference contract, pending any separately justified composite design.\n6. **Atomicity and operation syntax:** the candidate concurrency-order annotations on inherited `writeShared` do not revise the frozen sequential O11 signature; a successful conditional atomic RMW is a `writeShared` mutation, while a failed comparison is a `readShared` observation. The new `readShared` is restricted to an entire single `Atomic` leaf. A multi-leaf atomic write is `reject`, not an implicit sequence of atomic stores. `read3`/`write3` remain non-atomic at the kernel, and D-C9 is a safe-interface rule.\n7. **Memory-model scope:** the order names include `seq_cst` but the exploratory Lean fragment excludes full global SC constraints and release sequences until they are formalized. RC11 is reference-only; `acyclic(po ∪ rf)` is an optional named assumption, not the chosen ERN normative no-thin-air rule. The proposed later release-sequence extension uses the C++20 form, with any mismatch from reference RC11 stated during comparison.\n8. **Handoff and completion [WC5.1/5.2]:** `HandoffOrdered` remains limited to exclusive conflicting pairs with a non-atomic member, with guarded-chain-excluding `cap` per `(context, leaf)` and a candidate event loss marker `r`. `join` is not exclusive, a bare O3 `release` creates no `sw`, and a child or grandchild that has not been appropriately ordered remains a possible source of an unsafe trace. The **scoped authority-step parts** of L1/L2 and the **abstract step-event** `po`/conditional `hb` composition are now proved as described in C45–C46. Concrete physical `po`/`rf`/`hb`, complete access coverage, and universal safe-program soundness are not.\n9. **Scope of the claimed DRF result:** `SafeConcurrentTrace` excludes kernel-permitted unsafe-interface behaviors; its DRF statement is a **target theorem**, not a completed guarantee. The generic `Synchronized` case is a composition assumption, while the obligation that well-typed safe code produces such traces belongs to Specs 3/4 and specific libraries. Physical refinement and absence of accesses after invalidation remain separate.\n\n10. **[WC5.1] Guarded-chain interpretation:** C22.6 is a proposed safe-interface trace restriction. It requires at least one valid holder-chain justification avoiding any live `guarded`, not all hypothetical derivations to avoid it; the full set of right premises and relevant pre/post/intermediate states must be covered. It does not alter Spec 1 §43. `resetLock` requires the narrowly scoped C18.1 own-`guarded` exception, with the `slot`-holder justification constrained against **other** live guards. Model-level premise coverage is now supported by `PremG`/`RunFull` and named theorems; `OB-C28` remains open for target safe-interface enforcement, §78.1 `reset` metadata premises and the manually inspected inventory points. This is not a physical-access proof.\n11. **[WC5.1] `cap5` and proof status:** the proof-oriented C43 `cap` is the guarded-chain-excluding `cap5`, distinct from raw contextual capacity (refuted L1 under W-GR). The report proves step-local loss for 28 kinds and state compatibility, and proves trace loss for the explicit access fragment under `Encap5C`/`Encap5R`. Step-level `po` and conditional abstract `hb` composition are proved (not unconditional C46 for all safe traces); concrete physical `po`/`hb`, `rf`/`mo`, complete byte-access coverage, and safe-language exposure remain unproved.\n12. **[WC5.3 — M4] Evidence boundary:** corpus v14, the earlier strict-step-event and herdtools reports, `spec2-m4-eventmodel.md`, `spec2-m4-mutexreal.md`, and `spec2-m4-encap.md` (final complement including `KillKinds`) document the finite event fragment, controller-model and complete-encapsulation proofs, and adversarial witnesses. The latest reported Lean audit has **2,822 theorems (1,878 non-internal)**, axioms confined to `{propext, Quot.sound}`, and successful `lake build`; this drafting pass has **not** independently re-executed them. `rc11.cat` E1 is a reference-model result; G18 is an executed **direct-`rf` fragment** result, not an executed C++20 release-sequence model. The modeled `RunFull`/`PremG` results have precise initial-state, `DDSound`, and `StaticsOK` scopes. Actual target physical direct-`rf`, all composite **physical events**, initialization ordering and safe-language soundness remain open. The earlier `Run6 ⊆ Run7` inclusion is proved for reachable initial-state runs only.\n\n**End of Working Candidate 5.3 — complete non-normative exploratory candidate, Milestone 4 complete-encapsulation evidence incorporated. [WC5.3]**\n","numLines":175,"startLine":1026,"totalLines":1202}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a5c3efc6-b3ea-4da3-a5bb-877ae5fc9b00","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","hash":"21d6598ef63cf040e5e931d9008542371d0925144c8a520f165bcccd4b945570","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm still reading the WC5.3 clauses (C26–C52) before designing anything."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GwVVZDZHWMnK7b4NthWhSg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":777,"limit":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XPrZE4J4f8VspVPNvaURY2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Downloads/ERN_WC5_3_FINAL.md","offset":1026,"limit":175},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","hash":"dbe2ec60276821c6c5b8e2f11c8fdcc315d0ce959dcbac22ce3ec2b8e41caa5c","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GwVVZDZHWMnK7b4NthWhSg","type":"tool_result","content":"777\tA **candidate concurrent execution** consists of:\n778\t\n779\t1. a finite or potentially unbounded trace of transitions of the globally coherent concurrent authority machine of C1–C25;\n780\t2. the external library-administration states required by those transitions, including `SyncEntry` states and the proposed `K_C.SharedDiscipline` contract;\n781\t3. an event graph induced by the abstract steps and their concrete realizations, with identifiable events and relations `po`, `rf`, `mo`, and `sw`;\n782\t4. evidence that the event graph satisfies the selected, explicitly named consistency predicate; and\n783\t5. where safe execution is asserted, the additional restrictions of C51.\n784\t\n785\tThe ordering of transitions in the authority-machine trace is an **abstract interleaving**, used to compute which operation can occur next. This total enumeration is **not** a hardware memory order, and it does not itself imply `happens-before` between events of distinct contexts. In particular, the fact that a permission becomes exercisable in a later state is not evidence that a physical write in an earlier state has been published.\n786\t\n787\tEach successful transition has a defined association with one or more semantic events. A pure abstract operation may produce only a logical event. A compound operation may produce several access, synchronization, and authority-marker events. Failure and temporary non-enablement do not create successful authority-state transitions. Any physical accesses performed while testing, retrying, or rejecting a call must nevertheless be accounted for by its realization; they cannot be silently discarded from the physical event graph.\n788\t\n789\tThe event model does not require a runtime record for every logical event. A logical event is a semantic point used to define and prove ordering, not necessarily an executed instruction.\n790\t\n791\t## C27. Event Attributes and Realization Origin\n792\t\n793\tAn event is associated with a context, the abstract transition or library phase that generated it, and a position within that transition. Its **attributes are cumulative, not mutually exclusive**:\n794\t\n795\t- `acc`: an actual memory-access component, carrying an access kind `read`, `write`, or `RMW`, a concrete byte range, an atomicity classification, and an applicable memory order for an atomic access;\n796\t- `sync`: an event eligible to participate as the source or target of a justified `synchronizes-with` (`sw`) relation;\n797\t- neither `acc` nor `sync`: a **pure authority marker**, recording a logical authority change without claiming a physical access or synchronization effect.\n798\t\n799\tAn event may carry **both** `acc` and `sync`, notably a successful atomic controller operation used by the reference mutex. The event kind and the ordering attributes are separate from a permission's mode (`Observe` or `Modify`).\n800\t\n801\tThe access components must come from the **realization** of the step, not just its high-level name. For example, `transfer`, the by-value phases of `spawn`, `store`, `issue`, and `newMutex` may write or copy bytes even when some of their logical authority effects are independent of memory representation. Metadata operations required by a library implementation must also be accounted for. The same abstract step may therefore have both pure authority markers and concrete access events.\n802\t\n803\tA pure `derive`, `release`, `split`, logical transfer of a holder, or entry/exit of an abstract frame creates no physical memory access **solely because of that abstract effect**. This statement does not authorize erasing physical accesses that a particular implementation of that step actually performs.\n804\t\n805\t## C28. Program Order, Lifecycle Markers, and Within-Step Order\n806\t\n807\t`po` is the strict order of semantic events within **one execution context**, including the ordered phases of a compound step. It includes pure authority markers so that the loss and acquisition of permissions can be positioned relative to actual physical accesses.\n808\t\n809\tFor the reference lifecycle protocol:\n810\t\n811\t- the parent emits a `spawn` synchronization event;\n812\t- the new child emits a distinguished `start(c)` synchronization marker, generated by the `spawn` protocol **before the child's first ordinary or authority step**;\n813\t- a context that successfully executes `terminate` emits a `complete(c)` synchronization marker after its last event;\n814\t- a successful `join(c)` creates a synchronization event in the joining context.\n815\t\n816\t`start(c)` is **not** the child's first arbitrary operation. This avoids attributing synchronization behavior to a `derive` merely because `derive` happens to execute first. Similarly, `complete(c)` is a distinguished termination marker, not a claim that the last ordinary operation was itself a synchronization operation.\n817\t\n818\tFor compound synchronization steps, internal event order is significant:\n819\t\n820\t1. a logical **loss** of an incompatible capacity is `po`-before the synchronization event that publishes that loss (for example, the loss marker preceding a release store of `unlock`);\n821\t2. a synchronization event that establishes acquisition is `po`-before the corresponding logical **gain** marker (for example, an acquiring RMW preceding exposure of a mutex grant);\n822\t3. ordinary accesses protected by the gained authority occur `po`-after that gain marker.\n823\t\n824\tThese are **proposed semantic order obligations**. They must be validated against the compositional transition definitions and their possible realizations; they are not assumed consequences of the sequential kernel.\n825\t\n826\t## C29. Pure-Authority Erasure and Concrete-Access Coverage\n827\t\n828\tTwo distinct realization obligations are required.\n829\t\n830\t**Pure-authority erasure.** A marker bearing neither `acc` nor `sync` is not a source or target of an `sw` edge. Such a marker may be removed from a lower-level graph only when the remaining `po` relation retains the order between its predecessor and successor events in the same context. The candidate erasure theorem states that removing all such markers, with the appropriate `po` projection, preserves `hb` between surviving events. It remains **unproved**. A compiler may not use this clause to erase required memory accesses, physical controller operations, or lifecycle synchronization.\n831\t\n832\t**Concrete-access coverage.** Every actual byte access performed by a step realization must have a corresponding `acc` event with its true byte footprint. The proof must relate these footprints to Spec 1 §82, `LayoutWF`, the actual access-related preconditions, and where applicable R4′. No claim is made that every concrete access requires an ordinary `Modify` authority: `read3` requires `Observe`, `writeShared` may work under `Observe`, and synchronization-library metadata must be justified by its own contract. The obligation is that **each** physical access has the correct kind of authority, atomicity, footprint, and synchronization justification, not that all accesses are ordinary writes.\n833\t\n834\tNeither erasure nor coverage is established by the existence of an abstract `SimRunC` alone. The relation to R1–R3 and the unresolved physical-access-after-invalidation obligation of Spec 1 §82.7 must be proved separately.\n835\t\n836\t---\n837\t\n838\t# VIII. Conflicts, Ordering, and Consistency — Proposed Block C\n839\t\n840\t## C30. Byte-Level Conflicts and Access Pairs\n841\t\n842\tTwo `acc` events from different execution contexts **conflict** if their concrete byte ranges intersect and at least one is a write. An RMW counts as both a read and a write for this purpose. Byte-level intersection is the criterion, even when each event corresponds to a larger logical leaf.\n843\t\n844\tAn `Atomic(size, align)` event is indivisible over the **entire concrete range of its leaf**; the fact that conflict detection is byte-based does not split that event into independently visible byte writes. The relationship between a leaf, its range of bytes and the concrete layout is governed by C38 and Spec 1 §82.\n845\t\n846\tTwo read-only events do not conflict. Conflict is distinct from whether authority preconditions are satisfied, whether the events may overlap in wall-clock time, and whether a sufficient event-order relation exists. This distinction is essential for W-HO, W-HO′ and W-HO″.\n847\t\n848\t## C31. Reads-From and Modification Order\n849\t\n850\tThe candidate graph contains:\n851\t\n852\t- `rf` (**reads-from**): a relation from the write event whose value supplies a read component to the corresponding read event. An RMW has a read component and may also be the source of a later `rf` edge. Initial writes or initialized storage must have an explicitly represented source in any concrete litmus or proof requiring a read value.\n853\t- `mo` (**modification order**): for each `Atomic` location, a strict total order over **all writes/modifications of that location**, not merely atomic writes. This includes the represented initial/initializing write (including `newMutex` controller initialization) and any exclusive ordinary non-atomic write admitted under C42, as well as subsequent atomic stores and successful RMW modifications. Locations are the indivisible units fixed by C38, not arbitrary overlapping regions. Non-atomic initialization and exclusive writes remain **non-atomic** events; their inclusion in `mo` does not confer atomicity or `sw` on them.\n854\t\n855\tFor a successful atomic RMW, its read component obtains the immediately preceding modification of its atomic location in `mo`, under the chosen coherence model; a failed comparison observes a value via a read-only event and contributes no modification to `mo`. The first successful `acquire` must therefore be able to read (`rf`) the initializing `newMutex` write, which is explicitly included in `mo`. An ordinary exclusive write under C42 also occupies its appropriate `mo` position and must be `hb`-ordered as required by the applicable safe-access contract.\n856\t\n857\tA well-formed graph must satisfy explicit typing, source-existence, range-identity, and coherence obligations on `rf` and `mo`. **Specifying `rf` and `mo` alone is insufficient to define a full permitted execution.** The fragment requires a named `EventConsistent` predicate (or equivalent) whose established conjuncts are listed in the corresponding proof statement. No unspecified or inconsistent graph may be accepted merely by postulating a convenient `rf` edge. **[WC5.2 — M4 evidence]** The implemented finite one-unit fragment names `RfWF`, `MoTotal`, `RmwAtomic`, `Coherent` (`hb ; eco?` irreflexive), and `HbIrrefl`; `WellTyped` is separate. The fragment establishes finite decidability, six general lemmas, and 19 nonvacuous witness results, but it does not cover all locations or the full coherence and order semantics required here.\n858\t\n859\t## C32. Synchronizes-With — `sw`\n860\t\n861\tThe following are **candidate reference** sources of synchronization:\n862\t\n863\t1. **Atomic protocol:** a release-capable write/RMW (including a `seq_cst` store or RMW) and an acquire-capable read/RMW (including a `seq_cst` load or RMW) on the same atomic unit may establish `sw` when the acquire reads the release through the applicable `rf` relationship and the relevant memory orders provide release/acquire semantics. **Address identity alone is insufficient.** The initial Lean fragment uses **direct `rf`** only; release-sequence-mediated synchronization awaits explicit modeling.\n864\t2. **Context creation:** the parent's `spawn(c)` event `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` event, with all prior parent events and subsequent child events related through `po`.\n865\t3. **Completion observation:** `complete(c)` of the terminated context `sw`-precedes a successful `join(c)`, with the completing context's preceding events ordered before `complete(c)` and the joining context's subsequent events ordered after `join(c)`.\n866\t\n867\tA pure `release` (O3), `derive`, `endUse`, or other authority marker **does not itself create `sw`**. A bare `spawn` in the abstract interleaving does not order parent operations executed **after** that `spawn` before the child's later operations. A `join(c)` orders only the events of the context whose completion it actually observes, subject to already established transitive edges; it does not implicitly join live descendants or unrelated contexts.\n868\t\n869\tAdditional synchronization mechanisms are admissible only under an explicit contract with a proof that their event edges have the claimed semantics.\n870\t\n871\t## C33. Happens-Before and Data Races\n872\t\n873\tLet `hb` denote the strict transitive closure:\n874\t\n875\t\\[\n876\t  hb=(po\\cup sw)^+.\n877\t\\]\n878\t\n879\tThe candidate requires the event graph to satisfy the applicable acyclicity and consistency conditions; a cycle of purported `hb` edges cannot be used to justify an otherwise unproved synchronization guarantee.\n880\t\n881\tIn the terms of the frozen Spec 1 §18, a **data race** occurs when two events:\n882\t\n883\t1. belong to distinct concurrent execution contexts;\n884\t2. conflict on at least one byte;\n885\t3. include at least one **non-atomic** event; and\n886\t4. are **not ordered by `hb` in either direction**.\n887\t\n888\tThe definition applies to physical access events, including accesses emitted by concrete realizations, and is not limited to the abstract operations named `read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`.\n889\t\n890\t**Spec 1 §18 remains unchanged:** a data race is UB and safe code must not cause one. This candidate does not infer data-race freedom solely from a locally enabled authority step, from the sequential interleaving, or from the atomicity of only one member of a mixed access pair.\n891\t\n892\t## C34. Admissible Event Graphs and Reference Memory Models\n893\t\n894\tRC11, including its published `rc11.cat` model, is a **comparative reference**, not an adopted normative definition of ERN (D-C8). The initial Lean fragment is intentionally smaller: it introduces `po`, `rf`, `mo`, direct-`rf` release/acquire `sw`, `hb`, per-location coherence and RMW rules, without assuming that it already implements RC11 release sequences, full global SC constraints, or all of RC11's consistency axioms.\n895\t\n896\tThe **no-thin-air** requirement is a separately named, optional hypothesis in the exploratory fragment:\n897\t\n898\t\\[\n899\t  NoThinAir_{RC11} := acyclic(po\\cup rf).\n900\t\\]\n901\t\n902\tWhether this stronger RC11 condition belongs to the final ERN model is **open**. Until a decision and a justified compilation scheme exist, a proof using `NoThinAir_RC11` must expose that assumption, and a proof that omits it must not claim to exclude all out-of-thin-air behavior. The differences between RC11, the ERN fragment, and any eventual final ERN model require explicit comparison; inclusion or equivalence is not presumed.\n903\t\n904\tFor exploration, a concrete load-buffering litmus is:\n905\t\n906\t```"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XPrZE4J4f8VspVPNvaURY2","type":"tool_result","content":"1026\t# X. Inter-Context Authority Handoff — Proposed Block D\n1027\t\n1028\t## C43. Context Capability and Current-Frame Exercise **[WC5.1]**\n1029\t\n1030\tDefine, provisionally, for state `s`, execution context `c`, and logical leaf `l`:\n1031\t\n1032\t```\n1033\tcap(s, c, l) ∈ { none, Observe, Modify }\n1034\t```\n1035\t\n1036\tFor the safe-handoff proof, it is the greatest **effective** mode on `l` attributable to `c` through active authorities held by its live frames, **including suspended frames**, or held in a place through a valid holder chain, **provided the attributing chain does not pass through the `guarded` authority of any live synchronization entry** **[WC5.1]**. This guarded-chain-excluding capability is `cap5` in the exploratory Lean model. Its `Attr5` attribution relation deliberately treats live `guarded` authorities as unavailable for attribution but permits a valid frame-held grant and its legally held descendants. It does not change the rights of any authority in the frozen Spec 1.\n1037\t\n1038\t`cap` is **not** `ExercisableAt`. The latter applies the frozen §43 rules to the executing context's **current frame** and is used to decide **kernel** operation preconditions; C22.6 separately restricts which successful kernel operations the **safe interface** may expose. `cap` summarizes guarded-chain-excluding contextual entitlement across the context's frames and relevant held places, including rights not currently exercisable from the top frame. Derivation, reborrowing and frame changes need not terminate a context's `cap` merely because an individual authority becomes inactive or non-exercisable. **[WC5.1]** The unrestricted contextual capability (`cap` in the earlier Lean exploration) is distinguished here as **raw contextual capability**, not the quantity used by L1/L2: W-GR refutes raw capability-loss locality.\n1039\t\n1040\tFor two distinct contexts, present `cap` values on a leaf are **incompatible** when at least one is `Modify`; for `Confined`, any two present capabilities are incompatible under the additional safe-storage contract. This is a proposed abstraction for L1/L2, not a new sequential mode or authority identity.\n1041\t\n1042\t## C44. Scope of Exclusive Handoff Ordering\n1043\t\n1044\tLet `e1` and `e2` be access events in **different contexts**, associated with the same logical leaf and conflicting byte ranges, where at least one is non-atomic. `HandoffOrdered` applies only if the pair is **exclusive**: its relevant context capabilities are incompatible under C43 and the declared storage discipline. It does not impose `hb` between two genuinely concurrent atomic accesses under compatible observation rights.\n1045\t\n1046\tTypical cases in scope are:\n1047\t\n1048\t1. accesses to ordinary non-`SharedMutable` storage, when a conflict necessarily includes an exclusive ordinary `write3`;\n1049\t2. a mixed atomic/non-atomic pair on `Atomic` storage, because C42 requires exclusive `Modify` for the ordinary member;\n1050\t3. conflicting accesses under `Confined`, because the safe-storage contract excludes simultaneous cross-context capacities;\n1051\t4. exclusive ordinary access pairs under `Synchronized`.\n1052\t\n1053\tConflicting **non-atomic** `writeShared` operations on `Synchronized` storage performed under shared compatible `Observe` rights are **not** covered by the exclusive-handoff premise. Their ordering is established by the particular `Synchronized` protocol, not by claiming that the shared rights are exclusive.\n1054\t\n1055\tThe scope is deliberately narrower than the class of all conflicts, and wider than just final restitution of a spawn parameter. It includes a capacity becoming available to a child **after** its `spawn`, and multi-context relays.\n1056\t\n1057\t## C45. Loss Marker, L1 and L2 — Scoped Authority Proofs and Open Event Obligations **[WC5.1]**\n1058\t\n1059\tFor a pair in the scope of C44, write `e1` for the earlier access in the abstract trace, issued by `c′`, and `e2` for the subsequent conflicting access by `c ≠ c′`. Let `r(e1,e2,l)` be the **loss marker** that ends the period containing `e1` during which `cap(·,c′,l)` is incompatible with the capability used by `e2`.\n1060\t\n1061\tThis marker may denote a release, a parameter yield, a derivation that reduces the relevant effective mode, frame exit, termination, unlock, or a justified holder/ownership change. It is a **pure logical marker** if its effect is only an authority change; a concrete step can additionally emit physical memory or synchronization events. The required loss threshold depends on the later access: a later exclusive write requires no incompatible surviving capability in `c′`; a later shared `Observe` read may be permitted after `c′` has lost its `Modify` exclusivity while retaining compatible `Observe`.\n1062\t\n1063\t**[WC5.1 — M4] Exploratory proof status (authority machine **and scoped abstract step-event model**, not a complete physical-event proof):**\n1064\t\n1065\t- **L1 — local capability loss:** `cap5_drop_own` is proved under `DDSound` for all **28 modeled kinds of authority-machine steps**, over reachable encapsulated traces: any strict reduction of a context's guarded-chain-excluding `cap5` originates in that context's own step. For an eligible direct-access exclusive pair, `lossStep_own` further proves a first strictly intermediate loss step `r` of the earlier context. In the one-event-per-step abstraction this gives `po(e1,r)`; placement among physical events inside a realized compound step remains open.\n1066\t- **L2 — state compatibility:** `cap5_compat` is proved: distinct contexts cannot simultaneously have incompatible `cap5` on the same leaf under the ordinary `Modify` exclusivity criterion. This is an authority-state theorem, not an ordering theorem.\n1067\t- **L2 — trace loss:** `handoff_loss_exists_C` is proved for conflicting **`read3`, `write3`, and `writeShared`** step-access pairs, at least one requiring `Modify`, over `EncapsulatedTrace5C` (the direct-exercise `ChainExcl` restriction, together with existing encapsulation and controller restrictions). It identifies a strictly intervening step of the earlier context reducing `cap5` on the leaf. The result transfers to the stronger tested `EncapsulatedTrace5R` by trace inclusion. Without this chain restriction, `handoff_loss_exists` is **refuted** under `EncapsulatedTrace5` by W-GC; `cap5_sound` is likewise refuted there. `cap5_sound_C` is proved under `EncapsulatedTrace5C`.\n1068\t\n1069\tThese are scoped results from the exploratory Lean formalization, **not** claims that C22.6 has been encoded for every operation or that the complete data-race-freedom theorem has been established. **[WC5.1 — M4]** `handoffOrdered_no_race` and its strict-graph analogue `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` prove that, given `HandoffOrdered`/`HandoffOrderedS`, eligible exclusive direct-access pairs are race-free in the corresponding abstract graph. They do **not** prove that arbitrary safe executions always satisfy C46. Full step-to-physical-event correspondence, access coverage beyond these three named primitives, physical `po` mapping, and actual protocol `rf`/`hb` remain obligations.\n1070\t\n1071\t**[WC5.1 — M4]** In the one-event-per-step abstraction, `lossStep_own` identifies the first relevant `r` and establishes its own-context, strictly intermediate position and hence the step-level `po` order. The exact **physical** marker position inside a realized compound step, partial byte footprints, and the concrete `po`/`rf`/`sw`/`hb` mapping remain open. A kernel-admitted trace outside C22.6 may refute the safe handoff premises without changing any frozen rule; such counterexamples must be retained and reported rather than concealed by hypotheses.\n1072\t\n1073\t## C46. HandoffOrdered — D-C10 **[WC5.1]**\n1074\t\n1075\tFor a pair within C44's scope, let `r(e1,e2,l)` denote the loss marker identified by the provisional capacity-period construction in C45. The **sole ordering requirement of `HandoffOrdered`** is:\n1076\t\n1077\t\\[\n1078\t  HandoffOrdered(e1,e2,l) \\;:\\!\\iff\\; hb(r(e1,e2,l),e2).\n1079\t\\]\n1080\t\n1081\t**[WC5.1]** The **authority-step parts** of L1 and L2 now have the restricted Lean proofs stated in C45 for `cap5` and the `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` access fragment under guarded-chain exclusion. These proofs are **not additional preconditions** of `HandoffOrdered`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The later `lossStep_own` proof establishes `po(e1,r)` for the scoped abstract one-event-per-step pairs; no **physical** `po` placement or concrete `hb` edge follows merely from authority proof. If the authority machine or concrete realization fails to provide a marker for a relevant pair, that is an unfulfilled proof obligation or counterexample, not permission to assume a hidden marker. The existence of **some** unrelated `sw` event is insufficient. When the event-model mapping has also established `po(e1,r)`, the protocol requirement `hb(r,e2)` implies `hb(e1,e2)` by transitivity. This is a **composition argument**, now proved for the abstract direct-access graph (`handoffOrdered_no_race`, `handoffOrdered_no_raceS`) under its stated run assumptions: C46 still specifies the ordering each admissible handoff protocol must justify, not a claim that R4 or an authority-only trace establishes physical `hb`. The requirement itself is unchanged.\n1082\t\n1083\tThe requirement applies to both **transmission** and **restitution** of authority. A bare inherited O3 `release` may restore an origin's effective right in the globally coherent abstract state, but it generates **no** `sw` by itself. The resulting trace can be kernel-`ok` and nevertheless be excluded from `SafeConcurrentTrace` if a later cross-context access lacks the required order.\n1084\t\n1085\t`join` is one reference protocol, not the only legal completion/handoff mechanism (C8). Other mechanisms may be added if they prove the requisite `hb` relation without introducing a new ambient authority source or weakening inherited invariants. Safe-language constructs that ensure these obligations belong to Specs 3/4.\n1086\t\n1087\t---\n1088\t\n1089\t# XI. Event Realization of Reference Protocols — Proposed Block D\n1090\t\n1091\t## C47. Spawn, Completion, and Join Events\n1092\t\n1093\tFor the reference by-reference `spawn` protocol, the creator's authority-yield **loss marker** precedes its `spawn` synchronization event within the parent context; the latter `sw`-precedes the child's `start(c)` marker. The initial child frame and parameter holder changes still obey C4/C5, and neither the event protocol nor `start(c)` duplicates an authority. A parent's operation executed after `spawn` is not ordered before a child's operation merely because the parent was the creator.\n1094\t\n1095\tFor `terminate`, every earlier event of the terminating context is `po`-before `complete(c)`; a successful `join(c)` in another context is the target of `sw` from that `complete(c)` event. The `join` event is placed in the caller's `po` before subsequent operations. By transitivity, a context's accesses preceding its completion are `hb`-before the caller's accesses following its successful join. An unsuccessful or non-enabled `join` contributes no such completed-observation edge.\n1096\t\n1097\tThe reference safe passing discipline may delay a creator's re-use of transferred exclusive capacity until appropriate completion observation, but C8 does **not** impose this restriction on all possible kernel traces or require universal structured concurrency. For a chain of delegated authorities, a successful join of an intermediate context does not imply completion of a surviving descendant. Correct reference reuse may require **transitive** joining or an equivalent ordering protocol.\n1098\t\n1099\tFor by-value `spawn`, access events emitted by the transfers belong to the actual producing context/phase, and their order must preserve C5's create–transfer–yield structure. Whether those byte accesses are correctly ordered with all other physical uses is part of the separate realization and safe-execution proofs, not a consequence of the logical transfer alone.\n1100\t\n1101\t## C48. Reference Mutex: Acquire, Unlock, and Grant Events\n1102\t\n1103\tThe reference `newMutex` initializes a physical controller in a state corresponding to `grant = none` and must establish the positional, identity and initialization requirements of C12–C13, C21 and Spec 1 §56. As clarified in C13, this is a physical non-atomic write of the controller synchronization value to an **already abstractly `Init`** leaf, not the abstract initialization of an `Uninit` structural leaf. C13.11 requires ordinary `Modify` authority, with applicable O10.1/O10.2 conditions. Its concrete metadata operations have events as required by C27/C29.\n1104\t\n1105\tA successful `acquire` has an atomic controller-acquisition event, for example a successful CAS/RMW with an acquire-capable memory order. This event must observe (`rf`) the appropriate released/available controller state, satisfy atomic coherence, and linearize the reservation of the controller before the new grant is made externally exercisable. A **gain marker** is placed `po`-after the successful acquire event. A failed CAS does not grant authority; a busy controller and an invalid request remain classified under C16, with `reject` taking priority per abstract state.\n1106\t\n1107\tAn `unlock` has a logical **loss marker** for the released grant, followed in `po` by a corresponding atomic release publication of the controller's available state. The abstract library transition remains coherent and does not expose an intermediate invalid grant/controller pairing. When a later acquiring RMW **directly reads** this release modification (`rf`), the protocol yields the requisite `sw` and thus `hb` from the preceding protected critical section to the succeeding one.\n1108\t\n1109\tThe event-level atomicity of this logical transition, the absence of an observable partial state, `SyncInv`/`GrantDiscipline` preservation and the correspondence to the concrete controller are still obligations of `OB-C05` and C25. C48 proposes the required event behavior; it does **not** claim these obligations have been discharged.\n1110\t\n1111\tThe initial Lean fragment limits the mutex `sw` and `LockOrder` proof to the **direct `rf`** pattern. Protocols with intervening RMWs or release sequences require an extension. When such sequences are added, the candidate uses the C++20 form in which intervening RMWs, but not ordinary same-thread stores, extend a release sequence. **[WC5.1 — M4] Reference comparison performed:** herdtools7 7.58 E1, with the unmodified `rc11.cat`, admits a release sequence extended by a **same-thread atomic non-RMW** `store(f, 2, relaxed)` following `store(f, 1, release)`. A later `acquire` reading the `2` is synchronized with the earlier `release` in that reference; the tested ordinary data read has no race. The candidate C++20-form rule stated here **does not** extend that release sequence through a non-RMW atomic store, so the analogous race has now been **observed by executing G18 in the finite ERN direct-`rf` fragment**, but **not** by executing an ERN implementation of the C++20-form release-sequence rule. Because G18 has no release-sequence semantics at all, it does not decide whether that candidate extension or the `rc11.cat` extension is correct for ERN. This is a documented comparative divergence, not a new normative selection or evidence of an ERN compiler realization.\n1112\t\n1113\t**[WC5.2 — M4 reference controller evidence]** Under the declared model (`newMutex`: non-atomic initial `0` write; successful `acquire`: CAS/RMW reading `0` and writing `1`; `unlock`: atomic `0` release; `resetLock`: atomic `0` relaxed), with `mo` constructed from protocol steps and every successful RMW reading its `mo` predecessor, `ctl_invariant` and `ctlGraph_coherent` hold for the modeled traces. The `bridge` theorem proves that the strict abstract unlock-to-acquire edge induces a direct `rf` from that unlock to the acquisition; `bridge_sw_ref` gives `sw` under reference release/acquire orders. Conversely `bridge_converse` recovers the strict edge from direct `rf` under `CtlFixed`, and W-TR refutes that converse without controller stability. This proof is **conditional on the constructed event graph and declared realization**; it does not prove that compiled controller operations have that history. Successful CAS attempts are modeled; failed attempts, actual byte metadata and internal gain/loss marker events remain open.\n1114\t\n1115\t## C49. LockOrder, Recovery, and Delegation\n1116\t\n1117\t**`LockOrder`** is the obligation that the protected accesses in an earlier critical section of one mutex are `hb`-before conflicting protected accesses in a later critical section whose lock-acquisition event receives the preceding release. The proof must use the concrete `rf`, `mo`, `sw`, `po`, and grant-lifecycle relations, not merely the total order of abstract `SyncEntry` updates or the fact that controller identities match.\n1118\t\n1119\tFor a successful `resetLock`, the library must prove exclusive recovery authority under C18, a coherent physical controller update, and the event-order consequences of any protected memory that becomes newly accessible. Recovery is **not automatically** a substitute for an acquire/release edge missing from an abandoned grant. A proposed recovery protocol that cannot establish necessary ordering is not justified for safe concurrent access. **[WC5.1 — M4]** C18 imposes no precondition on the recorded grant state when `resetLock(e)` executes: the recorded grant may be `none`. The exploratory kernel admits `resetLock` on an already free lock, as witnessed by W-RL. This does **not** introduce any new C18 precondition or imply a new `sw` edge.\n1120\t\n1121\tWhen a grant is reborrowed, passed to another frame, or transmitted between contexts, the reference implementation must show how ordinary authority rules, grant constraints, and the relevant handoff protocol preserve `hb` across the whole path. Exiting a frame that released a grant without `unlock` does not imply controller release, and a logically returned capability does not imply a physical publication.\n1122\t\n1123\t**[WC5.1 — M4] Strict abstract mutex-edge evidence:** The first experimental graph connected `unlock(e)` to the next successful `acquire(e)` by entry identity alone. W-RL (`resetLock(e)` between them) and W-TR (whole-mutex `transfer` changing `ControlOf(e)`) prove that this approximation can invent `hb`. The corrected **abstract** edge is absent if an intervening `resetLock(e)` occurs, or if `ControlOf(e)` becomes undefined or changes leaf from the state after `unlock` through the state before `acquire`. The strict graph excludes W-RL/W-TR, preserves W-RL₀/W-RL-J/W-TR-J and the earlier reference witnesses, and proves conditional `lock_ordersS`/`lock_ordersS_gaps`. **Any abstraction of the C48 mutex `sw` edge must respect interventions on the controller; the definitive physical rule remains the unchanged direct-`rf` condition on the same atomic unit in C32/C48.** **[WC5.2 — M4]** For the **constructed reference controller graph**, `bridge` now proves the direct-read implication; `completeness7_run` classifies the modeled controller footprints for `Run7` under reachable initial state and `CtlFixed`. The two strict abstract guards still do **not** prove that a target physical acquisition reads the earlier release (`OB-C05`), or that all compiled metadata and composite-step accesses are covered (`OB-C20`).\n1124\t\n1125\t## C50. Realization, Visibility, and Physical Correspondence\n1126\t\n1127\tThe implementation must relate every relevant successful `S_C` operation and its associated event graph to a concrete memory execution under the frozen Spec 1 §82 representation obligations. For `Atomic` storage this includes unit indivisibility, alignment, coherent `rf`/`mo`, and the compilation of its memory orders. For ordinary, `Synchronized`, and `Confined` storage it includes byte-level access coverage, validity, exclusivity and the required event ordering.\n1128\t\n1129\tThis obligation is **stronger** than establishing a sequence of abstract `ok` outcomes. It includes preservation of actual initialized bytes, pointer provenance, invalidation conditions, owners and holders, and physically executable synchronization operations. In particular, no successful abstraction proof may conceal an unmodeled physical access after invalidation (§82.7 / `OB-C12`).\n1130\t\n1131\tClaims about compilation to x86-TSO, ARM, or POWER, including the potential cost of a proposed no-thin-air axiom, must be supported by a target-specific compilation or refinement argument. The initial event fragment is not itself such an argument.\n1132\t\n1133\t---\n1134\t\n1135\t# XII. Safe Executions and Data-Race Freedom — Proposed Block D\n1136\t\n1137\t## C51. EncapsulatedTrace and SafeConcurrentTrace **[WC5.1]**\n1138\t\n1139\tTwo **distinct** restrictions apply to completed traces:\n1140\t\n1141\t1. `EncapsulatedTrace`: the safe **reference-mutex** trace contract of C22, including the WC5 D-C6 restriction against ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` and **[WC5.1]** the C22.6 restriction on all right-justification holder chains traversing a live `guarded` (subject to the specific C18.1 `resetLock` exception). This restricts the **public safe interface**, not O9/O10 or any inherited kernel operation. A kernel-`ok` `read3(lock)` or a kernel-`ok` access through live `guarded` without the required grant can be outside this contract without being a counterexample to Spec 1. The earlier `Encap5R` checks only a subset of C22.6; the later `EncapFull`/`RunFull` exploratory model covers the 28 modeled step kinds and their inventoried right premises, while `OB-C28` retains safe-interface enforcement and the stated modeling/exhaustiveness gaps.\n1142\t2. `SafeConcurrentTrace`: a proposed subset of `EncapsulatedTrace` in which (a) all relevant event-graph consistency conditions hold; (b) `Atomic` accesses obey C37–C42; (c) `Confined` has at most one context with a present **guarded-chain-excluding** capability in each state and any later cross-context conflict satisfies C46; (d) every non-atomic conflict under a `Synchronized` contract is either protected by a proven exclusive handoff or ordered by an established synchronization mechanism; and (e) all applicable `HandoffOrdered`, lifecycle, and `LockOrder` obligations are fulfilled. **[WC5.1]** Its safe-interface premise includes all of C22.6, not just the currently encoded `ChainExcl`/`PlaceExcl` subset; proof of target interface soundness and physical access coverage remains open, notwithstanding the modeled `RunFull` premise-coverage results.\n1143\t\n1144\t`SafeConcurrentTrace` is a **semantic safe-execution contract**. It is not the set of all kernel-`ok` traces. The earlier witness `read3(lock)` concurrent with `writeShared(lock)` demonstrates why `EncapsulatedTrace` in WC4 was not enough; under C42/C22 it is excluded from the candidate safe interface while the ordinary kernel operation remains available.\n1145\t\n1146\tThe statement “a safe, well-typed ERN program generates only `SafeConcurrentTrace` executions” is a **separate soundness obligation** of Specs 3/4 (and the relevant safe libraries), not a theorem derived merely from a name or by construction of this predicate.\n1147\t\n1148\t## C52. Candidate Data-Race-Freedom Theorem and Proof Partition **[WC5.1]**\n1149\t\n1150\t**Target theorem (open, not yet proved).** Every execution graph satisfying the stated `EventConsistent` obligations and arising from a `SafeConcurrentTrace` is free from data races as defined in C33 and frozen Spec 1 §18.\n1151\t\n1152\tThe proof is deliberately partitioned by **conflicting access pair**, not just by storage label:\n1153\t\n1154\t| Pair or discipline | Intended argument | Independent proof boundary |\n1155\t|---|---|---|\n1156\t| Ordinary non-`SharedMutable` leaves | Conflicts require exclusive ordinary mutation. Show the loss marker exists (L2) and follows the prior access in `po` (L1); a certified handoff provides `hb(r,e2)` and therefore `hb(e1,e2)`. | **[WC5.1 — M4]** `lossStep_own` and `handoffOrdered_no_raceS` now prove the abstract direct-access composition conditional on C46. Proving C46 for all safe executions and concrete physical events remains open; R4 alone is insufficient. |\n1157\t| `Atomic`, atomic/atomic | Both accesses are atomic, so the non-atomic conjunct of the Spec 1 §18 race definition is false. | Atomicity of entire unit and `rf`/`mo` coherence must still be established. |\n1158\t| `Atomic`, mixed or non-atomic/non-atomic | C42 restricts every ordinary member to effective/exercisable `Modify`, giving abstract exclusivity, then uses certified handoff ordering for accesses of distinct contexts. | D-C9 enforcement, L1/L2, C46, concrete access coverage. |\n1159\t| `Confined` | At most one context has an attributable capability at a time; cross-context conflicting accesses require an ordered handoff. | Confinement contract and L1/L2/C46; exclusivity alone does not imply `hb`. |\n1160\t| `Synchronized` with exclusive ordinary writer | Use the ordinary exclusive-handoff argument. | L1/L2/C46 and proof of the particular protocol. |\n1161\t| `Synchronized` under shared compatible observation | The generic storage contract requires conflicting non-atomic accesses to be sufficiently `hb`-ordered. | **Composition case**: the requirement is an assumption until each declared mechanism proves its actual `sw` and `hb` edges. |\n1162\t\n1163\tThe generic `Synchronized` case is **not presented as a non-circular proof**. Likewise, assuming `HandoffOrdered` in a generic trace establishes only a composition theorem. **[WC5.1]** The former L1/L2 proof boundaries in the table now have **scoped authority-machine proofs** (`cap5_drop_own`, `cap5_compat`, `cap5_sound_C`, `handoff_loss_exists_C`) for directly modeled `read3`/`write3`/`writeShared` accesses under the tested chain restriction. They are **not** proofs for physical accesses generated by `store`, `transfer`, `issue`, allocation, or other compound operations; those require `OB-C20` and safe-interface enforcement, notwithstanding the expanded `RunFull` abstract premise proofs under `OB-C28`. **[WC5.1 — M4]** The abstract graph now proves the first own-context loss marker with step-level `po` (`lossStep_own`), the **conditional** composition `HandoffOrderedS → ¬ RaceS` (`handoffOrdered_no_raceS`), negative missing-`HBS` results for W-HO/W-HO′/W-HO″, and positive ordered counterparts W-HO-J/W-HO″-J/`lc_trace`. W-RL/W-TR show why the old mutex edge was overbroad; their strict-graph failures and join-based positive companions are proved. These are scoped **one-event-per-step** results, not concrete data-race-freedom for all realizations. The substantial remaining work is physical marker placement/byte-event coverage, concrete `rf`/`mo`/`sw` and controller `LockOrder`, safe-program/interface soundness, and proving C46 for every safe execution.\n1164\t\n1165\tA reference-mutex theorem may conclude DRF for protected ordinary accesses only after the concrete controller protocol supplies `LockOrder` and the requisite `rf`/`sw` links. A `.cat` model that simply discards racy graphs does not prove that safe ERN programs could not generate such graphs before that filter.\n1166\t\n1167\t## C53. Proof Boundary, Conservativity, and No Freeze\n1168\t\n1169\tThe following obligations remain separate and **unproved unless supported by named proofs**:\n1170\t\n1171\t1. **Inherited-operation conservativity:** the unchanged Spec 1 O1–O16 must retain their projected kernel meaning. Because the exploratory Spec 1 Lean `WriteSharedPre` does not explicitly represent O11.3's shared-discipline predicate, T1 must be **restated and proved** for `K_C.SharedDiscipline`: every concurrency-`ok` inherited write projects to a Spec 1 `ok` under its valid `K`, with equality of relevant admissible sets only under explicitly established matching/accept-all discipline assumptions. No equality is asserted without proof.\n1172\t2. **Authority lemmas:** L1, L2, and any preservation properties required by the chosen definition of `cap` and holder attribution.\n1173\t3. **Graph and realization lemmas:** pure-authority erasure, coverage of physical accesses, full `EventConsistent` coherence beyond the finite one-unit fragment, **target** direct-`rf` protocol correctness beyond the conditional constructed-controller bridge, and reconstruction/preservation under Spec 1 §82.\n1174\t4. **Program/interface soundness:** safe interfaces exclude kernel-admissible operations outside C22/C42; the typing and library rules of Specs 3/4 prove that safe programs yield `SafeConcurrentTrace`.\n1175\t5. **Full memory-model selection:** no-thin-air, release sequences, global `seq_cst` semantics, cross-target compilation, and the proven relationship (inclusion or equivalence, if appropriate) of the final ERN model to any reference model.\n1176\t\n1177\tThe baseline comparison is **three-level**: complete external RC11 reference; the explicitly scoped exploratory ERN-C/D Lean fragment; and the eventual normative ERN model. No equivalence is implied by similar names or successful litmus tests. The working decisions D-C1–D-C10 remain proposals pending consolidation and proof, and no clause of the frozen Charter or Spec 1 v0.1 is changed here.\n1178\t\n1179\t---\n1180\t\n1181\t# Drafting choices\n1182\t\n1183\tThese choices resolve ambiguities for this **non-normative working draft**; they are not amendments to the frozen Charter or Spec 1, not approved D-C decisions, and not substitutes for the proof obligations above.\n1184\t\n1185\t1. **Preservation of WC4 [WC5.3]:** The following clauses are textually identical to WC4: C1–C12, C14–C15, C19–C20, C23 and C24. C25 differs from WC4 only in the corrected reference to C22.1–6. The original seven numbered `SyncInv` invariants of C21 are unchanged; explanatory material follows C21.5 without adding an invariant. C13 retains the accepted item 11 and controller-initialization clarification; C18 retains the accepted item 4 and leaves C18.1–C18.3, outcomes, and effects unchanged; C22 retains the accepted item 4 and the earlier item 6, and clarifies item 1 without narrowing its ban on **any** ordinary authority issued directly from `guarded`, including by compound steps. Items C22.2–3 and C22.5–6 retain their rule text. C16–C18 remain marked **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]** as earlier candidate material. The WC4 Part V seed table, including S10 and S11 corrections, is preserved. Other revisions record Milestone 4 proof evidence, reference fixes, and unresolved obligations; no other candidate rule is silently changed.\n1186\t2. **`SyncAuthorized` and contention:** C14's original combined authorization predicate is preserved exactly. C16 classifies the *enclosing library call*, separating C14.1/C14.3 invalidity (`reject`) from C14.2 or physical controller occupation (temporarily non-enabled). This choice does not rewrite C14 or C15 and requires a refined Lean operation classification.\n1187\t3. **Additional event clauses:** new C26–C53 clauses are appended as Parts VII–XII after the preserved WC4 Parts I–VI, so the original parts remain complete and in their original order. Attribute flags `acc` and `sync` may coexist; pure authority markers have neither. `start(c)`/`complete(c)` are separate lifecycle markers, avoiding a contradiction when the child's first substantive operation is an authority step.\n1188\t4. **Access mapping and memory representation:** one high-level step may generate several physical access events; `transfer`, by-value `spawn`, `store`, `issue`, and library metadata cannot be declared physically inert. Only **pure** logical markers are candidates for erasure, with `po` preserved by projection.\n1189\t5. **`SharedMutable`/O11.3:** the existing predicate `K.SharedMutable` is not replaced. `K_C.SharedDiscipline` is additional. In this draft, “applies” in O11.3 means that the discipline is **satisfied by the access**; the open Lean representation gap and conditional T1 are reported rather than hidden. A region mixing unsupported discipline kinds is rejected by the reference contract, pending any separately justified composite design.\n1190\t6. **Atomicity and operation syntax:** the candidate concurrency-order annotations on inherited `writeShared` do not revise the frozen sequential O11 signature; a successful conditional atomic RMW is a `writeShared` mutation, while a failed comparison is a `readShared` observation. The new `readShared` is restricted to an entire single `Atomic` leaf. A multi-leaf atomic write is `reject`, not an implicit sequence of atomic stores. `read3`/`write3` remain non-atomic at the kernel, and D-C9 is a safe-interface rule.\n1191\t7. **Memory-model scope:** the order names include `seq_cst` but the exploratory Lean fragment excludes full global SC constraints and release sequences until they are formalized. RC11 is reference-only; `acyclic(po ∪ rf)` is an optional named assumption, not the chosen ERN normative no-thin-air rule. The proposed later release-sequence extension uses the C++20 form, with any mismatch from reference RC11 stated during comparison.\n1192\t8. **Handoff and completion [WC5.1/5.2]:** `HandoffOrdered` remains limited to exclusive conflicting pairs with a non-atomic member, with guarded-chain-excluding `cap` per `(context, leaf)` and a candidate event loss marker `r`. `join` is not exclusive, a bare O3 `release` creates no `sw`, and a child or grandchild that has not been appropriately ordered remains a possible source of an unsafe trace. The **scoped authority-step parts** of L1/L2 and the **abstract step-event** `po`/conditional `hb` composition are now proved as described in C45–C46. Concrete physical `po`/`rf`/`hb`, complete access coverage, and universal safe-program soundness are not.\n1193\t9. **Scope of the claimed DRF result:** `SafeConcurrentTrace` excludes kernel-permitted unsafe-interface behaviors; its DRF statement is a **target theorem**, not a completed guarantee. The generic `Synchronized` case is a composition assumption, while the obligation that well-typed safe code produces such traces belongs to Specs 3/4 and specific libraries. Physical refinement and absence of accesses after invalidation remain separate.\n1194\t\n1195\t10. **[WC5.1] Guarded-chain interpretation:** C22.6 is a proposed safe-interface trace restriction. It requires at least one valid holder-chain justification avoiding any live `guarded`, not all hypothetical derivations to avoid it; the full set of right premises and relevant pre/post/intermediate states must be covered. It does not alter Spec 1 §43. `resetLock` requires the narrowly scoped C18.1 own-`guarded` exception, with the `slot`-holder justification constrained against **other** live guards. Model-level premise coverage is now supported by `PremG`/`RunFull` and named theorems; `OB-C28` remains open for target safe-interface enforcement, §78.1 `reset` metadata premises and the manually inspected inventory points. This is not a physical-access proof.\n1196\t11. **[WC5.1] `cap5` and proof status:** the proof-oriented C43 `cap` is the guarded-chain-excluding `cap5`, distinct from raw contextual capacity (refuted L1 under W-GR). The report proves step-local loss for 28 kinds and state compatibility, and proves trace loss for the explicit access fragment under `Encap5C`/`Encap5R`. Step-level `po` and conditional abstract `hb` composition are proved (not unconditional C46 for all safe traces); concrete physical `po`/`hb`, `rf`/`mo`, complete byte-access coverage, and safe-language exposure remain unproved.\n1197\t12. **[WC5.3 — M4] Evidence boundary:** corpus v14, the earlier strict-step-event and herdtools reports, `spec2-m4-eventmodel.md`, `spec2-m4-mutexreal.md`, and `spec2-m4-encap.md` (final complement including `KillKinds`) document the finite event fragment, controller-model and complete-encapsulation proofs, and adversarial witnesses. The latest reported Lean audit has **2,822 theorems (1,878 non-internal)**, axioms confined to `{propext, Quot.sound}`, and successful `lake build`; this drafting pass has **not** independently re-executed them. `rc11.cat` E1 is a reference-model result; G18 is an executed **direct-`rf` fragment** result, not an executed C++20 release-sequence model. The modeled `RunFull`/`PremG` results have precise initial-state, `DDSound`, and `StaticsOK` scopes. Actual target physical direct-`rf`, all composite **physical events**, initialization ordering and safe-language soundness remain open. The earlier `Run6 ⊆ Run7` inclusion is proved for reachable initial-state runs only.\n1198\t\n1199\t**End of Working Candidate 5.3 — complete non-normative exploratory candidate, Milestone 4 complete-encapsulation evidence incorporated. [WC5.3]**\n1200\t"}]}}
{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","id":"3f13f401-3a6a-4f66-8a20-0df7927f83c8","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:52:35.747Z","version":"2.1.321","querySource":"sdk","shapeHash":"9f52ff250c3bf9a62496bf9c3c72b82c93ae96b1c01a28d5faa78501f67d1ec2","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","dangerous-tool-use-2026-09-03","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","thinking-display-updates-2026-08-18","afk-mode-2026-01-31","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"updates"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"xhigh"}},"base":"3106323b-b11a-4aab-8777-6faf1e12ae0b","keep":32,"tail":["21d6598ef63cf040e5e931d9008542371d0925144c8a520f165bcccd4b945570","dbe2ec60276821c6c5b8e2f11c8fdcc315d0ce959dcbac22ce3ec2b8e41caa5c"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",33,"content",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"ef8f317fd646c5c02f4ec29d1f98dfe46285b8c96717fbac42ff2770beda89b0"}
