{"parentUuid":"032f81ee-5c77-44a1-9492-f70ca391b352","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch2TdieNVBig2eNY2GNQH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018PwJC8azGjM8SzQJbvywo3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":467,"limit":70},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1758,"cache_read_input_tokens":274302,"output_tokens":271,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1758,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":271,"cache_read_input_tokens":274302,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1758,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1758},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":846,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018PwJC8azGjM8SzQJbvywo3":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":467,"limit":70}},"serverClassifierRequest":"e12d339e-cb57-4466-8c37-13d1cb85b4f2","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch2TdiMWjULAikuK6gENQ","requestRef":"2747dc6c-336a-4041-b825-225fc79285c3","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"3a76d00d-2340-4e49-bf74-79bf48f5ebb9","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:54:13.533Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"3a76d00d-2340-4e49-bf74-79bf48f5ebb9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018PwJC8azGjM8SzQJbvywo3","type":"tool_result","content":"467\t## 40. Logical Places, Leaves, and Ownership\n468\t\n469\tA place has an identity, optionally a structural parent, an abstract `kind`, an owner `owner : Frame | Place`, and a logical death marker. Structural parenthood `partOf(p,q)` describes the subplaces of a value; `owner` describes **who keeps the storage** of a root place valid. An owned place may lie outside its owner's direct footprint; ownership and composition are therefore distinct.\n470\t\n471\tA **leaf** is a place with no structural children. `footprint(p)` contains the leaves of `p`, and access rights over a place are evaluated on those leaves. Places transitively owned by a value are not automatically leaves of its `footprint`. A logical unit of the access discipline does not automatically constitute an additional allocation in the elementary memory machine.\n472\t\n473\tA place `p` is **usable** (`Usable(s,p)`) if and only if:\n474\t\n475\t1. `p` exists and is not marked dead;\n476\t2. if its owner is a frame, that frame belongs to the stack;\n477\t3. if its owner is a place `q`, then `q` is itself usable and the chain terminates at a frame in the stack.\n478\t\n479\tAn ownership cycle makes no place usable. `placeFrame(p)` is the terminal frame of this chain when `p` is usable. Structural parts share their parent's owner and death marker. A value may be logically ended by `endUse` without the place that contained it becoming unusable; the substorages it owned may, however, die.\n480\t\n481\tA **root place** has no structural parent. Every **usable** root place has a root authority of own mode `Modify`, with no origin, held exactly by that place's owner (`RootHeld`). This authority cannot be transferred in isolation; root ownership follows a legal transfer of the owning value when necessary.\n482\t\n483\t## 41. Leaf Initialization States\n484\t\n485\tEvery tracked leaf has exactly one of the states `Uninit`, `Init`, or `Moved`.\n486\t\n487\t- `Uninit`: no initialized value available in this leaf.\n488\t- `Init`: initialized value, subject to type, representation, and storage obligations.\n489\t- `Moved`: value consumed by transfer, inaccessible as its former value.\n490\t\n491\tAggregates may be partially initialized. Both `Uninit` and `Moved` prohibit ordinary reads, while remaining distinct states for diagnostics and end-of-use obligations. An initialization state creates no access permission.\n492\t\n493\t## 42. Authorities, Modes, Holders, and Origins\n494\t\n495\tAn authority conceptually has the following fields:\n496\t\n497\t```text\n498\tAuthority {\n499\t    id\n500\t    mode: Observe | Modify\n501\t    origin?: AuthorityId\n502\t    footprint: Set<Leaf>\n503\t    holder: Frame | Place\n504\t    active: Bool\n505\t}\n506\t```\n507\t\n508\t`origin` links a derived authority to its current origin. Authorities have fresh identities and well-formed origin chains. A root authority has no origin; a derived authority has one. Creating derived rights does not increase the scope or permitted mode of the origin.\n509\t\n510\tThe **holder** (`holder`) is not the **owner** (`owner`). An authority derived by `derive` or `split` is initially held by the current frame; `store` or a permitted transfer may make it held by a place. The derived `issue` operation proceeds through `split` followed by `store`. `release` does not release a root. There is no persistent `Suspended` field: suspension of rights is inferred from active descendants and applies leaf by leaf.\n511\t\n512\t## 43. Effective Rights and Exercise of Authorities\n513\t\n514\tFor an authority `a` and a leaf `l`, `effective(s,a,l)` is derived from the active descendants of `a` whose footprint contains `l`:\n515\t\n516\t1. if `a` is inactive or does not cover `l`, its effective mode is absent;\n517\t2. if no active descendant covers `l`, the effective mode is the own mode of `a`;\n518\t3. if an active descendant with own mode `Modify` covers `l`, the effective mode of `a` is absent;\n519\t4. otherwise, when the covering active descendants are `Observe`, the effective mode of `a` on `l` is `Observe`.\n520\t\n521\tThe effect is **local to each leaf**: after splitting a region, the origin may retain effective `Modify` over undistributed leaves.\n522\t\n523\tRequested modes are ordered `Observe ≤ Modify`. `ExercisableAt(s,a,m)` means:\n524\t\n525\t- `a` is active and its own mode permits `m`;\n526\t- either `a` is held by the current frame;\n527\t- or `a` is held by a place `p`, and the current frame has, over **all** leaves of `p`, an authority itself exercisable in mode `m` with sufficient effective mode.\n528\t\n529\tThe latter clause is recursive along the holder chain. Thus, being active or covering a leaf does not automatically make an authority exercisable. `HasRight(s,m,p)` means the current frame has exercise in mode `m` and sufficient effective mode **over every leaf in `p`'s footprint**. Every operation requires the modes it actually needs; an authority's own mode alone is insufficient.\n530\t\n531\t## 44. Dependencies and Effective Anchors\n532\t\n533\t`D(p)` is the set of authorities serving as **validity anchors** for place `p`. An anchor is valid as long as the authority it names is active. Dependencies of contained values are accounted for transitively. Relevant dependencies follow the value during transfer; those of its container do not automatically follow a part transferred in isolation.\n534\t\n535\tFor an authority `x`, its **effective anchor** is the root reached by traversing its origin chain. It does not necessarily coincide with its current holder. Lifetime constraints are calculated from the frame of that effective anchor and the frames of places that remain usable. A dependency cannot create a right or artificially extend a lifetime.\n536\t"}]},"uuid":"cc84f715-c0bf-4117-a7a8-c52960ad7e65","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:54:13.548Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","content":"## 40. Logical Places, Leaves, and Ownership\n\nA place has an identity, optionally a structural parent, an abstract `kind`, an owner `owner : Frame | Place`, and a logical death marker. Structural parenthood `partOf(p,q)` describes the subplaces of a value; `owner` describes **who keeps the storage** of a root place valid. An owned place may lie outside its owner's direct footprint; ownership and composition are therefore distinct.\n\nA **leaf** is a place with no structural children. `footprint(p)` contains the leaves of `p`, and access rights over a place are evaluated on those leaves. Places transitively owned by a value are not automatically leaves of its `footprint`. A logical unit of the access discipline does not automatically constitute an additional allocation in the elementary memory machine.\n\nA place `p` is **usable** (`Usable(s,p)`) if and only if:\n\n1. `p` exists and is not marked dead;\n2. if its owner is a frame, that frame belongs to the stack;\n3. if its owner is a place `q`, then `q` is itself usable and the chain terminates at a frame in the stack.\n\nAn ownership cycle makes no place usable. `placeFrame(p)` is the terminal frame of this chain when `p` is usable. Structural parts share their parent's owner and death marker. A value may be logically ended by `endUse` without the place that contained it becoming unusable; the substorages it owned may, however, die.\n\nA **root place** has no structural parent. Every **usable** root place has a root authority of own mode `Modify`, with no origin, held exactly by that place's owner (`RootHeld`). This authority cannot be transferred in isolation; root ownership follows a legal transfer of the owning value when necessary.\n\n## 41. Leaf Initialization States\n\nEvery tracked leaf has exactly one of the states `Uninit`, `Init`, or `Moved`.\n\n- `Uninit`: no initialized value available in this leaf.\n- `Init`: initialized value, subject to type, representation, and storage obligations.\n- `Moved`: value consumed by transfer, inaccessible as its former value.\n\nAggregates may be partially initialized. Both `Uninit` and `Moved` prohibit ordinary reads, while remaining distinct states for diagnostics and end-of-use obligations. An initialization state creates no access permission.\n\n## 42. Authorities, Modes, Holders, and Origins\n\nAn authority conceptually has the following fields:\n\n```text\nAuthority {\n    id\n    mode: Observe | Modify\n    origin?: AuthorityId\n    footprint: Set<Leaf>\n    holder: Frame | Place\n    active: Bool\n}\n```\n\n`origin` links a derived authority to its current origin. Authorities have fresh identities and well-formed origin chains. A root authority has no origin; a derived authority has one. Creating derived rights does not increase the scope or permitted mode of the origin.\n\nThe **holder** (`holder`) is not the **owner** (`owner`). An authority derived by `derive` or `split` is initially held by the current frame; `store` or a permitted transfer may make it held by a place. The derived `issue` operation proceeds through `split` followed by `store`. `release` does not release a root. There is no persistent `Suspended` field: suspension of rights is inferred from active descendants and applies leaf by leaf.\n\n## 43. Effective Rights and Exercise of Authorities\n\nFor an authority `a` and a leaf `l`, `effective(s,a,l)` is derived from the active descendants of `a` whose footprint contains `l`:\n\n1. if `a` is inactive or does not cover `l`, its effective mode is absent;\n2. if no active descendant covers `l`, the effective mode is the own mode of `a`;\n3. if an active descendant with own mode `Modify` covers `l`, the effective mode of `a` is absent;\n4. otherwise, when the covering active descendants are `Observe`, the effective mode of `a` on `l` is `Observe`.\n\nThe effect is **local to each leaf**: after splitting a region, the origin may retain effective `Modify` over undistributed leaves.\n\nRequested modes are ordered `Observe ≤ Modify`. `ExercisableAt(s,a,m)` means:\n\n- `a` is active and its own mode permits `m`;\n- either `a` is held by the current frame;\n- or `a` is held by a place `p`, and the current frame has, over **all** leaves of `p`, an authority itself exercisable in mode `m` with sufficient effective mode.\n\nThe latter clause is recursive along the holder chain. Thus, being active or covering a leaf does not automatically make an authority exercisable. `HasRight(s,m,p)` means the current frame has exercise in mode `m` and sufficient effective mode **over every leaf in `p`'s footprint**. Every operation requires the modes it actually needs; an authority's own mode alone is insufficient.\n\n## 44. Dependencies and Effective Anchors\n\n`D(p)` is the set of authorities serving as **validity anchors** for place `p`. An anchor is valid as long as the authority it names is active. Dependencies of contained values are accounted for transitively. Relevant dependencies follow the value during transfer; those of its container do not automatically follow a part transferred in isolation.\n\nFor an authority `x`, its **effective anchor** is the root reached by traversing its origin chain. It does not necessarily coincide with its current holder. Lifetime constraints are calculated from the frame of that effective anchor and the frames of places that remain usable. A dependency cannot create a right or artificially extend a lifetime.\n","numLines":70,"startLine":467,"totalLines":1051}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3a76d00d-2340-4e49-bf74-79bf48f5ebb9","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"cc84f715-c0bf-4117-a7a8-c52960ad7e65","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch2TdieNVBig2eNY2GNQH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KM2PYmMt4T7YEo4BsSxHLD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":652,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1758,"cache_read_input_tokens":274302,"output_tokens":271,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1758,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":271,"cache_read_input_tokens":274302,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1758,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1758},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":802,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KM2PYmMt4T7YEo4BsSxHLD":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":652,"limit":300}},"serverClassifierRequest":"e12d339e-cb57-4466-8c37-13d1cb85b4f2","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch2TdiMWjULAikuK6gENQ","requestRef":"2747dc6c-336a-4041-b825-225fc79285c3","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"7a351ee2-59e5-44c2-83b7-8e508fe30712","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:54:14.341Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"7a351ee2-59e5-44c2-83b7-8e508fe30712","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c6363317-3632-459e-8779-ebed992e8329","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KM2PYmMt4T7YEo4BsSxHLD","type":"tool_result","content":"652\t## 58. Transition Outcomes\n653\t\n654\tEvery operation has a conceptual outcome among:\n655\t\n656\t```text\n657\tOutcome3 = ok(new_state) | reject | fail\n658\t```\n659\t\n660\t`reject` means that the static discipline does not permit the step; it is neither an implicit runtime trap nor `ub` in the elementary memory machine. `fail` is a **defined failure** leaving the state unchanged; **only `alloc` may produce it in the O1–O16 kernel**. Other kernel operations produce `ok` or `reject`. Library-abstraction operations, including `issue` (§77), may define their own `fail` cases. Where such failure is provided, static preconditions are checked before dynamic availability, according to the operation's contract.\n661\t\n662\tAn `ok` transition must satisfy its preconditions, apply exactly its prescribed effects, and preserve the relevant invariants. No transition fabricates a permission to make its conclusion true. Holder and anchor changes are evaluated in the **resulting state** when a rule explicitly so provides.\n663\t\n664\t## 59. Implicit Preservation of Modes and Anchors\n665\t\n666\tChanges in effective authority are **derived** from active authorities and their origins; no `Suspended` indicator is mutated. Access operations (O9–O11) do not change an anchor without an explicitly authorized effect of transfer, `depend`, `store`, release, or reanchoring. Leaves becoming `Init` do so through a permitted write (`write3`, `writeShared`, `store`, including during a derived `issue`) or a transfer from a corresponding source leaf already `Init`; leaves initialized in `S3.init` are declared as such.\n667\t\n668\t## 60. O1 — Derivation of an Authority\n669\t\n670\t**Preconditions**\n671\t\n672\t1. `source` is an active derived or root authority, and `ExercisableAt(s,source,mode)` holds; its origin chain and anchors are valid: every authority of its origin chain, its effective anchor included, is active.\n673\t2. The leaves of `region` exist and belong to the footprint of `source`; `mode` does not exceed the available effective right on these leaves.\n674\t3. The addition preserves exclusivity I1: no unrelated active authority becomes incompatible with the derived right, and already-active descendants are respected.\n675\t4. The new holding frame is the live current frame. The admissible state's footprint and anchoring restrictions remain true.\n676\t\n677\t**`ok` effect:** A new active authority with fresh identity, origin `source`, requested mode and footprint, and held by the current frame is added. The source remains active; its effective modes are recomputed per leaf. No storage or place anchor is created. Multiple compatible observations remain permitted. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n678\t\n679\t## 61. O2 — Splitting Authorities\n680\t\n681\t**Preconditions**\n682\t\n683\t1. `source` is active and exercisable by the current frame with sufficient modification rights over the relevant leaves.\n684\t2. Each region lies within the available, effectively modifiable footprint of the source.\n685\t3. Regions are pairwise disjoint under structural composition or contract `K.DeclaredDisjoint`, subject to obligation `DDSound(K)`; no exclusive right already distributed is overlapped.\n686\t4. Creation of the derivatives respects origins, conservation I1/I2, and validity obligations.\n687\t\n688\t**`ok` effect:** An active derived `Modify` authority held by the current frame is created for each region. `source` remains active; its effective modification capability disappears only over distributed leaves and remains on undistributed leaves. Authorities are split logically: `split` performs no physical allocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n689\t\n690\tAn unjustified declaration of disjointness may invalidate the proof: without `DDSound`, an accepted `split` may violate I1. Constructing zero-sized subregions is not identified with an allocation of the elementary memory machine; the case of an empty shape in `alloc` is distinct.\n691\t\n692\t## 62. O3 — End of a Derived Borrow\n693\t\n694\t**Preconditions**\n695\t\n696\t1. `authority` is active **and derived**: an authority with no origin is rejected.\n697\t2. It is held by the current frame, or by a place `p` on which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n698\t3. No active descendant of the authority prevents its release; no other still-usable value may lose a required anchor.\n699\t4. If the authority is stored in its holding place, that place is exempted from the check “this authority still appears among the anchors” **only because the effect removes that anchor at the same time**. An anchor with the same identity attached by `depend` to another usable place continues to block the step.\n700\t5. Applicable abstraction contracts remain satisfied.\n701\t\n702\t**`ok` effect:** The authority becomes inactive and can no longer be exercised. It is removed from its holding place's anchors when stored there. Effective modes of its origins are recomputed, allowing unblocked rights to return. There is no implicit deallocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n703\t\n704\t# IX. Transfer, Dependencies, and Storage of Rights\n705\t\n706\tThese transitions govern value consumption and the controlled movement of their dependencies. A valid transition can neither create an additional permission nor discard a surviving anchor.\n707\t\n708\t## 63. O4 — Consuming Value Transfer\n709\t\n710\t**Preconditions**\n711\t\n712\t1. `source` and `destination` are usable, and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over their relevant footprints.\n713\t2. Source and destination have the **same shape of ordered subtrees**. The `i`-th child of each source part corresponds to the `i`-th child of the corresponding destination part; all nodes are matched. Structural field identity is positional, not an arbitrary matching of children.\n714\t3. **All** source leaves are `Init`, and **no** destination leaf is `Init`. The source and destination footprints are disjoint.\n715\t4. No exclusive authority is duplicated; validity of derived authorities and active projections is preserved. No active derived authority held outside `source` covers a source leaf, except the authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `source`: the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain. Other active authorities remain subject to this check. Authorities held by parts of `source` are transferred with the value and are controlled by §63.8.\n716\t5. **No in-place source leaf is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). Storage merely **owned** by the source, which is not itself moved by the transfer of that value, does not prevent this transfer.\n717\t6. Dependencies of all still-usable places following the value remain valid; non-escape constraints and `AnchorOrder` are checked in the **resulting state**, including transferred anchors of owned places that remain usable.\n718\t7. All moved held authorities, including those of owned storage, satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` in the resulting state; no authority may escape a local anchor on the grounds that its containing value was transferred.\n719\t8. **Local self-blocking prevention:** For each active authority held before the step by a part `r` of `source` whose holder becomes the corresponding part `corr(r)` of `destination`, that authority's footprint contains **no leaf** of `corr(r)` in the resulting state. Transfer is rejected if this local check fails; no global authority-acyclicity check is imposed.\n720\t9. **Destination usability:** `destination` is usable in the resulting state.\n721\t\n722\t**`ok` effect:**\n723\t\n724\t- every source leaf becomes `Moved` and each corresponding destination leaf becomes `Init`;\n725\t- every authority held by source part `r` acquires as its new holder the corresponding destination part `corr(r)`, not indiscriminately the destination root;\n726\t- places owned by `r` become owned by `corr(r)`; their roots follow this owner according to `RootHeld`;\n727\t- value dependencies follow the corresponding places under validity guarantees; no authority is created or duplicated;\n728\t- destination places receive the transferred values and rights without automatically moving storage not owned by a source part.\n729\t\n730\t**Other outcome:** `reject`. `transfer` to a call result is subject to these same rules.\n731\t\n732\tA partially initialized structure may be transferred **field by field**, but only for fields individually eligible for transfer. This does not automatically destructure a value that owns storage **at the container level**: that storage stays with the container if the container is not transferred. Conversion between structures with permuted fields must be handled separately, not as a `transfer`.\n733\t\n734\t## 64. O5 — Explicit Attachment of an Anchor\n735\t\n736\t**Preconditions**\n737\t\n738\t1. `result` is a usable place over which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n739\t2. `anchor` designates an active, valid authority representing a real dependency of the relevant rights or storage; a declared dependency cannot extend an origin's lifetime.\n740\t3. Existing anchors are preserved and the addition respects `AnchorOrder`: the effective anchor's frame is the frame of `result` or an ancestor thereof.\n741\t4. Every dependency carried by a contained value remains represented in the transitive obligations of its container.\n742\t\n743\t**`ok` effect:** `anchor` is added to `D(result)`; the container's transitive dependencies account for it. No additional access right is created. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n744\t\n745\tA safe interface must declare dependencies covering those actually required by its implementation; the annotation itself is not presumed true. An unsafe abstraction bears the obligations of its contract.\n746\t\n747\t## 65. O8 — Controlled Storage of an Authority\n748\t\n749\t**Preconditions**\n750\t\n751\t1. `authority` is active, **derived**, and held by the current frame; a root authority can never be stored by `store`.\n752\t2. `target` is a usable **leaf** under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It holds no other **active** authority. Its data already being `Init` does not by itself prohibit the operation.\n753\t3. The stored authority covers neither a leaf of `target` nor the leaves of storage transitively owned by it.\n754\t4. The effective anchor of `authority` respects `AnchorOrder` relative to the frame of `target`; other dependencies are preserved.\n755\t5. **In the resulting state**, `target` remains under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame (`store_keeps_target_reachable`), and `AnchorOrderHeld` remains true. This is a genuine additional precondition, not an automatic consequence of the preceding ones.\n756\t\n757\t**`ok` effect:** The holder of `authority` becomes `target`; the authority is added to `D(target)`; **only** leaf `target` becomes `Init`. Other fields of a structure, even adjacent ones, do not change their initialization state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n758\t\n759\tOverwriting an active authority already held by the leaf is prohibited; its explicit release may make the target reusable. The resulting-state clause does not by itself prohibit all cycles possible after other operations: general freedom from blocking (`NoLock`) is not adopted here.\n760\t\n761\t# X. Reading, Writing, and Initialization\n762\t\n763\tA leaf's initialization state and the effective right to access it are two distinct conditions.\n764\t\n765\t## 66. O9 — Safe Reading of Regions\n766\t\n767\t**Preconditions**\n768\t\n769\t1. Every leaf being read belongs to a usable place and is `Init`.\n770\t2. `access` is an authority exercisable in `Observe` mode over the region; its **effective** mode actually permits observation of each leaf being read.\n771\t3. Required anchors are valid, and the type and storage contract permits this read.\n772\t\n773\t**`ok` effect:** The logical read succeeds, without modifying `F`, `P`, `I`, `A`, `D`, or arena entries. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n774\t\n775\t`Moved` and `Uninit` cannot be read. This definition does not replace concrete representation and pointer checks of the elementary memory machine.\n776\t\n777\t## 67. O10 — Ordinary Write\n778\t\n779\t**Preconditions**\n780\t\n781\t1. Targeted leaves belong to usable places, and `access` is exercisable in `Modify` mode with **effective** `Modify` right over each leaf.\n782\t2. No incompatible ordinary authority holds an incompatible effective mode over the written leaves (I1).\n783\t3. Anchors and constraints of the relevant types, storage, and abstractions remain valid.\n784\t\n785\t**`ok` effect:** The logical write sets the actually written leaves to `Init` and does not modify authorities or their anchors. An unwritten leaf does not become `Init` by this effect. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n786\t\n787\t## 68. O11 — Write Under Shared Mutation\n788\t\n789\t**Preconditions**\n790\t\n791\t1. Every leaf in the region is usable and declared `K.SharedMutable`.\n792\t2. `access` is exercisable in a mode **at least** `Observe` and has a present effective mode on every leaf, either `Observe` or `Modify`; exercising an authority without an effective mode is insufficient.\n793\t3. Dependencies are valid and the particular shared-mutation discipline guaranteed by `K.SharedMutable` applies.\n794\t\n795\t**`ok` effect:** Actually written leaves become `Init`; authorities and their structural exclusivity I1 do not change. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n796\t\n797\t`writeShared` may update metadata under an `Observe` authority when the contract permits. Stability of observed contents is not automatically guaranteed, and this kernel **does not prove** the absence of data races: that requires the concurrency model's guarantees.\n798\t\n799\t# XI. End of Use, Deallocation, and Allocation\n800\t\n801\tThe logical end of a value does not necessarily entail deallocation of its storage. The following operations treat initialization, ownership, and physical release separately.\n802\t\n803\t## 69. O12 — Logical End of a Value's Use\n804\t\n805\t**Preconditions**\n806\t\n807\t1. `place` is usable and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over it.\n808\t2. No active descendant over the leaves of `place` **or storage that dies with it** is held outside the places dying with `place`, in a way that would prevent rights from returning or being invalidated. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from this check. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (held by `place` or by a place dying with it, transitively owned storage included) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n809\t3. No place **remaining usable after the effect** depends on an authority held by `place`, its root, or an authority that would become inactive when an owned holder dies.\n810\t4. **No leaf whose use ends, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). A dynamic pinning obligation must be legally removed by `unpin` before this step.\n811\t\n812\t**`ok` effect:** The leaves of value `place` become `Uninit`; `place` **remains usable as a place** and may legally be reinitialized. Authorities held by `place` and dead substorages are released; dependencies of the ended value are removed; transitively owned places that cease to live are marked dead. The operation does not automatically trigger physical deallocation in the elementary memory machine: an unfreed allocation may constitute a leak, which this guarantee does not prohibit. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n813\t\n814\t## 70. O13 — Release of an Owned Storage Unit\n815\t\n816\t**Preconditions**\n817\t\n818\t1. `place` is a **root of storage owned by a value** (`OwnedRoot`), not an ordinary local directly owned by a frame and not part of an aggregate.\n819\t2. `K.Deallocable(place)` is established, and `K.FreeCompat(place,auth)` justifies deallocation authority `auth`; this identifier is opaque to the authority kernel.\n820\t3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify`; no active derivative over the footprint being freed is held outside holders that die; no surviving place is anchored to an authority of the released region or of its dead holders. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from the check on active derivatives. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (an authority of the released region, its root included, or an authority whose holder dies) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n821\t4. No leaf of the released unit is statically or dynamically pinned. Ending external use does not silently turn a still-pinned leaf into a releasable unit.\n822\t\n823\t**`ok` effect:** `place` and the storage that ceases to live are marked dead; authorities of those regions, including their corresponding roots, become inactive, as do authorities whose holders die. Surviving dependencies must remain valid. Every realization of `free` must satisfy the corresponding deallocation relation of the elementary memory machine (Part III). **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n824\t\n825\t## 71. O14 — Allocation of a New Storage Root\n826\t\n827\t**Static preconditions**\n828\t\n829\t1. `shape` is a **nonempty** abstract shape, represented like the local places of `enterFrame`; an allocation of **zero physical size** may be represented by a logical one-leaf shape.\n830\t2. The provider is a usable place, and **all leaves of the provider** are `Init`; updates to its metadata are permitted through exercisable `Modify` or a valid `writeShared` over `SharedMutable` metadata.\n831\t3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify` over `owner`; new identities and ownership relations must respect `WF3`, `RootHeld`, and liveness restrictions.\n832\t\n833\t**Outcomes**\n834\t\n835\t- `reject`: a static precondition does not hold;\n836\t- `fail`: static preconditions hold, but `K.Satisfiable(provider,shape,s)` is false in the state under consideration; **no state change**;\n837\t- `ok(s')`: creation of a **new storage root** with fresh identity, owned by `owner`; its leaves start `Uninit`, its new root `Modify` authority with no origin is held by `owner`; provider metadata is updated only in accordance with its modification rights.\n838\t\n839\tAllocation success depends on the provider's state: it does not constitute a right to invent physical memory. Any realization of this operation must satisfy the `AllocStep` relation defined by the rules of Sections 9 and 13.\n840\t\n841\t# XII. Call Frames and Return of Authorities\n842\t\n843\tFrames are execution units that receive authorities, introduce locals, and have exit rules distinct from Spec 3's value-passing conventions.\n844\t\n845\t## 72. O6 — Entering a Frame\n846\t\n847\t**Preconditions**\n848\t\n849\t1. The calling frame is the current frame and is live. Each parameter passed to the callee is an active, derived authority held by the caller before the step; the caller is **not necessarily** the frame in which the authority was first derived.\n850\t2. Modes, footprints, anchoring, and parameter-passing rules permit this change of holder; introduction of locals preserves well-formedness and the validity of their roots.\n851\t3. **Origin protection on entry:** for each `Modify` parameter with direct origin `o`, `o` is exercisable in **no mode** (neither `Observe` nor `Modify`) in the **resulting state**, once the new frame's parameters and locals have been created.\n852\t4. **G4:** `AnchorOrderHeld` is checked over the complete resulting state.\n853\t\n854\t**`ok` effect:** A new child frame is pushed onto the stack; active parameters **are yielded** to that frame, which becomes their sole holder; local places and their root authorities are created; valid anchors inherited from the caller are preserved. This transfer creates no additional rights. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n855\t\n856\tThis clause establishes **entry protection**. It does not prove the stronger property of protection throughout the **entire** invocation following other releases or reanchorings. While a `Modify` parameter remains active, its direct origin remains subject to effective-mode restrictions. Any additional protection throughout the invocation is deferred to Spec 3.\n857\t\n858\t## 73. O7 — Exiting a Frame\n859\t\n860\t**Preconditions**\n861\t\n862\t1. The current frame is not the initial frame and has a surviving caller.\n863\t2. Results that must survive have already been legally transferred to their destinations; no active authority or dependency may remain invalidly attached to locals that are about to die.\n864\t3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n865\t4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n866\t5. Validity of anchors of surviving values and `AnchorOrderHeld` are checked **in the resulting state**. Other state invariants and resource contracts are respected.\n867\t\n868\t**`ok` effect:**\n869\t\n870\t- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n871\t- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n872\t- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n873\t- reanchoring changes **neither the footprint nor the own mode** of an authority; parameter identities and relations specific to the frame cease their operational role;\n874\t- the calling frame becomes current again.\n875\t\n876\t**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n877\t\n878\t# XIII. Pinning, Statics, and External Commitments\n879\t\n880\tPinning forbids certain movements or ends of lifetime. It is not a general guarantee of content immutability.\n881\t\n882\t## 74. O15 — Dynamic Pinning\n883\t\n884\t**Preconditions:** `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over the relevant leaves, and the operation respects applicable pinning obligations.\n885\t\n886\t**`ok` effect:** A dynamic pinning obligation of fresh identity is installed on the relevant leaves. A leaf is dynamically pinned while at least one active pinning obligation covers it. While an obligation is active, `transfer`, `endUse`, `free`, and `exitFrame` are rejected if they act on leaves it pins; removal of this obligation requires `unpin` under `K.ExternalDone`. Pinning does not make contents immutable. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n887\t\n888\t## 75. O16 — Removal of Dynamic Pinning\n889\t\n890\t**Preconditions:** `unpin` designates one active dynamic pinning obligation, installed by `pin` on `place`; `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify`, and contract `K.ExternalDone`, applied to that obligation, attests that all relevant external use has ended.\n891\t\n892\t**`ok` effect:** The designated dynamic pinning obligation ends, and only that one: a leaf it covered stays pinned while another active obligation covers it. The step does not itself modify values, their initialization, or their access authorities. Static obligations are not removed by this operation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n893\t\n894\t**Associated static rule (`StaticPinnedBottom`):** Statics designated by `K.Pinned` must be places **directly** owned by the **base frame**, not merely transitively owned by a static value. This restriction ensures that they do not die when an intermediate frame exits.\n895\t\n896\t# XIV. Arenas and Collective Invalidation — Reference Abstraction\n897\t\n898\tAn arena is a **library abstraction**, not a memory-kernel primitive. Operations O17 through O19 are **derived** from operations O1 through O16 and abstraction-specific metadata: `newArena` registers an administration without modifying the kernel; `issue` performs `split` followed by `store` (after checking availability); `reset` performs a `Modify` derivation over all storage—through `derive` or `split`—then `release`, before restoring availability. Their contract below is **normative for the standard-library arena** until Spec 10 takes responsibility for it. It authorizes neither new permissions nor an additional access machine. Arena entries (Section 46) constitute the abstract state of this administration; the guard in Section 56 belongs to its consistency contract. The contracts of `HandlePool` and `DataStack` belong to their library abstractions and are not defined here.\n899\t\n900\t## 76. O17 — Creation of an Administered Arena\n901\t\n902\t**Preconditions**\n903\t\n904\t1. `storage` is an existing, usable place under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It may be local, static, allocated, or a block issued by a previous arena.\n905\t2. `internal` is explicitly named by the caller, exists, is active, has own mode `Modify`, and `ExercisableAt(s,internal,Modify)` holds.\n906\t3. `internal` is **held by a place**: that place is the arena value; `footprint(internal) = footprint(storage)`.\n907\t4. **Unified administration guard (Section 56):** no **live** administrative abstraction, whether an arena or a library instance such as a handle pool, already uses this `internal` identity. The relevant registries of library administrations are consulted before registration. Two distinct identities may cover common leaves: this is not in itself grounds for rejection.\n908\t5. All leaves of the arena value holding `internal` are already `Init`; construction does not initialize them implicitly.\n909\t6. Dependency, holder, storage, and administration relations respect, in the resulting state, `WF3`, I1, `AnchorOrder`, and `AnchorOrderHeld` over the whole kernel state, and the new entry satisfies `ArenaInv` (Section 55).\n910\t\n911\t**`ok` effect:** A live entry `{storage, internal, available, issued}` is added; the initial `available` set represents the administered storage, and `issued` is initially empty. Registering the entry fabricates no place, access right, or `Init` state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n912\t\n913\tThe identity of `internal`, not the index of `storage`, is the key to preventing duplication of live administrations. No global constraint requires footprints of live arenas to be pairwise disjoint.\n914\t\n915\t## 77. O18 — Issuing an Arena Block\n916\t\n917\t**Static preconditions**\n918\t\n919\t1. The `arena` entry exists and is live; `internal` is active. `MetaRight` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value holding `internal` (Section 36). Mutation of metadata through `writeShared` alone does not satisfy this condition.\n920\t2. `region` belongs to the administered storage; `destination` is a usable **leaf** holding no active authority; the destination can legally receive the block value in mode `Modify`. The fact that `destination` is already `Init` is not in itself grounds for rejection, in accordance with O8.2.\n921\t   **Local self-blocking prevention: leaf `destination` is not in `region`, including when the region is declared available.** This condition is static and is checked before an availability `fail`.\n922\t3. Applicable **static** validity and non-escape restrictions for the destination are satisfied. The `AnchorOrder` check depending on the new anchor is performed on the **simulated resulting state**, after the availability check described in point 4; it cannot turn an unavailable issuance (`fail`) into `reject`.\n923\t\n924\t**Availability and exercisability checks**\n925\t\n926\t4. If static conditions hold but **at least one leaf of `region` does not belong to `available`**, the operation produces **`fail` and leaves the entire state unchanged**. The empty region is always available. An unavailable region does not permit a prior static rejection to be bypassed.\n927\t5. If the region is available, the effective mode of `internal` on that region must permit `Modify`; otherwise the outcome is `reject`.\n928\t6. The simulated result must satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` **in the resulting state**; moreover, **in that resulting state, `destination` must remain under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame**, in accordance with O8.5. Issuance into a value with a longer lifetime than a local anchor is not made valid merely by storing it in the caller.\n929\t\n930\t**`ok` effect:** A block authority of fresh identity, derived from `internal` and of mode `Modify`, is issued for the region; it is held by `destination`, which receives the corresponding anchor. The leaves of the region leave `available`, and the issuance is added to `issued` with its authority. **Only leaf `destination` becomes `Init`**; no other leaf of the arena value changes initialization. Previous issuances remain intact and valid. Issuance does not automatically install anchors on third-party values: they must use their lawful dependency mechanisms.\n931\t\n932\t**Outcomes:** `ok`, `fail` (region unavailable after static validation), `reject` (static rule, effective right, or anchoring not satisfied). The destination may be a leaf of the arena value itself: its initialization then constitutes **an explicit write to that destination**, not implicit initialization of the entire arena value's metadata.\n933\t\n934\t## 78. O19 — Collective Arena Reset\n935\t\n936\t**Preconditions**\n937\t\n938\t1. The entry exists and is live. **`MetaRight(s,arena)` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value**, in accordance with Section 36. `writeShared` over metadata alone, even when legal, does not replace such exercise. `internal` and the arena's other restrictions remain valid.\n939\t2. **No active authority derived from `internal` covers a leaf of the administered storage.** A derived authority with an empty footprint does not, by itself, block `reset`.\n940\t3. No still-usable place depends, directly or transitively, on an anchor in the **strict origin subtree of `internal`** (`internal` itself is excluded) that covers a leaf of the administered storage. Under precondition 2 and invariant I3, this condition follows from the absence of active descendants covering the storage, and remains explicit in the abstraction contract. Validity obligations of other nested arenas remain satisfied.\n941\t\n942\t**`ok` effect:** All **leaves** of the administered storage become available again, and the issuance list is cleared under the arena contract. No still-active derived authority covering those leaves may remain, by precondition 2; an empty-footprint derivative may remain active without designating any recycled leaf. **No leaf is made `Init` by `reset`**, including in the arena value. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n943\t\n944\tSafe reuse after `reset` requires that previous issuances **covering recycled leaves**, and the dependencies on which their validity rests, have effectively ceased preventing their reuse. Any active empty-footprint derivatives cover none of those leaves. A historically issued region does not remain permanently unavailable solely for that reason.\n945\t\n946\t# XV. Transitive Dependencies, Returns, and Value Behavior\n947\t\n948\tDependent values may survive transfers and calls only if the required anchors and rights remain valid. Second-class access does not imply an arbitrary persistent reference.\n949\t\n950\t## 79. Non-Escaping Values and Returns\n951\t"}]},"uuid":"2210a3d0-28c7-4522-82d7-a1df5e68c802","timestamp":"2026-11-14T07:54:14.538Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","content":"## 58. Transition Outcomes\n\nEvery operation has a conceptual outcome among:\n\n```text\nOutcome3 = ok(new_state) | reject | fail\n```\n\n`reject` means that the static discipline does not permit the step; it is neither an implicit runtime trap nor `ub` in the elementary memory machine. `fail` is a **defined failure** leaving the state unchanged; **only `alloc` may produce it in the O1–O16 kernel**. Other kernel operations produce `ok` or `reject`. Library-abstraction operations, including `issue` (§77), may define their own `fail` cases. Where such failure is provided, static preconditions are checked before dynamic availability, according to the operation's contract.\n\nAn `ok` transition must satisfy its preconditions, apply exactly its prescribed effects, and preserve the relevant invariants. No transition fabricates a permission to make its conclusion true. Holder and anchor changes are evaluated in the **resulting state** when a rule explicitly so provides.\n\n## 59. Implicit Preservation of Modes and Anchors\n\nChanges in effective authority are **derived** from active authorities and their origins; no `Suspended` indicator is mutated. Access operations (O9–O11) do not change an anchor without an explicitly authorized effect of transfer, `depend`, `store`, release, or reanchoring. Leaves becoming `Init` do so through a permitted write (`write3`, `writeShared`, `store`, including during a derived `issue`) or a transfer from a corresponding source leaf already `Init`; leaves initialized in `S3.init` are declared as such.\n\n## 60. O1 — Derivation of an Authority\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `source` is an active derived or root authority, and `ExercisableAt(s,source,mode)` holds; its origin chain and anchors are valid: every authority of its origin chain, its effective anchor included, is active.\n2. The leaves of `region` exist and belong to the footprint of `source`; `mode` does not exceed the available effective right on these leaves.\n3. The addition preserves exclusivity I1: no unrelated active authority becomes incompatible with the derived right, and already-active descendants are respected.\n4. The new holding frame is the live current frame. The admissible state's footprint and anchoring restrictions remain true.\n\n**`ok` effect:** A new active authority with fresh identity, origin `source`, requested mode and footprint, and held by the current frame is added. The source remains active; its effective modes are recomputed per leaf. No storage or place anchor is created. Multiple compatible observations remain permitted. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n## 61. O2 — Splitting Authorities\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `source` is active and exercisable by the current frame with sufficient modification rights over the relevant leaves.\n2. Each region lies within the available, effectively modifiable footprint of the source.\n3. Regions are pairwise disjoint under structural composition or contract `K.DeclaredDisjoint`, subject to obligation `DDSound(K)`; no exclusive right already distributed is overlapped.\n4. Creation of the derivatives respects origins, conservation I1/I2, and validity obligations.\n\n**`ok` effect:** An active derived `Modify` authority held by the current frame is created for each region. `source` remains active; its effective modification capability disappears only over distributed leaves and remains on undistributed leaves. Authorities are split logically: `split` performs no physical allocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nAn unjustified declaration of disjointness may invalidate the proof: without `DDSound`, an accepted `split` may violate I1. Constructing zero-sized subregions is not identified with an allocation of the elementary memory machine; the case of an empty shape in `alloc` is distinct.\n\n## 62. O3 — End of a Derived Borrow\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `authority` is active **and derived**: an authority with no origin is rejected.\n2. It is held by the current frame, or by a place `p` on which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n3. No active descendant of the authority prevents its release; no other still-usable value may lose a required anchor.\n4. If the authority is stored in its holding place, that place is exempted from the check “this authority still appears among the anchors” **only because the effect removes that anchor at the same time**. An anchor with the same identity attached by `depend` to another usable place continues to block the step.\n5. Applicable abstraction contracts remain satisfied.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The authority becomes inactive and can no longer be exercised. It is removed from its holding place's anchors when stored there. Effective modes of its origins are recomputed, allowing unblocked rights to return. There is no implicit deallocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n# IX. Transfer, Dependencies, and Storage of Rights\n\nThese transitions govern value consumption and the controlled movement of their dependencies. A valid transition can neither create an additional permission nor discard a surviving anchor.\n\n## 63. O4 — Consuming Value Transfer\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `source` and `destination` are usable, and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over their relevant footprints.\n2. Source and destination have the **same shape of ordered subtrees**. The `i`-th child of each source part corresponds to the `i`-th child of the corresponding destination part; all nodes are matched. Structural field identity is positional, not an arbitrary matching of children.\n3. **All** source leaves are `Init`, and **no** destination leaf is `Init`. The source and destination footprints are disjoint.\n4. No exclusive authority is duplicated; validity of derived authorities and active projections is preserved. No active derived authority held outside `source` covers a source leaf, except the authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `source`: the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain. Other active authorities remain subject to this check. Authorities held by parts of `source` are transferred with the value and are controlled by §63.8.\n5. **No in-place source leaf is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). Storage merely **owned** by the source, which is not itself moved by the transfer of that value, does not prevent this transfer.\n6. Dependencies of all still-usable places following the value remain valid; non-escape constraints and `AnchorOrder` are checked in the **resulting state**, including transferred anchors of owned places that remain usable.\n7. All moved held authorities, including those of owned storage, satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` in the resulting state; no authority may escape a local anchor on the grounds that its containing value was transferred.\n8. **Local self-blocking prevention:** For each active authority held before the step by a part `r` of `source` whose holder becomes the corresponding part `corr(r)` of `destination`, that authority's footprint contains **no leaf** of `corr(r)` in the resulting state. Transfer is rejected if this local check fails; no global authority-acyclicity check is imposed.\n9. **Destination usability:** `destination` is usable in the resulting state.\n\n**`ok` effect:**\n\n- every source leaf becomes `Moved` and each corresponding destination leaf becomes `Init`;\n- every authority held by source part `r` acquires as its new holder the corresponding destination part `corr(r)`, not indiscriminately the destination root;\n- places owned by `r` become owned by `corr(r)`; their roots follow this owner according to `RootHeld`;\n- value dependencies follow the corresponding places under validity guarantees; no authority is created or duplicated;\n- destination places receive the transferred values and rights without automatically moving storage not owned by a source part.\n\n**Other outcome:** `reject`. `transfer` to a call result is subject to these same rules.\n\nA partially initialized structure may be transferred **field by field**, but only for fields individually eligible for transfer. This does not automatically destructure a value that owns storage **at the container level**: that storage stays with the container if the container is not transferred. Conversion between structures with permuted fields must be handled separately, not as a `transfer`.\n\n## 64. O5 — Explicit Attachment of an Anchor\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `result` is a usable place over which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n2. `anchor` designates an active, valid authority representing a real dependency of the relevant rights or storage; a declared dependency cannot extend an origin's lifetime.\n3. Existing anchors are preserved and the addition respects `AnchorOrder`: the effective anchor's frame is the frame of `result` or an ancestor thereof.\n4. Every dependency carried by a contained value remains represented in the transitive obligations of its container.\n\n**`ok` effect:** `anchor` is added to `D(result)`; the container's transitive dependencies account for it. No additional access right is created. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nA safe interface must declare dependencies covering those actually required by its implementation; the annotation itself is not presumed true. An unsafe abstraction bears the obligations of its contract.\n\n## 65. O8 — Controlled Storage of an Authority\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `authority` is active, **derived**, and held by the current frame; a root authority can never be stored by `store`.\n2. `target` is a usable **leaf** under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It holds no other **active** authority. Its data already being `Init` does not by itself prohibit the operation.\n3. The stored authority covers neither a leaf of `target` nor the leaves of storage transitively owned by it.\n4. The effective anchor of `authority` respects `AnchorOrder` relative to the frame of `target`; other dependencies are preserved.\n5. **In the resulting state**, `target` remains under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame (`store_keeps_target_reachable`), and `AnchorOrderHeld` remains true. This is a genuine additional precondition, not an automatic consequence of the preceding ones.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The holder of `authority` becomes `target`; the authority is added to `D(target)`; **only** leaf `target` becomes `Init`. Other fields of a structure, even adjacent ones, do not change their initialization state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nOverwriting an active authority already held by the leaf is prohibited; its explicit release may make the target reusable. The resulting-state clause does not by itself prohibit all cycles possible after other operations: general freedom from blocking (`NoLock`) is not adopted here.\n\n# X. Reading, Writing, and Initialization\n\nA leaf's initialization state and the effective right to access it are two distinct conditions.\n\n## 66. O9 — Safe Reading of Regions\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. Every leaf being read belongs to a usable place and is `Init`.\n2. `access` is an authority exercisable in `Observe` mode over the region; its **effective** mode actually permits observation of each leaf being read.\n3. Required anchors are valid, and the type and storage contract permits this read.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The logical read succeeds, without modifying `F`, `P`, `I`, `A`, `D`, or arena entries. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n`Moved` and `Uninit` cannot be read. This definition does not replace concrete representation and pointer checks of the elementary memory machine.\n\n## 67. O10 — Ordinary Write\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. Targeted leaves belong to usable places, and `access` is exercisable in `Modify` mode with **effective** `Modify` right over each leaf.\n2. No incompatible ordinary authority holds an incompatible effective mode over the written leaves (I1).\n3. Anchors and constraints of the relevant types, storage, and abstractions remain valid.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The logical write sets the actually written leaves to `Init` and does not modify authorities or their anchors. An unwritten leaf does not become `Init` by this effect. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n## 68. O11 — Write Under Shared Mutation\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. Every leaf in the region is usable and declared `K.SharedMutable`.\n2. `access` is exercisable in a mode **at least** `Observe` and has a present effective mode on every leaf, either `Observe` or `Modify`; exercising an authority without an effective mode is insufficient.\n3. Dependencies are valid and the particular shared-mutation discipline guaranteed by `K.SharedMutable` applies.\n\n**`ok` effect:** Actually written leaves become `Init`; authorities and their structural exclusivity I1 do not change. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n`writeShared` may update metadata under an `Observe` authority when the contract permits. Stability of observed contents is not automatically guaranteed, and this kernel **does not prove** the absence of data races: that requires the concurrency model's guarantees.\n\n# XI. End of Use, Deallocation, and Allocation\n\nThe logical end of a value does not necessarily entail deallocation of its storage. The following operations treat initialization, ownership, and physical release separately.\n\n## 69. O12 — Logical End of a Value's Use\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `place` is usable and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over it.\n2. No active descendant over the leaves of `place` **or storage that dies with it** is held outside the places dying with `place`, in a way that would prevent rights from returning or being invalidated. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from this check. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (held by `place` or by a place dying with it, transitively owned storage included) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n3. No place **remaining usable after the effect** depends on an authority held by `place`, its root, or an authority that would become inactive when an owned holder dies.\n4. **No leaf whose use ends, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). A dynamic pinning obligation must be legally removed by `unpin` before this step.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The leaves of value `place` become `Uninit`; `place` **remains usable as a place** and may legally be reinitialized. Authorities held by `place` and dead substorages are released; dependencies of the ended value are removed; transitively owned places that cease to live are marked dead. The operation does not automatically trigger physical deallocation in the elementary memory machine: an unfreed allocation may constitute a leak, which this guarantee does not prohibit. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n## 70. O13 — Release of an Owned Storage Unit\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `place` is a **root of storage owned by a value** (`OwnedRoot`), not an ordinary local directly owned by a frame and not part of an aggregate.\n2. `K.Deallocable(place)` is established, and `K.FreeCompat(place,auth)` justifies deallocation authority `auth`; this identifier is opaque to the authority kernel.\n3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify`; no active derivative over the footprint being freed is held outside holders that die; no surviving place is anchored to an authority of the released region or of its dead holders. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from the check on active derivatives. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (an authority of the released region, its root included, or an authority whose holder dies) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n4. No leaf of the released unit is statically or dynamically pinned. Ending external use does not silently turn a still-pinned leaf into a releasable unit.\n\n**`ok` effect:** `place` and the storage that ceases to live are marked dead; authorities of those regions, including their corresponding roots, become inactive, as do authorities whose holders die. Surviving dependencies must remain valid. Every realization of `free` must satisfy the corresponding deallocation relation of the elementary memory machine (Part III). **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n## 71. O14 — Allocation of a New Storage Root\n\n**Static preconditions**\n\n1. `shape` is a **nonempty** abstract shape, represented like the local places of `enterFrame`; an allocation of **zero physical size** may be represented by a logical one-leaf shape.\n2. The provider is a usable place, and **all leaves of the provider** are `Init`; updates to its metadata are permitted through exercisable `Modify` or a valid `writeShared` over `SharedMutable` metadata.\n3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify` over `owner`; new identities and ownership relations must respect `WF3`, `RootHeld`, and liveness restrictions.\n\n**Outcomes**\n\n- `reject`: a static precondition does not hold;\n- `fail`: static preconditions hold, but `K.Satisfiable(provider,shape,s)` is false in the state under consideration; **no state change**;\n- `ok(s')`: creation of a **new storage root** with fresh identity, owned by `owner`; its leaves start `Uninit`, its new root `Modify` authority with no origin is held by `owner`; provider metadata is updated only in accordance with its modification rights.\n\nAllocation success depends on the provider's state: it does not constitute a right to invent physical memory. Any realization of this operation must satisfy the `AllocStep` relation defined by the rules of Sections 9 and 13.\n\n# XII. Call Frames and Return of Authorities\n\nFrames are execution units that receive authorities, introduce locals, and have exit rules distinct from Spec 3's value-passing conventions.\n\n## 72. O6 — Entering a Frame\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The calling frame is the current frame and is live. Each parameter passed to the callee is an active, derived authority held by the caller before the step; the caller is **not necessarily** the frame in which the authority was first derived.\n2. Modes, footprints, anchoring, and parameter-passing rules permit this change of holder; introduction of locals preserves well-formedness and the validity of their roots.\n3. **Origin protection on entry:** for each `Modify` parameter with direct origin `o`, `o` is exercisable in **no mode** (neither `Observe` nor `Modify`) in the **resulting state**, once the new frame's parameters and locals have been created.\n4. **G4:** `AnchorOrderHeld` is checked over the complete resulting state.\n\n**`ok` effect:** A new child frame is pushed onto the stack; active parameters **are yielded** to that frame, which becomes their sole holder; local places and their root authorities are created; valid anchors inherited from the caller are preserved. This transfer creates no additional rights. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nThis clause establishes **entry protection**. It does not prove the stronger property of protection throughout the **entire** invocation following other releases or reanchorings. While a `Modify` parameter remains active, its direct origin remains subject to effective-mode restrictions. Any additional protection throughout the invocation is deferred to Spec 3.\n\n## 73. O7 — Exiting a Frame\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The current frame is not the initial frame and has a surviving caller.\n2. Results that must survive have already been legally transferred to their destinations; no active authority or dependency may remain invalidly attached to locals that are about to die.\n3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n5. Validity of anchors of surviving values and `AnchorOrderHeld` are checked **in the resulting state**. Other state invariants and resource contracts are respected.\n\n**`ok` effect:**\n\n- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n- reanchoring changes **neither the footprint nor the own mode** of an authority; parameter identities and relations specific to the frame cease their operational role;\n- the calling frame becomes current again.\n\n**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n\n# XIII. Pinning, Statics, and External Commitments\n\nPinning forbids certain movements or ends of lifetime. It is not a general guarantee of content immutability.\n\n## 74. O15 — Dynamic Pinning\n\n**Preconditions:** `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over the relevant leaves, and the operation respects applicable pinning obligations.\n\n**`ok` effect:** A dynamic pinning obligation of fresh identity is installed on the relevant leaves. A leaf is dynamically pinned while at least one active pinning obligation covers it. While an obligation is active, `transfer`, `endUse`, `free`, and `exitFrame` are rejected if they act on leaves it pins; removal of this obligation requires `unpin` under `K.ExternalDone`. Pinning does not make contents immutable. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n## 75. O16 — Removal of Dynamic Pinning\n\n**Preconditions:** `unpin` designates one active dynamic pinning obligation, installed by `pin` on `place`; `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify`, and contract `K.ExternalDone`, applied to that obligation, attests that all relevant external use has ended.\n\n**`ok` effect:** The designated dynamic pinning obligation ends, and only that one: a leaf it covered stays pinned while another active obligation covers it. The step does not itself modify values, their initialization, or their access authorities. Static obligations are not removed by this operation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\n**Associated static rule (`StaticPinnedBottom`):** Statics designated by `K.Pinned` must be places **directly** owned by the **base frame**, not merely transitively owned by a static value. This restriction ensures that they do not die when an intermediate frame exits.\n\n# XIV. Arenas and Collective Invalidation — Reference Abstraction\n\nAn arena is a **library abstraction**, not a memory-kernel primitive. Operations O17 through O19 are **derived** from operations O1 through O16 and abstraction-specific metadata: `newArena` registers an administration without modifying the kernel; `issue` performs `split` followed by `store` (after checking availability); `reset` performs a `Modify` derivation over all storage—through `derive` or `split`—then `release`, before restoring availability. Their contract below is **normative for the standard-library arena** until Spec 10 takes responsibility for it. It authorizes neither new permissions nor an additional access machine. Arena entries (Section 46) constitute the abstract state of this administration; the guard in Section 56 belongs to its consistency contract. The contracts of `HandlePool` and `DataStack` belong to their library abstractions and are not defined here.\n\n## 76. O17 — Creation of an Administered Arena\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. `storage` is an existing, usable place under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It may be local, static, allocated, or a block issued by a previous arena.\n2. `internal` is explicitly named by the caller, exists, is active, has own mode `Modify`, and `ExercisableAt(s,internal,Modify)` holds.\n3. `internal` is **held by a place**: that place is the arena value; `footprint(internal) = footprint(storage)`.\n4. **Unified administration guard (Section 56):** no **live** administrative abstraction, whether an arena or a library instance such as a handle pool, already uses this `internal` identity. The relevant registries of library administrations are consulted before registration. Two distinct identities may cover common leaves: this is not in itself grounds for rejection.\n5. All leaves of the arena value holding `internal` are already `Init`; construction does not initialize them implicitly.\n6. Dependency, holder, storage, and administration relations respect, in the resulting state, `WF3`, I1, `AnchorOrder`, and `AnchorOrderHeld` over the whole kernel state, and the new entry satisfies `ArenaInv` (Section 55).\n\n**`ok` effect:** A live entry `{storage, internal, available, issued}` is added; the initial `available` set represents the administered storage, and `issued` is initially empty. Registering the entry fabricates no place, access right, or `Init` state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nThe identity of `internal`, not the index of `storage`, is the key to preventing duplication of live administrations. No global constraint requires footprints of live arenas to be pairwise disjoint.\n\n## 77. O18 — Issuing an Arena Block\n\n**Static preconditions**\n\n1. The `arena` entry exists and is live; `internal` is active. `MetaRight` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value holding `internal` (Section 36). Mutation of metadata through `writeShared` alone does not satisfy this condition.\n2. `region` belongs to the administered storage; `destination` is a usable **leaf** holding no active authority; the destination can legally receive the block value in mode `Modify`. The fact that `destination` is already `Init` is not in itself grounds for rejection, in accordance with O8.2.\n   **Local self-blocking prevention: leaf `destination` is not in `region`, including when the region is declared available.** This condition is static and is checked before an availability `fail`.\n3. Applicable **static** validity and non-escape restrictions for the destination are satisfied. The `AnchorOrder` check depending on the new anchor is performed on the **simulated resulting state**, after the availability check described in point 4; it cannot turn an unavailable issuance (`fail`) into `reject`.\n\n**Availability and exercisability checks**\n\n4. If static conditions hold but **at least one leaf of `region` does not belong to `available`**, the operation produces **`fail` and leaves the entire state unchanged**. The empty region is always available. An unavailable region does not permit a prior static rejection to be bypassed.\n5. If the region is available, the effective mode of `internal` on that region must permit `Modify`; otherwise the outcome is `reject`.\n6. The simulated result must satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` **in the resulting state**; moreover, **in that resulting state, `destination` must remain under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame**, in accordance with O8.5. Issuance into a value with a longer lifetime than a local anchor is not made valid merely by storing it in the caller.\n\n**`ok` effect:** A block authority of fresh identity, derived from `internal` and of mode `Modify`, is issued for the region; it is held by `destination`, which receives the corresponding anchor. The leaves of the region leave `available`, and the issuance is added to `issued` with its authority. **Only leaf `destination` becomes `Init`**; no other leaf of the arena value changes initialization. Previous issuances remain intact and valid. Issuance does not automatically install anchors on third-party values: they must use their lawful dependency mechanisms.\n\n**Outcomes:** `ok`, `fail` (region unavailable after static validation), `reject` (static rule, effective right, or anchoring not satisfied). The destination may be a leaf of the arena value itself: its initialization then constitutes **an explicit write to that destination**, not implicit initialization of the entire arena value's metadata.\n\n## 78. O19 — Collective Arena Reset\n\n**Preconditions**\n\n1. The entry exists and is live. **`MetaRight(s,arena)` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value**, in accordance with Section 36. `writeShared` over metadata alone, even when legal, does not replace such exercise. `internal` and the arena's other restrictions remain valid.\n2. **No active authority derived from `internal` covers a leaf of the administered storage.** A derived authority with an empty footprint does not, by itself, block `reset`.\n3. No still-usable place depends, directly or transitively, on an anchor in the **strict origin subtree of `internal`** (`internal` itself is excluded) that covers a leaf of the administered storage. Under precondition 2 and invariant I3, this condition follows from the absence of active descendants covering the storage, and remains explicit in the abstraction contract. Validity obligations of other nested arenas remain satisfied.\n\n**`ok` effect:** All **leaves** of the administered storage become available again, and the issuance list is cleared under the arena contract. No still-active derived authority covering those leaves may remain, by precondition 2; an empty-footprint derivative may remain active without designating any recycled leaf. **No leaf is made `Init` by `reset`**, including in the arena value. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n\nSafe reuse after `reset` requires that previous issuances **covering recycled leaves**, and the dependencies on which their validity rests, have effectively ceased preventing their reuse. Any active empty-footprint derivatives cover none of those leaves. A historically issued region does not remain permanently unavailable solely for that reason.\n\n# XV. Transitive Dependencies, Returns, and Value Behavior\n\nDependent values may survive transfers and calls only if the required anchors and rights remain valid. Second-class access does not imply an arbitrary persistent reference.\n\n## 79. Non-Escaping Values and Returns\n","numLines":300,"startLine":652,"totalLines":1051}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7a351ee2-59e5-44c2-83b7-8e508fe30712","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","hash":"b2a6e39fba015d797a6b35f638259a524f490a4d313a248a9f77697bbd6a80d4","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018PwJC8azGjM8SzQJbvywo3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":467,"limit":70},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KM2PYmMt4T7YEo4BsSxHLD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/specs/ERN_Memory_Model_Specification_v0.1.md","offset":652,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","hash":"9cd555ae9be8924d9b16583a7f60f4902df40abb30a9a5b271f4daa334d241fb","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018PwJC8azGjM8SzQJbvywo3","type":"tool_result","content":"467\t## 40. Logical Places, Leaves, and Ownership\n468\t\n469\tA place has an identity, optionally a structural parent, an abstract `kind`, an owner `owner : Frame | Place`, and a logical death marker. Structural parenthood `partOf(p,q)` describes the subplaces of a value; `owner` describes **who keeps the storage** of a root place valid. An owned place may lie outside its owner's direct footprint; ownership and composition are therefore distinct.\n470\t\n471\tA **leaf** is a place with no structural children. `footprint(p)` contains the leaves of `p`, and access rights over a place are evaluated on those leaves. Places transitively owned by a value are not automatically leaves of its `footprint`. A logical unit of the access discipline does not automatically constitute an additional allocation in the elementary memory machine.\n472\t\n473\tA place `p` is **usable** (`Usable(s,p)`) if and only if:\n474\t\n475\t1. `p` exists and is not marked dead;\n476\t2. if its owner is a frame, that frame belongs to the stack;\n477\t3. if its owner is a place `q`, then `q` is itself usable and the chain terminates at a frame in the stack.\n478\t\n479\tAn ownership cycle makes no place usable. `placeFrame(p)` is the terminal frame of this chain when `p` is usable. Structural parts share their parent's owner and death marker. A value may be logically ended by `endUse` without the place that contained it becoming unusable; the substorages it owned may, however, die.\n480\t\n481\tA **root place** has no structural parent. Every **usable** root place has a root authority of own mode `Modify`, with no origin, held exactly by that place's owner (`RootHeld`). This authority cannot be transferred in isolation; root ownership follows a legal transfer of the owning value when necessary.\n482\t\n483\t## 41. Leaf Initialization States\n484\t\n485\tEvery tracked leaf has exactly one of the states `Uninit`, `Init`, or `Moved`.\n486\t\n487\t- `Uninit`: no initialized value available in this leaf.\n488\t- `Init`: initialized value, subject to type, representation, and storage obligations.\n489\t- `Moved`: value consumed by transfer, inaccessible as its former value.\n490\t\n491\tAggregates may be partially initialized. Both `Uninit` and `Moved` prohibit ordinary reads, while remaining distinct states for diagnostics and end-of-use obligations. An initialization state creates no access permission.\n492\t\n493\t## 42. Authorities, Modes, Holders, and Origins\n494\t\n495\tAn authority conceptually has the following fields:\n496\t\n497\t```text\n498\tAuthority {\n499\t    id\n500\t    mode: Observe | Modify\n501\t    origin?: AuthorityId\n502\t    footprint: Set<Leaf>\n503\t    holder: Frame | Place\n504\t    active: Bool\n505\t}\n506\t```\n507\t\n508\t`origin` links a derived authority to its current origin. Authorities have fresh identities and well-formed origin chains. A root authority has no origin; a derived authority has one. Creating derived rights does not increase the scope or permitted mode of the origin.\n509\t\n510\tThe **holder** (`holder`) is not the **owner** (`owner`). An authority derived by `derive` or `split` is initially held by the current frame; `store` or a permitted transfer may make it held by a place. The derived `issue` operation proceeds through `split` followed by `store`. `release` does not release a root. There is no persistent `Suspended` field: suspension of rights is inferred from active descendants and applies leaf by leaf.\n511\t\n512\t## 43. Effective Rights and Exercise of Authorities\n513\t\n514\tFor an authority `a` and a leaf `l`, `effective(s,a,l)` is derived from the active descendants of `a` whose footprint contains `l`:\n515\t\n516\t1. if `a` is inactive or does not cover `l`, its effective mode is absent;\n517\t2. if no active descendant covers `l`, the effective mode is the own mode of `a`;\n518\t3. if an active descendant with own mode `Modify` covers `l`, the effective mode of `a` is absent;\n519\t4. otherwise, when the covering active descendants are `Observe`, the effective mode of `a` on `l` is `Observe`.\n520\t\n521\tThe effect is **local to each leaf**: after splitting a region, the origin may retain effective `Modify` over undistributed leaves.\n522\t\n523\tRequested modes are ordered `Observe ≤ Modify`. `ExercisableAt(s,a,m)` means:\n524\t\n525\t- `a` is active and its own mode permits `m`;\n526\t- either `a` is held by the current frame;\n527\t- or `a` is held by a place `p`, and the current frame has, over **all** leaves of `p`, an authority itself exercisable in mode `m` with sufficient effective mode.\n528\t\n529\tThe latter clause is recursive along the holder chain. Thus, being active or covering a leaf does not automatically make an authority exercisable. `HasRight(s,m,p)` means the current frame has exercise in mode `m` and sufficient effective mode **over every leaf in `p`'s footprint**. Every operation requires the modes it actually needs; an authority's own mode alone is insufficient.\n530\t\n531\t## 44. Dependencies and Effective Anchors\n532\t\n533\t`D(p)` is the set of authorities serving as **validity anchors** for place `p`. An anchor is valid as long as the authority it names is active. Dependencies of contained values are accounted for transitively. Relevant dependencies follow the value during transfer; those of its container do not automatically follow a part transferred in isolation.\n534\t\n535\tFor an authority `x`, its **effective anchor** is the root reached by traversing its origin chain. It does not necessarily coincide with its current holder. Lifetime constraints are calculated from the frame of that effective anchor and the frames of places that remain usable. A dependency cannot create a right or artificially extend a lifetime.\n536\t"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KM2PYmMt4T7YEo4BsSxHLD","type":"tool_result","content":"652\t## 58. Transition Outcomes\n653\t\n654\tEvery operation has a conceptual outcome among:\n655\t\n656\t```text\n657\tOutcome3 = ok(new_state) | reject | fail\n658\t```\n659\t\n660\t`reject` means that the static discipline does not permit the step; it is neither an implicit runtime trap nor `ub` in the elementary memory machine. `fail` is a **defined failure** leaving the state unchanged; **only `alloc` may produce it in the O1–O16 kernel**. Other kernel operations produce `ok` or `reject`. Library-abstraction operations, including `issue` (§77), may define their own `fail` cases. Where such failure is provided, static preconditions are checked before dynamic availability, according to the operation's contract.\n661\t\n662\tAn `ok` transition must satisfy its preconditions, apply exactly its prescribed effects, and preserve the relevant invariants. No transition fabricates a permission to make its conclusion true. Holder and anchor changes are evaluated in the **resulting state** when a rule explicitly so provides.\n663\t\n664\t## 59. Implicit Preservation of Modes and Anchors\n665\t\n666\tChanges in effective authority are **derived** from active authorities and their origins; no `Suspended` indicator is mutated. Access operations (O9–O11) do not change an anchor without an explicitly authorized effect of transfer, `depend`, `store`, release, or reanchoring. Leaves becoming `Init` do so through a permitted write (`write3`, `writeShared`, `store`, including during a derived `issue`) or a transfer from a corresponding source leaf already `Init`; leaves initialized in `S3.init` are declared as such.\n667\t\n668\t## 60. O1 — Derivation of an Authority\n669\t\n670\t**Preconditions**\n671\t\n672\t1. `source` is an active derived or root authority, and `ExercisableAt(s,source,mode)` holds; its origin chain and anchors are valid: every authority of its origin chain, its effective anchor included, is active.\n673\t2. The leaves of `region` exist and belong to the footprint of `source`; `mode` does not exceed the available effective right on these leaves.\n674\t3. The addition preserves exclusivity I1: no unrelated active authority becomes incompatible with the derived right, and already-active descendants are respected.\n675\t4. The new holding frame is the live current frame. The admissible state's footprint and anchoring restrictions remain true.\n676\t\n677\t**`ok` effect:** A new active authority with fresh identity, origin `source`, requested mode and footprint, and held by the current frame is added. The source remains active; its effective modes are recomputed per leaf. No storage or place anchor is created. Multiple compatible observations remain permitted. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n678\t\n679\t## 61. O2 — Splitting Authorities\n680\t\n681\t**Preconditions**\n682\t\n683\t1. `source` is active and exercisable by the current frame with sufficient modification rights over the relevant leaves.\n684\t2. Each region lies within the available, effectively modifiable footprint of the source.\n685\t3. Regions are pairwise disjoint under structural composition or contract `K.DeclaredDisjoint`, subject to obligation `DDSound(K)`; no exclusive right already distributed is overlapped.\n686\t4. Creation of the derivatives respects origins, conservation I1/I2, and validity obligations.\n687\t\n688\t**`ok` effect:** An active derived `Modify` authority held by the current frame is created for each region. `source` remains active; its effective modification capability disappears only over distributed leaves and remains on undistributed leaves. Authorities are split logically: `split` performs no physical allocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n689\t\n690\tAn unjustified declaration of disjointness may invalidate the proof: without `DDSound`, an accepted `split` may violate I1. Constructing zero-sized subregions is not identified with an allocation of the elementary memory machine; the case of an empty shape in `alloc` is distinct.\n691\t\n692\t## 62. O3 — End of a Derived Borrow\n693\t\n694\t**Preconditions**\n695\t\n696\t1. `authority` is active **and derived**: an authority with no origin is rejected.\n697\t2. It is held by the current frame, or by a place `p` on which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n698\t3. No active descendant of the authority prevents its release; no other still-usable value may lose a required anchor.\n699\t4. If the authority is stored in its holding place, that place is exempted from the check “this authority still appears among the anchors” **only because the effect removes that anchor at the same time**. An anchor with the same identity attached by `depend` to another usable place continues to block the step.\n700\t5. Applicable abstraction contracts remain satisfied.\n701\t\n702\t**`ok` effect:** The authority becomes inactive and can no longer be exercised. It is removed from its holding place's anchors when stored there. Effective modes of its origins are recomputed, allowing unblocked rights to return. There is no implicit deallocation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n703\t\n704\t# IX. Transfer, Dependencies, and Storage of Rights\n705\t\n706\tThese transitions govern value consumption and the controlled movement of their dependencies. A valid transition can neither create an additional permission nor discard a surviving anchor.\n707\t\n708\t## 63. O4 — Consuming Value Transfer\n709\t\n710\t**Preconditions**\n711\t\n712\t1. `source` and `destination` are usable, and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over their relevant footprints.\n713\t2. Source and destination have the **same shape of ordered subtrees**. The `i`-th child of each source part corresponds to the `i`-th child of the corresponding destination part; all nodes are matched. Structural field identity is positional, not an arbitrary matching of children.\n714\t3. **All** source leaves are `Init`, and **no** destination leaf is `Init`. The source and destination footprints are disjoint.\n715\t4. No exclusive authority is duplicated; validity of derived authorities and active projections is preserved. No active derived authority held outside `source` covers a source leaf, except the authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `source`: the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain. Other active authorities remain subject to this check. Authorities held by parts of `source` are transferred with the value and are controlled by §63.8.\n716\t5. **No in-place source leaf is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). Storage merely **owned** by the source, which is not itself moved by the transfer of that value, does not prevent this transfer.\n717\t6. Dependencies of all still-usable places following the value remain valid; non-escape constraints and `AnchorOrder` are checked in the **resulting state**, including transferred anchors of owned places that remain usable.\n718\t7. All moved held authorities, including those of owned storage, satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` in the resulting state; no authority may escape a local anchor on the grounds that its containing value was transferred.\n719\t8. **Local self-blocking prevention:** For each active authority held before the step by a part `r` of `source` whose holder becomes the corresponding part `corr(r)` of `destination`, that authority's footprint contains **no leaf** of `corr(r)` in the resulting state. Transfer is rejected if this local check fails; no global authority-acyclicity check is imposed.\n720\t9. **Destination usability:** `destination` is usable in the resulting state.\n721\t\n722\t**`ok` effect:**\n723\t\n724\t- every source leaf becomes `Moved` and each corresponding destination leaf becomes `Init`;\n725\t- every authority held by source part `r` acquires as its new holder the corresponding destination part `corr(r)`, not indiscriminately the destination root;\n726\t- places owned by `r` become owned by `corr(r)`; their roots follow this owner according to `RootHeld`;\n727\t- value dependencies follow the corresponding places under validity guarantees; no authority is created or duplicated;\n728\t- destination places receive the transferred values and rights without automatically moving storage not owned by a source part.\n729\t\n730\t**Other outcome:** `reject`. `transfer` to a call result is subject to these same rules.\n731\t\n732\tA partially initialized structure may be transferred **field by field**, but only for fields individually eligible for transfer. This does not automatically destructure a value that owns storage **at the container level**: that storage stays with the container if the container is not transferred. Conversion between structures with permuted fields must be handled separately, not as a `transfer`.\n733\t\n734\t## 64. O5 — Explicit Attachment of an Anchor\n735\t\n736\t**Preconditions**\n737\t\n738\t1. `result` is a usable place over which the current frame has exercisable `Modify`.\n739\t2. `anchor` designates an active, valid authority representing a real dependency of the relevant rights or storage; a declared dependency cannot extend an origin's lifetime.\n740\t3. Existing anchors are preserved and the addition respects `AnchorOrder`: the effective anchor's frame is the frame of `result` or an ancestor thereof.\n741\t4. Every dependency carried by a contained value remains represented in the transitive obligations of its container.\n742\t\n743\t**`ok` effect:** `anchor` is added to `D(result)`; the container's transitive dependencies account for it. No additional access right is created. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n744\t\n745\tA safe interface must declare dependencies covering those actually required by its implementation; the annotation itself is not presumed true. An unsafe abstraction bears the obligations of its contract.\n746\t\n747\t## 65. O8 — Controlled Storage of an Authority\n748\t\n749\t**Preconditions**\n750\t\n751\t1. `authority` is active, **derived**, and held by the current frame; a root authority can never be stored by `store`.\n752\t2. `target` is a usable **leaf** under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It holds no other **active** authority. Its data already being `Init` does not by itself prohibit the operation.\n753\t3. The stored authority covers neither a leaf of `target` nor the leaves of storage transitively owned by it.\n754\t4. The effective anchor of `authority` respects `AnchorOrder` relative to the frame of `target`; other dependencies are preserved.\n755\t5. **In the resulting state**, `target` remains under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame (`store_keeps_target_reachable`), and `AnchorOrderHeld` remains true. This is a genuine additional precondition, not an automatic consequence of the preceding ones.\n756\t\n757\t**`ok` effect:** The holder of `authority` becomes `target`; the authority is added to `D(target)`; **only** leaf `target` becomes `Init`. Other fields of a structure, even adjacent ones, do not change their initialization state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n758\t\n759\tOverwriting an active authority already held by the leaf is prohibited; its explicit release may make the target reusable. The resulting-state clause does not by itself prohibit all cycles possible after other operations: general freedom from blocking (`NoLock`) is not adopted here.\n760\t\n761\t# X. Reading, Writing, and Initialization\n762\t\n763\tA leaf's initialization state and the effective right to access it are two distinct conditions.\n764\t\n765\t## 66. O9 — Safe Reading of Regions\n766\t\n767\t**Preconditions**\n768\t\n769\t1. Every leaf being read belongs to a usable place and is `Init`.\n770\t2. `access` is an authority exercisable in `Observe` mode over the region; its **effective** mode actually permits observation of each leaf being read.\n771\t3. Required anchors are valid, and the type and storage contract permits this read.\n772\t\n773\t**`ok` effect:** The logical read succeeds, without modifying `F`, `P`, `I`, `A`, `D`, or arena entries. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n774\t\n775\t`Moved` and `Uninit` cannot be read. This definition does not replace concrete representation and pointer checks of the elementary memory machine.\n776\t\n777\t## 67. O10 — Ordinary Write\n778\t\n779\t**Preconditions**\n780\t\n781\t1. Targeted leaves belong to usable places, and `access` is exercisable in `Modify` mode with **effective** `Modify` right over each leaf.\n782\t2. No incompatible ordinary authority holds an incompatible effective mode over the written leaves (I1).\n783\t3. Anchors and constraints of the relevant types, storage, and abstractions remain valid.\n784\t\n785\t**`ok` effect:** The logical write sets the actually written leaves to `Init` and does not modify authorities or their anchors. An unwritten leaf does not become `Init` by this effect. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n786\t\n787\t## 68. O11 — Write Under Shared Mutation\n788\t\n789\t**Preconditions**\n790\t\n791\t1. Every leaf in the region is usable and declared `K.SharedMutable`.\n792\t2. `access` is exercisable in a mode **at least** `Observe` and has a present effective mode on every leaf, either `Observe` or `Modify`; exercising an authority without an effective mode is insufficient.\n793\t3. Dependencies are valid and the particular shared-mutation discipline guaranteed by `K.SharedMutable` applies.\n794\t\n795\t**`ok` effect:** Actually written leaves become `Init`; authorities and their structural exclusivity I1 do not change. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n796\t\n797\t`writeShared` may update metadata under an `Observe` authority when the contract permits. Stability of observed contents is not automatically guaranteed, and this kernel **does not prove** the absence of data races: that requires the concurrency model's guarantees.\n798\t\n799\t# XI. End of Use, Deallocation, and Allocation\n800\t\n801\tThe logical end of a value does not necessarily entail deallocation of its storage. The following operations treat initialization, ownership, and physical release separately.\n802\t\n803\t## 69. O12 — Logical End of a Value's Use\n804\t\n805\t**Preconditions**\n806\t\n807\t1. `place` is usable and the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over it.\n808\t2. No active descendant over the leaves of `place` **or storage that dies with it** is held outside the places dying with `place`, in a way that would prevent rights from returning or being invalidated. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from this check. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (held by `place` or by a place dying with it, transitively owned storage included) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n809\t3. No place **remaining usable after the effect** depends on an authority held by `place`, its root, or an authority that would become inactive when an owned holder dies.\n810\t4. **No leaf whose use ends, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`). A dynamic pinning obligation must be legally removed by `unpin` before this step.\n811\t\n812\t**`ok` effect:** The leaves of value `place` become `Uninit`; `place` **remains usable as a place** and may legally be reinitialized. Authorities held by `place` and dead substorages are released; dependencies of the ended value are removed; transitively owned places that cease to live are marked dead. The operation does not automatically trigger physical deallocation in the elementary memory machine: an unfreed allocation may constitute a leak, which this guarantee does not prohibit. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n813\t\n814\t## 70. O13 — Release of an Owned Storage Unit\n815\t\n816\t**Preconditions**\n817\t\n818\t1. `place` is a **root of storage owned by a value** (`OwnedRoot`), not an ordinary local directly owned by a frame and not part of an aggregate.\n819\t2. `K.Deallocable(place)` is established, and `K.FreeCompat(place,auth)` justifies deallocation authority `auth`; this identifier is opaque to the authority kernel.\n820\t3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify`; no active derivative over the footprint being freed is held outside holders that die; no surviving place is anchored to an authority of the released region or of its dead holders. The authorities through which the step exercises its `Modify` right over `place`, namely the exercisable authority on which that right rests and its origin chain, are exempt from the check on active derivatives. Moreover, no authority that the effect makes inactive (an authority of the released region, its root included, or an authority whose holder dies) has an active descendant, whatever its footprint, that the same step does not make inactive.\n821\t4. No leaf of the released unit is statically or dynamically pinned. Ending external use does not silently turn a still-pinned leaf into a releasable unit.\n822\t\n823\t**`ok` effect:** `place` and the storage that ceases to live are marked dead; authorities of those regions, including their corresponding roots, become inactive, as do authorities whose holders die. Surviving dependencies must remain valid. Every realization of `free` must satisfy the corresponding deallocation relation of the elementary memory machine (Part III). **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n824\t\n825\t## 71. O14 — Allocation of a New Storage Root\n826\t\n827\t**Static preconditions**\n828\t\n829\t1. `shape` is a **nonempty** abstract shape, represented like the local places of `enterFrame`; an allocation of **zero physical size** may be represented by a logical one-leaf shape.\n830\t2. The provider is a usable place, and **all leaves of the provider** are `Init`; updates to its metadata are permitted through exercisable `Modify` or a valid `writeShared` over `SharedMutable` metadata.\n831\t3. The current frame has exercisable `Modify` over `owner`; new identities and ownership relations must respect `WF3`, `RootHeld`, and liveness restrictions.\n832\t\n833\t**Outcomes**\n834\t\n835\t- `reject`: a static precondition does not hold;\n836\t- `fail`: static preconditions hold, but `K.Satisfiable(provider,shape,s)` is false in the state under consideration; **no state change**;\n837\t- `ok(s')`: creation of a **new storage root** with fresh identity, owned by `owner`; its leaves start `Uninit`, its new root `Modify` authority with no origin is held by `owner`; provider metadata is updated only in accordance with its modification rights.\n838\t\n839\tAllocation success depends on the provider's state: it does not constitute a right to invent physical memory. Any realization of this operation must satisfy the `AllocStep` relation defined by the rules of Sections 9 and 13.\n840\t\n841\t# XII. Call Frames and Return of Authorities\n842\t\n843\tFrames are execution units that receive authorities, introduce locals, and have exit rules distinct from Spec 3's value-passing conventions.\n844\t\n845\t## 72. O6 — Entering a Frame\n846\t\n847\t**Preconditions**\n848\t\n849\t1. The calling frame is the current frame and is live. Each parameter passed to the callee is an active, derived authority held by the caller before the step; the caller is **not necessarily** the frame in which the authority was first derived.\n850\t2. Modes, footprints, anchoring, and parameter-passing rules permit this change of holder; introduction of locals preserves well-formedness and the validity of their roots.\n851\t3. **Origin protection on entry:** for each `Modify` parameter with direct origin `o`, `o` is exercisable in **no mode** (neither `Observe` nor `Modify`) in the **resulting state**, once the new frame's parameters and locals have been created.\n852\t4. **G4:** `AnchorOrderHeld` is checked over the complete resulting state.\n853\t\n854\t**`ok` effect:** A new child frame is pushed onto the stack; active parameters **are yielded** to that frame, which becomes their sole holder; local places and their root authorities are created; valid anchors inherited from the caller are preserved. This transfer creates no additional rights. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n855\t\n856\tThis clause establishes **entry protection**. It does not prove the stronger property of protection throughout the **entire** invocation following other releases or reanchorings. While a `Modify` parameter remains active, its direct origin remains subject to effective-mode restrictions. Any additional protection throughout the invocation is deferred to Spec 3.\n857\t\n858\t## 73. O7 — Exiting a Frame\n859\t\n860\t**Preconditions**\n861\t\n862\t1. The current frame is not the initial frame and has a surviving caller.\n863\t2. Results that must survive have already been legally transferred to their destinations; no active authority or dependency may remain invalidly attached to locals that are about to die.\n864\t3. **No leaf of a place that dies with the frame, including transitively owned storage, is pinned**, either statically (`K.Pinned`) or dynamically (`pin`).\n865\t4. Iterated reanchoring of surviving authorities and anchors reaches the **first surviving active origin**, held either by a surviving frame or by a usable place whose terminal owner survives; it is not permitted to skip a surviving origin merely because it is held by a place. The operation is rejected when no suitable surviving origin exists, particularly if the chain reaches a root of the exiting frame.\n866\t5. Validity of anchors of surviving values and `AnchorOrderHeld` are checked **in the resulting state**. Other state invariants and resource contracts are respected.\n867\t\n868\t**`ok` effect:**\n869\t\n870\t- the current frame is removed from the stack; its untransferred locals and substorages dying with them become unusable;\n871\t- authorities still held by that frame, or by places that have become unusable, are released;\n872\t- origins of surviving derived authorities are reanchored to the **first surviving active origin** of their chains, whether held by a frame or a usable place, and the `D` anchors of surviving values are correspondingly reanchored;\n873\t- reanchoring changes **neither the footprint nor the own mode** of an authority; parameter identities and relations specific to the frame cease their operational role;\n874\t- the calling frame becomes current again.\n875\t\n876\t**Other outcome:** `reject`. Exiting does not entail automatic physical deallocation of all dead places. Deferred cleanup (`defer`) lies outside this kernel. Reanchoring may change the current `origin` relation; protection of a historical borrow lineage during an invocation is a distinct, deferred question.\n877\t\n878\t# XIII. Pinning, Statics, and External Commitments\n879\t\n880\tPinning forbids certain movements or ends of lifetime. It is not a general guarantee of content immutability.\n881\t\n882\t## 74. O15 — Dynamic Pinning\n883\t\n884\t**Preconditions:** `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify` over the relevant leaves, and the operation respects applicable pinning obligations.\n885\t\n886\t**`ok` effect:** A dynamic pinning obligation of fresh identity is installed on the relevant leaves. A leaf is dynamically pinned while at least one active pinning obligation covers it. While an obligation is active, `transfer`, `endUse`, `free`, and `exitFrame` are rejected if they act on leaves it pins; removal of this obligation requires `unpin` under `K.ExternalDone`. Pinning does not make contents immutable. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n887\t\n888\t## 75. O16 — Removal of Dynamic Pinning\n889\t\n890\t**Preconditions:** `unpin` designates one active dynamic pinning obligation, installed by `pin` on `place`; `place` is usable, the current frame has exercisable `Modify`, and contract `K.ExternalDone`, applied to that obligation, attests that all relevant external use has ended.\n891\t\n892\t**`ok` effect:** The designated dynamic pinning obligation ends, and only that one: a leaf it covered stays pinned while another active obligation covers it. The step does not itself modify values, their initialization, or their access authorities. Static obligations are not removed by this operation. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n893\t\n894\t**Associated static rule (`StaticPinnedBottom`):** Statics designated by `K.Pinned` must be places **directly** owned by the **base frame**, not merely transitively owned by a static value. This restriction ensures that they do not die when an intermediate frame exits.\n895\t\n896\t# XIV. Arenas and Collective Invalidation — Reference Abstraction\n897\t\n898\tAn arena is a **library abstraction**, not a memory-kernel primitive. Operations O17 through O19 are **derived** from operations O1 through O16 and abstraction-specific metadata: `newArena` registers an administration without modifying the kernel; `issue` performs `split` followed by `store` (after checking availability); `reset` performs a `Modify` derivation over all storage—through `derive` or `split`—then `release`, before restoring availability. Their contract below is **normative for the standard-library arena** until Spec 10 takes responsibility for it. It authorizes neither new permissions nor an additional access machine. Arena entries (Section 46) constitute the abstract state of this administration; the guard in Section 56 belongs to its consistency contract. The contracts of `HandlePool` and `DataStack` belong to their library abstractions and are not defined here.\n899\t\n900\t## 76. O17 — Creation of an Administered Arena\n901\t\n902\t**Preconditions**\n903\t\n904\t1. `storage` is an existing, usable place under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame. It may be local, static, allocated, or a block issued by a previous arena.\n905\t2. `internal` is explicitly named by the caller, exists, is active, has own mode `Modify`, and `ExercisableAt(s,internal,Modify)` holds.\n906\t3. `internal` is **held by a place**: that place is the arena value; `footprint(internal) = footprint(storage)`.\n907\t4. **Unified administration guard (Section 56):** no **live** administrative abstraction, whether an arena or a library instance such as a handle pool, already uses this `internal` identity. The relevant registries of library administrations are consulted before registration. Two distinct identities may cover common leaves: this is not in itself grounds for rejection.\n908\t5. All leaves of the arena value holding `internal` are already `Init`; construction does not initialize them implicitly.\n909\t6. Dependency, holder, storage, and administration relations respect, in the resulting state, `WF3`, I1, `AnchorOrder`, and `AnchorOrderHeld` over the whole kernel state, and the new entry satisfies `ArenaInv` (Section 55).\n910\t\n911\t**`ok` effect:** A live entry `{storage, internal, available, issued}` is added; the initial `available` set represents the administered storage, and `issued` is initially empty. Registering the entry fabricates no place, access right, or `Init` state. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n912\t\n913\tThe identity of `internal`, not the index of `storage`, is the key to preventing duplication of live administrations. No global constraint requires footprints of live arenas to be pairwise disjoint.\n914\t\n915\t## 77. O18 — Issuing an Arena Block\n916\t\n917\t**Static preconditions**\n918\t\n919\t1. The `arena` entry exists and is live; `internal` is active. `MetaRight` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value holding `internal` (Section 36). Mutation of metadata through `writeShared` alone does not satisfy this condition.\n920\t2. `region` belongs to the administered storage; `destination` is a usable **leaf** holding no active authority; the destination can legally receive the block value in mode `Modify`. The fact that `destination` is already `Init` is not in itself grounds for rejection, in accordance with O8.2.\n921\t   **Local self-blocking prevention: leaf `destination` is not in `region`, including when the region is declared available.** This condition is static and is checked before an availability `fail`.\n922\t3. Applicable **static** validity and non-escape restrictions for the destination are satisfied. The `AnchorOrder` check depending on the new anchor is performed on the **simulated resulting state**, after the availability check described in point 4; it cannot turn an unavailable issuance (`fail`) into `reject`.\n923\t\n924\t**Availability and exercisability checks**\n925\t\n926\t4. If static conditions hold but **at least one leaf of `region` does not belong to `available`**, the operation produces **`fail` and leaves the entire state unchanged**. The empty region is always available. An unavailable region does not permit a prior static rejection to be bypassed.\n927\t5. If the region is available, the effective mode of `internal` on that region must permit `Modify`; otherwise the outcome is `reject`.\n928\t6. The simulated result must satisfy `AnchorOrderHeld` **in the resulting state**; moreover, **in that resulting state, `destination` must remain under exercisable `Modify` by the current frame**, in accordance with O8.5. Issuance into a value with a longer lifetime than a local anchor is not made valid merely by storing it in the caller.\n929\t\n930\t**`ok` effect:** A block authority of fresh identity, derived from `internal` and of mode `Modify`, is issued for the region; it is held by `destination`, which receives the corresponding anchor. The leaves of the region leave `available`, and the issuance is added to `issued` with its authority. **Only leaf `destination` becomes `Init`**; no other leaf of the arena value changes initialization. Previous issuances remain intact and valid. Issuance does not automatically install anchors on third-party values: they must use their lawful dependency mechanisms.\n931\t\n932\t**Outcomes:** `ok`, `fail` (region unavailable after static validation), `reject` (static rule, effective right, or anchoring not satisfied). The destination may be a leaf of the arena value itself: its initialization then constitutes **an explicit write to that destination**, not implicit initialization of the entire arena value's metadata.\n933\t\n934\t## 78. O19 — Collective Arena Reset\n935\t\n936\t**Preconditions**\n937\t\n938\t1. The entry exists and is live. **`MetaRight(s,arena)` requires exercisable `Modify` over the arena value**, in accordance with Section 36. `writeShared` over metadata alone, even when legal, does not replace such exercise. `internal` and the arena's other restrictions remain valid.\n939\t2. **No active authority derived from `internal` covers a leaf of the administered storage.** A derived authority with an empty footprint does not, by itself, block `reset`.\n940\t3. No still-usable place depends, directly or transitively, on an anchor in the **strict origin subtree of `internal`** (`internal` itself is excluded) that covers a leaf of the administered storage. Under precondition 2 and invariant I3, this condition follows from the absence of active descendants covering the storage, and remains explicit in the abstraction contract. Validity obligations of other nested arenas remain satisfied.\n941\t\n942\t**`ok` effect:** All **leaves** of the administered storage become available again, and the issuance list is cleared under the arena contract. No still-active derived authority covering those leaves may remain, by precondition 2; an empty-footprint derivative may remain active without designating any recycled leaf. **No leaf is made `Init` by `reset`**, including in the arena value. **Other outcome:** `reject`.\n943\t\n944\tSafe reuse after `reset` requires that previous issuances **covering recycled leaves**, and the dependencies on which their validity rests, have effectively ceased preventing their reuse. Any active empty-footprint derivatives cover none of those leaves. A historically issued region does not remain permanently unavailable solely for that reason.\n945\t\n946\t# XV. Transitive Dependencies, Returns, and Value Behavior\n947\t\n948\tDependent values may survive transfers and calls only if the required anchors and rights remain valid. Second-class access does not imply an arbitrary persistent reference.\n949\t\n950\t## 79. Non-Escaping Values and Returns\n951\t"}]}}
